\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Activists Take Their Fight To The Streets And Outside The Homes Of At Least Two Supreme Court Justices\b0\par\par\uc2 Jamie Yuccas, Elise Preston \par \uc2 476 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Today, abortion rights activists used Mother`s Day to launch what they called a National Women`s Strike. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston joins us in New York for more -- Elise.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jamie, here in New York, barricades are in place outside St. Patrick`s Cathedral as police try to stay prepared for ongoing demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: What side are we on, my people? What side are we on --\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Abortion rights supporters nationwide are vowing to keep up the fight from Oklahoma to Ohio and outside the Supreme Court today, outrage over a leaked draft majority opinion indicating the Supreme Court may be poised to overturn Roe v. Wade, which would end the constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I will be damned if I don`t fight, and if we don`t fight because we cannot go backwards.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: In Wisconsin today, firefighters confirmed that headquarters of an anti-abortion rights group was a target of arson. Demonstrators on Saturday took their battle directly to the homes of Supreme Court Justices \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: You don`t get to take away my bodily autonomy and enjoy your Saturday night at home.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The Justices` home addresses were leaked online renewing concerns for their safety.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  told a Judicial Conference: "The Court cannot be an institution that can be bullied into giving you just the outcomes you want."\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll out today shows 64 percent of Americans believe the Supreme Court should keep Roe v. Wade in place. The poll also shows those who want to make abortion illegal in their state say abortion is not a right, that abortions are too easy to obtain, or that abortion is against their religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TATE REEVES (R-Mississippi): If you believe as I believe, that`s an unborn child in that mother`s womb. That is -- what we are trying to do is stand up for the rights of those unborn children.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Democrat leaders say the battle on Capitol Hill is not over.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): And here we are on Mother`s Day, a week where this Court has slapped women in the face in terms of disrespect for their judgments about the size and timing of their families.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Today in New York, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  outlined a vote planned in Congress this week aimed at protecting abortion rights. It is not expected to pass, but Schumer says he wants Americans to know where lawmakers stand ahead of the midterm elections -- Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Elise Preston in New York, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020220510ei580002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 8, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Charlie D`Agata, Margaret Brennan, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 8,782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: The future of a woman`s right to choose an abortion is in jeopardy in many states across the country, as an unprecedented leak of a draft Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade creates the political equivalent of an earthquake.\par \par \uc2 There is turmoil around the nation, as Republicans and Democrats scramble to figure out what the political and the practical impact of new abortion restrictions could be.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will be with us. Plus, we will hear from South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Then: Ukraine`s military is on high alert this weekend, bracing for more attacks, as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  plans to celebrate Russia`s annual Victory Day.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 , will be here with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will hear from the CEO of \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 , \uc2 Jim Taiclet\uc2 , about what his company is doing to help provide weapons for the war effort in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, we will take a look at the politics of this year`s round of congressional redistricting fights with former Obama Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 . His new book is "Our Unfinished March."\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 It has been a turbulent week across the country, as one of the most esteemed institutions in our government, the Supreme Court, experienced something that happens all the time here in Washington, the leak of a document to the media. But this leak was explosive.\par \par \uc2 Not only does it draw into question the sanctity of the court, but, if the draft opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  holds, Roe vs. Wade may be overturned early this summer. Currently, abortion access is federally protected up to the point of viability.\par \par \uc2 If overturned, abortion could become illegal or significantly restricted in 23 states. Republicans have been reserved in their reaction, but Democrats are furious.\par \par \uc2 And we go now to the top Democrat in Congress, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who joins us this Mother`s Day from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Happy Mother`s Day to you, Madam Speaker.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): Thank you. Happy Mother`s Day to you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And before we get to abortion, we did have this surprise visit on Mother`s Day by the first lady, Dr. Jill Biden, to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Last Sunday, you were in Kyiv meeting with Ukraine`s president. How quickly can Congress deliver this $33 billion in aid that has been promised?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I think we will be able to do it as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 We have great bipartisanship in terms of our support for the fight for democracy that the people of Ukraine are making. We have respect for the strategy of the president of Ukraine. And we have a recognition of the need for weapons, for sanctions -- more weapons, more sanctions, more economic assistance, and more humanitarian assistance.\par \par \uc2 I was very proud to be there with my colleagues to talk about those very specific issues and our bipartisan support for them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you think you can get that done before the end of the month?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I think we have to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Yes, I think we have to.\par \par \uc2 The specificity with which we discuss these matters with the president, president of Ukraine, the connection that we have with the ambassador, whom you will have on the show later, we`re very current on the needs and the urgency. And, again, we will have bipartisanship as we go forward with it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Madam Speaker, I want to talk to you, of course, about abortion. California`s Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said Democrats have failed to target Republicans on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what he had to say:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (D-California): Where is the Democratic Party? Where`s the party? Why aren`t we calling this out? This is a concerted, coordinated effort. And, yes, they`re winning. We need to stand up. Where`s the counteroffensive?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Madam Speaker, why were pro-abortion rights Democrats outmaneuvered?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I have -- I have no idea. The fact is that we have been fighting for a woman`s right to choose, and that is to choose.\par \par \uc2 We have been fighting against the Republicans in the Congress constantly because the fact is, they`re anti -- not just anti a woman`s right to choose in terms of terminating a pregnancy, but in terms of access to contraception and family planning and the rest, both domestically and globally.\par \par \uc2 This is a constant fight that we`ve had for generations -- I mean, decades, I should say...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : ... in my case in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 And the -- we had been bipartisan early on, support for a woman`s right to choose, until the politics have changed. And that`s what happened to the court.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The science hasn`t changed. The -- but the court changed. And, therefore, they`re deciding that it will be different.\par \par \uc2 I have no idea why anybody would make that statement, unless they were unaware of the fight that has been going on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you have been fighting for decades on this issue.\par \par \uc2 But back when Democrats held majorities in the House and the Senate, 2009, when you were speaker, President Obama was asked about codifying Roe versus Wade and said abortion is a moral and ethical issue and -- quote -- "not the highest legislative priority."\par \par \uc2 Do you think it was a mistake for him, for other presidents not to push harder when Democrats had the majority?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : If I just may, the focus we have right now is an urgent one in order to try to improve -- and try to improve this, whether we`re calling it fake or draft decision, whatever it is. I think that this is a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, in `09, we really did not have a pro-choice Democratic Party. I had to fight against some of the people who did not want to pass the Affordable Care Act because they were concerned that it might enable more freedom of choice. It really didn`t go down that path.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we do have a pro-choice Democratic Congress, and we passed the law months ago, in last, I think, September.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You did in the House, yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : It`s been a while. It`s a number of votes.\par \par \uc2 The -- but the fact also is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the votes aren`t there in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, the Senate is -- you`ll have to talk to the Senate about the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that it puts an urgency on what`s happening in the election. Two more -- one or two more senators could sweep back the filibuster rule for this purpose, and then women would have a right to choose.\par \par \uc2 This is about something so serious and so personal and so disrespectful of women. Here we are on Mother`s Day, a week where the court has slapped women in the face, in terms of the disrespect for their judgments...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : ... about the size and timing of their families.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact is, let`s keep our eye on the ball. And the ball is in the court of those justices, one of whom at least said over and over again that precedent -- that precedence has been established again and again on Roe v. Wade and that -- so, this decision is about being anti-precedent and anti- privacy, and has serious ramifications as we go -- as we go down this path. And it has to be softened.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think there`s a good outcome, but there`s a better outcome as far as this is concerned.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of the...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : And, again, let`s just be prayerful about this. This is -- this is about respect for privacy.\par \par \uc2 What`s next? What`s next? Marriage equality? What`s next, contraception? What is next?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you need to write bills to enshrine those things? Those things you just said you`re concerned about, do you need bills to enshrine those now? Birth control access.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The things you think might be next, do you need to legislate to enshrine those in law right now to protect them, if you think the court may overturn?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : What is really interesting, Margaret, about this is, for decades, I have been trying to say to my Republican friends and women who care about a woman`s right to choose, who contribute to Planned Parenthood and all of those organizations, you can`t do that and expect -- you`ve got to weigh in with your own party on this.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Bush, early on, Republicans were very much about family planning and respect for women. So, the thing is, is that most people always thought that this debate in the Congress was about the termination of a pregnancy, but it wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 My Republican colleagues have said to me on occasion, we`re not for any family planning domestically or globally, because I was trying to get them to support us on some global family planning issues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : We`re not for any of it.\par \par \uc2 And most people don`t know that. And we don`t want to be -- you know, this is a fact. This is a fact. That`s what they believe. And they`re true to their beliefs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But given the -- given the urgency of with which you`re speaking, the Reproductive Choice Act, two pro-abortion rights Republicans in the Senate, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, put that forward. Leader Schumer says it`s not even worth putting to a vote.\par \par \uc2 But when you do have Republicans interested in working together, is that a strategic mistake? You say this is an emergency.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, it depends on what the legislation is and what the impact that it has on women`s lives.\par \par \uc2 The enshrinement of Roe v. Wade into the law is the way in order to protect a woman`s right to choose. I don`t know why they say they`re for that and can`t be for this legislation. Should we all have a discussion and find our common ground? Always. Always.\par \par \uc2 But this -- you`re either for the enshrinement of Roe v. Wade or you`re not. It`s the law of the land 50 -- or nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: year\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The precedence -- precedence of it has been reaffirmed, what, 14 times.\par \par \uc2 And just because there has been...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The Republicans were very clear when they had a presidential campaign that their campaign was to elect a president who would appoint judges who would vote to overturn Roe v. Wade. It didn`t say the science would change.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI: And one more point in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  pulled back the filibuster rule in order to have those justices confirmed by 51 -- by not needing 60 votes, by 51.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : So, this is a political decision on the part of this...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The rule of law in our country should be respected. Women should be respected to make their own judgments with their family, their doctor, their God.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Pelosi, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The CIA director said yesterday that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is doubling down on his invasion of Ukraine and does not believe that he can afford to lose. Violence is now escalating in the east.\par \par \uc2 CBS News senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine -- Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian government blames Russian forces of bombing a school where dozens of people were taking shelter in Luhansk. Here in Kramatorsk, the bombardment has worsened too. This is some of the destruction left behind after a rocket attack struck several residential neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): Amid the ruins, everywhere evidence of ordinary lives violently interrupted, a woman`s shoe, children`s toys scattered on the ground, a favorite jacket now hanging from a tree.\par \par \uc2 A few miles further east in Luhansk, emergency crews dig for survivors in a bombed school, where Ukrainian authorities say 90 people were taking shelter. In Mariupol, government officials say, while all women, children and the elderly have finally escaped from the besieged steel plant, as many as 2,000 Ukrainian forces remain, some medics, some badly wounded, some still fighting to the end.\par \par \uc2 We traveled to the battered village of Velyka Novosilka, around 100 miles north of Mariupol, where 10 Russian battalion groups, around 10,000 troops, have already been redeployed.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and Ukrainian intelligence says those Russians are now massing on the outskirts of this town. It`s clearly come under artillery attack. The explosions are ringing out now.\par \par \uc2 We found Irina Ilyenko and neighbor Valentina Hogenova in the ruins of their homes.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: All around, shredded metal, downed trees, shattered windows, belongings blown out of bedrooms and onto branches.\par \par \uc2 Can you describe what happened?\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "I was just sitting in the corridor when the explosions happened," she said, "covering my ears and praying. The explosions deafened me."\par \par \uc2 Wow.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Oh, my God," she says.\par \par \uc2 Does that happen a lot?\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: There`s no water or electricity. She`s desperate to flee, but says she can`t leave her bedridden husband behind.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "I just want peace to come to this land," she said. "I don`t want any more war and anger."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: That village is right on the path of Russia`s main advance from the south.\par \par \uc2 Here, Kramatorsk is on the firing line from the north. The Russian strategy is to close that gap and surround tens of thousands of Ukrainian troops -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And some breaking news this morning. CBS News has learned that the Biden administration is sending a small group of American diplomats, including the acting ambassador to the U.S. embassy in Kyiv, to counter Russia`s Victory Day celebrations.\par \par \uc2 State Department sources tell \uc2 CBS\uc2  that the embassy hopes to resume operations and raise the American flag there in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Ukraine`s ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Welcome back to the program.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the United States): Good morning, and happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Happy Mother`s Day to you as well and to all the mothers out there.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy has said that he will speak with President Biden and other world leaders this morning. What are you expecting in terms of further support?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, as we celebrate the 77th also anniversary of the end of the World War II, it`s critical that we all do everything possible to stop the war that Russian regime, very much like Nazi regime, started in Europe again.\par \par \uc2 So the president will raise everything that we have been discussing during the past -- this past 73 days, more military support, more sanctions, more financial support to Ukraine. We count on all of our friends and allies to help us with everything, so we can stop Russia while it`s still in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Putin is expected to make a speech tomorrow in Red Square. It`s not clear exactly what he is going to announce, but the CIA director said yesterday Putin is doubling down. What exactly are you preparing for?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, we know that there are no red lines for the regime in Moscow, so we`re preparing for everything.\par \par \uc2 They said they will not go into -- that they were not going to attack us. And they did. They said that there is no war in Ukraine for the past eight years. And we know it was. They said they didn`t take the Crimea. And they did. They said they`re not killing civilians, and yet we see everywhere the deaths of women, children. They torture them. They rape them. They kill them.\par \par \uc2 So we can count that Putin and imperialistic Russia will do everything bad they can possibly try to do. The question is, are we all prepared, the civilized world, to do everything possible to defend our democracy and freedom?\par \par \uc2 And Ukraine certainly is not only ready, but shows for the past 74 days that we bravely defend those values and defend our homes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There is some speculation that Putin could officially acknowledge the country is at war and then start conscripting soldiers, which would help him build up that offensive in the east.\par \par \uc2 Is that what you`re expecting?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, that would be the first time when Putin will say the truth, that it is war and that he is in dire need of conscripting soldiers.\par \par \uc2 I hope that then it will be evident for all -- to all Russians what they are doing in Ukraine, that it`s an aggressive war. They attacked a neighboring country, a peaceful country. And the question is, are they prepared to have more tens of thousands dying in Ukraine for no reason at all?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The U.S. said a few days ago that Russia is planning sham elections and they`re going to try to annex parts of your country, Donetsk, Luhansk in the east, also Kherson.\par \par \uc2 They`re already renaming schools and streets, teaching Russian curriculum, forcing the use of their currency. So, what does dismantling this part of your country actually do? Because if you want to get to a peace negotiation, they`re already sort of swallowing parts of your country and trying to integrate it.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, in addition to all the war crimes they`re doing in Ukraine, this is part of their M.O. We saw it in Donetsk and Luhansk, which they occupied in 2014. We saw it in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 So they tried to create the sham elections. They cannot find enough Ukrainians to participate in them, as we saw in Kherson, as we see in other places. We will never recognize it. The whole world will never recognize it. And we will do everything possible on the battlefield, but also diplomatically, to restore our territorial integrity and sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So the world will never recognize it?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: I`m positive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Meaning?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: I`m positive.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has to be whole within the internationally recognized borders.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, those sanctions the West has put on would stay in place...\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... is another way to say that.\par \par \uc2 You know, we are seeing these reports out of Mariupol that there were some successful evacuations. It`s just a dire humanitarian situation there. Can you tell us what is happening on the ground, who is left there?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Our brave defenders, lots of wounded, a lot of doctors are still there.\par \par \uc2 So, as of yesterday, we saw the reports. And our president has done everything possible to evacuate civilians. Now, while that is a success, of course, to get the civilians, women and children out, we have to remember that 95 percent of Mariupol is destroyed, that tens of thousand civilians died in Mariupol, were killed by Russians.\par \par \uc2 Actually, more Mariupol citizens were killed by Russians in two months than by Nazis during two years of Nazi occupation in -- during the World War II. So, we are calling on everyone to do everything possible and impossible to get our wounded soldiers, to get our heroes, to create all possible corridors in order to get our people still out from Azovstal, where they bravely defend the Ukrainian flag and Ukraine in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s that steel plant where...\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... fighters have been holed up with some civilians.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy said influential states were involved in efforts to rescue hundreds of wounded fighters there. Who exactly is helping? What does that mean? Is that on-the-ground help? Is it just diplomatic?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, we know that \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary-general has been in direct contact with our president, but also with others.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of diplomatic discussions with other states on that. So I think, after the war, we will be able to talk about all the efforts that were -- that were done...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Israel, for example.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: But, on the ground, it`s -- it`s our brave Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 And while evacuating civilians, we have to know that so many of our soldiers from Azov steel, from this plant who were trying -- helping civilians to get out have been killed and wounded during these attempts too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How significant is the intelligence sharing that the West is providing to Ukraine? We hear a lot about the weapons, but what about the actual sharing of information?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: I think the sharing of information between Ukraine and the West with all of our friends and allies is at the level which we never had before. And we really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR OKSANA MARKAROVA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to turn back to the fight over abortion rights with Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina. She`s in Charleston this morning.\par \par \uc2 Happy Mother`s Day to you, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (R-South Carolina): Thank you. And happy Mother`s Day to all the moms out there today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to have a conversation with you here and then we`ll continue it on the other side of this break.\par \par \uc2 But, first up, you are against abortion, but you believe that victims of rape and victims of incest should still have access to abortion. Do you think those exceptions should be backed up with a federal law?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I`m someone who -- I am pro-life, but I do support exceptions for rape. I`m a rape victim myself. And when you realize what`s happened in your life, the trauma, the emotional, the mental, the physical trauma in a woman`s life, that decision -- she should make that decision with her doctor and between her and her God.\par \par \uc2 And I have worked to support those exceptions in my life, not only as a state lawmaker, but now as a member of Congress. And South Carolina has a fetal heartbeat bill that was signed into law that had those exceptions because I told my rape story.\par \par \uc2 And those stories are often missed and criticized, and women are attacked when they tell those stories. And that`s something that I have talked about extensively throughout the years as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you have. And I want to talk to you about those as well, because there is so much nuance here.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to take a quick commercial break.\par \par \uc2 And I want to ask you in more detail what kind of legislation you think could pass at the federal level, what needs to happen at the state level.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us, if you would.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We want to continue our conversation with South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you have spoken publicly about being molested when you were 14, raped at age 16, and how that has shaped your feelings and convictions about rape.\par \par \uc2 I read that you said that it took you 25 years to talk about your attack and that you only shared it with your mother and one of your good friends. So, I wonder what you think about some of these restrictions in states that would require rape victims to provide police reports in order to obtain an abortion.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE:: Right. Well, I can`t speak to other states. I - - from experience as a state lawmaker, I know that South Carolina`s fetal heartbeat bill would not have passed without exceptions for rape, incest, and life of the mother. And I told that story. I felt it was really -- a really important story. A story that`s often missed and not told because women are afraid.\par \par \uc2 And you can even see in public comments and on social media when I talk about it, the ways in which that I get attacked for telling that story. And one of the things that I think, you know, partially that`s missing in this conversation is that, when you have victims. When Ohio did their fetal heartbeat bill, there was a 10-year-old girl that had been found to be pregnant, who was raped repeatedly by her father. And so, as -- I now it`s part of the Republican Party platform, the vast majority of Republicans support those exceptions for rape, and incest, and life of the mother. And it`s important for -- for some of us to step forward and tell those stories that are often missed in all of this as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, to be clear, you would support a vote in Congress, federal legislation, to enshrine those exceptions?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, yes, and I think that -- I think one of the things that`s missing, and I`m glad that you`re bringing this up, in all of the conversation, the media coverage about Roe vs. Wade being overturned, is that what this does -- it`s not an all-out federal ban on abortion, but it puts it back into state legislators and into Congress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: You saw Congress a couple of years ago ban late- term abortions, for example. And so what this does is it puts it back to the states, it puts it back into Congress to deal with and figure out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And it was even \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who we all know was a -- was working for women`s right and thought there was a constitutional right for women to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: She did.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Knew that Roe v. Wade was flawed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, she did. She said that. She said that back in the `90s. It was a problem in her confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 But -- so let`s talk about states then.\par \par \uc2 The governor of South Carolina, your home state, fellow Republican, said if Roe v. Wade is overturned, he wants further restrictions without those exceptions of rape or incest. He`s considering restricting abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. Is that too restrictive?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, I would only support legislation in South Carolina that had exceptions for rape and incest and life of the mother. I don`t believe that that would pass without those exceptions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what about six weeks of pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, that -- that bill has already been signed into law, the fetal heartbeat bill for South Carolina that he signed, I guess it was last year, had a -- it was -- it was six to eight weeks is when the heartbeat is found, but that bill had exceptions for rape and incest and life of the mother. So that law is already on the books in South Carolina. And it will be up to the legislature to determine if they want even more restrictions on it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- but that`s where this gets messy, right, is if you`re saying it`s up to the states. When we look at national polling, it shows that there is a majority of Americans who want to kind of keep the status quo.\par \par \uc2 At the state level, do you actually think the South Carolina legislature is in tune with public opinion here because, I mean, our polling shows more than two-thirds of Republicans say abortion should be generally available or available with stricter limits. Is it a political mistake to just paint this as pro-life, pro-choice?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, I think that some of the polling is murky, too. It depends on how you ask the question and who`s paying for the polling. There is some polling out there that says that there are only 25 percent of Americans, some say up to 30 percent, that want abortion in ever case. They don`t want any restrictions. So that says to me that there`s a vast -- a vast majority of Americans that are OK with restrictions on abortion. And we have some of the most liberal abortion laws in the world. If you look at Europe, there are many European countries that don`t allow abortion after the first trimester or after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Twelve weeks or so.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And in Poland, for example, they don`t allow any abortions unless there it`s rape, incest or life of the mother. And so it is a complicated issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. It`s a bit of an outlier there in Europe. It is.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Right. But, you know, Portugal is 12 weeks. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I want to quickly ask you, you`re being primaried by a Trump-backed candidate. Do you think your position on this is going to make it more complicated for you? Do you think President Trump is still the leader of your party?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, my position on life with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother is in line with my district, it`s in line with the majority of, I believe, voters in my state as well. We`ve raised over $4.5 million for this race. My opponent has raised less than $300,000. And, you know, I`m working very hard to win this, not just by single digits, but by double digits. And I think he`s been given bad advice.\par \par \uc2 My opponent lost -- had her top secret security clearance revoked for leaking classified information about our military.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I live in a very fiscally conservative district, and she voted for the highest tax hike in South Carolina history.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And so there are very stark contrasts in our record.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And we`ve raised the most money. We have the highest polling. And I`ve got 40 days to go until the June primary and I`m looking forward to winning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll watch for it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We now turn to \uc2 Jim Taiclet\uc2 , the CEO of defense giant \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 , which makes some of the weapons the U.S. is sending to Ukraine in its fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 LOCKHEED MARTIN\uc2 : Good morning, Margaret. Happy Mother`s Day to you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you very much for saying that.\par \par \uc2 We hear time and again one of the most powerful tools that the Ukrainians have are these anti-tank, Russian tank busting missiles known as javelins. That`s what your company jointly produces with \uc2 Raytheon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How quickly can you scale up production to get more to them and to backfill what the U.S. has given up?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET\uc2 : Sure, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president visited us in Troy, Alabama, to thank the workforce earlier this week and we really appreciate what he`s done for us.\par \par \uc2 We are, therefore, on our side, accelerating our investment in that factory and in our workforce there. So we`re already investing ahead of time to buy tooling, to expand the plant and also support our suppliers to get ready to ramp up production. So, right now our capacity 2,100 javelin missiles per year. We`re endeavoring to take that up to 4,000 per year. And that will take a number of months, maybe even a couple of years to get there because we have to get our supply chain to also crank up as we do.\par \par \uc2 So, we think we can almost double the capacity in a reasonable amount of time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  had said that a different system, the stingers, they couldn`t even get going on ramping that up until 2023. But you can start when, exactly?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: We`re starting now to ramp it up because we have an active production line right now that the president saw. And, also, we`ve got a supply chain that`s active, in addition to that. So we can start turning up the heat now and ramping the production immediately because of those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said -- well, you implied your basically doing on spec, right?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re anticipating that order is going to come through from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But you`re a businessperson. You have to plan ahead. We don`t know how long this war is going to last. The CIA says, you know, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  thinks he`s got to double down here. So, how long are you planning for with this ramp up?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: We`re planning for the long run, and not just in the javelin, because this situation, the Ukrainian conflict, has highlighted a couple of really important things for us. One is that we need to have superior systems in large enough numbers. So like javelins, stingers, advanced cruise missiles, equipment like that. So, we know there`s going to be increased demand for those kinds of systems from the U.S. --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Throughout -- OK.\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: And for our allies as well and beyond into Asia Pacific most likely too.\par \par \uc2 The second really valuable lesson was control of the air space is really critical. So, the Ukrainians are managing to control their air space. The Russian air force doesn`t have free reign over the entire country. And the reason that they don`t is because the Ukrainians can still fly their aircraft and they also have a pretty effective integrated air and missile defense system. So products and systems like F-16, F-35, patriot missiles, THAAD missiles, we know that there`s going to be increased demand for those kinds of equipment, too, because the threat between Russia and China is just going to increase even after the Ukrainian war, we hope is over soon. Though two nations, and regionally Iran and North Korea, are not going to get less active. Probably they`re going to get more active. So we want to make sure we can supply our allies and our country what they need to defend against that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what do you need to do that, because you did say supply chain is an issue? I read that there`s over, what, 250 microchips or semiconductors in each javelin.\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We know there`s an effort in Congress to get legislation to try to create more semiconductors here instead of relying on Asian suppliers. Can you do this scale-up without that kind of legislation?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: It will be extremely helpful to have the bipartisan innovation act passed, for example, because we do need to invest more in the infrastructure in the U.S. so we have domestic supply, especially in microprocessors. And so our production line can run today, but in the future we`re going to need more domestic capability in microprocessor, not only design, but manufacturing, testing, et cetera, so that were have assured supply of those microprocessors in the future.\par \par \uc2 And there will be other inputs too, but that`s one of the highlighted ones.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But we`ve heard on this program time and again from business people how important that is to get done. Congress still hasn`t voted on it or voted it through.\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have any commitments from anyone here in Washington to get this to the president`s desk soon?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: Well, we know that there`s a lot of support for it both in Congress, in the administration, the Commerce Department, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because it takes time to scale that up?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: Yes, it does. It takes years. And so we`re collaborating right now, for example, with \uc2 Intel\uc2 . It`s one of our partners in trying to drive what we call 21st century security into national defense. And we`re going to need the most advanced processors and we`re going to need them to be customizable to defense needs as well. So having that domestic capability again to go all the way through production and testing is going to be more important in the future than it is even today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You also make F-35s fighter jets, you referenced there. Germany is now trying to buy them. I mean you have a lot of buyers in Europe right now potentially.\par \par \uc2 Do you have enough workers to meet all of these requests?\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: We have enough now, but we know, like, for example, in the F- 16 line as well that we`re building up in South Carolina, actually, we need more workers. And so we`re recruiting heavily. We`ve got a very strong workforce in Fort Worth, Texas, where we make the F-35s. So that production line is running just fine now. We`ve got sufficient employees there to do that. But in other parts of the country, and ultimately in Texas, we`re going to need to actually hire more people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, thank you very much for giving us insight to your business.\par \par \uc2 JIM TAICLET: Glad to do it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And for being here in person.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Every 10 years the 50 states redraw their political boundaries based on population changes. Some states can gain seats in Congress, others might lose some. The process is almost always messy as state parties try to secure an edge for the decade to come.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Ed O`Keefe took a look at four redistricting fights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice over): In four big states this year, two red and two blue, Democrats and Republicans couldn`t agree on a redistricting plan, so courts were asked to intervene.\par \par \uc2 New York`s highest court tossed out a map drawn by the Democratic controlled state legislature saying lawmakers did an end run around a non- partisan commission.\par \par \uc2 New York lost one House seat in the census, and although Republicans currently hold eight House seats, the new map drawn by Democrats would have make it difficult for Republicans to win more than four.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They`re trying to silence the voices of the people in this district.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The court has appointed an official to draw up yet another map due this month.\par \par \uc2 In Illinois, after the state lost a House seat, Democrats eliminated two districts where Republicans were expected to win. Republicans challenged the new map in court, but it`s likely to stand.\par \par \uc2 The GOP is also guilty of creative cartography. Texas is gaining two seats in Congress. And although minorities accounted for 95 percent of the state`s population growth in the last decade, Republicans redrew the map to protect their incumbents by eliminating competitive districts were Democrats were making gains.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department and civil rights groups sued.\par \par \uc2 VANITA GUPTA, ASSOCIATE U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Texas` redistricting plans will dilute the increased minority voting strength that should have developed from these significant demographic shifts.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And in Florida, which also gained a House seat, Republican governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  insisted state lawmakers draw a map that eliminated an historically black congressional district, stretching from Jacksonville to Tallahassee, potentially giving Republicans four more seats.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We are not going to have a 200 mile gerrymander that divvies up people based on the color of their skin. That is wrong.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Democrats have filed suit, but they`re running out of time before November`s elections.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. TRACIE DAVIS (D-FL): The black population in Florida that lives north of the I-4 corridor, their voices will be diluted.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (on camera): When the dust settles after this decades round of redistricting, it remains to see whether either party will emerge with a distinct structural advantage nationwide. Now, whether the new lines are fairly drawn, well, that`s a matter of political preference.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Ed O`Keefe in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we turn now to former Obama administration attorney general, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 . He started a group in 2016, the National Democrat Redistricting Committee, to help the party redraw congressional lines. And he has a new book out called, "Our Unfinished March."\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the book you write both parties have embraced gerrymandering when they were in control of state governments, but you say Democrats were caught asleep at the wheel when Republicans started investing 12 years ago in some of these local races.\par \par \uc2 You know, critics call your strategy just sue to blue. That it`s just all about partisanship. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: No, ours is a fight for fairness. And, yes, we`ve brought a lot of lawsuits -- successfully brought a lot of lawsuits in order to make sure that the process is done in a fair way and so that the American people actually pick their representatives as opposed to politicians choosing their voters. And so sue to blue, that`s what they say when you`re winning in court, which is what we have done at a whole bunch of levels.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think Democrats have an advantage then going into the midterms versus where you started out?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Well, I think we certainly stopped the Republicans when they said they wanted to secure a decade of power in this next decade based on the redistricting that they were going to do. We have blunted that effort and we certainly have more fair maps than we did coming out of the last redistricting cycle.\par \par \uc2 The thing that really worries me, however, is that we have 40 percent fewer competitive seats than I think we should have as a result of what both parties have done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You say this is about fairness, but you haven`t challenged any Democratic gerrymanders. Ed O`Keefe laid out some of those examples there. Both of the maps passed by Democrats were thrown out by courts in Maryland and in New York. Do you have a problem with what happened there?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: I indicated my opposition to what had happened, what the legislature did in Maryland. I agreed with the judge, which he did there. And In New York what I`ve said is that those are not the maps that I would have drawn in New York. My guess is that after the courts look at what happened in New York, you will see maps that are different but not fundamentally different.\par \par \uc2 I think you can`t compare, however, what happened in New York and Maryland to what is going on in Texas, Georgia, potentially Florida, Wisconsin, where Republicans have really gone to town in terms of gerrymanders. Fundamentally different from what Democrats have done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How so?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: They are -- if you look at Texas, which is getting two additional seats strictly as a result of the increase in the Hispanic population, they have not increased that -- the power of Hispanics in Texas at all. In fact, they have created more majority white districts in Texas. The map that you see in New York reflects really a population shift, a hollowing out of the rural areas in New York, as well as an increase in the urban areas in New York.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: So there`s a census basis -- census bureau basis of what`s happening in New York that does not exist in the -- in Republican states.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You heard there what is happening in Florida and what Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  describes. He says that what he is doing with redrawing is race neutral. I know you strongly disagree. Are you saying the gerrymandering there is rooted in racism?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: It`s certainly race conscious. What he is doing there, by doing away with a traditionally black seat is certainly a factor. Race is a factor there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you think it`s to intentionally disenfranchise?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: That`s certainly a component of, I think, their thinking. They`re going after Democrats. And the fact that that -- the Democrats that they`re going after happen to be black I don`t think is necessarily a coincidence. The suit that we won in Alabama was -- where we said that you should have additional representation for a black -- the black inhabitants of Alabama, those districts were certainly drawn with the thought that they would disenfranchise African-Americans in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about Alabama because as I understand it, the Supreme Court has tried not to directly get involved in what they deemed political gerrymandering, but they have signaled a willingness to hear cases that involve issues of race. There are still elections scheduled in November, in the state of Alabama, even though the court will hear this case. Do you think that the maps being redrawn in Alabama will ultimately be deemed to be illegal and therefore the election should be invalid? Is that what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Well, you know, it`s interesting -- no, it`s an interesting thing. Well, they`re going to have an election in November based on maps that judges, including two Trump judges, said were inappropriately, unconstitutionally drawn. The Supreme Court said too close to the election and so we`re going to allow the election to go ahead on those maps that were found to be defective.\par \par \uc2 Now, that the Supreme Court will ultimately do with Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act, which was the basis for the lawsuit in Alabama, will remain to be seen.\par \par \uc2 But that`s one of the things I talk about in my book, this notion of us getting to some structural changes, that we need to look -- we need to ban partisan gerrymandering. We need the structures of our democracy if we`re going to try to -- if we`re going to try to save it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you have a lot of different recommendations in the book, but, I mean, it`s a long to-do list. And the problem that you sketch out here, you say that the entire democratic system, essentially, is broken, as I understand it. Unrepresentative Senate, unnecessary anti- democrat Electoral College, gerrymandered House of Representatives, panoply of state legislatures, and a stolen Supreme Court. A stolen Supreme Court. You say every person having an equal say in our democracy, one person, one vote, is far from a reality.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Yes, I think that`s true.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re saying the entire system is broken. What -- so if Republicans win control of Congress in November, is that election -- does it not have integrity? Do you not accept the outcome of it?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: No, I think your premise goes a little far. I wouldn`t say that everything is broken, but there is --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I was reading from your book there.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: No, what I`m saying -- I would say is, there is a substantial amount of our structure that needs to be repaired, that needs to be examined. And I think we should -- what I`ve tried to put out -- point out in the book is that we have faced these issues before and that we`ve had heroes and heroines in our history that have faced similar kinds of issues and through sacrifice, commitment, they have made a difference. And we have the capacity, I think, to make these kinds of changes. Banning partisan gerrymanders.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the Supreme Court, we have two seats, one stolen from the Democrats that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  should have now. That -- that seat was not filled because it was to close to an election. And then Amy Coney Barrett was placed into a seat while people were actually voting. I mean those are the kinds of things that I think need to be addressed. And what I talk about in the book is to say, look, we should term limit the justices, 18 years, and that every president should have an opportunity to nominate two justices per term and to try to take some of the pressure out of this -- the partisanship and the confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Republicans would, obviously, disagree with your characterization of how that played out.\par \par \uc2 But I do want -- \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , you mentioned, he`s now in your old job as attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There have been critics of him who say that he isn`t being aggressive enough around the prosecutions regarding January the 6th. Do you think that`s right?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: No one knows. I mean, you know, I have great faith in Merrick and in the people at the Justice Department. We won`t really know how aggressive they have been until they are before a camera and announcing a decision, either to indict certain people or not indict certain people. And here`s my prediction, at some point people at the Justice Department, perhaps that prosecutor in Atlanta, are going to have to make a determination about whether or not they want to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The air is going to be --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you do it?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER: Well, I think there`s going to be sufficient factual information. And I think that there`s going to be sufficient proof of intent.\par \par \uc2 And then the question becomes, what`s the impact of such an indictment. I`m an institutionalist. My initial thought was not to indict the former president out of concern of what -- how divisive it would be. But given what we have learned, I think that he probably has to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll leave it on that incredible note.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Holder, thank you for your time and for sharing your book.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. And to all of the mothers watching, Happy Mother`s Day to you. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020220508ei5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Justice Thomas Warns The Supreme Court Cannot Be Bullied\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Today, Americans for abortion rights rallied in several cities including here in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of protesters chanting)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Here, thousands gathered following this week`s leak of a Supreme Court draft opinion revealing that Roe versus Wade could be overturned.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is at the White House with more -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Biden administration announced, it will launch a free 24-hour mental help hotline for pregnant women and new moms who need help.\par \par \uc2 This, as states prepare for a possible end to federal abortion protections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voiceover): From pro-abortion rights protests in Missouri --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We`re not just going to lay down and let the Supreme Court take away our rights.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN leading prayer)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: To a day of prayer in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I think it is murder to kill a baby in the womb.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Americans are voicing strong and conflicting opinions about the Supreme Court`s potential overturn of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  told the Judicial Conference, the Court can`t be " ... an institution that can be bullied into giving you just the outcomes you want."\par \par \uc2 But what Americans say they want on the issue of abortion is complicated. According to a recent Pew survey, a majority say abortion should be legal in all or most cases, but many are open to restrictions and many opponents of legal abortion say it should be legal in some circumstances.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): We are once again forced to defend fundamental principles that we hoped were long settled.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Speaking at a graduation in Tennessee, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said those fundamental principles include voting rights and - -\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The rights of women to make decisions about their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Senate Democrats are scrambling to try to get a Reproductive Rights Bill passed, but so far they don`t have the 60 votes they need -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Christina Ruffini, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, Afghanistan`s Ministry of Vice and Virtue ordered all Afghan women to wear a face-covering burka in public and to return to the directives set when the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was last in power in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 Since last year`s takeover, they also banned girls from attending school after sixth grade.\par \par \uc2 Today, the death toll from an explosion at one of Havana`s most luxurious hotels grew to 26. New video shows the moment of the massive blast due to a suspected gas leak. Dozens were hurt.\par \par \uc2 Police in a suburb of Washington, D.C. are searching for suspects following a brazen drive-by shooting. It happened in District Heights, Maryland and was captured on a surveillance camera.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Multiple gunmen drew weapons and opened fire. Two boys in a nearby park were wounded.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020220510ei570005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Jericka Duncan, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 2,310 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a picture of the Capitol there.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren is urging the Senate to take action to support abortion rights after a leaked draft of a majority opinion shows the Supreme Court could be set to overturn the landmark 1973 abortion rights ruling, Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In her book "Persist" now in paperback, Warren writes, "In the same way that Congress passed laws to provide access to health care -- think Medicare and Medicaid -- Congress should pass a law to protect nationwide access to abortion."\par \par \uc2 But such a bill would face tough odds passing the Senate and Senator Warner joins us now. Tough odds is an understatement. Do I have that right?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): You do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t have the votes for it, but you`re going to go forward to make everybody vote. What`s the purpose of that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): We want to get everybody on the record, because I think most of all, we have to remember who will be affected by this Supreme Court decision. It`s not well-to-do women. Well-to-do women can still get on an airplane, fly to another state, fly out of the country, if they need to, to have access to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Who is this going to fall on? It is going to fall on poor women. It`s going to fall on women of color. It`s going to fall on girls who are molested by a family member. It`s going to fall on people who were raped. It`s going to fall on the mama who right now is already working two jobs to try to support the children she has.\par \par \uc2 This is about making sure that everyone in this country has a right to make the decision for themselves about a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And we just saw a map on the screen that said "certain or likely to end abortion."\par \par \uc2 When you look at some of the states where the numbers are not as high as the overall national numbers of at least 60 and sometimes 80 percent, you`ll find where people support this. Why not leave it up to the states when you have so many where the numbers may not be that high, and you look at that map and you see how many are almost certain to try to get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): You know, actually, I do want to focus very much on the idea of what it means to outlaw abortion. I lived in an America where abortion was illegal, but it didn`t mean that all abortions stopped. It just meant the safe legal abortion stopped.\par \par \uc2 I lived in an America where women bled to death from back alley abortions. I lived in an America where women developed infections, horrible complications from unclean abortions.\par \par \uc2 I lived in an America where young women took their lives rather than go forward with an abortion -- with a pregnancy that they could not manage.\par \par \uc2 Look, we pass laws at the Federal level to decide minimum wage, to decide access to healthcare. This is about protecting, not the people with power. This is about protecting all of the people in our country who don`t have power.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator, what you`re saying is true and yet there are states that have said despite that, we want to live by a different principle, and a common refrain you hear is, if you don`t like those laws in your state, you should just move. What do you say that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): We don`t do that anyplace else in our Federal government. We don`t say if you don`t like the minimum wage in Alabama, move somewhere else. We say, as a nation, we care for each other and we decide, for example, that work is worth a certain amount.\par \par \uc2 We decide, for example, on Medicaid, that there is going to be for the poorest among us at least some access to healthcare. We decide this for children to make sure that there`s going to be money, we decide this on food, and we should decide this on this basic health decision that will hit the most vulnerable among us.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who feel like, oh, we`re in states where this will never happen. First of all, we can`t turn our backs on the rest of those who will be hurt. But secondly, remember what this opinion is about. It has opened the door to a whole lot of ugliness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Opened the door to the question, contraception, criminalizing a woman who seeks an abortion. Will every miscarriage now be investigated? Will women be charged if they are working too many hours and put their pregnancy at risk? This is a kind of invasion that if the Supreme Court has its way here, and we don`t fight back at the Federal level, could touch the lives of every one of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, just like you`re saying now. In the book you write, you believe in a woman`s choice, no matter how difficult the decision. There is a question, should there be any limits on abortions?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I`m going to put it this way. I think that the woman who is most affected should be at the heart of this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I think that she should be able to call on her partner.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Her mother, her priest, her rabbi. She should be able to bring in the people she needs. But in a pregnancy, I don`t believe that it is the state that should intervene with its heavy hand and make the decision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s an extraordinary thing for a government to tell a person they need to go forward with a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Senator Elizabeth Warren, we have a lot more we could talk to you about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Please come back. Please come back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I will.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Please come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I will. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The paperback edition of "Persist" is out right now.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up, why more women are choosing to start a family on their own.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we hear from an adult child from a single mom about what made their childhood so special.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. Jericka, you are going to kick it off for us.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay, so we all like social media. We use \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I use it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay. You use it. All right, we`ve got a grumpy old man on our table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, my "Talk of the Table" is a new study showing how social media may affect mental health. The study from England looked at 154 people between the ages of 18 and 72 who said they use social media every single day.\par \par \uc2 Well, some of them were asked to stop using social media for a week. Others were told to keep scrolling as usual, spending about eight hours -- eight hours a week on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and TikTok. When the week was over, researchers said the group that took a break from social media had significant improvements in wellbeing, depression and anxiety compared to those who kept up with the social media sites.\par \par \uc2 So seeing a connection there, and I think because mental illness affects so many people, millions of people in this country and around the world, and a lot of people don`t get the treatment. So just something like this could be a potential player in helping to relieve those stresses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`ve noticed when I`ve turned my phone off, or my battery went dead, and I panicked for a little bit, I had that anxiety that ran over me. And then I just left my phone alone. When I came back to it, I realized the world didn`t end and I actually felt a lot better about myself because I wasn`t as stressed responding to text, e-mails and on social media, just scrolling for hours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The e-mails, this is the new news. I think I want to skip that phone like a stone and then share.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you`re right. Just take a break.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You`ve got to take a break.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take a break from social media.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Take a break, take a breath.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is about a young woman named Laura Young who was shopping at a Goodwill in Austin, Texas and came upon what she thought was a very, very old looking bust. Do we have a picture of the bust?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have we got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was priced for $34.99 at Goodwill. She picked it up and four years later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see where this is going.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Four years later, she has managed to get it confirmed, identified as a bust from the dawn of the Roman Empire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The dawn of the Roman Empire. It is going to -- it`s believed that it was last seen in Germany.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No price on this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nate wants to know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two things that I think are great about this. It was last seen in Germany in a heavily bombed area and it`s believed after the U.S. occupation of Germany, post-World War Two that a U.S. soldier brought it back.\par \par \uc2 So first of all, I just love the image of the U.S. soldier coming home and someone picking up his luggage and had been like, "What do you have in here?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is so heavy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is so heavy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Second thing is, the name of the person, the bust there is Sextus.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Sextus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sextus.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, not a name you see and you hear much anymore. I think we might want to bring it back. He`s a military leader. He tried to take on Caesar and lost. He was beheaded, executed, and there is --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And there he is in the seatbelt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The happy ending to this other than the name Sextus may be making a comeback is, it`s going back to Germany and it`s going to be placed in its prior location.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m bringing in Sextus back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice. Well done. I thought you were going to give me a price tag.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. There`s no price tag.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People go antiquing and they find something special.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is priceless.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right, that makes sense. All right, here we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an ancient artifact.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my talk with the table is some unwanted visitors caught on camera. They were so cute.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Better not be roaches.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no. Not roaches. Jericka, roaches growing up?\par \par \uc2 All right, the homeowner didn`t even mind. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I leave my garbage and their own little furry babies. Hi. My goodness. You are so sweet. Hi, Mama.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: That`s the mama bear.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hi mama. Hi. I don`t even mind because you`re so pretty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t even mind you`re spreading trash around. You`re so pretty. I`m not sure I would respond like that if there were bears right outside. But you know what, this is a metaphor on life. You know what I mean? Sometimes there`s people throwing around trash. But if you notice the pretty things, if you notice --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: From afar. Because she was that high. Let`s just be honest. She was not on the ground level.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, who`s going to pick up all of that blowing trash around?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, not with the bears out there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, more women are making the decision to become single mothers by choice.\par \par \uc2 CBS News producer Wendy McNeal --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: There she is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Wendy -- will share her journey to motherhood on her own. But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220510ei56000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Demand For Abortion Medication Increases Ahead Of SCOTUS Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 484 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Janet Shamlian joins us now from Texas with a look at another major issue in this debate, abortions using pills.\par \par \uc2 Janet, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Jericka, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the laws on a medication abortion really are a patchwork from state to state. But if abortion is no longer constitutionally protected, a lot of women could lose access to it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A medication abortion is two medications used together to terminate a pregnancy, generally within the first 10 weeks. The pills are taken over two or three days. It is the most popular option in 2020, making up more than half of all U.S. abortions.\par \par \uc2 What are you seeing in terms of demand for medication abortions?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE PITNEY (Telehealth abortion provider): We are seeing a huge increase in demands.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Christie Pitney is a telehealth abortion provider licensed to prescribe the pills in five states and the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Our rights are not up for --")\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Medication abortion is seen as the next battleground in the abortion battle. Nineteen states have already banned telehealth abortions, requiring a clinician be present when the woman takes the medication including South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Right now, two appointments are required for a medication abortion, one for the initial consultation and one to receive the medication. Some lawmakers want to mandate three appointments, the consultation, and one for each of the medications.\par \par \uc2 This woman is the clinic director.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Because it is already a very huge lift to have these women come back for two appointments, come back after their informed consent then asking them to come back to finish taking the medication that they have been taking at home safely for years and there is no medical reason.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In other cases, women are using overseas providers like the Aid Access in Austria, which sends the medication from pharmacies in India. \uc2 Pitney\uc2  works with that group and says interest has soared.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE PITNEY: Monday was 1,290 visitors, Tuesday was 38,530 visitors. So, that`s really significant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Aid Access says many of the inquiries that it has been fielding over the last few days have been women wanting to get the pill prescribed now not because they need it now, but just in case they do in the future -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Janet, thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220510ei560002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Russia Intensifies Mariupol Assault; Three Killed in Israel Stabbing Spree; Wall Street`s Wild Ride; The Future of Abortion; Abortion Fight`s Next Battleground; F.D.A. Restricts Use of J&J Vaccine; Severe Weather Looming; Heard-Depp Defamation Trial; The Rising Cost of Bread; The Quick Wit of Ben Schwartz; Elizabeth Warren Live; Single Mother By Choice; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jericka Duncan, Charlie D`Agata,  Jill Schlesinger, Scott MacFarlane, Anthony Mason, Janet Shamlian, Rikki  Klieman, Vladimir Duthiers, Carter Evans, Wendy McNeal \par \uc2 13,467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings on this first Friday in May. You made it. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I`m Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off. Happy Mother`s Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Here is today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Everything that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wanted, has essentially backfired on him.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): Another way to put it, everything \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  didn`t want is happening.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Attempts are underway to evacuate civilians from Mariupol while Russia bombards a U.S. steel plant.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The markets have their worst day in years as the Fed works to keep their economy on track.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Our U.S. economy could year off that road and land in a recessionary ditch.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And we spoke to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  about the possible long- term consequences if Roe v. Wade is overturned.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (Former Presidential Candidate): You have no idea who they will come for next.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  will officially become the first Black White House Press Secretary.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jean-Pierre will replace the \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY (Fox News Channel White House correspondent): Sorry to see you go.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (Outgoing White House Press Secretary): Are you?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A suspected terrorist attack in Israel: At least three people were stabbed to death and four more wounded.\par \par \uc2 MAN: "Game of Thrones" fans, listen up. A new trailer for the prequel is full of intrigue, and dragons, of course.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Game of Thrones" prequel.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FOXX (Actor): I was incensed.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jamie Foxx reportedly rush to help Dave Chappelle when he was attacked on stage. Now, check out his spot-on impression of his fellow comedian.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE FOXX: All the other comedians just stood there, waiting for me to die, so they could take over. I`ll see you, Chris Rock. It`ll never happen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The stock market takes a nosedive after the Fed announced a 0.5 percent increase in the interest rate.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  Chairman): We have a good chance to have a soft or soft-ish landing.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): Anytime you have to add "ish" to something, I don`t feel reassured anymore. Yes, this roller coaster is safe-ish. These clams are fresh-ish. Yes, baby. We`re monogamish, you know how this is going to end.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy. Noah is right again. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trevor is on fire right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he really is. Jericka, glad to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy early Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is already off celebrating.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a great show for you today, no "ish" about it including a look at how the war in Ukraine could affect the cost of bread right here in the U.S. Prices have already surged and are set to go even higher.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane shows us how it could affect bakeries in your neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, Anthony Mason talks to fast-talking actor Ben Schwartz, the voice of "Sonic, the Hedgehog." You may know him for playing some shadier characters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You kind of made a career out of playing slippery people.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ (Actor): Oh, define slippery. I want to see how you navigate this. Define slippery.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Come on, you know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I would say, you know what I love to play? I love to play the arrogant idiot. I love to play the idiot that has no idea he is not nailing it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony looks at Ben`s rise in comedy starting as a humble page for \uc2 David Letterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that. But we`re going to begin with the news this morning and breaking news in Ukraine where soldiers trapped inside a Mariupol steel works maybe making their last stand against Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 A Ukrainian military drone filmed, gigantic explosions rocking that plant, the last holdout in the city that Russia now controls. British Intelligence believes there is new urgency for Russia to seize that facility for propaganda reasons.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in Ukraine for us once again. Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Some soldiers making a last stand there, but there are new efforts to help civilians who are trapped in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we can confirm an evacuation is currently underway as we speak, and it is going well according to the Red Cross. They are reluctant to share any details until everybody is out safely.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In between the bombing and the bullets at the besieged steel plant, rescue teams has somehow managed to evacuate almost 500 civilians. Yet the gun battles have continued and a Russian separatist Commander says Ukrainian forces have run out of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "By the ninth of May, we`ll try to take it all," he said.\par \par \uc2 That`s Victory Day when Russia celebrates the defeat of Nazi Germany conquering Mariupol for good would hand President Putin a badly needed trophy at a horrific human cost.\par \par \uc2 Survivors describe a living hell and many are psychologically scarred by their ordeal, International Red Cross spokesman Chris Hanger told us.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HANGER (International Red Cross spokesman): When our teams met these civilians, they were obviously devastated. They`ve not seen the sky for over two months, and the moment that they got out, they saw their city, their home destroyed, completely destroyed. So some people were asking, actually, for a priest, some people were just crying and some people were just silent.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Twenty-five-year-old Tatiana Trotsak (sp?) cuddled her dog, Daisy and marveled at the blue sky and bright sun.\par \par \uc2 (TATIANA TROTSAK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "I hope there will never be any kind of booms here," she said, just fireworks and thunder.\par \par \uc2 Having survived the Russian onslaught in Bucha herself, Yana Melnichenko (sp?) drove down here to meet her brother and mother, who fled from Azov just north of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 Can you describe what it was like there?\par \par \uc2 (ALEXEI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "It was hard. We didn`t have any water, no internet connection. We were hiding in the basement," Alexei (sp?) told us and the Russians looted the supermarkets.\par \par \uc2 Is the whole family back together now?\par \par \uc2 (YANA MELNICHENKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Yes," she says. "This is the best day."\par \par \uc2 And yet this whole region is bracing for much darker days to come.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The Pentagon has confirmed that the U.S. has been sharing Intelligence with the Ukrainian forces since this conflict began. We have now learned that some of that Intelligence was used to help sink the Moskva battleship last month. Officials tell CBS News, U.S. Intelligence provided the location of the ship in the Black Sea, but had no role in the decision to strike it -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Charlie D`Agata and our CBS News crew, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israel is on edge this morning after another deadly attack blamed on Palestinians. Three men were stabbed to death and four others were put in - - were hurt, rather, in a town near Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Last night, police are still searching for two suspects from a town in the West Bank. The Israeli Prime Minister called the suspects terrorist and said they will pay the price.\par \par \uc2 It`s the latest in a series of attacks on Jewish Israelis over the last two months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here at home, new concerns over inflation led Wall Street stocks to have their worst day in two years. A huge selloff yesterday erased all of the gains from Wednesday, the market`s best day since 2020.\par \par \uc2 All the major indexes made big up and down moves after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates by half a percentage point to fight inflation. CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here and I`m glad she`s here because we have a lot of questions, we`ll jump right into it.\par \par \uc2 So the stocks jumps Wednesday, after the announcement and then yesterday why the huge selloff?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think that what happened at the Fed meeting day was that people said oh, the Fed is not going to raise rates by 0.75 percent. It`s just going to be 0.5, and they kind of got excited and happy about that, drove stocks higher. That`s why I said yesterday morning, check your 401 (k)`s this morning.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday after more thought, I think the same issue remains: Can the Fed raise interest rates by enough to kind of clamp down on inflation without throwing the economy into a recession? And that`s the big question and that is making people nuts right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It has been a rough year for the stock market even before yesterday. So what do people do with their money now? Are there safe places to invest?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Okay, couple of things. Number one, take a deep breath. If you`re long term --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t panic.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I mean, you can panic, but you`ve got to calm yourself down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Because we don`t want you to act. If you`re a long-term investor, this is actually not bad news. You`re going to put new money in, you`re going to buy shares at lower levels, things are on discount, 10 to 20 percent from the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 However, if you need your money within the next year, you`ve got to make sure it`s safe, it cannot be at risk. Maybe you made a mistake, you had too much at risk. You`ve got to get that money out and you want to put it in someplace safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you`re talking about those that are thinking about retiring or maybe sending the kids to school.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, let`s just think of it two ways. One is, if I`m about to retire, I may not want to go to cash because my money has got to last me 20 to 30 years. But if I need to write a tuition check, I`ve got to get that money out of risky assets, put it in something safe.\par \par \uc2 What is safe? A checking, a savings, or money market. You get the best rates by using an online institution. If you don`t need your money for one to five years, I encourage people to go check out the government savings bonds that are linked to inflation, I bonds.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Treasury Department announced that I bonds can be purchased with a 9.62 percent guaranteed interest rate, you can buy 10,000 --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Guaranteed?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Guaranteed, my friend, by Uncle Sam, and by the way, you can buy $10,000.00 per calendar year, so check it out treasurydirect.gov.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it when you join us. Dollar, dollar Jill, you all. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Taking a deep breath, Jill. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Also, I bonds. Good to know.\par \par \uc2 All right, moving on. Former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is weighing in on the potential decision of the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade. In fact, she told "CBS Evening News" anchor and Managing Editor, Norah O`Donnell, it is about abortion rights, yes, but also more than abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON: This opinion is dark. It is incredibly dangerous and it is not just about a woman`s right to choose it. It`s about much more than that. And I hope people now are fully aware of what we`re up against because the only answer is at the ballot box to elect people who will stand up for every American`s rights.\par \par \uc2 And any American who says look, I`m not a woman. This doesn`t affect me. I`m not Black. That doesn`t affect me. I`m not gay. That doesn`t affect me.\par \par \uc2 Once you allow this kind of extreme power to take hold, you have no idea who they will come for next.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Janet Shamlian joins us now from Texas with a look at another major issue in this debate, abortions using pills.\par \par \uc2 Janet, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Jericka, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the laws on a medication abortion really are a patchwork from state to state. But if abortion is no longer constitutionally protected, a lot of women could lose access to it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: A medication abortion is two medications used together to terminate a pregnancy, generally within the first 10 weeks. The pills are taken over two or three days. It is the most popular option in 2020, making up more than half of all U.S. abortions.\par \par \uc2 What are you seeing in terms of demand for medication abortions?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE PITNEY (Telehealth abortion provider): We are seeing a huge increase in demands.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Christie Pitney is a telehealth abortion provider licensed to prescribe the pills in five states and the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Our rights are not up for --")\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Medication abortion is seen as the next battleground in the abortion battle. Nineteen states have already banned telehealth abortions, requiring a clinician be present when the woman takes the medication including South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Right now, two appointments are required for a medication abortion, one for the initial consultation and one to receive the medication. Some lawmakers want to mandate three appointments, the consultation, and one for each of the medications.\par \par \uc2 This woman is the clinic director.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Because it is already a very huge lift to have these women come back for two appointments, come back after their informed consent then asking them to come back to finish taking the medication that they have been taking at home safely for years and there is no medical reason.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In other cases, women are using overseas providers like the Aid Access in Austria, which sends the medication from pharmacies in India. \uc2 Pitney\uc2  works with that group and says interest has soared.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE PITNEY: Monday was 1,290 visitors, Tuesday was 38,530 visitors. So, that`s really significant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Aid Access says many of the inquiries that it has been fielding over the last few days have been women wanting to get the pill prescribed now not because they need it now, but just in case they do in the future -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Janet, thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning now to a big development in the fight against the coronavirus. The F.D.A. has announced it is putting strict new restrictions on the use of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine due to the risk of rare, but potentially life-threatening blood clots.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s only recommended for people who cannot get another vaccine or won`t otherwise be vaccinated. This comes as COVID cases are rising nationwide and the U.S. nears an incomprehensible one million deaths from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 For more on all of it, we`re joined now by White House COVID-19 Response Coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jha, thank you for being with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2  (White House Coronavirus Response Coordinator): Good morning. Thanks for having me here, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the country has been on quite a journey and the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . has been on quite a journey with this \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine from approval to guidance in December and now new guidance, what`s behind the latest information?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Yes, so Tony, there is not any new data that`s driving this. What you`re hearing from the F.D.A. is we do know of this very, very rare side effect, called TTS, and what this is, is a clot that some people develop, you know, it happens about three in a million, so very rare.\par \par \uc2 So what they`re suggesting is that given that people have good alternatives, \uc2 Moderna\uc2  and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , two excellent vaccines that don`t have this rare side effect that they`re strongly recommending that people get those instead of getting the J&J.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In retrospect, did we not do enough testing ahead of time?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Now, it`s a very good question. These are very rare side effects. Again, just to put this into context, a bad side effect from this vaccine is far rarer than the bad side effect from taking a daily aspirin.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not like we`re talking about something that would have been easily picked up in a clinical trial. These were large clinical trials that were done. This rare side effect wasn`t seen.\par \par \uc2 Again, I still believe this is a very good vaccine. The point the F.D.A. and \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . are making is because there are alternatives that don`t have this, people should consider getting one of those instead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dr. Jha, we`ve got Mother`s Day weekend ahead of us, people are going to be gathering on Sunday and it comes at a moment that the country is approaching one million fatalities at a minimum tied to COVID- 19, and case numbers are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 So what guidance if any, do you have for families looking to get together this weekend?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Yes, so first of all, this million deaths we`ve been tracking, you know, depending on which measure you use, Hopkins or the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 ., at some point this month, we`re going to hit that one million mark, just a really catastrophic, horrible mark for our country. And I think we`re going to have to make sure that we, as a country resolved to do better moving forward.\par \par \uc2 In terms of this Mother`s Day weekend, what we`ve learned over the last two years is we know how to gather safely. If you have people who are vaccinated and boosted, it`s much, much safer. If you`re with a high risk person, let`s say an elderly person, I still recommend getting a test beforehand, it reduces your risk.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, we can gather safely if we do the things that we`ve learned over the last few years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is good news, indeed. Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ASHISH JHA\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  is talking to Norah O`Donnell for this week`s "60 Minutes" and giving new insight about what it was like to serve in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 In a new book, Esper says Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seriously proposed bombing Mexico while he was in office as a way to stop drug cartels. He also describes a plan proposed by one of Trump`s top advisers to send 250,000 troops to the Southern border to stop caravans of migrants. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER: And I told this story to General Milley and my Chief of Staff. I said, "Let`s be safe. Let`s just check and make sure that this isn`t being worked somewhere in the building." And Milley comes back days later and the door opens up and he is waiving a document is in hands and he says something like, "Secretary, you`re not going to believe this."\par \par \uc2 And that`s when he explains to me that yes, they were working that we had developed a plan, initial concept of how this might happen, and I was just flabbergasted, that not only was the idea proposed, but that people -- people in my department were working on it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, Esper says he ordered those plans to be halted. You can see more of Norah`s complete interview with former Defense Secretary Esper this Sunday night on "60 Minutes" and then Monday, he will join us live here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to both those conversations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Definitely looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A severe weather threat could affect up to 43 million Americans later today. It includes the potential for tornadoes from Alabama to Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, Mother Nature does know that it`s Mother`s Day weekend, right?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): I know, I`m trying to talk to her here. The beginning of the weekend is not so good, but it does improve, but we have to get through this severe weather first and we`re talking all hazards at play.\par \par \uc2 Hail, damaging winds, and lightning which of course can heat the air it passes through to 50,000 degrees Fahrenheit. That`s five times hotter than the surface of the sun.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got the storms, our TORCON value, the higher the number, the greater the threat. It goes from one to 10, we have threes and fours on our map. And as we go through the day today, those storms will go through Georgia, the Carolinas on into the Mid-Atlantic as well.\par \par \uc2 And we need the rain in Charleston. We`re nearly five inches below average. Then of course, the big derby this weekend. Thankfully, the weather will be cooperating and of course, you can catch all-day coverage featuring our virtual view technology on The Weather Channel Network and Nate, there is a horse with a weather theme name "Summer" is tomorrow. I am putting all my money on that guy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so am I. If you`re betting on it, I`m betting on the horse, too.\par \par \uc2 All right, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more graphic testimony from Amber Heard to back her claims that her ex-husband, Johnny Depp, physically abused her. We`ll have more on that in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, the price you pay for bread is expected to rise sharply this summer. Bakers tell us how the war in Ukraine is playing a role in higher costs for American families.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, more details on the man accused of attacking Dave Chappelle on stage. We`ll show you why he is now facing lesser charges than the ones he was originally arrested for.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Johnny Depp testified under oath for four days that he never hit his ex- wife Amber Heard. Now, she is telling the same Court that he was lying. Heard testified in great detail yesterday about Depp`s alleged physical abuse. Depp is suing her for $50 million because he claims she ruined his career by going public and describing herself as a domestic abuse survivor.\par \par \uc2 Heard is countersuing Depp for $100 million. She claims his lawyers falsely accused her of making up claims against him.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has the latest from the Courtroom. We warn you that Heard`s testimony includes graphic accounts of alleged abuse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD (Actress): It felt like he was on top of me and I`m looking at him in his eyes, I don`t see him anymore. I don`t see him anymore. It wasn`t him. It was black. I`ve never been so scared in my life.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Sobbing through her second day of testimony, Amber Heard described Johnny Depp`s alleged attacks in graphic detail.\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD: My head was bashing against the back of the bar and I couldn`t breathe.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: She accused Depp of slapping and kicking her during a private flight with others.\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD: No one did anything. I felt so embarrassed that he could kick me to the ground in front of people.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And Heard broke down while claiming that during an alcohol fueled rage in Australia, Depp sexually assaulted her.\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD: I didn`t want to do this. I don`t want to do this.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Her lawyers then showed images she took of the trashed home from that trip, including bloodstains.\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD: There`s blood on the carpet. I could see blood drips. My forearms were cut. My bottoms of my feet are sliced up pretty good.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS. I`m Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And CBS News Legal Analyst, Rikki Klieman joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Rikki, we`ve covered stories in the same wheelhouse before. When you look at what this case is about, jurors need to be convinced that Johnny Depp did not physically abuse Amber Heard.\par \par \uc2 When you listen to the testimony over these last couple of days, what do you make of what Amber Heard has had to say thus far?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Jurors will always have their own opinions, so you`re going to have some jurors, there is no doubt, who are going to be dramatically moved by Amber Heard`s testimony, because it`s not only the graphic detail she has, but it`s also backed up by photographs, by text messages. The photographs of the bruises themselves.\par \par \uc2 So how does another juror who is skeptical about her testimony argue with the first juror? Well, that juror says, "She`s an actress." This is something that she does for a living.\par \par \uc2 This is, to quote Johnny Depp`s lawyers, "This is the performance of her life." But the only way for that juror to be able to say how she got those horrendous bruises is to say, well, she hit him, he defended himself.\par \par \uc2 Well, that may be a stretch for many a juror. Her testimony was very, very powerful, because of what backed it up.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: When you talk about the performance of a life that came from Johnny Depp`s team, and then you had statements that were also released by her where she said that, you know, if you`re truly innocent, then you wouldn`t have promised to, quote, "put the monster away." She talked about the libel case in the U.K., the restraining order that was granted when they divorced.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think they released those statements? What was the legal strategy there?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Both sides really needed to release statements because you are now going to have a 10-day break, which is almost unheard of, in any case. So you have jurors who are going home, and the last thing that they have heard is Amber Heard in agonizing testimony or on the other hand, an acting testimony.\par \par \uc2 But if it is agonizing testimony, Johnny Depp needed to go forward and really make a statement, and then she needed to respond, blaming not only Depp for absolutely not taking responsibility, but blaming Depp`s lawyers as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Rikki, at the end of this, the jury is going to rule simply on that question that Jericka mentioned, did Johnny Depp abused Amber Heard or is there a bigger ruling in there?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Well, there is a bigger ruling involved because what the jury is really being told is, were the statements of Amber Heard proved by Johnny Depp`s side to be done with malice? Meaning, that they were false.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: So we have to believe in order for Johnny Depp to collect anything that Johnny Depp has proven by clear and convincing evidence that she has lied. It is a very high burden.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Only in the op-ed is the question because they`ve made all these other claims.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That outside of the Court context, you would think would be defamation lawsuits in their own right.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: That is correct. But the op-ed is the only thing that we are considering here because as Johnny Depp`s witnesses put forth, the op- ed was in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right, and he says it lost him money and work.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Well, and I think, on that score, whatever lost him money and work, the damages are proven. The question is where they caused by Amber Heard`s op-ed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Rikki Klieman, thank you as always for coming in.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, everything is more expensive now including dread. We`ll show you why and how you could be paying even more for a loaf this summer.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Americans hit by the worst inflation in decades could face a summer surge in prices for bread and baked goods. The overall cost of groceries already up 10 percent in March from a year earlier, and now, folks in the bread industry are warning that prices could rise another 35 percent in the months ahead, pun not intended, due in part to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is outside a grocery store in Bethesda, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a number of leaders in the industry behind breads and baked goods and they raised a red flag. They said likely by the middle of summer, bread could be added to the list of things many Americans have more difficulty affording.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In Baltimore, the H&S Bakery, which makes then ships breads to grocery stores and restaurants throughout the East Coast says it is scrambling as ingredients are increasingly in short supply.\par \par \uc2 When people go to the grocery store, they see it cost more now than it did last year, two years ago. Have we hit the ceiling or are we going higher?\par \par \uc2 JOHN "JR" PATERAKIS (H&S Bakery co-owner and senior VP Marketing and Sales.): We`re going higher. You`re going to see another increase in July that is going to impact another 35 percent increase on flour, and so that`s strictly related to the Ukraine-Russian war.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The war has disrupted production of wheat, barley and cooking oils in a nation considered the breadbasket of the world.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine and Russia produce a quarter of the global wheat supply, but war has halted much of the production. There are still some reserves, but they are dwindling quickly. Add to that supply chain disruptions, a shortage of workers partly fueled by the pandemic and the increased cost of gas driving up delivery costs.\par \par \uc2 The baking industry calls it a perfect storm.\par \par \uc2 ROBB MACKIE (\uc2 American Bakers Association\uc2  President and CEO): Unfortunately, those costs have to get borne by consumers and like I said, the most vulnerable in our society are going to feel that earlier than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: For mom and pop shops like Stella Kouros` bakery in Rockville, Maryland, it`s not just flour in short supply.\par \par \uc2 What has gone up in price that you`ve noticed?\par \par \uc2 STELLA KOUROS (Stella`s Bakery owner): Almost everything went up. Right now, the things that went even 30 percent up.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Ingredients up by 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 STELLA KOUROS: The milk, the cream, chocolate went up, too.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Her bakery has survived 30 years, but she expects to raise prices in August, an anguishing decision, she says with only a slight profit margin, with 35 employees and customers potentially unwilling to pay.\par \par \uc2 STELLA KOUROS: In the past may increases were many years apart. Now, I have done it twice since COVID, and probably will have to do it again, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The industry has been leaning on Washington to free up more Federal land for domestic wheat production and also to tap into new oil and gas supplies. At this point, the U.S. makes about six to seven percent of the world`s wheat supply. But in the meantime, the industry is scrambling for replacement ingredients or for wheats and flours made outside of Ukraine -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hear you, Scott. Speaking of scrambling, you might want to turn around and pick up some flowers for Mother`s Day there. You`re in a good location for that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 My advisory to all of you out there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad here is going to throw his back out. Careful. You`ve got a big Mother`s Day weekend ahead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is going to bite his mom`s lip balm.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nicely, brothers. It`s your thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Careful now.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Just do what you want to do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, go ahead and do what you want do, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m going to do it. I`m going to do it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start here with the man accused of attacking comedian, Dave Chappelle on stage. He will not face felony charges.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say 23-year-old, Isaiah Lee, is being charged with four misdemeanors: Battery, possession of a weapon with intent to assault, and two counts related to interfering with a performer.\par \par \uc2 The suspect was arrested after Tuesday`s attack on suspicion of assault with a deadly weapon, which is a felony. Police say the suspect was armed with a replica handgun that contained a knife blade when he tackled Chappelle during a performance at the Hollywood Bowl.\par \par \uc2 The DA`s office determined the evidence did not constitute felony conduct. That is the weapon that was found on him. We do have a picture that shows - -`\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not on him. I think it was in his bag.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In his bag.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is that thing that`s helping him with the lesser charges.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. And here`s what a real weapon -- we have a side by side graphic that shows you what the real thing would look like. Motive for the attack -- okay, I`m told by Jon Tower, no we don`t. The motive for the attack remains unknown.\par \par \uc2 So they`re not throwing the book at him, which is kind of interesting.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It sounds like they are doing --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Just the bizarre --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are doing what they think they can actually --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`re doing it by the book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, they are doing it by the book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right now to a first in the White House Briefing Room. \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has been named the new White House Press Secretary. She is the first Black woman and openly LGBTQ+ person to serve in that role.\par \par \uc2 Jean-Pierre will replace \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  who is leaving the Biden administration next week. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE (Incoming White House Press Secretary): This is a historic moment and it is not lost on me. I understand how important it is for so many people out there, so many different communities that I stand on their shoulders and I have been throughout my career, and so it is an honor and a privilege to be behind this podium.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know just away from the politics, objectively speaking, and this is a historic first as we just mentioned, and not only is she all the things that I mentioned, she`s also the child of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Her parents are from Haiti. She was born in Martinique, which is part of France, but she was raised right here in New York City, in Queens. She`s got a beautiful daughter with her partner, Suzanne Malveaux.\par \par \uc2 It`s a wonderful thing. It`s a wonderful thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In so many ways, it represents what some of America is made up of.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. You know, she -- in her book, which I read a couple of years ago, she describes as a child being on the South Lawn of the White House with her cousin, and just imagining the possibilities as an immigrant in the United States, and now to be one of the highest ranking Haitian-Americans ever in a presidential administration.\par \par \uc2 I know that she and her family, and I know everybody, people I`ve spoken to in Haiti and the diaspora are so proud of her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, very visible role.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there it is. Very much so.\par \par \uc2 All right, one last story about -- get this -- a reporter turned rescuer.\par \par \uc2 Weatherman, Paul Burt of Australia`s Seven News was on a beach reporting on dangerous currents. We`ve all done that, right? When a 10-year-old.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I don`t know about Nate though? Did you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, Nate has --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Dangerous current?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate is not on the weather thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got get. Shawna, well, Shawna, we`ve got to get Nate out into a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Give this guy some weather.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not scared. I took on linebackers for 11 years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I say nothing. I say nothing. So he is reporting, doing a live shot, and then behind him, a 10-year-old boy is swept away with the water behind him. So guess what Burt does? He jumps into action and he comes back on cam looking like this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAUL BURT (Weatherman, Australia`s Seven News): A 10-year-old boy got dragged out exactly as I was saying. And the pictures behind as you can see, he swam by the side there, but he was dragged out, so I had to race out there. Unfortunately, I hadn`t gotten my shirt off, which is not a good look.\par \par \uc2 But we got out there and we looked for him and then one of the other fellas found him and we got out there and basically grabbed him and pulled him back to shore. He`s very lucky --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes that was him running into the surf.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That is incredible.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. And there he is on air explaining just how he casually helped save a child before his live shot.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes. That wasn`t like -- he wasn`t related to him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: He just jumped into action.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He just saw what was happening and took action.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: David Hasselhoff would approve. The Hoff would approve, but just the idea of jumping into the water in the middle of a live shot and then coming back, no big thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 All right, Vlad, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I could see you doing --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You could see me doing that?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Anthony Mason talks with Emmy winner, Ben Schwartz.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil. Coming up in this hour, Senator Elizabeth Warren will be here to talk about why she wants to push Congress to vote on protecting abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m Jericka Duncan. More women are choosing to have kids on their own, including one of our producers. She`ll join us here to share her uplifting story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. If the voice of Sonic, the Hedgehog sounds familiar, it`s because the actor has been turning up in just about everything lately.`\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason follows the speedy success of Ben Schwartz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty quick. But first Thursday`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Defenders of a steel plant in Mariupol could be making their last stand, but more Western aid is arriving in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER: So all these things are happening in a way that is not working well for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Barricades go up in Washington amid concerns about new protests over Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What are you seeing in terms of demand for medication abortions?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE PITNEY: We are seeing a huge increase in demand.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The F.D.A. imposes new restrictions on the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine while COVID cases rise across the nation.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA: We do know this very, very rare side effect. The point of the F.D.A. and \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . are making is because there are alternatives, people should consider getting one of those instead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  will be the next White House Press Secretary, the first Black and openly LGBTQ+ person in the role.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE: This is a historic moment, and it`s not lost on me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And a new poll shows nearly half of moms would be happy with a nice meal for Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show with James Corden" host): Let`s cut to the chase, 48 percent of mums want to get absolutely bombed off mimosas, that`s what it is. Forty eight percent of moms want to go out to eat somewhere nice for Mother`s Day, and you`ll know if you`ve picked the wrong place when she says, "Oh, this is nice."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I can confirm that a lot of moms do want a meal. Katy has requested that I make a meal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My problem is that she told me like on Wednesday, and I can`t remember what the meal is, and I need to do the grocery shopping today and I`ve texted her to remind me what the meal is and she`s not responding.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You better figure it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, let`s ask Jericka. A meal? Mimosas? Does that sound like a good Mother`s Day?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Meals are always great, but again, it`s all about that relaxation. Rest time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about a good movie?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m simple. So yes, I would do a good movie as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. Well, that`s an easy transition. It is where I`m going right now.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with the voice of a movie breaking box office records.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Whoa, look at you. I just got goosebumps. Wait a second, did you steal that from Oprah?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Emmy winner Ben Schwartz doing his thing as the voice of Sonic in "Sonic the Hedgehog 2," which is in theaters now. It`s distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , which like CBS News is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip of "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony Mason took a tour with Schwartz of his early career, including the comedy club asylum, NYC, formerly home to the UCB Improv Theater where like so many others, he honed his craft and another iconic theater where Schwartz made the most of an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So what were you here?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I was a page, an audience page. So I would be the person to help you get to your seat. The person who gives you a ticket.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late Show with \uc2 David Letterman\uc2 .")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ben Schwartz was 22 when he started at the Ed Sullivan Theater, working for "The Late Show with \uc2 David Letterman\uc2 ." He even pitched jokes for the monologue.\par \par \uc2 So, did they take a lot of your jokes?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I got 21 jokes on, which is huge and also of course --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But who is counting?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That was just his day job.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I was addicted to comedy. I loved it so much.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At night, he worked at the Upright Citizens Brigade, an improv club then in this basement under a supermarket.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I was an intern here. I did the garbage, the recycling. I changed all the little bags that held all the liquids up in the pipes --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bags of the liquids?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Yes, there`s a Gristedes above us and sometimes, it would leak and the pipe would leak, and so these bags would hold them.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what gave it its charm.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Yes, that beautiful butcher blood.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And oh yes, during improv shows --\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I hear you get hammered a lot, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All the time. I`m hammered now.\par \par \uc2 He also worked the bar at the back.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: And I would watch every single show and that`s how I got better at improv.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from (Middleton & Schwartz.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Schwartz`s gift for improvisation helped land him the part of Sonic, the Hedgehog.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In the film about the video game superhero and its new sequel.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: And anytime I can think of a new joke, I`ll pop it in there.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I played him like a kid. In my head, I was like he`s an excited kid. He`s from a different place.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sonic the Hedgehog 2.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Schwartz himself a video game junkie as a kid, originally just voiced a short test scene as Sonic, which director Jeff Fowler pitched to studios.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: The goal was I did it for free as a favor, and then crossed my fingers, waited maybe eight months, and then I got a call from Jeff Fowler. And remember the opening credits of "ER?"\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: And like he gets on one knee and he goes like this. I literally did that in the street. I hit -- he told me I got the role, and I went -- and I was -- I`ve never been so excited.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s the biggest role yet for the 40-year-old actor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Parks and Recreation.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Who gave notice playing a series of insincere characters, Jean-Ralphio Saperstein on "Parks and Recreation."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Parks and Recreation.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He has been Dr. Clyde Oberholt on the Don Cheadle comedy "House of Lies."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "House of Lies.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And fast talking media manager F. Tony Scarapiducci on "Space Force."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Space Force.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You kind of made a career out of playing slippery people.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Oh, define slippery. I want to see how you navigate this. Define slippery.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Come on, you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I would say you know what I love to play, I love to play the arrogant idiot. I love to play the idiot that has no idea he is not nailing it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Parks and Recreation.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why do you play that so well?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I don`t know. I love -- and then I love also to give those characters heart, so you feel bad for them when they fail a little bit, too. Because why?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Do you think there`s any part of you that sort of Hollywood has overlooked in terms of what your potential is acting-wise?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Oh, like something I haven`t done?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "After Party.")\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I did a show called the "After Party."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: And I got to sing and dance and I`m getting more opportunities to stretch. And like, I did a drama with Billy Crystal who`s an idol of mine.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Standing up, Falling Down.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And he just finished filming "Renfield" a new Dracula film where he plays a tattooed bad guy.\par \par \uc2 Did you get to see Nicolas Cage as Dracula?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Did I get to see Nicolas Cage as Dracula? Yes. It was unbelievable. The last day of filming was just scenes with him and I.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What are you thinking when Nicolas Cage dressed as Dracula looks at you?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: Oh, it`s a dream come true.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When Schwartz isn`t acting, he is often writing.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I`ve sold four television shows and seven movies.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But as yet, not one has been filmed.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: And now it`s -- I get really frustrated, but like all right, let`s see if I can sell something else and hopefully that`ll get made.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. That`s hard.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: It`s a bummer. And also, it`s the exact opposite of improv which is instant gratification.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I say a joke, I get a laugh. Immediately or no, I don`t get a laugh immediately. I know exactly how that went.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Middleton and Schwartz.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Schwartz shot three long-form improv specials with Thomas Middleditch for \uc2 Netflix\uc2  in 2019.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Middleton and Schwartz.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Improv is still his first love.\par \par \uc2 What did you like about improv? Wasn`t improv scary to you?\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I think, in the beginning, it was terrifying. But to me, I love the idea of coming in with nothing. When you come to see one of my shows, people in the audience get a show that night that will never be the same. There will never be another show like it because it`s all made up.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Middleton and Schwartz.")\par \par \uc2 BEN SCHWARTZ: I love the idea of building something right there, and it disappearing. We all just have like a moment together. I love making people laugh very much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And he is really, really good at it. He is touring his improv show, "Ben Schwartz and Friends" now. He is also rewriting two of those scripts that he talked about, one a film with Sam Rockwell and a series that would star him. Hopefully, those will get made, but he`s got so much going on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Anthony, why do you think he makes such a great arrogant idiot because he didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I don`t -- I don`t know, but he is so good at it. He is so good at it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also perfect for Sonic. His voice sounds animated already.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He is extremely animated in the room. He tried to throw me off repeatedly. I said, the only one who`s ever tried to do this more than you was Jeff Goldblum. And he goes, "Well, actually, I`d like to play Jeff Goldblum`s son."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He would be terrific in the role.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That would be great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Anthony, Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, take a look at who is in our progressive greenroom. It`s Senator Elizabeth Warren. She`s here to tell us why she thinks there is a chance a divided Senate can pass an abortion rights bill. Is there?\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk about it. But first, it is 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a picture of the Capitol there.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren is urging the Senate to take action to support abortion rights after a leaked draft of a majority opinion shows the Supreme Court could be set to overturn the landmark 1973 abortion rights ruling, Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In her book "Persist" now in paperback, Warren writes, "In the same way that Congress passed laws to provide access to health care -- think Medicare and Medicaid -- Congress should pass a law to protect nationwide access to abortion."\par \par \uc2 But such a bill would face tough odds passing the Senate and Senator Warner joins us now. Tough odds is an understatement. Do I have that right?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): You do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t have the votes for it, but you`re going to go forward to make everybody vote. What`s the purpose of that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): We want to get everybody on the record, because I think most of all, we have to remember who will be affected by this Supreme Court decision. It`s not well-to-do women. Well-to-do women can still get on an airplane, fly to another state, fly out of the country, if they need to, to have access to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Who is this going to fall on? It is going to fall on poor women. It`s going to fall on women of color. It`s going to fall on girls who are molested by a family member. It`s going to fall on people who were raped. It`s going to fall on the mama who right now is already working two jobs to try to support the children she has.\par \par \uc2 This is about making sure that everyone in this country has a right to make the decision for themselves about a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And we just saw a map on the screen that said "certain or likely to end abortion."\par \par \uc2 When you look at some of the states where the numbers are not as high as the overall national numbers of at least 60 and sometimes 80 percent, you`ll find where people support this. Why not leave it up to the states when you have so many where the numbers may not be that high, and you look at that map and you see how many are almost certain to try to get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): You know, actually, I do want to focus very much on the idea of what it means to outlaw abortion. I lived in an America where abortion was illegal, but it didn`t mean that all abortions stopped. It just meant the safe legal abortion stopped.\par \par \uc2 I lived in an America where women bled to death from back alley abortions. I lived in an America where women developed infections, horrible complications from unclean abortions.\par \par \uc2 I lived in an America where young women took their lives rather than go forward with an abortion -- with a pregnancy that they could not manage.\par \par \uc2 Look, we pass laws at the Federal level to decide minimum wage, to decide access to healthcare. This is about protecting, not the people with power. This is about protecting all of the people in our country who don`t have power.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator, what you`re saying is true and yet there are states that have said despite that, we want to live by a different principle, and a common refrain you hear is, if you don`t like those laws in your state, you should just move. What do you say that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): We don`t do that anyplace else in our Federal government. We don`t say if you don`t like the minimum wage in Alabama, move somewhere else. We say, as a nation, we care for each other and we decide, for example, that work is worth a certain amount.\par \par \uc2 We decide, for example, on Medicaid, that there is going to be for the poorest among us at least some access to healthcare. We decide this for children to make sure that there`s going to be money, we decide this on food, and we should decide this on this basic health decision that will hit the most vulnerable among us.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who feel like, oh, we`re in states where this will never happen. First of all, we can`t turn our backs on the rest of those who will be hurt. But secondly, remember what this opinion is about. It has opened the door to a whole lot of ugliness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Opened the door to the question, contraception, criminalizing a woman who seeks an abortion. Will every miscarriage now be investigated? Will women be charged if they are working too many hours and put their pregnancy at risk? This is a kind of invasion that if the Supreme Court has its way here, and we don`t fight back at the Federal level, could touch the lives of every one of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, just like you`re saying now. In the book you write, you believe in a woman`s choice, no matter how difficult the decision. There is a question, should there be any limits on abortions?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I`m going to put it this way. I think that the woman who is most affected should be at the heart of this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I think that she should be able to call on her partner.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Her mother, her priest, her rabbi. She should be able to bring in the people she needs. But in a pregnancy, I don`t believe that it is the state that should intervene with its heavy hand and make the decision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s an extraordinary thing for a government to tell a person they need to go forward with a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Senator Elizabeth Warren, we have a lot more we could talk to you about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Please come back. Please come back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I will.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Please come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I will. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The paperback edition of "Persist" is out right now.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Coming up, why more women are choosing to start a family on their own.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we hear from an adult child from a single mom about what made their childhood so special.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. Jericka, you are going to kick it off for us.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay, so we all like social media. We use \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I use it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay. You use it. All right, we`ve got a grumpy old man on our table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, my "Talk of the Table" is a new study showing how social media may affect mental health. The study from England looked at 154 people between the ages of 18 and 72 who said they use social media every single day.\par \par \uc2 Well, some of them were asked to stop using social media for a week. Others were told to keep scrolling as usual, spending about eight hours -- eight hours a week on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and TikTok. When the week was over, researchers said the group that took a break from social media had significant improvements in wellbeing, depression and anxiety compared to those who kept up with the social media sites.\par \par \uc2 So seeing a connection there, and I think because mental illness affects so many people, millions of people in this country and around the world, and a lot of people don`t get the treatment. So just something like this could be a potential player in helping to relieve those stresses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I`ve noticed when I`ve turned my phone off, or my battery went dead, and I panicked for a little bit, I had that anxiety that ran over me. And then I just left my phone alone. When I came back to it, I realized the world didn`t end and I actually felt a lot better about myself because I wasn`t as stressed responding to text, e-mails and on social media, just scrolling for hours.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The e-mails, this is the new news. I think I want to skip that phone like a stone and then share.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you`re right. Just take a break.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You`ve got to take a break.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take a break from social media.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Take a break, take a breath.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is about a young woman named Laura Young who was shopping at a Goodwill in Austin, Texas and came upon what she thought was a very, very old looking bust. Do we have a picture of the bust?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have we got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There it is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was priced for $34.99 at Goodwill. She picked it up and four years later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see where this is going.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Four years later, she has managed to get it confirmed, identified as a bust from the dawn of the Roman Empire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The dawn of the Roman Empire. It is going to -- it`s believed that it was last seen in Germany.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No price on this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nate wants to know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two things that I think are great about this. It was last seen in Germany in a heavily bombed area and it`s believed after the U.S. occupation of Germany, post-World War Two that a U.S. soldier brought it back.\par \par \uc2 So first of all, I just love the image of the U.S. soldier coming home and someone picking up his luggage and had been like, "What do you have in here?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is so heavy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is so heavy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Second thing is, the name of the person, the bust there is Sextus.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Sextus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sextus.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, not a name you see and you hear much anymore. I think we might want to bring it back. He`s a military leader. He tried to take on Caesar and lost. He was beheaded, executed, and there is --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And there he is in the seatbelt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The happy ending to this other than the name Sextus may be making a comeback is, it`s going back to Germany and it`s going to be placed in its prior location.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I`m bringing in Sextus back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice. Well done. I thought you were going to give me a price tag.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. There`s no price tag.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People go antiquing and they find something special.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is priceless.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right, that makes sense. All right, here we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an ancient artifact.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my talk with the table is some unwanted visitors caught on camera. They were so cute.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Better not be roaches.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no. Not roaches. Jericka, roaches growing up?\par \par \uc2 All right, the homeowner didn`t even mind. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I leave my garbage and their own little furry babies. Hi. My goodness. You are so sweet. Hi, Mama.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: That`s the mama bear.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hi mama. Hi. I don`t even mind because you`re so pretty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t even mind you`re spreading trash around. You`re so pretty. I`m not sure I would respond like that if there were bears right outside. But you know what, this is a metaphor on life. You know what I mean? Sometimes there`s people throwing around trash. But if you notice the pretty things, if you notice --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: From afar. Because she was that high. Let`s just be honest. She was not on the ground level.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, who`s going to pick up all of that blowing trash around?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, not with the bears out there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, up next, more women are making the decision to become single mothers by choice.\par \par \uc2 CBS News producer Wendy McNeal --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: There she is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Wendy -- will share her journey to motherhood on her own. But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In honor of Mother`s Day this Sunday, we`re taking a look at a growing trend in motherhood: Women becoming single moms by choice.\par \par \uc2 Advancements in fertility treatments and the expansion of medical insurance coverage have made it possible for more women to have children on their own. One of our very own CBS MORNINGS producers did just that. I spoke with her and others about creating a family with only mom at the helm.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I had an aching to the mother. I had a longing to be a mother.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: At 40 years old, \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer Wendy McNeal gave birth to her daughter, Harlow, a lifelong dream so important to her that she chose to have the baby on her own.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I said to myself, in 15 to 20 years, would I be more upset that I didn`t have a kid or more upset that I didn`t fall in love and get married? And I was like, if I didn`t have a kid for me, I feel like my life wouldn`t be as fulfilled.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She picked a donor and went through three rounds of fertility treatments. Holding Harlow in her arms is a reminder she made the right choice.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Even when she`s like -- even when she`s like this and gets a little fussy, I`m grateful that like she made me a mother and that like I got this opportunity.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Forty years ago when New York psychotherapist, Jane Mattes became pregnant, fewer women were purposely having children on their own.\par \par \uc2 JANE MATTES (Single Mothers By Choice): I was 36 and all my friends were actually having fertility problems, and it occurred to me that I might not have this opportunity again. I was one of these people who was told a lot, you`ll be such a wonderful mother.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So she decided to have the baby knowing his father would not be in the picture. To get the support she needed, she created her own village and started Single Mothers by Choice.\par \par \uc2 JANE MATTES: You really can`t raise your child alone, but you can make sure that as a single parent, you have a lot of people in a child`s life who love that child.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Over the years, that network has connected 30,000 women. Mattes attributes the growth to better fertility treatments, more inclusive medical insurance, a change in societal attitudes and higher incomes for women.\par \par \uc2 As for Mattes` son, he started his own family making his mother, a grandmother.\par \par \uc2 Brothers, Mackenzie and Cooper Shallenthal (sp?) were chosen by their mother, a doctor more than two decades ago. She had them at 41 and 45 using the same donor.\par \par \uc2 Do you miss not knowing who your father is?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE SHALLENTHAL (Raised by a single mother by choice): Frankly, no. You know, we have an amazing family and amazing family relationships.\par \par \uc2 COOPER SHALLENTHAL (Raised by a single mother by choice): We loved our childhood, like we have a very caring mother.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE SHALLENTHAL: We didn`t see our parents cry, you know, and scream and throw things because there weren`t two parents.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How has it impacted your own relationships by not seeing tension, by not seeing people work through those same things that you spoke of?\par \par \uc2 COOPER SHALLENTHAL: I think I am more conflict avoidant than other people as a result, because I was raised in a household with zero conflict and perhaps that`s one of the drawbacks.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The brothers have few demographic details about the man who contributed to their DNA, but they do have his extensive medical history.\par \par \uc2 What would you ask him?\par \par \uc2 COOPER SHALLENTHAL: I mean, I would thank him for giving away sperm because I know that can be sort of just an odd thing for someone to do, especially knowing that they have potentially 10 or more kids running around the world. But I would also thank him for being tall.\par \par \uc2 We`re definitely appreciative of being taller than the rest of our family.\par \par \uc2 MACKENZIE SHALLENTHAL: He`s given us nothing, but genetics, and that`s crucial.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: For baby Harlow, she can decide if she wants to know who her donor is when she turns 18. Until then, McNeal is prepared to tell her daughter how she got here.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I`m always going to be open and honest and transparent. You know, she doesn`t have a dad, she has a donor. I had her on my own.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She never closed the door on finding love, just opened the one that led her to baby Harlow first.\par \par \uc2 So now you can still do that, but you have fulfilled the dream, the want, the desire to be a mom.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Yes, and I can do it on my own terms.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We are now joined by Wendy McNeal and her beautiful baby, her first live television interview. Four months old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Harlow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Wendy, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Hi, friend.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hello.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: For joining us, and I tell -- you know, when I saw you a few months ago, more than a few, but you told me, you know that you were making this decision, and I thought we should do a story.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Because I`ve heard more and more women over the years making that decision. What was the tipping point for you? Because you talk about saying, would I be more upset if I didn`t, you know, have a child but I did go for trying to get married and whatnot. But what was that tipping point?\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I had an aching, a longing to be a mother. And for me that meant doing it by any means necessary. And I had to get realistic about my circumstances.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the fact of matter is, I was in my late 30s. And you know, there are certainly biological pressures that we face.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: And --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You said it was like an article, too, that you saw.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Yes --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And it felt like --\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I basically was, like, I had asked the universe, I was like, give me a sign that this is the path that I need to take, and the sign came in the form of this op-ed in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." It was called "Single at 38: Have that Baby."\par \par \uc2 And I was like, done. And I read the story and the woman I completely related to and I was like, this is it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We know that insurance played a role in this, too. I mean, you get the coverage. So talk to us about that in terms of you know, women who are watching this or thinking about that.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Yes, I mean, I`m fortunate that I`ve work for an employer that you know, provides reproductive insurance and I was able to do this basically almost nothing out of pocket, little to nothing out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: And that`s not you know, something that is extended to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a blessing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a blessing. Nate and I are making googly eyes, I`ve got to get a smile because Nate got a smile.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I know, he got a smile. I know.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: He is doing it so well.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: It is a fun age right now where like she is showing a little bit of personality and the smiles are coming out, and so it`s a fun time.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How do you deal with the tough parts because it is just you?\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: I breathe through it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: And I always find gratitude. When I have the moments where like it`s a little tough, I will work through that and then I`ll come back to gratitude because the fact that I`m even -- I was able to have her and you know, and it`s been a pretty smooth process at this point. Like, I always come back to gratitude.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I know you know this, but here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , she has a bunch of cousins, uncles, aunties, grandmothers, grandfathers. This is family. So you know, we`re all a phone call away.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Yes. I love you guys so much. I`ve already had so much support from you guys. I`ve had conversations with each of you about parenting and asking questions and so thank you so much everyone for all your support for sure.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We are here for you, Harlow. We got you, girl. Go, Lo-Lo. Yes, Lo-Lo is her nickname.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lo-Lo.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Go Lo-Lo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go Lo-Lo.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s looking at Brian.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: She`s looking at herself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking of our podcast, hear from the showrunner of "Winning Time: The Rise of the Lakers Dynasty." That`s Max Borenstein. He discusses why the \uc2 HBO\uc2  series is a love letter to basketball and the \uc2 NBA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up on "CBS Saturday Morning," meet a man who has dedicated his life to saving a language and a culture that was almost lost. We will visit the extraordinary Yiddish Book Center in Western Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 And on "CBS Sunday Morning," \uc2 Rita Braver\uc2  sits down with the seven-member all female cast of the new Broadway play "POTUS." Its stars include Rachel Dratch, Julianne Hough and Vanessa Williams.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that. That nearly does it for us, but before we go, could get one more look there.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, pretty baby.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: She says, hey, Uncle Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Mother`s Day, Wendy. First one.\par \par \uc2 WENDY MCNEAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations. Before we go, a look back at all that mattered this week. Have a great weekend, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Spreading fallout from that stunning Supreme Court leak of a draft opinion that Roe v. Wade is set to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Biden says it`s now up to lawmakers to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): We are not going back.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The Russian airstrike destroyed that bridge trying to stop Western weapons from reaching Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It`s only managed to capture one city so far, which is perhaps slides going on out in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Officials searching for murder suspect and his jailer released surveillance video that seems to show the moment they ran off together.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You have confidence that you find Officer Vicky White alive.\par \par \uc2 RICK SINGLETON (Lauderdale County Sheriff): I have concerns that we might not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dave Chappelle, he is doing his act when a man got on stage and tackled him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad Dave is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Glad he is okay, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has announced the biggest increase in its key short-term interest rate.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Ease prices without triggering a recession or job losses. So Tony, good news. You can stop texting me and you can actually check your 401 (k) statements.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My 401 (k) because I want to retire like tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS and hello to your viewers on the West Coast as we get --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re out. I was like -- oh, we are not out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, we`re not out. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the Gayle King -- that fell.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be talking about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clean up on aisle three.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s all you`ve got to do. You don`t have to move your hips. Hey.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See, I know you could dance. You`ve been holding back. Tony is like, "Okay."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please carry on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing, Queen?\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH (Actress): I`m good. I`m blessed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are so good at the physicality of it.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH: You know, I say what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is there anything you want?\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH: What did you say?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got to do what?\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH: You called me what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. All Hail the Queen. Thank you, Queen Latifah.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH: Hey.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What are the regrets?\par \par \uc2 JENIFER LEWIS (Actress): Well, that was that one man in Chicago. Yes. Come on, girl. You better put that --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Just one? Because I read the book.\par \par \uc2 JENIFER LEWIS: Well, all right. There were about 85. But that`s another story.\par \par \uc2 I`m 65, hey, and I`m still alive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to try that when I get home. I hope we are not calling 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hold the rock, you basically have to be The Rock in order to afford it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. Jon Tower just said to me, you have a shot, grab it and go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, grab it and go.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Well, you have security here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say, do you have armed security, so anybody who`s watching right now?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So, for anybody with any ideas.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How much did you say it was?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My man pulling out his wallet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I left my wallet upstairs.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You left it upstairs. My hands are shaking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, I don`t trust you. It`s in your hands.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s really tempting.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220506ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Tightened Security Around The Supreme Court In The Wake Of The Draft Majority Opinion\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, tightened security around the Supreme Court in the wake of the draft majority opinion, as Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  makes his first public appearance since the leak.\par \par \uc2 New eight-foot fencing goes up around the Supreme Court, the new intelligence tonight about possible violence against the justices, plus the next battle over the abortion pill, and our exclusive interview with former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY RODHAM\uc2  CLINTON (Former U.S. Secretary of State): This is about controlling women. This is about turning the clock back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wall Street freefall. Stocks plummet over inflation fears and rising interest rates. What does it mean for your wallet? And what should you do if you`re close to retirement?\par \par \uc2 New tornado threat, 48 million Americans in the path of severe storms.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s other top headlines: the bloody battle for the control of Mariupol; breaking news, as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  restricts the J&J vaccine; the concerning health alert about some blood pressure medications and a bad reaction with ibuprofen.\par \par \uc2 Inside Trump`s Oval Office, our 60 Minutes interview with former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , the tense standoff.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former U.S. Secretary of Defense): I just turn squarely around to him, face him and say: "I don`t have a quarter-million troops to send on some ridiculous mission to the border."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And a field of dreams for a young \uc2 Yankees\uc2  fan, the special moment between fans of two rival teams.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS Evening News With Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening and thank you so much for joining us on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 And as we come on the air tonight, public protests are spreading across the nation following the leaked draft decision from the Supreme Court that would end federal protection of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 While the demonstrations have been mostly peaceful so far, there is growing concern about potential violence leading up to the official ruling. Fences have gone up around the Supreme Court as crowds are expected to grow in the days and weeks ahead. And CBS News has learned that the justices themselves have been given extra security, while several justices have canceled upcoming public appearances.\par \par \uc2 CBS News sat down with former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who called the opinion incredibly dangerous and warned that more could be at risk than just abortion rights. We will have more of our interview with Secretary Clinton in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe will start us off from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 That draft majority opinion on the future of abortion rights continues to reverberate nationwide. And now there are growing concerns about the potential for political violence against the Supreme Court justices themselves.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): The Supreme Court under lockdown. Eight-foot-high fencing now surrounds the building. All entry points are blocked to vehicles, and more officers on patrol, a scene reminiscent of what Washington looked like after the January 6 attacks.\par \par \uc2 The nine justices are also under heavier security. CBS News has learned general threats of violence against them are up. Their home locations have been shared online, and they`re receiving more targeted personal phone threats.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , author of the leaked majority draft opinion, canceled a scheduled appearance today in Nashville. The other justices are also cutting back on public events.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-Texas): The risk is real. The U.S. Marshal Service is there to provide protection for judges in the federal judiciary. But it`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  addressed the leak today, calling it absolutely appalling and called the leaker foolish.\par \par \uc2 But the fallout has only intensified. In Illinois, surrounded by five states set to restrict abortion services, clinics are trying to prepare for a flood of new patients.\par \par \uc2 YVONNE OLDAKER (Associate Medical Director, Planned Parenthood of Illinois): If every state that touches Illinois suddenly has no access, we project even up to a five-times increase in patient demand, especially at our health centers that border other states.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Many of those out-of-state patients would be seen virtually and sent abortion pills, but those are also in jeopardy. At least 17 states have now introduced legislation to ban or restrict access to the pills.\par \par \uc2 Michigan`s Democratic Attorney General Dana Nessel worries is it could happen in her state.\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (Michigan Attorney General): Whether it`s by medication, whether it`s surgical, it doesn`t matter. Abortion will be illegal in our state, period.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Meanwhile, here at the White House, plans for a new press secretary. \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  is set to become the first black person, first openly gay person to serve in the role. She`s set to succeed Jen Psaki, who is stepping down next Friday -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220507ei550002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Leak Fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Ed O`Keefe, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning now to the political fallout from the Supreme Court leak on Roe v. Wade. Lawmakers on both sides of the aisle have some big decisions ahead if the Court overturns the landmark ruling.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is following this and he joins us from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Nate, great to see you\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and Democrats now trying to figure out how to protect abortion rights and it is set to become a major issue in this fall`s midterm elections. With voters now, at least the President thinks voters now have a clear choice to make.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "My body, my choice.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Democrats across the country are now under pressure from abortion rights advocates to take action after that bombshell leaked draft majority opinion shows the Supreme Court could soon overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (Planned Parenthood President and CEO): If anybody kicks you out of the Constitution, you`ve got to think about how you`re going to get yourself back in.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Planned Parenthood President and CEO, \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2  talked about taking action Wednesday night on "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 And she is not the only one.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): The Supreme Court doesn`t have to have the last word.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In the Senate, Democrats are planning a vote on making abortion rights, Federal law.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): The \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  can act and protect a person`s right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But the mainly symbolic vote is unlikely to pass.\par \par \uc2 But you don`t have the votes for it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): By making people vote on this issue that matters to so many people, we`ve got a chance now, maybe to change the makeup of who is in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and I`m all in on that fight.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: If the Court rules as expected, abortion regulation reverts back to the States.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): Each of those elected representatives can make the decisions that they view best for their constituents.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Mississippi is at the center of the Supreme Court challenge with its ban on most abortions after 15 weeks, but if Roe versus Wade is overturned, it`s one of 13 states with a so-called trigger law prohibiting abortions immediately with limited exceptions.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): People in our state believe that when a woman chooses to have an abortion, that she is not only making a decision for herself, she is also making the decision for that unborn child.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Biden is now warning of what could come.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE Biden\uc2  (D): What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history -- in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The White House says it is exploring options for what it might do once Roe versus Wade is overturned, but doesn`t want to preview them until or unless the Court actually overturns that original ruling, and in more fallout from the leak of that draft majority opinion, they have boosted security over at the Supreme Court putting up fencing overnight, and Justice Alito who wrote the draft opinion, canceled the scheduled appearance for today at a legal conference in Nashville -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More fencing in the nation`s capital, not a good look.\par \par \uc2 Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220507ei550005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests At The Supreme Court And Across The Country\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now to the fallout following the unprecedented leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion overturning the 1973 landmark Roe vs. Wade decision that legalized abortion nationwide. Demonstrations for and against were held across the country, as some Democratic governors hurried to shore up abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Our rights!\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): At the Supreme Court and across the country today, Americans took to the streets to protest the potential end of Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the leaked draft opinion, President Biden took a swipe at former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his GOP coalition who helped install the court`s conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Democrats now see the possible end of abortion rights as another way to call out differences with Republicans ahead of the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): How dare they? How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On Capitol Hill, there is a push among Democrats, like Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren, to write federal abortion rights into law.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (D-Massachusetts): The United States commerce can act and protect a person`s right to an abortion. And, in fact, that is exactly what we`re going to vote on next week.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But you don`t have the votes for it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN: By making people vote on this issue that matters to so many people, we have got a chance now maybe to change the makeup of who is in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: At the state level Democratic governors, like Connecticut`s \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2 , are preparing to sign a bill protecting and expanding abortion access.\par \par \uc2 But 26 states are certain or likely to ban at least some access if Roe is defeated. Mississippi, whose ban on abortion after 15 weeks sparked the case that led to the draft opinion, is one of those states.\par \par \uc2 Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  says, if Roe is ending, the focus now should be on helping women.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TATE REEVES (R-Mississippi): Making sure that we support every single one of these individuals that has a pregnancy that maybe unwanted, but convince them that that child is wanted and that that child is loved.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: To bypass some of those state abortion bans, some companies are now offering to pay travel expenses for workers seeking abortion services.\par \par \uc2 One of those companies is \uc2 Yelp\uc2 . A top official there telling us today they think it is -- quote -- "vitally important" for companies to safeguard their employees -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: With important interviews tonight, Ed O`Keefe at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020220505ei54000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Ed O`Keefe, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 2,156 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this May 4th. May the Fourth be with you. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Strong reaction across the country over the Supreme Court draft opinion that indicates Roe v. Wade could be overturned.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice President of the United States): How dare they? How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 European Union\uc2  proposes a complete ban on Russian oil as President Putin`s assault on Ukraine continues.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN KIRBY (RET.) (Pentagon Press Secretary): It`s not going well for the Russians right now. They have not made very good progress.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: New video showing the moment a corrections officer took off with an escaped inmate out of the Lauderdale County Jail in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And in Ohio, a Senate race has big implications for who is really the face of the GOP.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R) (Ohio Senatorial Candidate): I have absolutely got to think the 45th, the President United States \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Dave Chappelle was attacked on stage.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The comedian was performing at the Hollywood Bowl when a man tackled him.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: An anti-abortion activist under arrest in San Francisco after he climbed a nearly 1,100-foot building.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You feel like --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Senator Susan Collins expressed surprise that the Justices would join a draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She feels she was misled.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Just like that time my niece sent me the e-mail saying she was stranded in Guadalajara and needed money to get out of jail. So, I sent her $10,000.00 and then I found out I don`t even have a niece.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): Democrats could legalize abortion to Congress, if they get rid of the filibuster. The only problem is they don`t have the votes to do that.\par \par \uc2 Although who knows? Susan Collins is so gullible. You know, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  can probably get her to sign on to the filibuster reform just hiding it in a birthday card.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that`s right. Just sign here. It`s a group thing for the whole group and everyone`s going to -- It`s a generous birthday. We`re all signing. Just sign here. Yes, his name is Phil Buster. Sign here.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trevor Noah strikes again. It certainly is a topic of conversation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes. It`s going to drive the news again today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s going to be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. And today, we`ve got a story about second chances. We like that. David Begnaud introduces us to one young woman who made a mistake and was facing jail time when an act of kindness helped turn her life totally around.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE PERRY (New Orleans native): It was almost like it was a collaboration, like we were just having a meeting about my future. I felt like I was meeting with an advisor and this was the Judge on my criminal case, and it really changed the trajectory of my entire life.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow is right. The woman with the great hair. That`s Gabrielle Perry. She will show us how she is paying it forward in a big, big way. And of course, because this is how David Begnaud rolls, he has got a huge surprise just for her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, grab them tissues.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you might recognize actress Jenifer Lewis as the mother in some of your favorite shows, but her journey to superstardom hasn`t been easy. As she puts it, the elevator to success was broken, so she had to take the stairs.\par \par \uc2 She shares her insight and wit with our own Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that Nate. The elevator was broken, she had to take the stairs and she is still working.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait for Jericka`s piece.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin today with the spreading fallout from that stunning -- and stunning is the word -- Supreme Court leak of a draft opinion that suggests that Roe v. Wade is set to be overturn.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are the headlines today showing how the shockwaves are reverberating -- she is trying to say -- across the nation with alarm on one side of the abortion rights debate and criticism, big criticism on the other.\par \par \uc2 It has also galvanized politicians on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Here at the White House, President Biden believes that if the draft opinion stands, it is going to put other rights at risk and he says it`s now up to lawmakers to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Even the possibility that Roe versus Wade is going to be overturned has poured millions of dollars into the campaign to Democrats and their related causes, scrambling already contentious midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Abortionists have got to go.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Protesters took to the streets across the country --\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Pro-abortion rights rise up for abortion rights.")\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting Roe v. Wade is going to go.")\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For and against the Supreme Court`s draft opinion to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Well, that says abortion is murder and I feel that way. Period.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Women will be trying to shuttle back and forth from different states to try to get their rights fulfilled.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We are not going back.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On Tuesday night, Vice President Harris joined the fight.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) (United States President): It`s really quite a radical decision.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Earlier in the day President Biden blasted the draft opinion warning the decision could threaten other rights like same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It would mean that, every other decision we made the notion of privacy is thrown into question.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Republicans were outraged as well, not about the decision, but about the leak.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): This is the most egregious breach of trust at the Supreme Court that has ever happened.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Most Republicans even avoided commenting on the implications of the opinion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): This is the first time we`ve had somebody on the inside try to attack the institution.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: With narrow House and Senate majorities, Democrats have failed so far to enact Federal abortion rights into law ever since Texas passed a restrictive abortion law last year.\par \par \uc2 While a bill passed the House in the fall, it was blocked this year in the Senate, but party leaders say they`ll try again.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): It is time to wake up.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And Democrats also see political opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Just minutes after the news leaked, some campaign started e-mailing and texting supporters for money.\par \par \uc2 ActBlue, which funnels donations to Democratic candidates and causes has processed at least $10 million in donations and counting since Monday night.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans doubt abortion rights will be a top issue by November.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I think this election is going to be decided based on the quality of the economy and safety.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So a little more on the potential politics of this, a Biden adviser tells CBS News, they believe if the ruling ultimately stands, this is going to help mobilize what they call harder to engage voters, maybe younger people, or those that only show up every four years to vote for President, believing they`ll now understand that a real fundamental right is at risk.\par \par \uc2 The adviser said that polling that CBS News and other outlets have done that shows a majority of Americans want to sustain abortion rights and they argue that quote, "Every single swing race will be impacted by this decision" -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The leaked document led to demonstrations nationwide with people on both sides of the abortion rights debate strongly expressing their opinions. These are voices from across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Abortion is violence.")\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Abortion saves lives.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Shame on the Supreme Court. Shame on you for not protecting the bodies of women that you claim to love.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We`re excited that people are realizing the importance of life from conception to natural death.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When did it start that we became a country that our voices didn`t matter and we were no longer heard?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I had been praying along with brothers and sisters since 1973 to have this mis-justice in America overturned.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think it could be overturned and then it is going to become a war on women`s rights again, something that our mothers and grandmothers already did before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Underlying all that, of course, is the leak itself.\par \par \uc2 In a statement yesterday, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  called the release of that draft opinion a quote, "singular and egregious breach of trust" end quote, intended to undermine the integrity of the bank`s operations or sorry -- the Court`s operations.\par \par \uc2 He stressed that the opinion does not represent the final position of any member of the Court, and Roberts has now launched an investigation into the underlying leak.\par \par \uc2 Chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford, joins us now from the Supreme Court. Jan, you`ve been covering the courts for a long time, I want to get to the question of integrity, what this breach means for the Court`s operation, but first, the implications of it.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has said that a whole range of rights would be at risk if this opinion is published. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, he specifically is talking about the right to say same sex marriage and other cases, including the right to contraceptive devices, those rights are grounded in the interest of liberty and the right to privacy that we saw in Roe versus Wade in a 1992 called Casey.\par \par \uc2 So the concern is if you pull out Roe and Casey, those legs out from under the table that is just going to collapse and the other cases will fall as well.\par \par \uc2 But there are other cases that provide a foundation for some of the same sex marriage cases, and Justice Alito, in that draft opinion was very clear that a right to contraceptive devices or marriage or those things are different than abortion because unlike abortion, which is an individual right for the woman, there`s also a competing right to a potential life.\par \par \uc2 So he is stressing that abortion is critically different in those other cases.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it remains to be seen, Jan, if future cases if that ring will hold if future cases are brought.\par \par \uc2 Now to the leak itself, very concerning both sides of the aisle coming up against it. Chief Justice Roberts is saying it will not affect the courts operations. Do you think that`s true?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, he flatly said that in his statement yesterday, a very strong statement kind of surprising it to see from the Chief Justice of the United States that will not succeed in undermining the Court`s operations. But I think that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 I mean, as big of a story as it is that the Court would overturn Roe is a body blow to the Court. It has never happened and confidentiality at the Supreme Court as they go through their deliberations is crucial. That trust will take some time to get back, if they can.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jan Crawford for us once again in front of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something tells me they will find the leaker though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020220505ei540002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Abortion Rights Fight; Abortion Rights Protests; Supreme Court Leak Fallout; J.D. Vance Wins Ohio Primary; Escape from Mariupol; Escaped Inmate Manhunt; Severe Storm Alert; The Fight Against Inflation; Dave Chappelle Attacked; A Second C