nded.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No parent should ever have to get news about a school shooting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, those talks on finances.\par \par \uc2 Fears of the new coronavirus variant and rising prices sent stocks tumbling again yesterday. All three major markets dropped following the latest news from the Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell. He said the Fed could end some emergency COVID measures to boost the economy sooner than expected, such as buying bonds every month.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL (U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman): We now look at an economy that`s very strong and inflationary pressures that are high and that means it`s appropriate I think for us to discuss at our next meeting, which is in a couple of weeks, whether it will be appropriate to wrap up our purchases a few months earlier.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to unpack it all. Jill, good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning. Good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good. Now the Fed indicated emergency COVID relief measures could end earlier than expected. How could that impact the economy?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, let`s just look at what the Fed has been doing. COVID hits and two things happen. The Fed announces that they are going to start buying bonds every month to boost the economy and help financial markets actually survive the volatile period, and they dropped interest rates down to zero percent.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Jerome Powell said we are seeing such good growth in the economy that we may start to end our bond buying sooner than expected -- excuse me -- and one of the things that we have to remember is that the recovery has been so strong, it`s also created inflation, and this is something that we`ve all been talking about.\par \par \uc2 I think the Fed acknowledged yesterday that inflation is running hotter than they`d like, they`re going to have to move a little bit more quickly, and this was somewhat of a surprise for the markets and for big investors, and that actually contributed to some of the selloff yesterday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the question I think, for a lot of people -- family of four, you`re wondering about the stock market, and you and I have talked about this before, when I was on the street, we used to show clients -- retail clients a chart that showed going back to 1930, if you missed the 10 best days over the market, your return is only 28 percent. If you stay in through the ups, through the downs, your return is over 17,000 percent.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, this is a thing that`s hard to grasp, because people think that they can figure out how to time the market -- when to get in, when to get out. But we don`t really know. I think that generally speaking, the news is unnerving because there`s a lot of uncertainty. There is uncertainty about the variant, how might that impact the economy? There`s unnerving news about the Fed, maybe they`re moving sooner, maybe they`re moving longer than we expect.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, it doesn`t matter. You`re a long term investor. Maybe you`ve got money in an IRA account, maybe you`re saving a few bucks for your kid`s education. Generally speaking, if you stick with your game plan, you should be okay.\par \par \uc2 Gayle always makes fun of me about this because I say don`t panic --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s coming.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It is coming, I know, it`s coming. I`m with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re swallowing, I am just waiting.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I know. Because it`s hard not to feel that panic, so what I think is good to know is that we rely on numbers. We look at the past, we say, you know what? I get it. It is scary to have big days, up and down.\par \par \uc2 But