par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: These are four of the very first photographs I ever took in my life.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At the zoo in Manchester, England.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: I`m only like 10 years old here. That`s why I`m looking way up at that giraffe like that. But you know --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I have to say, they show a lot of promise, even at 10.\par \par \uc2 He caught the bug from his father, who would turn young Graham`s bedroom into a dark room.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: He would put a blank sheet of paper into a colorless liquid and say, "Okay, wait." Okay, dad. What?" "Wait." "Okay, what`s good --" and then I saw an image come into view from nothing and it changed my life.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, it is mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Oh, it was magic. I`ve been a photographer longer than I`ve been a musician.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But his father`s gift came at a cost.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: The camera that he gave me that started my love of photography had been stolen. He bought it from a friend of his at work.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He didn`t know it was stolen.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: His father wouldn`t give up the name of the friend who sold it to him to the police.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: And so they put him in jail for a year and he died at 47.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow. You said in the book, basically, it broke him.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: There`s no question about it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Every picture you make now is a tribute to your father.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: It is. As every song that I sing is a tribute to the Cass Elliot. Cass Elliot from the Mamas and Papas.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There is a wonderful picture of Cass in the book.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Yes, she`s on the phone with Crosby right there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was Elliot who introduced Nash to David Crosby, who then introduced him to Stephen Stills.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: I think she understood in her mind what CSN would sound like singing together.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: One of the books most poignant pictures is a shot of Crosby in New York`s Chelsea Hotel. Soon after his girlfriend had died in a car accident.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: And I saw him at the end of that hall looking out of the window, and I realized what I thought he was thinking. He was thinking of jumping.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He told you that?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Yes, he did. Yes. David is a beautiful man, you know, and I`ve taken many pictures of him.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But after CSM`s last concert in 2015, Crosby and Nash had a massive falling out.\par \par \uc2 You guys aren`t speaking right now. So I was a little surprised to see so many pictures of him in this book.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: I have to take my personal feelings out and let the photographs speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. So there is no message to him in any way?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: No, none at all.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This photo captured Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young in Maui together.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Rehearsing an album called "Human Highway" that never got made. Anyway, on the last day, I realized that if we were going to make an album, we needed a cover.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He borrowed Stills` camera, set the scene, the exposure, and had a friend snap the picture.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: So there`s only one shot. This is it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Of the album cover of the album that didn`t get made.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: That didn`t get made.\par \par \uc2 I love to bring joy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is that when you get out of it?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Yes. Tremendous peace, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How do they give you peace?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: They don`t talk back to me. They don`t have an opinion. It`s a peaceful place only for me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Some of Nash`s most intriguing images are self-portraits, which can include his wife, the artist, Amy Grantham.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: I love this woman.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And I love the portrait -- the picture of her painting in the bathtub.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: Oh, yes. Yes. Well, you know, I realized that in New York City, every square inch is very, very valuable. That`s why Amy is painting while she`s sitting in the bath.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This is not really a side project for you. It`s always had a big place in your heart.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM NASH: No. It is, yes, always. You know, I`m still waiting for Elvis to come back on the back of an elephant. You know, I`ll be there with my camera waiting, you know, come on, show it to me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, thank you, Graham Nash for the photography and the music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And the stories, right? I mean, I asked him --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The story, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Graham is 79. He was born in Blackpool, England. I said, when did you come to New York for the first time? He said in 1965, he actually played a block from here in Times Square at the Paramount Theater on a soupy sales show, which also featured Little Richard. He said, Little Richard got into a fight in the elevator with his guitar player who he thought was showing off. It turns out that kid was Jimi Hendrix.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a great punch line.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And then a great story. We tell a lot of great stories at the 92nd Street Y on Sunday if you happen to be in the neighborhood, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: With Graham Nash.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, that`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you going to do the conversation --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No, not me. No, I got my treadle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Oh, gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What I love about this is that he says his first love is photography. But while your piece was playing and even as we were teasing him, everybody in on this set was singing their favorite Crosby, Stills, and Nash songs.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Don`t you hate people who have two great talents?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got a little bit of a ping when he said that he and David aren`t speaking.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But so many photos of him in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little bit. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, and many of them and beautiful photographs, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It may not be intended as a message to him, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But yes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It could be taken as such.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, I think it`s important he included them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Oh, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I thought Anthony was going to say, we`ll be right back. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you want to take a look who`s in our progressive green room? Emmy Award winner -- What your name is?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Trevor Noah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trevor Noah. He has got exclusive announcement.\par \par \uc2 The local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. We are very excited to make an exclusive announcement for you about music`s biggest night, the Recording Academy`s 64th Annual Grammy Awards. Only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS we can reveal -- well, shouldn`t you do the reveal? Shouldn`t you, special guest at the table do the reveal what`s happening? What`s happening, special guest?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: What is -- I`m doing the reveal?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. They told me --\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I don`t know what the reveal is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re about to tell you.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: What`s happening?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here is the reveal. That Trevor Noah -- the Emmy award winning host of "The Daily Show" is returning to host the Grammys right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What? What? That`s what I`m talking about.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations. You made it.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Again --\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: An H.R. thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Trevor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: This is good. This is great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trevor, come see me in my office.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here are some of monologue from his Grammy hosting debut this past March.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: My name is Trevor Noah, and I`ll be your host tonight as we celebrate the last 10 years of music that got us through the last 10 years of coronavirus. I know it`s been one year, but it feels like 10.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, this year, people, we have made the decision to socially distance from the Staples Center. But we`re still broadcasting to you from the heart of downtown Los Angeles. This is not a Zoom background, all right. This is real.\par \par \uc2 My uncle isn`t going to walk behind me naked even though I told him I was having an important meeting. That`s not going to happen tonight.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It happens in Trevor`s house, huh? Welcome, Trevor Noah.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that night, and I remember thinking how in the world are you going to pull it off? And you did.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: It was fun. It was really, really fun. Yes, it was a great team. I was just lucky to be a part of it. You know, and I guess we had such a good time that we were like, imagine if we had people there how much more fun would we have had?\par \par \uc2 And so this year, the Grammys is going to be back, you know, we`re going to be back 2022. Hopefully, it`s going to be like the full show. Everybody`s going to be there. We`re excited to celebrate the record nights, and I get to host.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when they asked you to come back a second time, did you say let me think about it, or did you think, yes, I want to go. Seriously because it was hard what you did.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I did. I did. I said, let me think about it. And then I was like, why would I not want to do the Grammys? First of all, it`s a front row ticket to the best music concert tour, because I don`t care who you are. You love music. It`s just your music that`ll be there, and then you get to pick and choose.\par \par \uc2 And then I realized how important it is for me on the night. Like as a host, I serve so many purposes. You know, like, if I`m not there, who lifts Kevin Hart into his seat? You know what I mean? There are important things that I am supposed to be doing on that night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: You know, I give Jay-Z emotional support. He`s very nervous. And I just tell him, it`s going to be okay, Jay. You know, you breaking a record, you`re beating Quincy Jones in nominations. I`m there to give people the moral support that they need.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do have a kind and generous heart.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s your mark. Go, go. It`s you.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: That`s me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now there isn`t a shortage of storylines that the world has provided you. How do you find that balance between being entertaining, informative, and like you always do, keeping it absolutely real?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I think you know, what it is, I try to put on "The Daily Show" what I`m feeling as a human being and what I`m experiencing with my team. I mean, you talk about things here, you know, we all do. We talk about things at home, we talk about things with our community, and on "The Daily Show," what I try and do is have those conversations no matter how complicated they get.\par \par \uc2 You know, like now with vaccines with omicron, with --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And all the variants. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Yes. It is complicated, but I`m trying to have honest conversations about it. What is normal? What should we be going back to as normal? What life do we want to live as people? And then in and all of that, just like how do you find the funny, right? Oh, by the way, I have omicron, I brought it for you guys. You should get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is in the back?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I brought you some, just a little you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Giving it out like Oprah.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Like a little gift bag thing, I just -- you know, as a South African, I thought I`d bring you some.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Specifically about that. You had Dr. Peter Hotez on your show earlier this week, and one of the things that it`s a headline right now on the front page of CBSnews.com, and "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " this omicron variant was actually first identified in the Netherlands. South African scientists were the first one to say, hey, world, you should know about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And your point was, could it be because it`s South -- that all of a sudden, you`ve got these travel bans?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Well, it`s just -- I mean, like we -- here`s the thing. People love to panic right now, you know. I don`t know if it`s because of how we read headlines. I don`t know if it`s because of how we write headlines. But discovering the variant doesn`t mean the variant was made now, and I think people are confused about these things, and rightfully so.\par \par \uc2 And so now we have this panic around the world where you get like African countries that are banned from traveling, and then the other countries that have the variant, they don`t get banned from traveling, but everybody -- you find the variant is just everywhere already. Even when people are like, it is marching north. It might not be, it might already just be everywhere, we just discovered it. So, let`s just kalmate, kalmate, everybody. Let`s just calm down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Trevor, I have many friends from South Africa who were really pissed to use it, to be delicate about it that South Africa identified it just to say, listen everybody, you should be aware and now South Africa is banned.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Yes. It was a very weird thing. It`s almost the he who smelt it dealt it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Of science. I mean that`s like a very strange way to do science, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they do feel like they`re being penalized, unlike China, they said, who didn`t really give anybody a warning. We`re just trying to give everybody a heads up to help you, and now we`re being penalized. Do you feel that way, too?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know the country very well.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Well, here is the thing. A lot of people don`t realize that South Africa has some of the best immunologists in the world, right? We developed some of the best science labs for gene sequencing, for figuring out what a virus is because of the HIV epidemic, right? Because of HIV we had to learn these things.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have some of the best doctors working on this to try and help figure out what the virus is, and so what South Africa did was say, hey, we helped figure it out. And then people were like, oh, then it`s yours. And it`s like, no, no, no, we`re just helping you figure it out.\par \par \uc2 And so I think this is part of the confusion in the world, is people not understanding what people are actually doing. Like, I feel like scientists are doing a great job, but there needs to be like a middle man who is explaining to people what the scientists are discovering because clearly, it goes from science to front page and then everybody just panics.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I had a chance to speak to your deep dive team, and Roy -- what -- a couple of weeks ago --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is the deep dive?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the deep dive team is the research team on "The Daily Show" that looks at what`s happening around the world in the headlines, and does exactly that, a deep dive into it, and then you turn it into comedy gold, right? Yes, and so what`s that process like? Describe that? Because I think it`s really interesting.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Well, the process is very un-American at times.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Un-American?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: What we do is -- yes, what we do is we try and breathe before we say something on a topic. So, we take our time, and we say maybe we don`t know everything about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You might want to say that out loud for the people in the back.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: And then what we do is we say like, what if we don`t know everything? And we take our time to see what there is to know about it, and we try to figure out what we don`t know about it. And then in that process, we try and have a fully formed opinion on a topic or an issue or an idea, because I think a lot of the time, you know, because of the instant world that we live in, people have been conditioned to have an opinion immediately.\par \par \uc2 What do you think? People were phoning me day one. Omicron. And I was like, I don`t even know what this thing -- what is it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you even saying?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Is that a new Transformer? What is it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You represent all of South Africa.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: What is going on here? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you pronounce it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Exactly. It`s like, well, I don`t know, and I`m allowed to say, I don`t know. And I think a lot of people need to get more comfortable being in a world of saying, I don`t know, and I don`t have an opinion yet. Let me form an opinion.\par \par \uc2 And so what we do the deep dives part of "The Daily Show" is we say, these are subjects where we`re trying to form an opinion based on information that we`ve gone over time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you worry, Trevor, explicitly being a comedian in these times of cancel culture, and you --\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: What is cancel culture?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not even -- not you.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Oh, we have to be careful when we use that phrase, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. No, but I`m serious. You could say one thing that could instantly change your whole life.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Yes, but that`s always been like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Has it always been like that?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Do you guys remember back in the day? Allie McBeal, people complained about something that happened with like the baby --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, the dancing baby.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: The baby of the show? The dancing baby and people complained.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. I forgot about that.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: They took a baby off the show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They took the baby of the show.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Yes, they took the baby off the show. The baby was cancelled. But no one said it was canceled. People have always complained about things, and then broadcasters or employers have always decided on what to do about that.\par \par \uc2 I understand, we have shorthand for it. I`ll play with cancel all the time with my friends. I`ll be like, you`re canceled. You`re canceled. But the truth is, your bosses can cancel you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At any time.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: \uc2 Twitter\uc2  can complain about you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: And there`s a difference between the two things. I think we`ve conflated everything. We go, Harvey Weinstein got canceled. No, Harvey Weinstein got prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: There`s a difference.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: You get what I am saying?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: And I think we have to be careful just saying everything is canceled, because it`s like, look, sometimes you`re just being criticized. Sometimes people are hating on you. Sometimes people are all ganging up on you. And it`s a mob, you know, and fundamentally, you know, the interesting thing about cancel culture is that the people talk about, they forget, you`re only like your side can cancel you these days, like Republicans cancel Republicans. You know what I mean, Democrats cancel Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people -- you can`t cancel the other. It`s too conflated to say cancel culture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you don`t live in a world worried about oh, I better not say X, Y, and Z. That`s not how you operate?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: No, I live in a world where I`m conscious. I do think about it. I go, am I trying to say that? And if I`m not, then how can I say it in a different way. But as a comedian, I know I`m going to say things that somebody is not going to appreciate, and that is the nature of what I`m doing. I`m playing with, you know, volatile materials. It might happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have to -- you have to identify where the outrage is coming from.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And say, okay, I don`t need to panic because they are.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I mean, people will get angry about things, but we don`t have to always say like, it`s cancel -- people just get angry. They just go like, ah, I hate that. I don`t like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We disagree and that`s okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we disagree.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: That`s fine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I wasn`t angry about you dancing with Cardi B though. That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With the moves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like the moves. I saw those moves. Remember that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see you with people.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Oh man. It`s going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, here we go.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Look at that. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Magic Mike act.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking forward to it, Trevor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: I mean, Cardi B is next to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look, I mean, look at you.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: You just get inspired. You get inspired.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve got to do some choreography though for this Awards show.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: Well, I don`t -- I don`t have great knees, so I mean this was like the best I could do, you know?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But the hips though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s say you`ve got a very good pelvis, I`ll say that. Thank you, Trevor Noah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: You`re going to get cancelled, Gayle. You`re going to get cancelled Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Gayle. I am out.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH: You can`t be saying that to me on national TV, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m on Times Square, taxi. Trevor, thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s called the pink slip.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are cheering you on. Right now, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In his famous novel, "The Plague," Albert Camus writes, quote, "We learn in time of pestilence that there are more things to admire in men than to despise." You could say that about the coronavirus and the AIDS epidemic that started 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 On this World AIDS Day, it is estimated about 36 million people have died from HIV related causes, and about 38 million people are living with the virus, and there is still no cure.\par \par \uc2 Our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook reflects on his own experience working at the height of the AIDS crisis. He went back to a place central to that time in his life.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: In the fall of 1976, I entered Medical School. I was eager to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re a first year Medical student, you don`t know a whole lot of Medicine. So my contribution to clinical care was basically limited to bringing my college singing group to the hospital to entertain sick children.\par \par \uc2 I graduated in May 1980 and off I went to start my internship as a newly minted doctor at what was then called Columbia Presbyterian Medical Center. Then, in March of 1981 --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Federal health officials consider it an epidemic, yet you rarely hear a thing about it.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: I saw my first patient with what would become known as AIDS. He was suffering from something I had never seen before, an eye infection from a common virus that a normal immune system could usually handle, but his immune system was anything but normal.\par \par \uc2 He died, and for years, so did every single patient with AIDS who I helped care for. So many of them were in what should have been the prime of their lives. They all had so-called opportunistic infections that attacked people with weakened immune systems.\par \par \uc2 HIV, the virus that causes AIDS, would not be discovered for another two years. But we were all discovering firsthand that it could decimate the immune system.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re all scared to death. We would like to know what the hell we`re supposed to do here. What should we do? Should we stay home and lock ourselves back in the closet or, you know, what should we do?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases Director): We don`t know what we`re dealing with and it`s deadly, and it`s transmissible.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: Wow. And it was right here in this very amphitheater, where students and staff got together for what was called Grand Rounds, trying to figure out what in the world was going on.\par \par \uc2 We all felt helpless, and I certainly didn`t think I was making a difference.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m 31 years old. And I`ve watched a great many of my friends die.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: Sometimes patients would get better for a while, if their infections were treatable, but they would always get readmitted to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 I remember one man especially well, a car mechanic in Washington Heights, a husband and a father, a lovely guy who could not have been more grateful for the care we gave him, even though our care didn`t prevent his death.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We want to live. That is all we want, America.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: Then in 1987 --\par \par \uc2 MAN: For the first time, a drug to treat AIDS patients got final government approval today. The drug AZT is not a vaccine against the disease nor a cure.\par \par \uc2 JON LAPOOK: It was astonishing. Finally, some hope. And thanks to scientific research, the treatment gradually improved over the years, and HIV-AIDS became a more chronic, though often still devastating condition.\par \par \uc2 Over the next 30 years, images of those brutal early days remained with me, but they mostly stayed in the background. Then 10 years ago, I saw "The Normal Heart," a gut wrenching play about the early days of the epidemic.\par \par \uc2 At the end, the names of people who had died of AIDS were projected on the walls of the theater.\par \par \uc2 I couldn`t move. My eyes filled with tears, as the sadness of that time came flooding back. Those memories were a big reason why in April of 2020, I helped out on the COVID wards at NYU Langone Health where I`m a Professor of Medicine.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to share what I had learned with the young students, doctors, and nurses who might be feeling as helpless as I did in 1981. They were way ahead of me. They had already arranged for their patients who were in isolation to video chat with loved ones, or kept them company by sitting at the bedside. They understood the therapeutic value of human touch, of reaching out and holding a hand.\par \par \uc2 They also seem to understand it was okay to shed a tear or two, and it was okay to take advantage of mental health services.\par \par \uc2 Over the years I`ve learned there all sorts of ways to make a difference, and it`s almost never something as dramatic as saving somebody`s life. Much more often, it is by making their journey gentler, by showing them compassion and empathy, by listening, by being present.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Jon LaPook. I`m telling you, you know, he is, to me one of the most empathetic caring people that I`ve ever met. So, it`s no surprise to see early in his career, that he was still that same person. His message is so important and so true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Especially on this World AIDS Day, which was essentially created to unite everybody around the world to try and fight this deadly disease and to bring empathy to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My mom`s brother, uncle Rick, he passed away from AIDS in 1990. I was nine years old. That forced us to become familiar with AIDS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that familiarity turned into education.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So that piece was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Remember, in the beginning, we were so afraid. Nobody knew what we were dealing with. Thank you, Jon.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was tough back then because no one knew what it was, no one knew what it was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. All right, we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ve got to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. We will see you tomorrow. Take care.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211201ehc100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 America`s Roman Catholic Bishops Are Set To Approve New Guidance For Giving Holy Communion\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Ed O`keefe, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 America`s Roman Catholic bishops are set to approve new guidance for giving Holy Communion. It`s become an issue that`s getting new attention because of President Biden. Some Catholic leaders say the president and others who support abortion rights are not fit to receive communion. Mr. Biden says \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  gave him the go ahead when they met last month.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe reports on the argument over one of the church`s most basic rights and who should be allowed to get it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: In the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: At a meeting of American Catholic bishops in Baltimore, the pope`s top envoy to the United States urged them to listen ahead of a debate on adopting a document about holy communion that was sparked in part by the election of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP CHRISTOPHER PIERRE (Apostolic Nuncio to the United States): A church that teaches must be firstly a church that listens.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Mr. Biden met with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  last month at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We just talked about the fact that the was happy I was a good Catholic and I should keep receiving Communion.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The visit and the pope`s apparent encouragement to continue receiving communion came as American Catholic Bishops continue debating whether Catholic politicians like the president and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who support abortion should be barred from communion if they don`t follow church doctrine that opposes the procedure. Certain bishops say they`re trying to clarify the importance of communion amid recent polls showing many Catholics don`t fully understand or believe in the sacrament.\par \par \uc2 SALVATORE CORDILEONE (Archbishop of San Francisco): This has been lingering for a long time.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: San Francisco`s Archbishop Salvatore Cordileone and other supporters of the document being voted on have said it`s not directed at any particular Catholic, but --\par \par \uc2 SALVATORE CORDILEONE: The election of President Biden, a church-going Catholic who also takes position on human life, gave more impetus to this.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So, in essence, the election of President Biden is what compelled you all to say we need to have a bigger conversation about this?\par \par \uc2 SALVATORE CORDILEONE: I think this gave us stimulus to doing it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president attends mass nearly every weekend and has said that his Catholic faith is central to his public life. He`s known Francis since the start of his papacy and said they`ve bonded over the death of Beau Biden.\par \par \uc2 Father Anthony Figueiredo, a \uc2 CBS\uc2  news papal contributor, believes what American bishops are doing runs counter to the pope`s wishes.\par \par \uc2 MSGR. ANTHONY FIGUEIREDO: It`s a very different vision to some of the bishops who want to use the Eucharist as a political football. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  says no to a political football.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Archbishop Salvatore Cordileone is one of the church leaders who said he would deny communion to the president because of his support of abortion rights. The White House declined to comment on the conference. When asked during his visit to the Vatican, the president would only say of the conference that it`s a private conversation -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a personal matter also becoming more political, though. Ed, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211118ehbh0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Intense Debate At The Supreme Court Over Possible Challenges To The Controversial Abortion Law In Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be watching. Turning now to the Supreme Court where the justices heard arguments over a unique anti-abortion law in Texas. The court must decide if the law can be challenged in lower courts. Some of the justices questioned the way the law was enforced during oral presentations yesterday. Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court. Jan, good morning. JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, good morning, you know, these were some of the most interesting arguments I have ever covered. I mean, it was three hours, incredibly high stakes, and it was like being in the middle of this giant legal Rubik`s Cube almost. You know, they would turn here, and you`d think they had it resolved with the questions they asked. But then they`d turn here because that would raise a different problem. I mean, no state has ever tried to do what Texas is doing here. Which is banning something here, you know, abortion after about six weeks that the Supreme court has said is protected under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2  And then they`re saying, well, you know, we won`t enforce it, we`ll just let private citizens file civil lawsuits in state courts to enforce the law. And, oh, by the way, with damages of at least $10,000. I mean, it was clear that a majority of the justices did not like it. I mean, even some of the conservatives who recognize this law could have an impact well beyond abortion rights. There was a real concern that it would be a model for other states to pass similar laws that could target other constitutional rights. You know, if the court would allow this law as liberal Justice Elena Kagan had put it, that would mean "We would live in a very different world." A point that was also made by conservative Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . (Begin VT) \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (Associate Justice, United States Supreme Court): It could be free speech rights. It could be free exercise of religion rights. It could be Second Amendment rights if this position is accepted here. (End VT) JAN CRAWFORD: Now the court is not reconsidering Roe versus Wade in this case. But next month it`s going to take up a different case. It`s asking the justices to overturn Roe and allow Mississippi`s 15-week ban on abortion. Tony.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211103ehb2000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Election Day: The Governor`s Race Could Have National Implications\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Ed O`keefe, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to "\uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings" on this second day of November. I`m Gayle King. TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson. GAYLE KING: We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s eye opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds. (Begin VT) GLENN YOUNGKIN (Republican Candidate for Virginia Governor): This is a defining moment for the future of our commonwealth. \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2  (Democrat Candidate for Virginia Governor): We`re at the end zone. Are you ready to take that ball over the line. (End VT) GAYLE KING: Virginia is for voters on this Election Day. While the Governor`s race there could have national implications. TONY DOKOUPIL: A blunt warning from President Biden on climate change, he says America will lead by example. (Begin VT) \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (United States President): It`s not a hypothetical threat. It is destroying peoples` lives and livelihoods.\par \par \uc2  (End VT) TONY DOKOUPIL: Joe Manchin says he cannot support Biden`s social spending package until he gets "greater clarity." (Begin VT) SEN JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): What I see are shell games, budget games. (End VT) TONY DOKOUPIL: And intense debate at the Supreme Court over possible challenges to the controversial abortion law in Texas. (Begin VT) UNKNOWN: If someone needs to have an abortion, they should be able to do so. (End VT) TONY DOKOUPIL: More travel delays for passengers flying American airlines, the carrier was forced to cancel hundreds of more flights. Monday night football, the Chiefs beat the Giants 20 to 17. (Begin VT) MAN: Throws the throw (INAUDIBLE). Intercepted. (End VT) TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden is at the U.N. Climate Change Conference in Scotland. (Begin VT) UNKNOWN: Biden appearing to be dosing off a little bit, caught on camera with his eyes closed. UNKNOWN: In Biden`s defense, he is conserving energy and that is what the conference is all about. (End VT) GAYLE KING: President Biden began his five-day diplomatic trip to Europe on Friday by meeting with Pope Francis. (Begin VT) STEPHEN COLBERT (Host, The Colbert Report): When it was time to say goodbye the president had a message for the Holy Father. BIDEN: God love ya. STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes. Yes, he does. Pretty sure that`s the papal job description on LinkedIn. God love ya, must be good with crowds. Some Latin required, 5-10 years waving experience. TONY DOKOUPIL: That is very good. GAYLE KING: Steven Colbert. You`re right, Tony, that was very good. It`s a common thing, God love you. But the President did look like he was dosing a little bit. NATE BURLESON: Nothing like a good nap. TONY DOKOUPIL: A bit delicious, closing his eyes. That felt good. GAYLE KING: There you go. We`ve got a lot going on for you today on this Tuesday with vaccine approval for younger kids likely coming later this week. There`s bound to be some nerves around those needles, I`ll say but it doesn`t have to be the experts say. Dr. Tara Narula shows us a program that`s using superheroes. Who doesn`t love a superhero to help kids overcome their fears and introduces us to a very brave young man who`s tackling his concerns. TONY DOKOUPIL: We like that. We`re also going to go to Mark Phillips who continues his journey across the globe to explore the impact of climate change. His latest trek takes him to the Scottish highlands where it may not look like much but the land there is actually critical to reversing the effects of carbon in our atmosphere. Mark`s going to show us why and how it is now in danger. But we are going to begin here in this country were we are covering a number of importance developments in American politics. First in Congress where Senator Joe Manchin is wavering, yet again, on President Biden`s massive agenda and that could he throw a big wrench in the Democrats plans. Meanwhile, over at the Supreme Court the justices are facing tough legal questions over that controversial anti-abortion rights bill in Texas. And it is also Election Day where a key race in Virginia could have national consequences and we`re going to start there with Ed O`Keefe who`s been following it all for us. Ed, it looks very close there. Good morning. ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Tony. And welcome to Virginia`s largest county. We`re smiling through the masks this morning because nearly 50 people were in line before the polls opened. One woman came in here a little while ago, pumped her fist and said I`m ready. We like to see that kind of enthusiasm on Election Day. This is just one governor`s race in the southern state but this contest may give us early hints of what to expect in elections next year across the country. (Begin VT) GLENN YOUNGKIN: Wake up Virginia, here we come. \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : Let`s bring this baby home. ED O`KEEFE: More than 1.1 million estimated early votes cast in a race between Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Republican Glenn Youngkin that polls show is a tossup. Youngkin is a wealthy businessman and first-time candidate. GLENN YOUNGKIN: We get to stand up and say no to this left liberal progressive agenda that`s trying to take us over. No. ED O`KEEFE: He`s keeping his focus on parental control of education. GLENN YOUNGKIN: What we won`t do is teach our children to view everything through a lens of race. ED O`KEEFE: Youngkin also appears to be benefitting from an increasingly unpopular President Biden. \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Terry is running against an acolyte of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . ED O`KEEFE: McAuliffe is a former governor and he`s running again by running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : I`ve beaten Trump twice in Virginia. Tomorrow, we go three and zero. ED O`KEEFE: He`s relentlessly tried tying Youngkin to the former president by, among other things, raising concerns about abortion rights after Texas passed one of the strictest abortion laws in the country. \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : Men like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin should not be telling women what to do with their bodies. O`KEEFE: Youngkin says he voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last year. And although he`s avoided campaigning with the former president, that didn`t stop Trump from holding a telephone rally with Virginia Republicans last night. (Begin VT) \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President Of The United States): You`ve got to send a message to this really corrupt media, the fake news and all of the people who are destroying our country. Vote for Glen Youngkin. He`s going to do a fantastic job. (End VT) ED O`KEEFE: Also today, up in New Jersey they`re voting for governor. And in big cities across the country from Atlanta to Boston, Cleveland to New York they`re picking new mayors to fill city halls. But again, this contest here could be a big indicator of what to expect. Because if we turnout in a swing state, a blue state where the president`s approval is sagging, it could ultimately give us a good sense of the results, Gayle. It`s going to be a long day. GAYLE KING: A long day. And you up for it? I know you ate your Wheaties this morning, Ed. We`ll be counting on you. We`ll talk to you a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. A