ake that ball over the line.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Virginia is for voters on this Election Day. While the Governor`s race there could have national implications.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A blunt warning from President Biden on climate change, he says America will lead by example.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (United States President): It`s not a hypothetical threat. It is destroying peoples` lives and livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joe Manchin says he cannot support Biden`s social spending package until he gets "greater clarity."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): What I see are shell games, budget games.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And intense debate at the Supreme Court over possible challenges to the controversial abortion law in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If someone needs to have an abortion, they should be able to do so.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More travel delays for passengers flying American airlines, the carrier was forced to cancel hundreds of more flights.\par \par \uc2 Monday night football, the Chiefs beat the Giants 20 to 17.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Throws the throw (INAUDIBLE). Intercepted.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden is at the U.N. Climate Change Conference in Scotland.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Biden appearing to be dosing off a little bit, caught on camera with his eyes closed.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: In Biden`s defense, he is conserving energy and that is what the conference is all about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden began his five-day diplomatic trip to Europe on Friday by meeting with Pope Francis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT (Host, The Colbert Report): When it was time to say goodbye the president had a message for the Holy Father.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: God love ya.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes. Yes, he does. Pretty sure that`s the papal job description on LinkedIn. God love ya, must be good with crowds. Some Latin required, 5-10 years waving experience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is very good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Steven Colbert. You`re right, Tony, that was very good. It`s a common thing, God love you. But the President did look like he was dosing a little bit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing like a good nap.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A bit delicious, closing his eyes. That felt good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go. We`ve got a lot going on for you today on this Tuesday with vaccine approval for younger kids likely coming later this week. There`s bound to be some nerves around those needles, I`ll say but it doesn`t have to be the experts say.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Tara Narula shows us a program that`s using superheroes. Who doesn`t love a superhero to help kids overcome their fears and introduces us to a very brave young man who`s tackling his concerns.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We like that. We`re also going to go to Mark Phillips who continues his journey across the globe to explore the impact of climate change. His latest trek takes him to the Scottish highlands where it may not look like much but the land there is actually critical to reversing the effects of carbon in our atmosphere. Mark`s going to show us why and how it is now in danger.\par \par \uc2 But we are going to begin here in this country were we are covering a number of importance developments in American politics. First in Congress where Senator Joe Manchin is wavering, yet again, on President Biden`s massive agenda and that could he throw a big wrench in the Democrats plans.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, over at the Supreme Court the justices are facing tough legal questions over that controversial anti-abortion rights bill in Texas. And it is also Election Day where a key race in Virginia could have national consequences and we`re going to start there with Ed O`Keefe who`s been following it all for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it looks very close there. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Tony. And welcome to Virginia`s largest county. We`re smiling through the masks this morning because nearly 50 people were in line before the polls opened. One woman came in here a little while ago, pumped her fist and said I`m ready. We like to see that kind of enthusiasm on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 This is just one governor`s race in the southern state but this contest may give us early hints of what to expect in elections next year across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN: Wake up Virginia, here we come.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : Let`s bring this baby home.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: More than 1.1 million estimated early votes cast in a race between Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Republican Glenn Youngkin that polls show is a tossup.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin is a wealthy businessman and first-time candidate.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN: We get to stand up and say no to this left liberal progressive agenda that`s trying to take us over. No.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He`s keeping his focus on parental control of education.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN: What we won`t do is teach our children to view everything through a lens of race.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Youngkin also appears to be benefitting from an increasingly unpopular President Biden.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Terry is running against an acolyte of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: McAuliffe is a former governor and he`s running again by running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : I`ve beaten Trump twice in Virginia. Tomorrow, we go three and zero.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: He`s relentlessly tried tying Youngkin to the former president by, among other things, raising concerns about abortion rights after Texas passed one of the strictest abortion laws in the country.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 : Men like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin should not be telling women what to do with their bodies.\par \par \uc2 O`KEEFE: Youngkin says he voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last year. And although he`s avoided campaigning with the former president, that didn`t stop Trump from holding a telephone rally with Virginia Republicans last night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President Of The United States): You`ve got to send a message to this really corrupt media, the fake news and all of the people who are destroying our country. Vote for Glen Youngkin. He`s going to do a fantastic job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Also today, up in New Jersey they`re voting for governor. And in big cities across the country from Atlanta to Boston, Cleveland to New York they`re picking new mayors to fill city halls.\par \par \uc2 But again, this contest here could be a big indicator of what to expect. Because if we turnout in a swing state, a blue state where the president`s approval is sagging, it could ultimately give us a good sense of the results, Gayle. It`s going to be a long day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A long day. And you up for it? I know you ate your Wheaties this morning, Ed. We`ll be counting on you. We`ll talk to you a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 It has been a wild ride in Washington for President Biden`s plan for a new social safety net, and now it`s hit another bump. For weeks, West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin has been demanding a lower price tag. And he got that, but now he says he needs to know more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE MANCHIN: As more of the real details outlying the basic framework are released, what I see are shell games, budget gimmicks that make the real cost of the so-called $1.75 trillion bill estimated to be almost twice that amount.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Manchin is also upset that a smaller infrastructure bill has yet to pass. He blames House progressives for that, and they blame conservative Democrats like him.\par \par \uc2 Here now to break it all down, our Chief Washington Correspondent Major Garrett. We are so glad. When we heard you were coming yesterday, we all did the hula (ph) --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Gayle, it`s been a year since I`ve been here. Can I just say, I love what you`ve done with the place --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- seriously.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re so glad --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- you`re here on this day in particular. OK. Joe Manchin, do you think --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- the White House has had it up to here with him these days? And how do you see this all playing out?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So the White House knows that Democrats in Congress don`t trust each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That`s the biggest problem. Joe Manchin doesn`t trust the progressives and the progressives don`t trust Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has to be the trust builder. He has to say, look, I`ve given everyone lots of time to go over the details, the numbers and the legislative language, but I`ve got to blow the whistle.\par \par \uc2 Nate, you know what happens when the whistle blows. The place stops --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- and everyone knows what to do next, line up for the next play. The next play is to vote. The next play is to vote and decide on both these initiatives so the Biden domestic agenda can have something it`s lacked for weeks, and weeks and weeks --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So why doesn`t (ph) he blow the whistle?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- clarity and action.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why doesn`t he blow the whistle then?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The president has --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And say enough (ph).\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- to make that decision himself. And those around him have to say, Mr. President, now is the time.\par \par \uc2 He has had that opportunity before and has let it pass. At some point, you have to say it`s time to vote --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- and time to clarify what`s actually at stake. And important things are at stake.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want to suggest Democrats are simply bickering over trivia. They`re not. Home health care via Medicaid for the sandwich generation for their elderly parents is a huge deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Universal pre-K is a huge deal. These are monumental changes. And how they happen, and at what speed and what price tag matter a great deal. That`s why they continue to negotiate. But at some point, you`ve got to say let`s move forward, define what we`ve done and begin to explain it to the country that`s going to receive it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So on the subject of "at some point" is there any kind of natural barrier or deadline? Or can Democrats keep pushing this down the calendar infinitely?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Not infinitely. Time does --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: At least until the midterms --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes, yes. Practical time is flexible, as Democrats have proved. \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  was begging for three weeks, vote on infrastructure, give me a win in Virginia so I can point to Democrats governing in a way that is coherent, meaningful and responsive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He didn`t get that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Progressives said, you know what, run on what we`ve already done. And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  said, no, no I want infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 If he loses that race in Virginia, and he very well might, one of the reasons -- not the only reason, but one of the reasons will be Democrats failing to define what governing looks like.\par \par \uc2 So to your point, they could take this another week. There is a December 3rd deadline on the continuing resolution to keep the government open and extend the debt ceiling. They could go there. I`ve watched Congresses take it all the way to Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Until the president says, folks, we`ve talked, we`ve negotiated, lock arms, vote, pass it, we`re going to continue to move down this sort of era of drift and disorganization. Two words that don`t work --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: -- well for Democrats and work very well for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, there`s other key elections going on around the country. What are you watching out for?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Other thing I`m keeping an eye on is the New Jersey governor`s race. Should not be close, it is closer than you would think based on the blue state history of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Education has been the issue that has galvanized Republican voters in Virginia and New Jersey. If Glenn Youngkin wins in Virginia against \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and this race in New Jersey is close Republicans will take that education issue and run with it harder in the mid-terms. So, I`m keeping a very close eye on New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Major, we`re keeping a very close eye on you. Thank you for coming in today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s good to have you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really is. I love seeing Major Garrett. We will see you tonight along with Ed, Elaine Quijano, and the entire CBS News political team for coverage of all of today`s elections. And, of course, you can stream the coverage tonight on CBSN, available on that CBS News app that you have on your phone and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be watching. Turning now to the Supreme Court where the justices heard arguments over a unique anti-abortion law in Texas. The court must decide if the law can be challenged in lower courts. Some of the justices questioned the way the law was enforced during oral presentations yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court. Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, good morning, you know, these were some of the most interesting arguments I have ever covered. I mean, it was three hours, incredibly high stakes, and it was like being in the middle of this giant legal Rubik`s Cube almost.\par \par \uc2 You know, they would turn here, and you`d think they had it resolved with the questions they asked. But then they`d turn here because that would raise a different problem. I mean, no state has ever tried to do what Texas is doing here.\par \par \uc2 Which is banning something here, you know, abortion after about six weeks that the Supreme court has said is protected under the Constitution. And then they`re saying, well, you know, we won`t enforce it, we`ll just let private citizens file civil lawsuits in state courts to enforce the law. And, oh, by the way, with damages of at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was clear that a majority of the justices did not like it. I mean, even some of the conservatives who recognize this law could have an impact well beyond abortion rights. There was a real concern that it would be a model for other states to pass similar laws that could target other constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 You know, if the court would allow this law as liberal Justice Elena Kagan had put it, that would mean "We would live in a very different world." A point that was also made by conservative Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (Associate Justice, United States Supreme Court): It could be free speech rights. It could be free exercise of religion rights. It could be Second Amendment rights if this position is accepted here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now the court is not reconsidering Roe versus Wade in this case. But next month it`s going to take up a different case. It`s asking the justices to overturn Roe and allow Mississippi`s 15-week ban on abortion. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big one. We`ll be watching that. Jan, thank you very much. All this action at the Supreme Court comes as President Biden attends the UN Climate Change Conference in Glasgow, Scotland.\par \par \uc2 The president says the U.S. is ready to lead by example and he also apologized for America`s withdrawal from the Paris Climate Agreement under the previous administration. Weijia Jiang is traveling with the president and she`s in Edinburgh, Scotland. Weijia, Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Tony, and good morning to everybody. President Biden is determined to convince the world that the U.S. is back at the head of the table when it comes to fighting climate change.\par \par \uc2 He spoke about the overwhelming obligation, especially to help poorer countries warning that the worst is yet to come. And the administration continues to call out big polluters like China.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Climate change is already ravaging the world.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): President Biden agreed with world leaders and climate activists who issued one dire warning after another.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We are digging our own graves.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WATHUTI (Climate And Environment Activist): Over two million of my fellow Kenyans are facing climate-related starvation.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): Delegations from almost 200 countries are attending the COP 26 Summit, eager to build on the 2015 Paris Climate Accord that set a goal of zeroing out global carbon emissions by 2050.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I do apologize for the fact the United States in the last administration pulled out of the Paris Accord and put us sort of behind the eight ball.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): Other countries like China and Russia have rejected the mid-century target. Even though they are major contributors to pollution. White House National Climate Advisor Gina McCarthy, who spoke with CBS News after a climate town hall in Glasgow acknowledged that pushing Beijing may require tougher tools.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY (National Climate Advisor, White House): I have faith that China will come around. But we know that their goals right now aren`t sufficient to be able to address their challenges.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (on-screen): Is diplomacy enough?\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY: No, probably not.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: So, then what`s the alternative?\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY: It`s continuing to look for ways in which we send economic signals about what products we want to buy, and what we expect in those products.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): Queen Elizabeth, who canceled her attendance following medical advice urged world leaders to rise above politics.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN ELIZABETH II: We are doing this not for ourselves but for our children and our children`s children, and those who will follow in their footsteps.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (on-screen): Despite all the promises that President Biden is making here in Scotland now that his own climate agenda back home is in trouble once again, as you guys were talking about with Major, some experts are skeptical that the U.S. can even honor its own commitments. Those are some tough questions the president will surely face during his press conference tonight. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Everyone`s looking to him for the answers. Thank you, Weijia, very much. The first six weeks of fall have been unusually warm in much of this country. This week that will change with the coldest air of the new season moving into northern areas.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Megan Glaros is here in the studio to show us where people need to bundle up. I say, Megan, we can start here in this studio. Welcome to you in Times Square. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: It`s nice to see you too. Nate, Tony, Gayle you`re absolute - - it`s a little chilly in here. So, I`m with you on that one. I should have worn pants. Let`s take a look. We`ve got Snowshoe, West Virginia today, which by the way, is actually getting snow. You mentioned it`s been so warm.\par \par \uc2 Now we see not only signs of fall but potentially signs of winter to come. This is the first blast of actual cold weather that we`ve had in recent days. And, in fact, I want to take a look at this because chilly afternoon temperatures are on tap for cities like Chicago at 45, that`s 11 degrees below normal. Wichita, 43, that`s 21 degrees below normal.\par \par \uc2 And a similar setup at Oklahoma City and also in Lubbock. Little Rock coming in at 54 degrees. So, clearly, getting a bit colder across the area. And when we take a look at the morning temperatures this is something that we want to mention because it has been so warm in the mornings and that`s changing as well today.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-eight in Chicago, we`re going to have freeze warnings all the way from Danbury down to Charlottesville. In fact, we have had days on end above 50 degrees, such a very warm stretch for Chicago, 138 days above 50, Boston 140 days above 50. So, that brings me to this, the warmest morning temperatures in October ever for more than 100 cities.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about 10 degrees above normal in Detroit, seven in Chicago, and eight in Pittsburgh. So, that`s why this blast of cold is going to feel so cold. Because it`s been so mild, we`ve had such a warm fall.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we were getting spoiled.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: Sure were. Coming to an end though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t even know where my coat is. In fact, I got caught this morning too cold, no jacket.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: Happening to most of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I just want to say well done. You look like Tom Cruise in the Minority Report --\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- swiping those screens --\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: I -- did you like that? Like that move?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Megan --\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and to quote Adele (ph), rumor has it you`re going to be here all week.\par \par \uc2 MEGNA GLAROS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re glad -- we`re glad --\par \par \uc2 MEGAN GLAROS: Looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- glad you`re here. Coming up right now it`s time to check your weather, it is now 7:17 -- 7:18.\par \par \uc2 ELISE FINCH: I expect chilly conditions as you step out the door this morning. But it will still be chilly this afternoon when we hit our high temperature. Most locations will max out in the low to mid-50s. Fifty-four degrees expected in New York City today.\par \par \uc2 We get that with more clouds and a chance for rain, a slight chance, especially south and east of New York City. For tomorrow the sunshine returns but temperatures will continue to cool down. Fifty-two degrees the forecast high for Wednesday. By Thursday the forecast high only 50 degrees.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up we`ll hear from a woman who got billed for getting no treatment at a hospital emergency room. That doesn`t sound right. This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have an update for you on the death penalty case of Julius Jones that we just told you about yesterday. Oklahoma`s Pardon and Parole Board voted three to one to recommend clemency for Jones. He`s on death row for the 1999 murder of Paul Howell.\par \par \uc2 Now Jones has consistently maintained his innocence and another man has even confessed to the crime.\par \par \uc2 The Parole Board recommended his sentence be commuted to life with the possibility of parole. One board member said she continues to believe that there is still doubt in this case.\par \par \uc2 The decision now on whether to grant clemency rests with Oklahoma governor, that`s Kevin Stitt, if he does not grant clemency Jones is scheduled to be executed November 18.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. Today, a CDC committee may endorse giving the Pfizer COVID vaccine to children ages five to 11. That would be the first vaccine authorized for patients so young.\par \par \uc2 More than 67,000 Americans under the age of 17 had been hospitalized for COVID infections since August of 2020.\par \par \uc2 However, that is far more than the number of younger patients hospitalized in the United Kingdom, where interestingly enough COVID restrictions are much looser.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata looks now at some of the possible reasons.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Children going to school today in England are far less likely to end up in the hospital with COVID than American kids.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the risk in the U.S. is several times higher. And it`s got the medical community on both sides of the pond puzzled.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Medical Contributor, Dr. David Agus.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: We`re afraid, I mean, when kids are hospitalized at pretty significant numbers, that 10 percent of the cases in the last few months are kids aged five through 11.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And here are the numbers in the most recent week of statistics comparing both countries in October. Per 100,000 people, the United States had more than 10 times more hospital admissions for those under 18 than in England. And we want to know why.\par \par \uc2 It`s not masks. It may be a hot button issue in the United States, but they are not mandatory here. In fact, none of these schoolchildren are wearing masks, which may have contributed to the outbreak at Abby Schroeder`s school.\par \par \uc2 MOTHER OF ABBY SCHROEDER: They`re sitting close together in a classroom, they weren`t wearing masks. I think it is probably very, you know, likely that actually that is why there was such a rapid rate of infection.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And Abby was not spared. What was it like?\par \par \uc2 ABBY SCHROEDER: I didn`t have that many symptoms. It was just like a bad cold kind of, and it was just annoying because I couldn`t see my friends.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Abby`s case is typical. Kids getting sick but very few, sick enough to be hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 It`s not that infection rates are any higher in the United States, in fact, it`s much worse here. Across the UK, they`ve got one of the highest infection rates in the world.\par \par \uc2 Britain is once again at the peak of another surge fueled by elementary school children with those under 15 reportedly accounting for more than a third of all recent cases.\par \par \uc2 It`s not the uptake of vaccines either. In the under 18`s, the percentage of the total population fully vaccinated is lower here than in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Which leaves medical experts to consider other influences, like whether diet or childhood diabetes, are driving up hospitalizations among kids in the States.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: We`re seeing those biological differences. Country to country, there are certain behavior differences because we`re all different.\par \par \uc2 Our genetics are different, our lifestyle is different, what we`re exposed to is different, our prior viruses are different. And they all go into the 