ssa Essaibi George\uc2 , both are Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Well, who had the early lead in pre-election polls, would also be one of the only Asian-American mayors in a major U.S. city.\par \par \uc2 Essaibi George also grew up in an immigrant family. Nancy Chen looks at why Boston is finally ready to embrace change at the top.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The birthplace of the American Revolution has led the way with a history of firsts. But in one of the only major U.S. cities yet to elect someone besides a white man as mayor, Boston politics is now having a revolution of its own.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: City councilors, \uc2 Annissa Essaibi George\uc2 , and Michelle Wu are facing off in the historic race. Whoever wins will not only be the first woman elected mayor but will also be the first person of color elected to that post.\par \par \uc2 Did you always want to get into politics?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU (Boston Mayoral Candidate): It was never on any list of imagination or possibility.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At all?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: Never.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Wu is the daughter of Taiwanese immigrants.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: I remember growing up and never seen anyone who looked like me on TV, in politics. And so, the thought never crossed my mind that one day I could be part of these conversations.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Essaibi George relates. Her parents also immigrated to the U.S., her father from Tunisia.\par \par \uc2 ANNISSA ESSAIBI GEORGE\uc2  (Boston Mayoral Candidate): I can`t help but think back to saying to my father that I had an interest in maybe running for office someday and he said an Arab girl with an Arab name will win nothing in the city.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Both \uc2 Essaibi George\uc2  and Wu say they strive to set a new example for their children.\par \par \uc2 What are the biggest issues that Boston is facing right now?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: It is housing affordability, first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Wu was the first woman of color to serve as City Council President. Wu had a pivotal moment in 2008 when her mother struggled with a mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: She went from being the person who held together so much of our family to not being able to care for herself much less my younger sisters at home.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And how did that impact you wanting to get into politics?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: It was a sense of just being invisible when it came to the types of help that my family needed. It felt like so many hoops to jump through.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Essaibi George, a former public school teacher is focused on education and economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 ANNISSA ESSAIBI GEORGE\uc2 : I think about the impact this pandemic has had on our small businesses.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: This election comes just months after Boston`s acting mayor \uc2 Kim Janey\uc2  was appointed, but only temporarily.\par \par \uc2 Could you have even imagined this for Boston 15 years ago?\par \par \uc2 LEANNE DOHERTY (Chair, \uc2 Simmons University\uc2  Political Science & International Relations): Not even close.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Leanne Doherty teaches a class on female political leadership at \uc2 Simmons University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why has it taken Boston so long to elect a mayor who is not a man, who is not white?\par \par \uc2 LEANNE DOHERTY: Perceptions of voters about how women can lead is very important. And Boston does take time in trying to make change.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And so, well, history is about to be made, both women are focused on the future. The race they say is about more than them.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WU: The first is only relevant if there`s a second, third, fourth, and then the door is wide open for everyone. And there`s a lot more change where this is coming from.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Nancy Chen in Boston, Massachuse