lowing Carey`s hit song to be played after December 1. And even then, it can only be played once a night.\par \par \uc2 The strict rules have left some of the singer`s fans in an uproar. Carey weighed in on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with this picture branding it a "what on Christmas".\par \par \uc2 It is the most popular Christmas song on Spotify.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. That`s when you know Christmas season is here. And Mariah is ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mariah`s ready. They`re drinking Hatorade down there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can`t argue they got some Hatorade. Give Mariah her love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, thank you very much. Coming up, Dr. Tara Narula on kids and shots. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ***************(08:00am ET)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King. Coming up this hour, COVID vaccines for younger kids, they`re on the way. And Dr. Tara Narula will give us tips on how to overcome your kids` fear of needles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. A new book by Michael Eric Dyson takes a unique look at race and identity in America. He`s going to join us on set for an essential conversation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We`ve also got actress, Emily Ashford, in the house talking about a major shift for a character in the hit show, Be Positive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Major shift is a major clue. But first, here`s today`s "EYE- OPENER" at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was that dance that was going on there? Is that an End Zone dance?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the end zone celebration.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: John still got the moves?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think everything is better when John Stewart`s in the room, myself. I think he`s terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree completely. But if you want him in the room, he was on a webcam. No, I don`t know that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: However, you can get him I think he`s terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re going to begin this hour with some important news. Children ages, five to 11 may be able to get COVID vaccines as soon as tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 That`s because an independent CDC advisory panel will meet this morning to consider authorizing Pfizer`s pediatric vaccine.\par \par \uc2 It would then be up to CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2  to give the final AOK for some 28 million American kids to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing, only 27 percent of parents say they will vaccinate their kids right away. Another third say they`ll wait and see how it`s working before making a decision. And another third say they will definitely not vaccinate their kids at all.\par \par \uc2 But even families who want the vaccine, face another challenge. Oh, it`s a classic one. Fear of needles.\par \par \uc2 Senior Medical Correspondent, Dr. Tara Narula is here to show us how one program uses science and technology to help families reduce their fear and anxiety. Can they do that Tara? Please help us.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: They can, Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Good morning to all of you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m listening with both ears. Oh, I`m scared.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: An estimated 63 percent of children are afraid of needles and so are many of their parents. One study found the fear is so bad. Some kids avoid routine vaccines.\par \par \uc2 But as I learned, there are proven strategies to reduce pain and give kids more control over the vaccination process.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Azariah Bannister likes to think he`s got superpowers. But when it comes to getting his upcoming flu shot, he needs a little help.\par \par \uc2 Meet SuperMeg a chatbot that helps kids overcome their fears about needles.\par \par \uc2 Created by the Meg Foundation, a nonprofit focused on pain management SuperMeg, guides nine-year-old Azariah through the vaccine process teaching him breathing and distraction techniques.\par \par \uc2 So, when he arrives at Inova Children`s Hospital in Virginia, Azariah is ready.\par \par \uc2 AZARIAH BANNISTER: I took a deep breath and looked away.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Science shows the source of pain lies in the brain.\par \par \uc2 JODY THOMAS, (Pediatric Psychologist): Pain is not just about the needle going in the skin it`s about the anxiety.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Jody Thomas is a pediatric psychologist and CEO of the Meg Foundation.\par \par \uc2 JODY THOMAS: Again, kids often feel like they don`t have a choice to get that poke, so as much choice as we can give them. Which arm do we want that poke in? Which toy do you want to bring? Do you want to sit on my lap?\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: How does the debate that obviously children have been hearing from the adults around them complicate the preparation for the vaccine rollout to young children?\par \par \uc2 JODY THOMAS: Kids are, of course, hearing everything they always do.\par \par \uc2 And we`re hearing kids talk about that they want to get this vaccination because it not only keeps themselves healthy, but it`s helping take care of others.\par \par \uc2 And that`s an incredibly powerful message for kids. They`re doing something good for the world.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: For Azariah, he plans to use his new superpowers for the next big shot, his COVID vaccine.\par \par \uc2 AZARIAH BANNISTER: It`s a pandemic, and it`s very fatal. They don`t want to get sick.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cute boy. Great, I like it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, what`s so tricky here for parents is they don`t want to trivialize fear of vaccines, they want to say, oh, you`re fine don`t worry about it, but at the same time, you don`t want to recognize it too much because then you can feed into it. So, what`s the balance? How do you find it?\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Well, there are a lot of proven strategies and you heard in the piece. One big one is really just finding ways to give your child control to put them in the driver`s seat of this process. And that starts with really making a plan, usually one to two days ahead.\par \par \uc2 So, as Dr. Thomas said to me, you don`t want to show up for, and find out two minutes before that you have to have the biggest meeting of your life, right?\par \par \uc2 You don`t want to tell your kids in the parking lot, by the way, we`re going to get a vaccine. They want to think of this like game day, right?\par \par \uc2 So, ask them how they want to have the shot, for example. So, my nine-year- old wanted to watch, my five-year-old didn`t when she got their flu vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So, giving them those choices is important. Language is important. So, you actually heard in the piece, she specifically used the word poke and not shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Also saying things like we`re going to go get some magic medicine to keep us healthy. Not necessarily a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Then there`s things you can do around --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like using the word superpower too.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Yes, Superhero.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Superhero.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Around the time of the shot. So, you know, comfort positioning, there are ways to hold your child, touch, holding hands, I know my husband likes that when he gets a flu shot. And then things like location.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very sweet by the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have a new -- a doctor.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Any subsurgent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s a doctor, yes.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: You may not want to take your child to a mass vaccination site where there are crying kids. Maybe to set a specific appointment where they wait in the car, maybe you get a text message for a time to come in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: Distraction. So, we all know children who come home, and they have bruises and you say how did you get that? They say, I don`t know because it`s easy to ignore pain when you`re focused on something else.\par \par \uc2 So, bringing a screen, a toy is not a bad idea. And then my favorite is reward. So, my mom used to do this for us when we were kids.\par \par \uc2 I had no idea this was really a strategy, and now we do it for our kids because it takes their minds off of what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the reward will you do?\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: So, we say you can go to the toy store and pick out something very small, like under $5.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I believe the other term of that is bribery.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: It is below bribery but yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like to reward. You brought Fuzzy Bee not sure what he does but I like it already. How does this work?\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: So, it`s amazing that parents don`t know about this and unfortunately, it`s not routine in a lot of pediatrician`s offices.\par \par \uc2 But this is a Fuzzy bee, this is a vibrating tool that you can basically bring with you, it also has these wings that you can freeze.\par \par \uc2 And so, what happens is you basically turn it on, Tony, I don`t know if you want to feel but it`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It vibrates a little bit.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: It is gently vibrating. You put it over the site of wherever the poke is going to be for about 30 to 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 And then, when they`re ready to get the poke, you move it up above it, leave it on for that time when they`re getting the poke.\par \par \uc2 And basically, the vibration causes this traffic jam of pain signals so that you don`t sense the pain as much. And of course, the ice works as well.\par \par \uc2 So, you know really, really great tool. And also numbing cream, like this is something you can buy over the counter that a lot of parents don`t even know about. And put it on your child before you take them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so considerate, they said Tara brought this for you.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: I gave you the ladybug. And that`s my favorite.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s scared of needles, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and I`m keeping it too.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: I gave you bar.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate you, Dr. Narula --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, thank you, Tara.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- I got all those notes.\par \par \uc2 TARA NARULA: OK.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to pass it along to the Wife.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have Best Selling Author Michael Eric Dyson, he is in our progressive Green Room. Ahead, he`ll talk about his new book that examines how you think about race and the black experience. But first, it is 8:09 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do know that theme song, that`s actress Annaleigh Ashford, channels her Broadway route. She can sing in the new season of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  show it`s called, B Positive.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ashford will be here to tell us what to expect in the show`s second season and why her character is finding new ways to give back. You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, what if we said race isn`t real, that it`s just an idea built by people? That`s the subject of entertaining race performing blackness in America, the latest book by Best Selling Author Michael Eric Dyson. In it, Dyson digs into why we still enter the -- entertain the idea of race.\par \par \uc2 And even though he says it doesn`t exist, he also looks at how people including black sports icons, musicians, politicians, and everyday Americans all play a role in shaping that identity.\par \par \uc2 In his own words, Michael Eric Dyson shares how early black ideas of being black in America were born and what that means for today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON (Author): On slave ships and plantations, the largest forced migration in world history shaped the act of performance into a tool of survival and defines.\par \par \uc2 In freedom, entertainment offered black artists to measure of independence. Black people forged a complex racial identity and preserved ties to cultural heritage under relentless fire.\par \par \uc2 In America, whiteness is seen as inherently valid, while blacks must produce each generation, fresh evidence of our intelligence and humanity.\par \par \uc2 Contemporary black performance carries the weight of this history in both exceptional and everyday expressions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If you fall in love with Van Gogh, or the music of Coltrane, and realize that that was created by a human being just like you know, no more human, no less.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Michael Eric Dyson joins us right now. Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON (Author): Good morning. It`s good to be here. Good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you. Now, let`s jump right into it. In your title, it`s Entertaining Race, and you use this in two separate ways.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can you explain that?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: I`m hanging out these rappers too much in getting these double and triple on a time, you know. Blame it on Jay Z.\par \par \uc2 First of all, black people, as I`ve said, from the very beginning, have had to entertain for white America. That`s on the slave ship on the plantation. Sing us a good song. Sing us a good Negro spirits. And so, we`ve had to entertain.\par \par \uc2 Then, we`ve had to entertain the idea of race, whether we`re -- our kids are selling water or eliminate, and somebody stops him at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , we`re asked to perform and to entertain.\par \par \uc2 But we also have to be entertaining about how we speak about race. That can`t be the old tried and true ways, people come up with new ways and challenges and we have to be entertaining.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve been an entertaining race. Louis Armstrong, Maya Angelou, \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , Gayle King, these iconic figures, and now Nick Burleson joins in as well.\par \par \uc2 These iconic figures, who, have had to perform different roles, different aspects, LeBron James on the court, LeBron James off the court. He`s a performer in both.\par \par \uc2 He`s shooting a jump shot but he`s also making sure that we get the right shot at an opportunity in America.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we`ve got to talk about and that`s what I`ve talked about in this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But you open the story with a very -- you open the book with a very jarring story. But 1792 --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- you said a white man of privilege kills a 15-year-old girl because she refused to entertain. What is the point you`re making?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Yes, my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`d never heard of this story.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Yes, may I? Well, it`s a remarkable story. And this young enslaved girl, you know, had been infected with a venereal disease by the doctor who examined her --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: -- because she was his prey. He -- she gets on board, the captain, you know, dances the slaves, they often did that, to keep them in shape but also to titillate the wife figures on board.\par \par \uc2 And she was sick and couldn`t do it, she couldn`t perform. And he whipped her, hung her upside down, one leg, with a long cloth on her, and whips her to death.\par \par \uc2 And it came out in court. When they took him to court for killing her, and another young lady, that she refused to perform. That is a metaphor for black people. We have had to perform as if our lives depended on it.\par \par \uc2 So yes, we`re smiling, we`re grinning, we`re on stage but we also have an internal life where we nurture ideas and ambitions of democracy.\par \par \uc2 And everything else that America wants, we want as well. But we`ve been forced to, as Paul Laurence Dunbar said, wear the mask.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When you -- when you use this phrase, performing blackness, you`re not just talking about black celebrities, you`re talking about everyday -- black people in everyday life.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Oh, and especially every day. It`s extraordinary, with the, you know, Kobe`s and the Michael Jordan`s and the Beyonce`s and the Jay-Z`s but it`s every day, black people if they have work. If that`s your hair, is it real? When you go to work --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I touch it?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Can I touch it? Yes. Or I`m in a corporate America, you say you`re an ally, you put a black space up on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , but internally, you`re rejecting ideas of blackness.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got to perform deference, but acceptance, but also resistance in a certain way. So, yes, every day black --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You even said that George Floyd`s death. I mean, I had to read that twice. And George Floyd`s death, you said, was it performance?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Well, what I meant -- he didn`t mean so, of course, but it was part of the performance, the pageantry of the American race in America -- in this country.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s a guy laying on the ground, crying for his mother, asking for intervention saying I can`t breathe, he`s performing. Rituals of black desire to be heard to breathe freely and what we do we integrate that into a larger drama.\par \par \uc2 The reason George Floyd struck universally across this country and indeed across the world, is because people could identify with his performance of deference and vulnerability and not being angry, and yet asking for something basic and primary is breath.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, Michael, we have to go, so I need you to be quick. Now, performing blackness, it can be a great thing --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is your message to those individuals that might be in the sports space, or entertainment, that have the money, that have the power of influence, but they don`t use it to pass it forward or help lift somebody up into the position that they are in?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Yes. Remember Paul Robeson, remember Jackie Robinson --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: -- Remember Hank Aaron, these are figures, you know, Althea Gibson, who use their platform for a broader reality because before you were a ballplayer, you were black. Before you were an entertainer, you were black. That blackness buys you to get --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re still black. You`re still black.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re still black to this day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what do you want us to do with this information?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Oh, I want us to think about it, I want us to meditate on it, and I want us to act on it. Be more sensitive, be more understanding.\par \par \uc2 Understand the roles that people have to play, and the roles that white brothers and sisters and other non-black people play.\par \par \uc2 We`re all involved in it it`s Shakespearean. All the world`s a stage, we`re players on it --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And get interested in the -- you get interested in the history. I mean, Americans love their history --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- but not the controversial aspects of it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Selectively. We live in what Gore Vidal called the United States of Amnesia, so we like some stuff.\par \par \uc2 America`s obsessed with reproducing Confederate plays but when it comes to 1619, or blackness or ideas, not so much.\par \par \uc2 I`m confused, either you like history or you don`t, like all of the histories because we all are part of American history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Michael, you`re the best. I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON: Thank you, my brother.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much. Entertaining Race is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s 500 pages, a lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, we`ll be right --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it gets your money`s worth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s a good way to describe her, this girl is on fire. We want to let you know something is happening here tomorrow. We`re transforming the studio.\par \par \uc2 Transforming is a word for an uplifting performance by the great Jon Batiste. He`s got a new song to share. That`s tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But today, this girl is on fire, we are thrilled to have you. This is Tony Award-winning actress, what`s your name Tony Award-winning actress?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD (American Actress): Annaleigh Ashford.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`s here. She`s a star of \uc2 CBS\uc2  comedy, B Positive. 