r way. And that`s proof. I hope it`s inspiration for people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I believe it will be.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Believe in your own power, I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Go Jessica Simpson. Thank you. My Talk of the Table is a new feature for iPhones and Apple Watches plan for next year. It`s called crash detection. Now, according to the Wall Street Journal, if you`re in a car accident, your iPhone or watch will dial 911 automatically, an Apple spokeswoman, because they`re notoriously tight-lipped about stuff they`re working on, declined to comment on this, I bring it up because I think this is such a great idea. I only wear an Apple Watch. I used to have lots of watches lots of colors. But I was walking, I had the Apple Watch I fell on the street, which by the way, people just kept walking by. But I fell on the street. You know, I stepped off the curb the wrong way. And I felt like a cartoon character. It wasn`t like a little fall was a major fall like splat. And all of a sudden, my watch started beeping and saying, "We think you`ve fallen, are you OK?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which I know -- number one, Nate, I didn`t even know that it could do that. And number two, the first thing I did was, yes, I`m fine. When really, it`s a natural inclination because you`re embarrassed, you`re laying in the street people walking by my Angelou told me once that if you fall, just lay there and wait for somebody to come help you. I did not do that. But it was great to know that they had that kind of feature that and sure enough, I`d fallen -- bad fall. But that they can look out for you that way. So, I`m very excited that now they`re adding another more bells and whistles to something I already think was pretty great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope it`s delicate enough to know when the accident is serious because if you`ve got a junker car that`s worth less than the watches, sometimes you run 