\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re bonded.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You guys tackle serious topic how does that shape the humor of the show?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: You know, it`s a beautiful Chuck Lorre show and then grand Chuck Lorre style. He does this amazing thing where he has this fantastic comedy that`s funny at every turn, but has like so much heart. So, this season, my character has a windfall, which I don`t really know what that means. But I know it means you`re rich. You got real --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lots of money.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Lots of money. I don`t know where that saying comes from, but I`m going to look it up later on --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you, and you buy the assisted living facility. Now, assisted living facilities, you have some history as a performer in them, as a kid as I understand it, you used to belt out show tunes in the Denver area at one particular home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Yes, it`s kind of wild, but uh, and not a usual story for somebody`s childhood. But yes, I`ve always loved intergenerational living. And I was super close to my great, great grandma, my great, great grandpa`s. And I used to perform at various assisted living facilities and nursing homes around the Denver Metro area. I would show up in a tuxedo. I had a karaoke machine with a, with a mic and I would do a set for them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I don`t know a lot about hearing aids, but I do know that kids` voices can be kind of tough?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Turn them down --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turn them down, oh, that`s --\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: I`d open with tomorrow, and you`d see everybody go -- just turning down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But in addition to the shows taking a different shift, because you`ve -- the kidney transplant was successful, there`s a dance between you and Drew. I keep going back and forth on whether I want them to get together or not. But now you`re in the assisted living facility that you`re now running. Ben Vereen`s joining, Jane Seymour is joining. So, you guys are changing it up big time on the show.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Yes, we have Hector Elizondo, the Linda Lavin is back. And it`s really this beautiful story that we`re kind of conveying about how, when you are younger, taking care of older really the older or taking care of the younger, I keep saying they`re not my older, they`re my elders, you know. We got to just love them and learn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go. Can we just talk about you on the impeachment story?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, especially because she`s worried sparkles here? Paula Jones --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a knockout in that, Annaleigh, you were so good in that role.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do we have a picture of the transformation? Because you`re wearing prosthetics, right?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Yes, I have a prosthetic nose and many wigs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what did you want people to know about her? Did you view her differently? Once you played her? Did you have any assumptions about her one way or the other?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Yes, I think people are going to be really surprised about her origin when it comes to this narrative. And you know, this was a woman who was really at the core of it trying to please her husband, which I think is, you know, another comment on kind of the greater theme of, of the whole project, which is these women who were so buried by the patriarchy that they were enveloped in. So, it`s kind of a different view of her hurt her side of this story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Now, Gayle mentioned, you`re a Tony Award winner. Broadway is back here.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Amen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is your message to everybody that`s in the Broadway space?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Well, my message to everybody home is go to the theater.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Go to the theater, bring your vaccine card and your mask, go to the theater.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Go support!\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Amen. Go to the theater and for everybody in the Broadway community. I mean, brings tears to my eyes. I`m just so grateful that people have a job again, it`s a miracle and you just need --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Would you hit that stage again?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Hopefully --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes?\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: I need to stretch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no, Annaleigh --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just bring your karaoke machine.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: I will. Every tax --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got such a beautiful voice. I first saw her; she was on a Zoom a mutual friend of ours Amy wine bloom had a baby. It was a baby shower. She was singing on the Zoom to everybody in the height of the pandemic. I go, is it Emily Ashford she and Amy friends I went -- Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so, so, so, so nice. It`s so great to have you here. You look great. We love the dress.\par \par \uc2 ANNALEIGH ASHFORD: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much for coming. You can watch "Be Positive." It`s like be positive blood type on Thursday night at 9:30 Eastern, 8:30 Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Viacom CBS streaming service it`s called Paramount Plus.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right coming up. Our eye on Earth series goes to the Scottish Highlands to find out how peat bogs, that`s right peat bogs, a type of wetland are helping fight climate change. But first, it`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (Local Weather Forecast)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gorgeous picture there of the Scottish Highlands. We`ve been reporting on the high stakes \uc2 U.N\uc2 . climate conference in Scotland where world leaders are working to reduce greenhouse gases. But carbon dioxide, the main culprit, has also been seeping into the air from some surprising places like peat bogs, that`s a type of wetland where it has been contained harmlessly, we should add, for thousands of years. As part of our "Eye on Earth" series, Mark Phillips, takes us now into the Scottish Highlands to see another battleground in the fight to save the planet.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: There`s always a thrill on the way to work. It seems so.\par \par \uc2 Quite often, stories about climate change required trekking to some remote corner of the planet, usually to discover the latest worrying effect. But this climb up through the Highlands of Scotland with conservationists, Shayla Rao is in search of a solution, or at least part of one.\par \par \uc2 Much of this landscape is covered in soaking wet peat, a kind of swamp of vegetation that has decomposed over thousands of years, and which, given the weather up here never dries out. In terms of fighting climate change, that`s important.\par \par \uc2 We`re standing on a big carbon sink here. This is --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: Carbon that`s in the ground and not in the air.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: Peat, it turns out is much better at sequestering carbon than even trees. Peat bogs make up just three percent of the Earth`s landmass, but store 30 percent of all land-based carbon, twice as much as the world`s forests.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So, what happens is the vegetation is capturing carbon from the atmosphere. But the saturation of the water in the grains and how wet it is, doesn`t allow any carbon to be lost. It`s kind of sealed up. So over time, more and more carbon has sequestered and stored in this habitat.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: And it stays in it because it`s not exposed to the air, to oxygen and can`t --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, well it stays in it until what we see here is happening.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: What we see here is happening is vast areas of peat being lost some of it because increased rainfall is washing it away. Some because people have been harvesting peat from bogs for centuries to use as fuel. It`ll burn if it`s dried out. Some because it can be collected and used as fertilizer. And some because huge tracts of peat wetlands have been drained around the world to be used in agriculture.\par \par \uc2 Effectively, we`re standing in the middle of the problem here. This was once picked up to the top and now it`s been eroded into this gully is that the problem?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, exactly that`s it. So, historically, repeat with the fold this but that`s all been eroded away, then he`ll say to me lately as the value of peat as a carbon store has been recognized people are trying to save it. A major Scottish project is using mechanical diggers to recover exposed areas of bog with wet moss to keep the peat under it from drying out and blowing away.\par \par \uc2 The gullies where the peat is washing away or being made shallower and also lined with moss to slow the water flow down and hopefully stop the erosion.\par \par \uc2 Stewart Brooks of the Scottish National Trust will be trying to convince others at the Glasgow Climate Conference, just how important peat bog preservation is. We find peatlands, wetlands and swamps all over the planet about 180 countries. So, in America, in North America, and South America in, in the, in the tropics, in Southeast Asia, and in Northern Europe as well. And the funny thing is happening to land values up here in these highlands.\par \par \uc2 We used to think countryside like this was almost worthless, nothing but windswept bog land. Now though, companies are falling over themselves trying to buy up chunks of it because it`s so valuable.\par \par \uc2 Companies like this Scottish brewery which is offsetting its carbon footprint by buying up and restoring peat land for carbon credit. There`s a boom going on in soggy real estate.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN CARROLL (Brewdog Brewery): We made the decision to buy the land because it really was about asking to take in full control of the process.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: Can you sell non sustainable beer these days?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN CARROLL: You know, I think, I think going forward, you won`t be able to.\par \par \uc2 MARK PHILLIPS: Saving the planet or trying to one shovel full of moss at a time. For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Mark Phillips in the Cairngorm Mountains of Scotland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All you want to know about peat bogs? I never thought about peat bog before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I had not as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I learned a lot right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go. Thank you, Mark Phillips. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us, but you know you`ve talked about people that look like you. Who`s that Nate Burleson?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s my brother, Kevin Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who you said looks just like you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s a coach for the Minnesota Timberwolves, shout out to my big bro.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, two years difference, are you older or younger?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m younger. Yes, he`s two years older than me growing up that we were fake twins. We tried to pull it off in the classroom a couple of times, but it didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want a fake twin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love you bro.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t try it here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye Kevin. That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Local News and Weather Forecast)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211102ehb200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Hears Arguments On The New Abortion Law In Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Jan Crawford, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court hears arguments this morning on the new abortion law in Texas, one of the significant cases that could reshape reproductive rights around the country. Next month, the high court will consider Mississippi`s law banning most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is outside the Supreme Court with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Jan, a lot of people paying very close attention to this. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Really, really big cases. In the Texas case that`s being argued today, it came to the court at lightning speed. The justices agreeing to consider it on an expedited basis after the Biden administration and a group of abortion providers challenged the so-called Texas heartbeat act that bans basically all abortions in the state after about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 But the focus of the case today is on how this law is actually enforced. It gives private citizens the right to file civil lawsuits with penalties of at least $10,000 for anyone violating the law.\par \par \uc2 So the question is like who can sue to challenge the law, not the bigger issue of whether Roe versus Wade should be overturned. As you said, that is the issue. It will be before the court exactly one month from today.\par \par \uc2 The justices are going to hear arguments December 1st in a case that challenges Mississippi`s 15-week abortion ban. That`s the case that`s the direct challenge to Roe v. Wade. You`re seeing these laws passing states across the country.\par \par \uc2 It is all part of a deliberate effort to get the issue of abortion before this newly conservative Supreme Court. Six conservative justices now, three just recently appointed by former president Trump. If the court were to overturn Roe v. Wade and say states can decide the issue of abortion, what would happen is you`re going to see these conservative-leaning states like Alabama with more restrictive laws and bans than liberal states like New York -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Once again, eyes on the Supreme Court when it comes to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to possible trouble for Democrats in a key governor`s race in Virginia. New polling averages show that Republican Glenn Youngkin is just slightly ahead of Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 . The two candidates spent the weekend trying to drum up last-minute support.\par \par \uc2 One key issue for Youngkin, opposition to the teaching of critical race theory in schools. The curriculum is not taught in video schools, and McAuliffe has called the tactic a racist dog whistle. If he wins, Youngkin would be the first Republican elected since 2009. Polls close tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning overseas now. A man with a knife on a Japanese commuter plane -- commuter train, rather, sent fellow passengers literally running for their lives yesterday. Police say the man dressed like Batman`s Joker character stabbed a man in the chest and then started a fire on the train heading to Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Some of the other people on board jumped out of the windows to escape. Tokyo`s fire department says 17 people were hurt here. The 24-year-old suspect is now in police custody and may be charged with attempted murder. Police say he told them he just wanted to kill people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Frightening scene out of Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211103ehb10005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Hears Arguments In The Texas Restrictive Abortion Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And back here at home, the new abortion law in Texas, the strictest in the nation, was at the center of arguments today before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, it`s unclear whether the court will issue an order to block the law which has been in effect for two months now.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS`s Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Outside the Supreme Court, protesters made clear the fight - -\par \par \uc2 MAN: Abortion the healthcare!\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: -- over the Texas law that bans abortions after about six weeks. A law that justices called unprecedented and specifically designed to get around Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE ELENA KAGAN (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ): The actual provisions in this law have prevented every woman in Texas from exercising a constitutional right as declared by this court.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: After three hours of arguments, it was evident, liberal and conservative justices didn`t like what Texas was up to.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ): There`s a loophole that`s been exploited here or used here.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The loophole is in how Texas wrote the law to keep it out of federal courts which would block it. Instead of state officials enforcing the law, at least that to private citizens, who can go to state court and sue anyone who helps perform an abortion, with fines starting at $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Bhavik Kumar is a doctor of Planned Parenthood in Houston.\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (Houston Planned Parenthood Center for Choice): I would be surprised if anyone has seen more than 10 to 20 percent to have the patient they usually see. That`s been my experience. It has been a very dramatic reduction.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But the case goes well beyond abortion rights. The justices were concerned the Texas law could be a model for statements to restrict other constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 : And it could be free speech rights, could be free exercise of religion rights, it could be Second Amendment rights, if this position is accepted here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, the court is not reconsidering Roe v. Wade in this case, but one month from today, it will take up a different case that`s asking the justices to overturn roe and allow Mississippi`s 15-week ban on abortion. It`s not clear if we will have a decision in the Texas case by then -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford at the Supreme Court, thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211102ehb100003\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Former Acting Attorney General Sally Yates Talks About Breast Cancer Diagnosis\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Every two minutes, a woman in the United States is diagnosed with breast cancer. And last year, one of those women was Sally Yates. Yates spent decades at the Justice Department and was unceremoniously fired by a newly-inaugurated President Trump. Well, tonight, Yates is sharing the story of her breast cancer battle for the very first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: It was invasive papillary cancer.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Sally Yates was suddenly grappling with a cancer diagnosis during a busy summer, testifying before Congress and speaking at the Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: This was one of those times where the personal and the professional collided. And so, it was, I think, just about five days before I was to testify in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  about the Russia investigation that I got the final diagnosis that it was invasive cancer.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And that`s some pretty important testimony that you were about to give.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: It was. I thought about trying to postpone, but it would have been a big thing--\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: --if I had postponed it. And there also was this false narrative out there called Obamagate that President Obama and then-Vice President Biden had directed the Russia investigation and directed us to investigate Mike Flynn, and they specifically pointed to a meeting in the Oval Office where people were alleging this had happened. Well, I had been in that meeting, and I knew that didn`t happen. So I did, also, want to be able to get the truth out there.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So, here you are, just diagnosed with breast cancer and you feel like you have to go and tell not only the Congress but the world the truth.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: Right. Not ideal timing.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How did you manage it all?\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: It was just a few days since I`d gotten the diagnosis, and I would find, as I`m trying to get ready for this testimony, I`d find myself Googling the survival rates for papillary cancer or different treatments. You know, you couldn`t-- I couldn`t help but be very distracted by that.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The type of breast cancer Yates had is rare and she needed a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: So, I testified in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 ; a couple of weeks later, spoke at the Democratic National Convention; and then the day after that went in for pre-op and had surgery the following day.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yates is now in private practice, and the National Women`s Soccer League has just hired her to investigate allegations of sexual abuse.\par \par \uc2 So, your message to women?\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: Get a mammogram. Get the other diagnostic tests that so many people have had to be putting off during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The statistics about the pandemic and cancer screening are really alarming. Nearly ten thousand people over the next decade will die from breast cancer and colorectal cancer because of pandemic-related delays.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: It may very well be a looming health crisis that we haven`t even really fully begun to comprehend yet. People can try to do something about it by getting in now. You may have missed those tests during the pandemic time, but it`s not too late to go in for those tests now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very important reminder. And Yates says she`s cancer free now and she writes about her journey in an exclusive essay on cbsnews.com.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. There is much more news ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS, including \uc2 Facebook\uc2  under fire and it`s changing its name.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The CDC is warning that several children have gotten sick from a product used to decorate birthday cakes. Cakes in two states were decorated with glittery dust containing lead and copper. The CDC urges consumers to check labels and make sure the glitter is marked edible.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tonight, the company formerly known as \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is now called Meta, that`s the new corporate name for the parent company overseeing \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and Messenger. The change comes amid all those allegations that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has knowingly caused social harm on its platform.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, how football is helping a town rise from the ashes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211029ehas000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Organizers Of A Right-Wing Protest In Charlottesville, Virginia, Will Go On Trial, More Than Four Years After That Demonstration Turned Into A Deadly Riot\b0\par\par\uc2 Michelle Miller, Jeff Pegues \par \uc2 1,457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: The organizers of a right-wing protest in Charlottesville, Virginia, will go on trial this morning, more than four years after that demonstration turned into a deadly riot. Ten plaintiffs are suing about two dozen organizers of the Unite the Right Rally, claiming they violated their civil rights and are responsible for their physical and emotional injuries. Jeff Pegues is at the federal courthouse in Charlottesville. Jeff, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES (CBS News Chief Justice and Homeland Security Correspondent): Good morning. We are expecting riveting opening statements today, with the plaintiffs arguing and trying to lay out a case that demonstrates that this was a conspiracy that led to racially-motivated violence. While on the other side, the defendants will argue that this was not a conspiracy and that they deserve to be cleared of breaking any laws.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: On Wednesday, jurors in the Charlottesville riot civil trial were sworn in. The defendants--members of white supremacist neo-Nazi groups--\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison; VICE News/HBO)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: --who in 2017 marched through the streets carrying torches and violently clashing with counter-protesters. One neo-Nazi plowed a car into a crowd, killing Heather Heyer. Liz Sines narrowly escaped injury during the clash and is the lead plaintiff in today`s case.\par \par \uc2 LIZ SINES (Lead Plaintiff): I don`t think I will ever not be scared.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The plaintiffs allege social media posts and text messages proved the defendants` intent to engage in violence leading up to the rally. In two social media posts, "I`m ready to crack skulls," "fight until the last drop."\par \par \uc2 JASON KESSLER: I hope they will support my right to free speech.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The defendants have argued that there was no conspiracy leading up to the rally and that they had a First Amendment right to protest.\par \par \uc2 AMY SPITALNICK (Integrity First For America Executive Director): We know that what happened four years ago was not an isolated incident, but really has previewed so much of the extremism that`s followed.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Amy Spitalnick`s organization Integrity First for America is providing support to Sines and other victims in the case.\par \par \uc2 AMY SPITALNICK: There was such a lack of accountability for the violent extremism we saw on display here. And that`s what this case is about-- making clear that if you are part of this violent hate, if you conspire to bring violence to attack people based on their race, their religion, their willingness to defend the rights of others, you will face consequences.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: And with cases like these, security is always a concern, and it will be a concern today and going forward in this case. Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: All right. thank you, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 And I just have to say, it`s, like, been four years, hard to believe on that. But this was really the first incident of its kind we`d seen of this kind of right-wing extremism.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. It raises a fascinating legal question about whether if you bring these people together, these powers, these forces, and they`re unleashed in a deadly direction, are you, the organizers, responsible?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, and there`s a clear distinction between a nonviolent protest and what we saw four years ago. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right. Still ahead, a conversation with a hockey player, Kyle Beach, who alleges he was sexually assaulted by a coach when he played in the \uc2 Chicago Blackhawks\uc2  organization. And he says the team failed to react the right way. This happened eleven years ago. And Kyle Beach went by the name John Doe when he filed this lawsuit in May. He tells us he finally comes forward and why he did.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s seven fifteen, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Coming up, Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a Republican governor, is one hundred percent behind COVID vaccines. We`ll talk about his state`s plan to supply them to younger kids.\par \par \uc2 Plus, meet a military wife with a new mission, helping hundreds of refugees from Afghanistan settle in America.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In an exclusive CBS News interview, former Acting Attorney General \uc2 Sally Yates\uc2 , who was fired, you`ll recall, in a confrontation with then-President Trump over travel bans. She`s revealing now her private battle with breast cancer. As we mark breast cancer awareness month, we`re pleased to say that Yates is now cancer free, very pleased indeed. She says she continued to do critical work during her health crisis Yates was diagnosed, believe or not, in 2020 around the same time she testified to Congress about the Justice Department`s investigation of the 2016 Trump campaign and Russian interference in the election. She also spoke at the Democratic National Convention while fighting her uphill battle behind closed doors trying to restore her health. Here`s part now of her conversation with CBS EVENING NEWS Anchor and Managing Editor Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL (CBS EVENING NEWS WITH NORAH O`DONNELL): I think about the timing of this, last summer. You were testifying before Congress and speaking at the Democratic National Convention, what was it like going through such a private battle while being a public person?\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES (CBS EVENING NEWS WITH NORAH O`DONNELL): Well, you know, that was-- that was odd. You know, I tend to compartmentalize my life in some ways. And professionally, you know, much of my professional life has been somewhat in the public eye. I`m a pretty private person, but this was one of those times where the personal and the professional collided. And so it was, I think, just about five days before I was to testify in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  about the Russia investigations that I got the final diagnosis that it was invasive cancer.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And that`s some pretty important testimony that you were about to give.\par \par \uc2 SALLY YATES: It was.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yates has a lot to say about that Senate hearing. And you can see more of Norah`s interview tonight on the CBS EVENING NEWS and you can go to cbsnews.com right now to read an exclusive essay that Yates wrote about her experience fighting breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s another case where you never know the personal battle a person is fighting--\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --as they are in public.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: We knew she was strong to begin with. But now, it just exposes a whole different side to this woman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It is mind blowing to me that in this day and age with social media and everything else that people can keep these types of things private.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That says something.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m actually heartened by that fact.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Me too. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`ve always said that women should wear capes because they can bear so much more than they let us know that they`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Aww.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, coming up, Ben Tracy hits the ice to show us how the country`s newest sports venue is living up to its unusual name.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Here in the home of the National Hockey League`s newest team, some of this ice is actually made with rainwater. It`s all part of their pledge to be the world`s most sustainable sports venue. We`ll take you inside the Climate Pledge Arena coming up on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about those skating skills?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ben Tracy can skate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And a reminder, you can always catch the morning news by subscribing to our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than twenty minutes.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (7:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211029ehas0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Rare Gene Mutation Increases Breast Cancer Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 702 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Tonight, there`s a new advancement in the fight against breats-- against breast cancer. The \uc2 Cleveland Clinic\uc2  is launching trials of a vaccine aimed at preventing one of the most aggressive forms of the disease. And while that is encouraging, we`re also learning about a lesser-known gene mutation that is causing alarm. Here is CBS` Nikki Battiste.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT (Chief of Breast Surgery, Mount Sinai Health System): So, go ahead and lie back.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Doctor Mathilde Crutchfield isn`t used to being the patient, but recent genetic testing revealed she has a rare mutation in the PALB2 gene, significantly increasing her risk for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. MATHILDE CRUTCHFIELD: When it came back positive, I was very surprised.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Now the forty-eight-year-old mother of three faces an excruciating decision; biannual mammograms and MRIs, or a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 DR. MATHILDE CRUTCHFIELD: You feel a little bit like a ticking time bomb because you know you have the gene.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Typically PALB2 genes help repair DNA damage in cells and stop tumor growth, but a mutation can result in cells that become abnormal, further increasing every woman`s nearly thirteen percent average lifetime risk of getting breast cancer. For carriers of the PALB2 mutation, with no family history, the risk goes up to thirty-three percent, and even higher with a family history to fifty-eight percent.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: BRCA1--\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Doctor Elisa Port is Crutchfield`s physician at the Dubin Breast Center.\par \par \uc2 Some people who consider genetic testing just would rather not know.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISA PORT: I would say that knowledge is power, and I tell all of my mutation carriers, the ball is in their court.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Doctor Crutchfield had genetic testing because of her family history of cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. MATHILDE CRUTCHFIELD: My maternal grandmother and her sister, and they both had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She says they have inspired her to move forward with a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 DR. MATHILDE CRUTCHFIELD: I wish they could have had the same information and the same choices that I have.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Nikki Battiste, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Having that new information and choice is so incredibly important. It will save lives.\par \par \uc2 All right. There is still much more news ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS, including new CDC guidance on why some may need a fourth--yes, fourth--COVID shot.\par \par \uc2 And the new evidence that Americans have increased some bad habits during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s some encouraging news tonight in the fight against COVID. New cases have dropped by nearly sixty percent since the delta variant surge in September. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is expected to green light Pfizer`s mini-dose for kids five to eleven years old by the end of this week. And those shots could start being given out as early as next Wednesday. And while many Americans are lining up for their third COVID shot, the CDC has updated its guidance for people with compromised immune systems, saying they can now get a fourth dose of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Moderna.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tonight, there`s new evidence that Americans have been smoking and drinking more during the pandemic. Researchers in Michigan say the number of people seeking liver transplants for alcoholic hepatitis shot up by more than fifty percent as sales of alcohol surged. And for the first time in twenty years, cigarette sales increased last year as smokers stocked up, buying nearly a billion more cigarettes than in 2019.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up next, it took a village to make sure a little girl wouldn`t miss Halloween.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211028ehar000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court To Hear Texas Abortion Law Challenges\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the Supreme Court has agreed to fast-track challenges to the strict new abortion law in Texas. The justices will hear arguments on November 1st. But for now, that Texas law remains in effect. CBS` Ed O`Keefe joins us now live from the Supreme Court. So, Ed, talk about the significance of today`s action.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (CBS News Senior White House and Political Correspondent): Yeah, Norah, this is now the second time the Supreme Court has refused to block the controversial law despite the most recent request by the Biden administration, which considers the law unconstitutional. It remains in effect across Texas and essentially bans abortion procedures after the sixth week of a pregnancy, which critics point out is often before many women realize they`re pregnant. It`s left millions of Texas women without access to abortion services, a point noted by Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  today in her partial dissent. She wrote the law should be blocked immediately because, quote, ".every day the court fails to grant relief is devastating, both for individual women and for our constitutional system as a whole.." This decision now means there will be two major abortion rights cases before the justices in the span of a month. The bigger case, set for December, involves a Mississippi law that could lead to overturning Roe versus Wade. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yeah, many people closely watching the Supreme Court. Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, tens of millions of people in the West are bracing for another wave of severe storms. Parts of the Bay Area got eight inches of rain in recent days, and up to four more inches are expected Sunday into Tuesday. Wildfire burn areas could see mudslides, huge waves will pound the coast of the Pacific Northwest, and the Sierra could get up to seven feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211023eham0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Search For Gunman Who Ambushed Texas Deputies Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We want to turn now to the intense manhunt for the gunman who opened fire on three deputies in Texas. One who had recently became a father was killed. CBS` Janet Shamlian is in Houston tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (CBS News Correspondent): Tonight, law enforcement is asking the public for help in identifying the gunman they say ambushed three county sheriff`s deputies, killing one and injuring the others.\par \par \uc2 MARK HERMAN (Harris County, Texas, Precinct 4 Constable): All three, law enforcement officers were ambushed by this individual. They were-- they were just totally ambushed.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The officers were working as side security job outside a sports bar. Two were shot from behind with an assault type rifle as they tried to detain someone about 2:00 AM Saturday. The third was fired on when he came running to help.\par \par \uc2 MARK HERMAN: We`re going to make it right. And I hope that swift justice comes quickly for this individual.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Deputy Kareem Atkins, a father of two died from his injuries. The thirty-year-old just returned from paternity leave. Twenty- year-old Juqaim Barthen was shot in the foot. And twenty-eight-year-old Darryl Garrett is in critical condition. Lajah Richardson is his fiancee.\par \par \uc2 They weren`t just co-workers?\par \par \uc2 LAJAH RICHARDSON: Right. They were brothers. They did everything together.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Fellow officers are devastated by the news. Lajah Richardson says she hasn`t had the heart to tell her fiance about his co- worker yet, calling Atkins an officer respected by all.\par \par \uc2 LAJAH RICHARDSON: He`s a very wonderful person. Very genuine. Whatever you need, he`s going to come through for you and that`s why everyone is heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: As for Deputy Garrett, he has gone into yet another surgery here at the hospital tonight. He remains on a ventilator. Nationwide, more than a hundred thirty law enforcement officers have died in the line of duty. Just this year. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And there is still much more news ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS. The Biden administration says the Supreme Court and ask them to block that strict Texas abortion law.\par \par \uc2 Plus, an update on the health of former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  now home from the hospital.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Today, the Biden administration asked the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  for an emergency halt to the new Texas abortion restrictions. That law effectively bans abortions after six weeks and allows private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman get an abortion. The Biden administration calls it, quote, "clearly unconstitutional."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  is back in New York after being released from a California hospital following treatment for an infection. The forty-second President walked slowly arm in arm with wife Hillary and shaking hands with doctors and nurses.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tony Bennett has sung his way into the Guinness Book of World Records with the release of his new album with Lady Gaga, Love for Sale. The ninety-five-year-old legend is now the oldest person to release an album of new songs. Lady Gaga, six decades younger, says she sees a young boy every time she sings with him, well, also appreciating the wisdom of his years. That`s nice to see.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up next, Colin Powell`s call to service, inspiring future generations.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211019ehai000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Rise In Stage Four Breast Cancer In Women In Their Twenties And Their Thirties\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Dr. Tara Narula, Nate Burleson, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,899 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And this morning, we`re taking a look into a very concerning trend involving the number of young women being diagnosed with stage four breast cancer. Women under the age of forty account for six percent of total stage four cases. One study shows more than four percent increase per year in stage four breast cancer cases in women in their twenties and their thirties, from 2016 to 2015. This is concerning. The researchers don`t really know why this is happening. CBS News senior medical correspondent Doctor Tara Narula spoke with thirty-year-old Paige Stables. She was diagnosed with stage four breast cancer back in April. Now, Stables is a beauty editor for Allure magazine and she wrote about living with the diagnosis in this month`s issue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAIGE STABLES (Diagnosed with Stage Four Breast Cancer): The thought of stage four breast cancer in your twenties or thirties seems unimaginable until it happens. Truly, in a moment my worst nightmare became my reality.\par \par \uc2 I was twenty-six years old. I was doing a self-exam, and I found a lump in my right breast. And within a month, I underwent a bilateral mastectomy. This past March, I celebrated being three years cancer free, and then around that same time I started to feel a pain in my hip.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (CBS News Senior Medical Correspondent): When they told you had it metastasized to your hip, what became your concerns at that point for the future?\par \par \uc2 PAIGE STABLES: Your whole world just stops, and it`s a completely different conversation when you find out you have breast cancer and that-- that alone is life-changing and challenging. But when you find out that it`s stage four, it just-- it is so different. I would be lying if I said it`s easy. I`ve always said that positivity is my shield.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: In the article that you wrote, you use the word, "thrivers." Why thrivers?\par \par \uc2 PAIGE STABLES: We are thrivers because we are going to be living with this the rest of our life. And an oncologist told me that it`s almost like a chapter book, and hopefully these are going to be really, really long chapters in terms of right now I`m doing a certain treatment and hopefully that chapter will continue for a really long time. And if we need to turn the page and do another treatment, then we can do that. But I am not looking ahead to that ending anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Tell me a little bit about the advice that your doctor gave you.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE STABLES: She said two simple words, "celebrate life." And since that moment, that`s what I`ve done. It`s big celebrations and small celebrations, and just really living my life to the fullest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Paige`s cancer is stage four. She is currently undergoing hormonal therapies to manage the cancer. A monthly injection and a daily pill. She`s required to get a PET scan every three months to make sure the cancer isn`t spreading. Looking forward, Paige is planning her wedded-- her wedding in Italy for next fall.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, does she have the right attitude. I love what she said to her that positivity is her shield.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s something we can all learn something from listening to her words. But we were saying in the lead, doctors aren`t really quite sure why this is happening, twenties, thirties, is so young to get this diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The experts don`t know why?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Well, there`s no clear understanding at this point, but it is a trend both here and abroad. And there are theories about why we might be seeing this, for example, exposures in your lifestyle. So, inflammation, stress, your diet, toxins. Also, we know that there`s not great screening for women under forty. So we definitely needed more research into what we could do to capture this smaller, you know, less often cases of women who are young because there`s less screening. We tend to find these cancers at later stages. Also, the cancers that are occurring in these younger women tend to be more aggressive. And so it speaks to the fact that the biology of this type of cancer may be different. But the bottom line is it`s so tricky and challenging. This is a population of women who are dealing with fertility issues if they are having treatment. In the beginnings of their, you know, career, they have families. So, there`s a unique set of challenges around this population.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She made a point of talking about the importance of she caught it during a self-breast exam which illustrates again how important it is for us to do that. Are we supposed to start at a certain age?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: So interestingly, self-breast exams and clinical breast exams are actually not officially recommended because the research has not backed those up as being beneficial. That being said, most experts say it`s still a good idea to know how your breasts normally look and feel. And in fact, Paige caught her own cancer a week after she had seen her doctor who had done an exam. So, you do need to be your own health advocate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: And if you feel something or see something, speak up to your doctor about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, when it comes to treatment, chemotherapy used to be the standard. But people are moving away from that.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are the alternatives?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Isn`t that great?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: So chemotherapy was really kind of the only go-to for so many years. And about ten to fifteen years ago, we started to shift toward these more targeted therapies like hormonal therapies, biologics, immunologics, and that`s based on actually genetically looking at the tumor itself and figuring out what the tumor will best be responsive to. The value in that is that you`re sparing women those side effects of the chemo, the hair loss--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Losing their hair. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: --the nausea, the fatigue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: And also nerve damage and, in my world, cardiotoxicity. We talked about this last year. But a lot of women survive their breast cancer and go on to die from cardiovascular disease because of the chemo that they might have received.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, what increases the risk of breast cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: So there are a lot of potential factors that we talk a lot about genetics. So BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations, family history, if you yourself have a personal history of previously have breast cancer, also some benign breast conditions. And then other things like if you had early onset of your periods, late menopause, if you never had children, if you never breast-fed. Some exposures like alcohol and tobacco. So, a lot of different factors. Good to talk to your doctor about whether you might be at increased risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about signs and symptoms? Because I noticed she said she felt something and then she talked about a pain in her hip.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Right. So the most common sign and symptom is going to be a new lump or mass. But certainly if you notice a change in the shape of your breasts, swelling of the breasts, also any change in the texture of the skin. So redness or dimpling or any dry, flaky skin that`s kind of continuing on. Nipple inversion or discharge, that`s not breast milk. And then if you notice a swollen node under your arm or around your collar bone, those are things you should be pointing out to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Tara.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Valuable information.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Yeah. I mean, she`s an amazing woman. And she hopes that really her story and those stories of the women she profiled will be inspirational to others because of their strength to show that there`s hope even though they don`t have a cure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I like her philosophy. Celebrate life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As we continue living, I love that she`s making-- going forward with wedding plans.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. I also like her philosophy getting married in Italy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nice plans to do it, I think, I hear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: She`s made a point of saying breast cancer does not define me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Cheering you on, Paige Stables. Cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One grandpa is getting attention for his fantastic reaction to a birthday surprise. His family who knows him well gave him Dua Lipa tickets for his eightieth birthday. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WALDORF (ph; internet video): Dua Lipa concert? Are you (EXPLETIVE DELETED) me? (EXPLETIVE DELETED) me, really? Is this a real thing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, it is real, mister. His granddaughter Kadie told us her grandfather, whose real name is Richard Waldorf, is a huge Dua Lipa fan. And as you can see--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --he loves dancing to her music and has been a fan ever since hearing her hit song "New Rules," "New Rules," indeed. The video of his reaction got so much attention that Dua Lipa herself saw it, shared it on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and said, "This melts my heart."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. Dua Lipa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you-- don`t you--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do the right thing. Get some backstage--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: --passes for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She will definitely do that. But don`t you want to go to the concert with him?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s going to be great fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, that`s a good time right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a good time. And I say Dua Lipa right. We have some history, Dua Lipa and I. I don`t say-- I`ve screwed the name up, but I`ve got it now. Gayle`s mortified.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re past that\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re past that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow. He`s much better.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025ehad0006b\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS for October 7, 2021, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jan Crawford, Ed  O`Keefe, Mireya Villarreal, Omar Villafranca, Debora Patta, Jill  Schlesinger, Catherine Herridge, Nikki Battiste, Vladimir Duthiers, Anthony  Mason; Carter Evans \par \uc2 12,738 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 > GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It`s Thursday, October 7th. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE: Yes, he is. We`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener". It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: It`s about paying for what we owe and preventing a catastrophic event occurring in our economy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a possible short-term deal on Capitol Hill to raise the debt ceiling. Why critics say that`s just not enough.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): We`re making good progress. We`re not there yet, but I hope we can come to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A federal judge has ordered Texas to suspend its new law banning most abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: An 18-year-old student is under arrest after a shooting during a fight a Texas high school that injured four people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shocking allegations of fraud at the ATF. A CBS News investigation spurs Congress to act.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Will you ask to find out if it`s happening at other agencies?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-Iowa): Well, my suspicion is it probably is.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Investigators say they have identified the zodiac killer who murdered at least five people in the `60s.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Dodgers advance to the National League Divisional series.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grammy winner Natalie Hemby writes hits for others. She tells Anthony about taking center stage herself.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY, SONGWRITER: It felt like the Beatles or something. We weren`t the Beatles, but I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: We felt like the Beatles.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  dismisses an emergency solution to the country`s debt ceiling issue, a trillion dollar platinum coin.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT (Late Night TV Host): I think there`s something so fitting about Biden fixing this but just pulling a coin out of thin air. Hey, America, come here, kiddo. Come see your Uncle Joe. What you got there behind your ear? Oh, look, it`s a trillion dollars. There you go. Buy yourself something nice.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 If you grew up in my house, it would be buy yourself something nice and put the rest of it in savings. That`s no fun. I have a greater appreciation for savings account.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS News.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this. We`ve got a possible deal on the debt limit that could avoid an economic crisis. We`ll get to that in just a second, but we begin with breaking news overnight from Texas on the state`s controversial abortion law.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge is putting the measure on hold, calling it unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is following that story.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. So, the reaction among Biden administration officials, abortion rights advocates was relief. The judge`s decision stems from a Justice Department lawsuit against Texas last month, just days after S.B. 8 went into effect. The law outlaws abortions once embryonic activity is detected, which is usually around six weeks, and that could be before some women even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 And it allows private citizens to enforce it by bringing civil lawsuit against anyone who helps a woman get an abortion after that with fines starting at $10,000. But in a strongly worded 113-page ruling, Judge Robert Pitman said the law was at blatantly unconstitutional. From the moment S.B. 8 went to effect, women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their lives. And this court will not sanction one more day of this offensive deprivation of such an important right.\par \par \uc2 Now, in a statement, the Biden administration cautioned that the fight has only just begun. Texas, of course, will appear and the Supreme Court is taking up that Mississippi abortion ban in December that is likely going to determine not only the fate of this law in Texas, but other states as well -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will keep an eye on that. Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to that potential deal to raise America`s debt ceiling for now. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is considering an offer from Minority Leader Mitch McConnell. It could prevent economic chaos for now, but set up another fight in December.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m sure you`ve done it, I`ve done it eve once in a while. You hit the snooze button before you get out of bed. Well, look, that`s what Congress is doing right now, delaying the inevitable fight over the debt limit by a few extra weeks at this point.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , as you said, signaled they`re on the verge of a deal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 : We are making good progress. We`re not there yet, but I hope we can come to an agreement tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Overnight amid an another standoff over the nation`s debt, Democrats led signaled they`re likely to pt accept Republican Leader Mitch McConnell`s concession, a plan to extend the nation`s borrowing limit into early December. Some Democrats were relieved.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-Connecticut): Well, it is a short-term solution, and hopefully Republicans will be more responsible in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While others didn`t buy it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MAZIE HIRONO (D-Hawaii): What`s my reaction to his so-called offer? Just what I said, it`s B.S. He should stop playing games, get out of the way so that the 50 Democrats can avoid what would be an economic catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The debt limit has been raised about 80 times since the 1960s in order to pay off the nation`s bills. The Treasury Department say it needs to happen again by October 18th, and failure to do so could put the nation`s credit rating at risk, send stocks tumbling and delay social security payments.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, President Biden met with top bank CEOs who agreed that partisan brinksmanship needs to stop.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: The United States pays its bills. That`s who we are. This shouldn`t be partisan.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2  (JPMorgan CEO): We should never even get this close. There are huge economic costs already being borne by companies and lawyers trying to figure out what this means if something like this ever happens.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So, White House aides warn that McConnell`s offer only means that they`re going to have to do a longer extension of the debt closer to the end of the year and they may have to use those tricky Senate procedural tactics to get it done.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, all of this happening while here at the White House, they`re looking at new poll numbers that show the president`s approval rating sitting at 38 percent, the lowest yet of his presidency -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Ed. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Texas where an 18-year-old student accused of a shooting at a high school there is in custody this morning. Four people were hurt in the incident. However, the suspect`s family says he was only trying to protect himself from bullies.\par \par \uc2 Mireya Villarreal is at the scene in Arlington. That is just outside of Dallas, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Mireya, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Hi. Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Right now, school is cancelled here at Timberview, although there are a lot of cars lingering in the parking lot, left behind by students and staff during the chaos. This all started when a fight broke out on the second floor of the school you see behind me, when one of the students allegedly pulled a gun, he started to shoot and everything went on lockdown.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: When reports came in about an active shooter inside Timberview High School, heavily armed police converged on the school searching the campus and surrounding areas for signs of shooting suspect Timothy Simpkins.\par \par \uc2 Frantic parents gathered as close to the school as possible, desperate for any information on their children.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We`re scared. We`re scared. We just want to go in and get our kids.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: As the chaos unfolded outside, inside the school, students were sharing this video. Police believe it shows the brawl that provoked the shooting.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KOLBYE (Arlington, TX Assistant Police Chief): This is not a random act of violence. This is a student, we believe right now preliminary, it was a student that got into a fight and drew a weapon.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Dale Topham, a teacher at a school and a father, told us he and the students barricaded the door with these desks during the shooting.\par \par \uc2 DALE TOPHAM (Professor): My thought was I my daughter were in a situation like this, I would want someone to keep her safe.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: It`s an all-too-familiar scene playing out in schools across the country. Federal data shows school shootings have more than tripled from 2000 to 2019.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE WADE (Timberview HS Student`s Mother): You never know when it`s going to be your last day, so I`m very thankful to have my daughter with me.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Relieved parents gathered at a nearby school where some 1,700 students were being bussed Wednesday morning after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Luckily I finally got a text saying, mommy, I`m okay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: A few hours after the shooting, Simpkins turned himself in with his attorney at the police station. He is now facing three charges of aggravated assault with a deadly weapon and he could face prison time. As for the victims right now, we do know one is still critical condition but they are all expected to be okay -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good news to hear that everybody is going to survive. So upsetting for the parents not knowing. That`s an awful feeling.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mireya.\par \par \uc2 For the second straight night, dozens of water rescues are needed in the state of Alabama because of torrential downpours. Here`s a look at what they`re dealing with down there. Police and fire officials had to take a boat out to rescue a woman who was stranded on the roof of her vehicle after it was submerged in the floodwater. This was in Hoover, Alabama, just outside Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 And here you can see a man trying to literally brush water out of his house. Parts of the area saw as much as 8 inches of rain over the last two days. That`s a lot of water, and more than a foot of rain over the last week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now to the pandemic and two dramatically different public responses. First, Los Angeles, which just approved one of the nation`s strictest vaccine mandates. It requires people 12 and over to prove they`ve been fully vaccinated if they want to enter indoor public spaces, shopping malls, restaurants, bars, gyms, nail salons, and sports arenas like the Staples Center. The mandate takes effect November 4th, four weeks from today and businesses that do not comply can face fines as much as $5,000. It`s an ordinance similar to others we`ve seen in San Francisco and New York City.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In Florida where the governor has blocked mask mandates, the Department of Education meets today to consider possible punishment for districts that enforce them. The latest data from the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  show that children make up more than a quarter of new COVID cases nationwide. Less than half of eligible children are fully vaccinated. Now, some state officials say that schools that require masks are breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca spoke with a family that believes this law is putting people`s lives at risk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REEFY KINDER (4th Grader): Every night, I wish upon a starfish that I can go back in person.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Nine-year-old Reefy Kinder has been learning virtually for almost two years.\par \par \uc2 REEFY KINDER: I miss socializing with my friends.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Being 9 years old and hanging out with your buddies?\par \par \uc2 REEFY KINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Reefy had 30 surgeries over six years leaving her immunocompromised. Her parents and doctor decided not to send her to school in person this year because there was no mask mandate in their county.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE KINDER (Reefy`s Mother): It was too dangerous for the other kids to go in without mask, let alone a child who`s immunocompromised.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The Kinders and 10 other families are part of a federal lawsuit against Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the State Department of Education and several school districts.\par \par \uc2 It alleges an executive order banning mask mandates violates the Americans with Disabilities Act.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE KINDER: School should be safe for all of the population, not just the people that believe in the governor at the time.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: A new state rule also lets families decide whether or not to send their child to school after being exposed to COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida): Anyone that may have been in contact without symptoms, you know, should be able to stay in school. They can be monitored. The parents can be notified that there may have been a case. But the parents have a right to have their healthy kids in school.\par \par \uc2 DR. CANDICE JONES (Pediatrician): Immunocompromised is immunocompromised.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Pediatrician Candice Jones said kids like Reefy need multiple layers of protection against COVID.\par \par \uc2 DR. CANDICE JONES: We need to do everything, hand hygiene, masks, social distancing, ventilation, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For Reefy, that means staying home until there`s a mask mandate or she can get a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE KINDER: We want a decision or a vaccine because she wants to go school.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We reached out to Reefy`s school district for comment, but they did not get back.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Kinders are extra cautious because Reefy`s older sister got COVID and had to quarantine in a separate house. She`s only 11 years old, so she`s not eligible for the vaccine yet, but that could change because an \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory panel lans to meet later on this month and they`re going to be talking about rolling out the vaccine for kids ages 5 to 11 -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The world`s first ever malaria vaccine got a thumbs up from the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  this morning. The WHO says children in Africa should get the shots from \uc2 GlaxoSmithKline\uc2  to try and prevent millions of illnesses every year.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta shows us how this could have a huge impact on public health.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Describing this new vaccine as groundbreaking is something of an understatement here in Africa. It really could mean the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 We have bed nets to stop mosquito bites and drugs to kill the parasites, and yet malaria remains one of the most deadly diseases on the planet. So, it`s no wonder the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  regards this as a game- changer.\par \par \uc2 TEDROS ADHANOM GHEBREYESUS\uc2  (\uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 ): This vaccine is a gift to the world, but its value will be felt most in Africa.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Malaria kills around half a million people every year. More than 50 percent are children, and nearly all those cases are in Sub-Saharan Africa.\par \par \uc2 The new vaccine has been given to more than 800,000 African children since 2019 in a pilot program. One of its ingredients is from a rare Chilean evergreen tree. Clinical trials showed a 30 percent efficacy but when combined with anti-malarial drugs, there was a 70 percent reduction in deaths.\par \par \uc2 In a year of unparalleled medical breakthrough, it`s easy to become blase about another new vaccine, but this one could change the course of history.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Debora Patta reporting there with some life-changing news, particularly for kids in Africa.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this country, we`re talking about a major overhaul of a program that wipes out student loans that could help more than a million borrowers.\par \par \uc2 The Education Department said yesterday it will relax its rules for public service loan forgiveness. It`s a program created to reward college students who decide to go into public service. Their student debt is erased after they make ten years of on-time consistent payments. However, the program`s rules have been criticized as too complicated.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is with us now.\par \par \uc2 Jill, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 So, this was meant to help people but wasn`t helping people. What`s changed now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Since 2007, the inception of this plan, it has been notoriously difficult to navigate, complicated, opaque, and in fact, 90 percent of the people who actually applied got rejected. And that meant that only 5,500 borrowers got that student debt wiped clean.\par \par \uc2 Now, with these new rules, the government is saying that they`re hopeful that 550,000 more people will be in the plan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What made it so complicated? Was it the paperwork?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So, it was not just paperwork but, you know, the plan was created so that anyone who was, say, a teacher, a nurse, a service member, someone who worked at a nonprofit, you had to have that first part, you had to be full-time. But then there was this other couple of hurdles. One was that you had to make continuous payments for ten years. Some people missed a payment here and there. New servicers came in.\par \par \uc2 And the other piece was the type of loan that you had. So, under these new rules, what the government is doing is basically temporarily relaxing some of their rules retroactively and this going to allow many more people to have those loans a those payments counted towards being in the plan.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, I know there is a gray area, but who qualifies?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I mean, again, full-time and, you know, teachers, nurses, service members. You work for a government, a state, municipal federal government member, but the reality is if you don`t really know if you qualify, if you have federal student loans and you work for a nonprofit or in one of those sectors, you should go to studentaid.gov because there, you`re going to be able register if you haven`t registered already, but there`s a lot more information. Most of this should bone automatically, according to the government.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So they`re looking for ways to include people instead of excluding people. Could it signal more changes in the world of student loans?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yeah. Well, we`ve heard about $10,000 to $50,000 of debt being erased for every borrower. Well, the White House has kicked that back to Congress. I think we know that Congress has a lot of other things on their plate. So if you have a federal student loan, don`t count on forgiveness right now.\par \par \uc2 Keep making those payments when they start up again in January.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jill, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A CBS News investigation into allegations of fraud at a federal agency prompts a leading senator to take action. Ahead, what a whistleblower told us about a possible three-decade-long scheme. But, first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, new video in connection with the Lori Vallow case. From the aftermath of her ex-husband`s death, it includes an interview with her daughter, who was later killed.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The National Women`s Soccer League returned to action last night after taking a weekend break to address the allegations of sexual harassment and abuse. Players in all three matches stopped playing after six minutes and stood arm in arm in a show of solidarity. They chose that moment to symbolize the six years that it took to force coach Paul Riley out of the league.\par \par \uc2 He was fired last week over claims that he was verbally and sexually abused players. His team, North Carolina Courage, says it removed Riley as soon as it learned of the allegations. Riley denies any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 We were talking about this during the break, guys. Remember, we had those two players on, Mana Shim and Sinead Farrelly, who said to women please speak up, please know you`re not