 don`t know. We weren`t The Beatles but I`m just saying, we felt like The Beatles, so --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Highwomen`s debut album hit number one on the country chart.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: I have no idea why it works so well. We are four completely different women. And our four voices sound completely different. But when we get together and sing, it is just like a lightning rod.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s got to be a pretty cool feeling.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It is. It is. It`s like, hold on to your seat.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That success helped give Hemby the confidence to put out "Pins and Needles."\par \par \uc2 Have you heard one of your own songs on the radio yet from this album?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: Actually my daughter did. She`s like, "Listen. Turn it up. Mommy, you`re on the radio."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It must feel good to hear your voice after all these years.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It is, it`s awesome. I hope I get to hear it more.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love her cackle.\par \par \uc2 Her daughter, Sammie Jo, sang with her at the Rymon earlier this year. Her husband, Mike Wrucke, produced this record and I`ve got to say beautifully.\par \par \uc2 Her dad is a session guitarist from Nashville, her mom works for Amy Grant. It`s all in the family and it`s a great family, it really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is what I love about your piece. I walked in, has anybody heard of Natalie Hemby?\par \par \uc2 I hadn`t heard of her. But then you hear her music and you go we do know who she is. What a beautiful voice.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You said a lot of song writers can`t sing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is why they`re writers.\par \par \uc2 Man This one can and that`s the real surprise of Natalie Hemby. I think that`s what surprised The 