rchers are getting up close to find out why this is happening.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: During what`s proving to be a very busy shark season off Southern California, just beyond the surf camp students in the shore break --\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s a fin out there. See the fin?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A sight that`s terrifying for most people.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is that a shark?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It`s actually one of many. Most swimmers never even see them.\par \par \uc2 But video from above shows countless baby great white sharks, up to 6 years old and 10 feet long, swimming among families and surfers. It`s becoming so familiar here, something unusual is happening to these kids and their parents.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hello, shark.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: They`re not afraid.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve seen sharks out there when your son is out here?\par \par \uc2 ANNA RUDNICKI (Beachgoer): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It sounds weird to say, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 ANNA RUDNICKI: Yes. I almost don`t want to think about it too much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Anna Rudnicki says her perception of the apex predator is changing.\par \par \uc2 ANNA RUDNICKI: I guess if my kid is right there and saw a shark go by, then, yes, it must be changing.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Surf camp owner Chris Keet has a plan when a shark gets too close.\par \par \uc2 What happens then?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KEET (Surf Camp Owner): We blow a whistle, we have everyone come inside the surf line. We call it Code Black.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So take a look here. Christopher Campbell (ph), he`s right on the water and that guy on the paddleboard there, he is a shark spotter, making sure to keep these kids safe.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They have basically come to accept the fact that there are sharks here and they`re learning to live with them.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Chris Lowe runs the shark lab at Cal State Long Beach. We met him and his team just a few hundred yards offshore from the surf camp.\par \par \uc2 I see it, I see it. There it is.\par \par \uc2 During a week-long expedition, tagging and tracking a record number of juvenile great whites.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We want to be able to predict where these sharks will show up.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And to better understand what`s attracting the sharks, they have to catch one.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ANDERSON\uc2  (Shark Researcher): It goes from zero to 100 very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Researcher \uc2 James Anderson\uc2  and the team wrangle it along the boat.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ANDERSON\uc2 : It`s thrashing and it`s trying to get away. Then you`ve got to untangle it from the net, get it onto its back so it then calms down and goes into a tonic inability state.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He takes blood and tissue samples to help learn what the shark has eaten; it may be a clue as to why the shark is here. Then he performs surgery at sea, implanting a tracking device that will last a decade.\par \par \uc2 That`s researcher Emily Spurgeon getting hands on with the 150-pound juvenile shark. She`s responsible for monitoring and maintaining a network of underwater hydrophones, listening for tagged sharks swimming by.\par \par \uc2 EMILY SPURGEON (Shark Researcher): So we`re tracking these juvenile white sharks from Mexico, all the way up to the central coast of California.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: This is all part of getting the data so you can predict when sharks will be around.\par \par \uc2 EMILY SPURGEON: That`s the goal. So if I can figure out this habitat is perfect for them, maybe we can see if another beach has similar conditions, then those sharks might be moving there.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Ultimately the team hopes to create a shark report to go with the surf report, so Southern California beachgoers know when to expect company in the water.\par \par \uc2 You saw "Jaws" when you 