amilton)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Christopher Jackson, nominated for a Tony award. This is George Washington in "Hamilton." Ahead -- are we on camera?\par \par \uc2 I`m like when you coming in, Clark?\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Christopher Jackson is here with us to discuss the return to Broadway. The hip-hop style show, what`s it called?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER JACKSON (Actor): Freestyle Love Supreme.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s really good in that, too.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL PROGRAMMING)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Katy Perry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes!\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It`s time for the stories that are the "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 Gayle King, are you ready to rock `n` roll?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is tonight`s season premiere of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  sitcom called "The United States of Al."\par \par \uc2 The show is about the friendship between a Marine veteran and his Afghan interpreter, living together in the state of Ohio. When Afghanistan fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  back in August, the writers of the show had to quickly pivot and they wrote a new episode to reflect the chaotic evacuation of Afghan citizens from Kabul. Here`s a clip.\par \par \uc2 (Clip From TV Show THE UNITED STATES OF AL)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is Al, trying to get his sister out of the country. The show`s executive producer said the episode is based on his own production staff, who were scrambling to help loved ones overseas. Now one of the writers, Habib Zahori, confirms this.\par \par \uc2 HABIB ZAHORI (Staff Writer): All those things that you will see that happens to Al and Riley in this episode, that was my life. I wanted this story to reflect reality as closely as possible, as I was experiencing it every day and every night. We managed to get out 11 people. My family is extremely lucky.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. He says that they were writing this episode, just think about this, as events were unfolding in real time. Adhir Kalyan, who plays Al, says it was a very emotional time for everybody on the set.\par \par \uc2 The season two premiere of "The United States of Al" airs tonight at 8:30, 7:30 Central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I started watching this show last season, just because I was curious. It`s very well done. Kudos to Chuck Lorre and his team. The fact that they could pivot and capture in real time with people who really understand what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Art imitating life, a story line we`re all familiar with.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m here for any Chuck Lorre production. He knows what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is about an interesting alert from \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2 , who are asking for the public`s help to find a criminal who disappeared in the late 1990s. That criminal is John Ruffo.\par \par \uc2 He was convicted in a $350 million tax fraud scheme or bank fraud scheme, one of the biggest ever; $13 million were never recovered. And John never showed up for his prison appointment. He disappeared.\par \par \uc2 Now federal marshals are saying he may have turned up in 2016, nearly 20 years later, at a Dodgers-\uc2 Red Sox\uc2  game, sitting behind home plate at Dodger Stadium.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this picture. This is who \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  believe they are looking for. He is described as 5`5, 170 pounds. He`s about 66 years old, has a lot of money. And it almost sounds like a dating service here. It sounds like he likes fine wines, nice hotels --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course he does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Be advised, he`s lactose intolerant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he`s 5`5, 175?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So he was just sitting back like, I think it`s about that time. I`m good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If $13 million is missing, he can afford to go to a baseball game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He can do whatever he likes. If you`ve seen that man and you were at that game, maybe you were sitting next to him, call the federal marshals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The search is on.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He should have just laid low and watched it from home.\par \par \uc2 So my "Talk of the Table" is about Leonardo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: DiCaprio?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, not DiCaprio, not da Vinci or the Ninja Turtle. It`s a new robot, built by researchers at \uc2 Caltech\uc2 , that can walk and fly, no, I am not kidding. Just check this out.\par \par \uc2 So here`s the thing. I mean Will Smith in "I, Robot" had me shook. OK? Who knows if robots are going to take over but I don`t want them skateboarding in celebration. I don`t want them doing alis and kick flips after they take over the world.\par \par \uc2 So he can fly. He has a drone attached to him. He can also ride a skateboard and I think that`s a tightrope so he can do it all. That is a new robot. So just to freak you out while you`re having your cup of coffee, there`s robots that can do it all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is the problem, every new technology has good things about it. But inevitably, there are unforeseen terrible things about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m worried about what they may be in the robot revolution.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not ready yet for the robots. Not quite ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Watch out for Leonardo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now we`ll look at who`s joining us next. We gave you a sneak peek. He`s in our green room. He`s a Tony award nominee, the only one in the building at this time. Christopher Jackson is in our progressive green room to talk about his return to Broadway in Freestyle Love Supreme.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:34. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL PROGRAMMING)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip From Theater Show Hamilton)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So talented. Actor Christopher Jackson received a Tony nomination for his starring role as George Washington in "Hamilton." The Emmy and Grammy Award winner is a special guest in the show Freestyle Love Supreme, an improvised hip-hop show. The audience contribute words to stories and they turn it into songs and rhymes. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip From Theater Show Freestyle Love Supreme)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Jackson performing during the 2019 run. It was filmed in a documentary, "We Are Freestyle Love Supreme." He is a founding member of the group which was started 17 years ago with "Hamilton" co- creator, Lin-Manuel Miranda.\par \par \uc2 How about that?\par \par \uc2 And last week Freestyle Love Supreme won a special Tony award. Chris Jackson is joining us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Chris, how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON (Actor): Good morning. That`s exhausting. Man, wow, that`s a lot of stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you look at that and go, that guy`s good.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: No, I look at that and think what a life I`ve been fortunate to have.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you`re just getting started.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What does it feel like to be back on Broadway?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: It is amazing. There`s only one place, there`s only one Broadway in the entire world. That`s why I came to New York. So walking down the street, seeing people walk into theaters, going out on that stage, seeing an audience that`s so thirsty to have that kind of communal experience once again, it`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Some people said they thought it would be intimidating or it would be -- they were a little nervous getting back onstage.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel any of that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: The beautiful part about Freestyle Love Supreme is that you absorb whatever the audience is coming in with. You know, I`ve been doing this long enough to have stood on stage throughout the course of some rather large global events, things that we were all thinking about. We take that and we use it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do you use it?\par \par \uc2 What do you mean you absorb it?\par \par \uc2 How does it work exactly?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: If an audience comes in the door and they`re hyped, that feeds us. If they come in and they`re a little tentative, we know we have to go out and get them, we have to reach out energy wise, looking for things that`s going to pique the audience, that`s going to get the reaction and build off of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But they are giving you words and phrases and ideas.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you work the story like that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve been a couple of times. Somebody said avocado, somebody said murder on Broadway. Somebody said flying on a plane, just three random things and you guys have to figure out a way on the spot to make all that make sense. And you do.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Words rhyme. Words rhyme. And you throw music behind it. A lot of what we do is like jazz. You have a form but then you`re able to improvise and get out what you`re feeling and get out what the audience wants to hear. It`s a neat little show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For those who aren`t familiar with the art of a freestyle, it`s a little --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate, you are familiar.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little bit. I can do a little something. But you have to go off the top of your head, sometimes a cappella and you`re literally pulling words not only from the crowd but coming up with stuff from yourself.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Absolutely, absolutely. I mean there are -- there are all different types of -- just like with jazz, there`s all different instruments, there`s all different trains of thought, all kinds of different techniques rhyming wise.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly, the end result is that you`re trying to either tell a story or trying to just engage with other artists in real time. It`s an exciting kind of thing. It takes a lot of practice, it takes a lot of work, it takes a lot of energy and effort sometimes. But it`s music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yesterday we got very exciting news from Sarah Jessica Parker, who said that you`re going to be part of the -- I was very psyched to hear this -- "Sex and the City" reboot. Like you don`t have enough to do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have two kids that you adore, you`re living, you`re working. Tell us about "Sex and the City."\par \par \uc2 What are you going to be doing?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: I`m playing a character, Herbert Wexley, who is the husband to the inimitable Nicole Ari Parker.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: So my -- the way that Michael Patrick King brings the character to support the stories of these amazing, dynamic women, it`s a really good position to sit, one, to see how an incredible show is made.\par \par \uc2 And the camaraderie that already exists in that cast because they have enveloped new characters into the show, it`s been really -- it`s been a -- because I loved watching the show when it was on in the original run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: So I kind of feel like I`m a fly on the wall watching how all of this magic gets made. I think it`s going to be a real fun season. I`m really excited about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you`ve got "Sex and the City" reboot, Freestyle Love Supreme, "Bull" in its sixth season.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Premiering tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a method to our madness. We`re going to say that at the end. But I appreciate the plug. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Of all the different venues that you do, which do you prefer?\par \par \uc2 Because there`s something different about being on stage, something different about taping a TV show, something different about being on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have a preference?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: I prefer whatever it is I get to do. Whatever new thing that`s in front of me. I came into knowing what the idea of a renaissance artist is, the idea of being able to do as many things as possible, as many outlets as I can have to be creative.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just about getting up in front of people for me. It`s about having an experience and then taking one experience and then bringing it to another one and bringing it to another one and all of these different disciplines are very difficult to get good at.\par \par \uc2 So the more that you do them, the more you understand that particular thing. And then you get to -- I`ve been fortunate to be able to shift over and use and incorporate that into other things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thing I like about Broadway is the reaction is immediate.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You get the audience and sometimes there`s a cranky Yankee just sitting there, refusing to engage. There`s the times that you feel you have the crowd from the time you step out on the stage.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: I distinctly remember, in the thousands of shows that I`ve done -- but I remember seeing people in the third row of "Hamilton," who were knocked all the way out, just asleep, within like the first 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Deep sleep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That doesn`t throw you?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: No, because you know what?\par \par \uc2 You never know what that person is going through. That person could be an air traffic controller and not able to sleep but they get there and they`re around all these people and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe they came to go to sleep.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An expensive nap.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JACKSON: They bought that ticket, so they have earned the right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, Chris, you are one of the most gifted, talented men in the business, one of the most versatile talents. Thank you so much for joining us, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Freestyle Love Supreme is back on Broadway tonight and you can watch season six of the premiere of "Bull" tonight. 10:00, 9:00 Central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: From McDonald`s` McPlant burger to Chipotle`s plant-based chorizo, meatless options are -- did I say that wrong?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All of a sudden I saw Patty cracking up.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: These options are going mainstream. The food revelation in restaurants across the country has its roots in America`s heartland and it`s powered by peas. Nancy Chen reports on what`s happening from table to farm.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Near Times Square, one of the best joints for a burger with all the fixin`s isn`t serving any meat. April Tam Smith is the co-founder of PS Kitchen. The vegan restaurant was one of the first in New York City to serve the brand Beyond Meat back in 2017.\par \par \uc2 APRIL TAM SMITH (restaurateur): I remember when we first opened, the questions were usually around, "What`s in this?"\par \par \uc2 "Wow, it`s really good. What am I eating?"\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Fast forward to today. As meat alternative products are creating new appetites for all.\par \par \uc2 The people who are coming in wanting to try this burger, are they vegetarian or meat eaters?\par \par \uc2 APRIL TAM SMITH: I would say mostly meat eaters. I think about 80 percent of our customers are just regular people, people who are just craving the burgers.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Changing the nation`s taste buds isn`t easy. While a recent survey found nearly nine in 10 Americans eat meat as part of their diet, 25 percent say they`re at least willing to try plant-based burger alternatives. That includes this Oklahoma girl.\par \par \uc2 So this is the Beyond Meat burger and there`s absolutely no meat in this.\par \par \uc2 APRIL TAM SMITH: No, not at all. It`s made with pea protein.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All right. This is really good. It just tastes like a regular burger.\par \par \uc2 As for where much of it comes from, near the South Dakota border in Dawson, Minnesota, are the seeds of a food revolution. Brother and sister Tyler Lorenzen and Nicole Atchison have the largest pea protein plant in North America.\par \par \uc2 What exactly is pea protein?\par \par \uc2 TYLER LORENZEN (Pea Protein Plant Owner): Pea protein is protein that comes from peas. Not green peas; actually yellow field peas. There`s actually 20 percent protein in peas, which is quite a bit for most vegetables.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A chance to change what we eat and how we farm.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, I was surprised to learn, I mean even on the drive in here, I`m passing fields of crops. But those crops aren`t necessarily going to people and for the most part they`re actually going to livestock.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE ATCHISON (Pea Protein Plant Owner): Yes, and that`s I think surprising for a lot of people because, as we drive around, we think, oh, look at all this food that we`re making.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Puris works for a network of 400 farmers to source their pea plants. Peas are actually one of the most soil-nurturing plants you can grow, which is great for healthy crop rotations.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean for farmers in the United States these days?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE ATCHISON: We`ve worked over the last 20 years to adapt varieties that can grow right in the heartland. Peas aren`t typically grown in Canada so we`ve really adapted them to new climates, which gives farmers more revenue opportunities.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: One of the biggest customers for their pea varieties is Beyond Meat, found not only in PS Kitchen but now McDonald`s, \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  and \uc2 Panda Express\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It seems like the industry of plant-based protein has really gone through a revolution these past few years.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE ATCHISON: Yes, I think the biggest change has been the products on the market really mimicking their counterparts that are coming from animal proteins.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: With flavor and looks, pea protein is proving to be more than just a food trend.\par \par \uc2 The meat eaters that are coming in and trying these plant-based burgers, what are they seeking?\par \par \uc2 APRIL TAM SMITH: I think often it`s -- it really is just genuinely delicious. You don`t really feel like you`re missing out on something but, at the same time, a lot of us are more aware of the environmental impact now, so it`s really a win-win for the consumer, for the environment and for our bodies.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Nancy Chen in Dawson, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Nancy Chen. There`s another option for you to try.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eha70004u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Federal Judge Has Ordered Texas To Suspend Its New Law Banning Most Abortions After Six Weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Jan Crawford, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 803 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. It`s Thursday, October 7th. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I am Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s Eye Opener-- it`s your world in ninety seconds.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: It`s about paying for what we owe and preventing a catastrophic event occurring in our economy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a possible short-term deal on Capitol Hill to raise the debt ceiling. Why critics say that`s just not enough.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 : We`re making good progress. We`re not there yet, but I hope we can come to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A federal judge has ordered Texas to suspend its new law banning most abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: An eighteen-year-old student is under arrest after a shooting during a fight at a Texas high school that injured four people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shocking allegations of fraud at the ATF. A CBS News investigation spurs Congress to act.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Will you ask to find out if it`s happening at other agencies?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2 : Well, my suspicion is it probably is.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Investigators say they have identified the Zodiac Killer who murdered at least five people in the sixties.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The Dodgers advance to the National League Divisional Series.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Walk them off, Chris Taylor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grammy winner Natalie Hemby writes hits for others. She tells Anthony about taking center stage herself.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It felt like the Beatles or something. We weren`t the Beatles but I`m just saying--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: --we felt like the Beatles, so.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  dismisses an emergency solution to the country`s debt ceiling issue, a trillion-dollar platinum coin.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I think there`s something so fitting about Biden fixing this by just pulling a coin out of thin air. Hey, America, come here, kiddo. Come see your uncle Joe. What you got there behind your ear? Oh, look, it`s a trillion dollars. There you go. Buy yourself something nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. If you grew up in my house, it would be buy yourself something nice and put the rest of it in savings. No, that`s no fun. I have a great appreciation for a savings account. Welcome to CBS News.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this. We`ve got a possible deal on the debt limit that could avoid an economic crisis. We`ll get to that in just a second, but we begin with breaking news overnight from Texas on the state`s controversial abortion law. A federal judge is putting the measure on hold, calling it unconstitutional. Jan Crawford is following that story. Jan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (CBS News Chief Legal Correspondent): Well, good morning, Gayle. So, the reaction among Biden administration officials, abortion rights advocates was relief. The judge`s decision stems from a Justice Department lawsuit against Texas last month, just days after S.B. 8 went into effect. The law outlaws abortions once embryonic activity is detected, which is usually around six weeks, and that could be before some women even know they`re pregnant. And it allows private citizens to enforce it by bringing civil lawsuits against anyone who helps a woman get an abortion after that with fines starting at ten thousand dollars. But in a strongly worded, a hundred-and-thirteen-page ruling, Judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  said the law was blatantly unconstitutional. "From the moment S.B. 8 went into effect, women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their lives and this court will not sanction one more day of this offensive deprivation of such an important right." Now, in a statement, the Biden administration cautioned that the fight has only just begun. Texas of course will appeal and the supreme court is taking up that Mississippi abortion ban in December that is likely going to determine not only the fate of this law in Texas, but other states as well. Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will keep an eye on that. Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eha70004v\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Federal Judge Temporarily Blocks Texas Abortion Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mireya Villarreal \par \uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to Texas, where abortions resumed today at some clinics. That`s after a federal judge blocked the country`s most restrictive abortion law, calling it an offensive deprivation of a woman`s constitutional rights. He also accused lawmakers of scheme to evade the courts. And CBS` Mireya Villarreal reports the fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): Bans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (CBS News Correspondent): Abortion rights advocates are calling this ruling a win for women in Texas and a strong defense of Roe v. Wade by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER (Press and CEO, Whole Woman`s Health): This is the justice we have been seeking for weeks.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: The \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  sued Texas and its governor to stop the law from being enforced. Last night, Judge Robert Pittman sided with the federal government and granted a temporary restraining order. During a special session last month, Texas legislators passed a law that bans abortions once cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks before many women know they`re pregnant. It also bans abortions in cases of rape or incest. The law is enforced by allowing private citizens to file a lawsuit against anyone who provides an abortion or assists a woman in getting one.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BRYAN HUGHES\uc2  (R-Texas): This law took effect September 1st, and since that time, there`s a hundred and thirty little babies every day that aren`t being aborted in Texas. So that`s one tangible effect already over four thousand as we speak today.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Since the law was passed, abortions in Texas have largely stopped, and some women are now traveling to other states to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER: Last night, we reached out to some of the patients that we had on a waiting list to come in to have abortions today.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: But other providers are fearful, waiting for a definite ruling.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER: It delays people access to safe abortion and displaces them, requires them to travel or delay the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: The state of Texas has already filed its intent to appeal with one of the most conservative courts in the country. Meanwhile, clinics here in Texas, not all of them have started abortions again after six weeks because they are fearful they could be retroactively sued. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mireya Villarreal, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, Senate leaders have an agreement to extend the government`s borrowing ability. That means the U.S. won`t default on its debts, which would have plunged the country into an economic catastrophe. But it is only a temporary fix. The extension lasts until early December, when we could be right back where we started.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211008eha70005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Rock Legend On New Memoir, Music Career, Family Life; Psychologist On Warning Signs For Domestic Abuse And How To Get Help; R. Kelly Survivor Azriel Clary On Getting Out Of Relationship\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Miri  Marshall, Annie Yu, Nate Burleson, Miri Marshall, Tony Perkins, Marc  Clarke, Lilia Luciano, Ed O`Keefe, Jan Crawford, David Agus, Manuel  Bojorquez \par \uc2 13,707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNIGNS. It`s a new month, it`s Monday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener". It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIM CARR, HUNTINGTON, CA BEACH MAYOR: We are in midst of a potential ecological disaster here in Huntington Beach.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A massive oil spill in California puts beaches and wildlife in danger. Inside the desperate efforts to contain it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can see the oil slick just kind of moving its way down. And there`s a lot out there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The massive leak exposes how politicians, billionaires and celebrities use offshore accounts to hide trillions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A landmark trial set to begin today over whether pharmacies played a role in the nation`s opioid crisis.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Stunning revelations from a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower. She tells CBS News division is in the design.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, FACEBOOK WHISTLEBLOWER: The version of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that exists today is tearing our societies apart and causing ethnic violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Tom Brady, one, Belichick, zero.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Brady`s going to pick up a first down. Look at that face --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Rocker Dave Grohl is here with his new book, and he has great stories.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL, MUSICIAN: My life flashes before my eyes every single day, and in writing this book, I`ve tried to capture those moments.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was cooking. It was good. How are you doing? How was everybody`s summer? Mine was bad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: "Saturday Night Live" opening up its 47th season poking fun at President Biden and the Democrats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The infrastructure bill, It`s been delayed indefinitely. So I guess we`ll just cross that bridge when it collapses on top of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive -- making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was good to see "Saturday Night Live" back with new material. I always like how -- their take on the news.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, yeah, new material, but we`ve been joking about infrastructure since the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not that new.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "SNL" and politics, anonymous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It goes together.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNING.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 And we start this morning with cleanup crews that are scrambling to contain a massive oil spill in southern California`s Surf City. Video from above shows an oil slick covering about 13 square miles. It`s forced beaches to close and wildfires in grave danger.\par \par \uc2 The worst of it is in Huntington Beach, about 40 miles south of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano was there.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Look, the mayor calls this a potential environmental catastrophe, and it`s felt everywhere. One of the most striking scenes I saw yesterday between the crude-filled shore and the boats working on cleanup was a pair of dolphins swimming by. That`s just one of the many species those crews and the teams are land are working to protect.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): Thick pockets of sludge continue to wash up along some of southern California`s most popular beaches this morning, coating everything from the sand to the wife.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The smell hits you, and there is just gunk everywhere.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: At least 120 gallons of oil began pouring into the Pacific Ocean over the week from an underwater pipeline about 4.5 miles offshore, sending a sheen of oil from Huntington Beach to Dana Point Harbor.\par \par \uc2 KIM CARR, HUNTINGTON, CA BEACH MAYOR: We are in the midst of a potential ecological disaster.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Amplify Energy which owns the pipeline shut the line down and suctioned out the remaining oil. The company`s CEO also pledged full cooperation with the cleanup effort.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are investigating the source and potential cause of this incident.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Officials have deployed more than 2,000 feet of protective booms and skimming equipment to try to slow the spread of the oil and prevent any further damage to sensitive marshlands.\par \par \uc2 How vulnerable is this area environmentally speaking?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR LEIPZIG, SEA & SAGE AUDUBON SOCIETY CONSERVATION DIRECTOR: This is one of the most important stretches of coastline along Orange County, and possibly all of southern California.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Victor Leipzig, conservation director of the Sea and Sage Audubon Society, says the disaster comes at a critical time as several different species of sea birds migrate to the area for the winter.\par \par \uc2 LEIPZIG: The spill is only a few hours old, and so how many birds or other wildlife are going to be impacted, what impact it`s going to haven vegetation and the marshes along the coast is yet to be seen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (on camera): We`ve talked about the environmental impact, but there`s also an economic impact. The beach is now closed because of the hazard, and to make space for the cleanup. But the beach might remain closed for weeks if not months according to the mayor, and there are so many businesses all along the shore that depend on beach-goers to survive - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some upsetting pictures there from Southern California. Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Washington, and President Biden says he will work like hell, direct quote, to bring Democratic lawmakers together, overcoming disagreement about his ambitious agenda. Party leaders insist they just need a little more time.\par \par \uc2 Our Ed O`Keefe is at the White House monitoring all this for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 You know, normally, here in Washington, work has slowed down because of fights between Democrats and Republicans over big legislation. Right now, it`s because of fighting amongst Democrats, liberals and moderates can`t agree exactly on how to proceed, and now some activists are confronting two of the senators at the middle of the fight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s because of the lack of protections that --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): On Sunday in Arizona, activists followed Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  into a ladies room angered by her opposition it a $3.5 trillion social policy bill.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In Washington, other activists have even circled the houseboat of West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin who tried addressing their concerns.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): I know it won`t be enough for some. It will be too much for others.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The anger comes as Democrats blew through their own deadlines, failing last week to pass a bipartisan infrastructure bill and failing to cut a deal amongst themselves over President Biden`s build-back-better plan.\par \par \uc2 The president spent the weekend mostly out of public view.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Seven hundred thousand COVID deaths. Can we get your reaction, sir?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: As other top Democrats say they`ll keep at it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Our goal is to get it done in the next month, both bills, get them passed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The bipartisan infrastructure bills expected to eventually pass in the House but only if a deal is reached on the size and scope of the much larger second bill packed with changes in healthcare, education, housing, and climate change policy.\par \par \uc2 Moderates like Manchin say they won`t support sing more than $1.5 trillion on the plan. Progressives disagree.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL\uc2  (D-WA): It`s going to be somewhere, you know, between 1.5 and 3.5.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: New York Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez explained how progressives might find a way to bring down the price.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I think that one of the ideas that`s out there is fully fund what we can fully fund, but maybe instead of doing it for ten years, fully fund it for five years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (on camera): As the talks continue, the president`s planning to hit the road to sell the plan even though we don`t know entirely yet what`s actually in it. He plans to visit Michigan tomorrow with possible stops later in the week -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. He said he`s going to work like hell. It seems like he needs to do that. Thank you very much, Ed, across the street from the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court begins a new term today. Court will be held in person for the first time since March of last year. The justices have a very controversial docket in front of them, including cases that challenge a longstanding abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with a look at what`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you. First Monday in October. That`s what it means, court`s in session. Hello to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: There`s this first day of school vibe up here today, Gayle. And let me tell you, a lot has happened since the court last heard arguments in person. We`ve got a new justice, Amy Coney Barrett, and that`s helped shift the court to the right. They`re taking up a host of contentious social issues, they`ve got gun rights, religious rights, and the most divisive of all, abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): Over the weekend, protesters left no doubt what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The most significant challenge to Roe versus Wade in a generation. A case asking the justices to uphold Mississippi`s ban on abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER, CLINIC DIRECTOR: This is the only clinic in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Shannon Brewer (ph) is director of the clinic in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: I`ve been here through a lot of different laws that we`ve fought. But this one, this is the one that I can honestly say worries me more than any of them.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The justices will hear arguments in the case in December. With Justice Amy Coney Barrett replacing liberal icon \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , abortion opponents hope this court could finally overturn Roe, the 1973 case that said abortion was a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Some states across the country already are preparing for the ruling, with mostly conservative states ready to outlaw or greatly restrict abortion if the court sides with Mississippi while some liberal ones are passing laws to guarantee that in their states, abortion still would be available.\par \par \uc2 The case comes with the court already in the political spotlight. Polls show public opinion of the court has declined. And there are other contentious cases that surely will divide the justices. In November, the court takes up a major gun rights case challenging a New York law that limits who can carry a concealed weapon for self-defense.\par \par \uc2 It also will decide a religious rights case, whether a tuition assistance program in Maine that bans state money from going to religious amounts to discrimination.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (on camera): Now, this week, the court`s going to be one justice short on the bench anyway. Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  tested positive last week for the coronavirus. So, he`s going to be working from home today, participating remotely.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, of course, the building, of course, not taking any chances. The building is still closed to the public. Most members of the media won`t be able to attend, but you can listen to these arguments on a live stream through the court`s website -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are approaching a pivotal moment in the COVID fight. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory panel meets later this month to decide if the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine should get emergency approval for children age 5 to 11. This decision could affect more than 28 million kids.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor Dr. David Agus, he joins us right now in studio.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Yeah, great to be here. I love the new studio. So nice to see you all in person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, it`s good to have you.\par \par \uc2 So, how soon can we see the first shots in the arms for younger kids?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Well, the answer is not soon enough. And so, later this month they`re going to meet, 5 through 11.\par \par \uc2 I`ve seen the data. It is safe, and they work. So, really, the hope is, you know, toward Halloween or shortly thereafter, we can start vaccinating kids. So, by Thanksgiving, they may have received two doses, and we can have a safe Thanksgiving. We want to push this as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-eight million kids, 9 percent of U.S. population in that group. We have to move.\par \par \uc2 So, you mentioned a safe Thanksgiving which speaks to the potential impact of vaccinating kids. Other than Thanksgiving, big picture, what impact could this have?\par \par \uc2 Well, we`re now getting mandates, right? And we`re seeing they`re working. You know, when companies say you have to have a vaccine to come to work, we`re seeing the numbers go up pretty dramatically within companies within schools, state of California said you need a vaccine to go to school. So, we will get out of this mess and get over delta when we can increase the vaccination rate.\par \par \uc2 And we are getting there. We add into them, these 25 million kids. We`re going to be much, much closer. So, I feel good about, you know, February, March, April, as we get there if we can really come through on these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we talk about boosters?\par \par \uc2 I`m getting my booster at high noon. I can`t wait. I can`t wait to go and get the shot. But --\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Never thought I would the day when Gayle King says, I can`t wait until I can get the shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too. Because you know I`m terrified of needles.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: You`re blowing my mind here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m terrified of needles.\par \par \uc2 But it`s still only for certain groups of people who can get it. Do you think everybody should be getting the booster now? And what`s the downside to getting the booster?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Well, that`s the key thing, is that, you know, the RNA vaccines, you lose 6 percent immunity per month. With J&J, the immunity was not as high when they were given than the RNA vaccine. So, I do think 40 and older, we should be boosterizing, if there`s such a word, everybody.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to see \uc2 FDA\uc2  meetings over the next 10 days on the Moderna boosters and J&J boosters. So, a lot more people will become eligible.\par \par \uc2 But these criteria, if you`re this age and in these conditions, I think we have to make it simpler.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mentioned safe Thanksgiving. As we approach Thanksgiving, Christmas, even New Year`s, should we be hesitant about large gatherings?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Yes, we should be hesitant about large gatherings. As long as everyone is vaccinated and, you know, people, high rescue, can be tested, testing is still a big part of this, we can gather at families for Thanksgiving and Christmas. But you have to do it right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But people feel I`ve got the double shot and it`s still not working. What do you say to people that think I don`t want to get another - - more medicine?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t want anymore.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: You don`t have to. I mean, it is working. It is blocking most hospitalizations and deaths from COVID-19. But it`s not blocking all breakthroughs. You lose about 6 percent of immunity per month.\par \par \uc2 So a booster will give you protection against most breakthroughs. And it`s your option, but I do think if you`re higher risk, if you`re over 60, you definitely want to be protected. It`s just not worth that risk. You want to step up and get that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it`s a new virus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: So, this is del virus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: So, with the old virus, we probably wouldn`t have needed these boosters. But clearly, a new virus came and changed the game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. You lose 6 percent a month, you said?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Six percent a month on a good day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: OK. All right. Thank you, David Agus.\par \par \uc2 Did I mention I`m get mine at high noon? Really --\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Did I mention I`m proud of you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cannot wait and I`m terrified of needles.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got new information on thousands of migrants, most of them from Haiti who camped out near a U.S. border crossing in south Texas last month. Many of them have made it through some of the most dangerous wilderness on this planet. The Darien Gap between Panama and Colombia is a jungle filled with wild animals, treacherous terrain, and armed criminals.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has reported extensively from this area.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Necocli, Colombia, talking to migrants who are still just trying to get through.\par \par \uc2 Manuel, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 It is estimated some 16,000 migrants are waiting here in Colombia for a chance to cross this bay to get themselves one step closer to a stretch of jungle known a the Darien Gap. Many of them wait weeks camped out along this beach here, and word that the U.S. has recently deported nearly 7,000 Haitians is doing very little to stop them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voice-over): A patchwork of tents has replaced the sunbathing tourists that once dotted the beaches of Necocli. Its spot along the Caribbean coast has made it the next stop for migrants heading north like Chares Patrick (ph).\par \par \uc2 CHARES PATRICK: USA, USA, USA.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: USA, the United States?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: The United States.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: That`s his goal. He fled poverty and violence in Haiti four years ago, he says only to find discrimination little work in Chile. He has two daughters back home.\par \par \uc2 A young woman and a girl, so everything you do is for them.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: I want to change their life -- my family.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: You want to change their lives, too?\par \par \uc2 The shortest route from here to Panama is by boat, but that nation`s government is only allowing 500 to cross a day, creating a bottleneck. There are clues here, pointing to the hard journey ahead.\par \par \uc2 They want these types of views because they know it`s so dangerous out there. But they`re expensive. He`s only sold two pairs so far. The danger is the Darien Gap. A 60-mile stretch of lawless jungle so treacherous at least 50 bodies have reportedly been found this year. But the real death toll is estimated to be four times higher.\par \par \uc2 More than 88,000 have already crossed this year. Panama estimates one out of every four are children.\par \par \uc2 Their roadmap is a network of WhatsApp groups with migrants sharing directions, tips and warnings.\par \par \uc2 But you won`t go back?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: No. No back. No, no go back.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: My dream is not back. My dream is out of there.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Your dream is not back, your dream is north?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: North.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: First, you must leave Necocli.\par \par \uc2 Leonel Nelson (ph), his wife and his infant daughter, are about to do just that and attempt to cross the gap.\par \par \uc2 It`s very difficult, that there are bandits, bandidos.\par \par \uc2 But despite the danger, Nelson is determined to push ahead. We were with them as they waited and finally got a chance to board.\par \par \uc2 (SPEAKING SPANISH)\par \par \uc2 Only God knows.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (on camera): We followed Leonel and his family as they crossed and handed themselves over to smugglers on the other side to continue their journey. We`ll have that part of the story for you tomorrow right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS -- Tony. z TONY DOKOUPIL: Boy, Manny. Showing us the dangers of that journey and in doing so suggest what dangers lay behind. What would you leave behind do that? That`s your choice.\par \par \uc2 Many, thank you very much. Look forward to that piece tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Coming up today, a closer look at startling allegations from a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: The version of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that exists today is tearing our societies apart and causing ethnic violence around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She tells "60 Minutes" what Facebook`s own research reveals about misinformation on the platform.\par \par \uc2 But, first, it is 7:17. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, a terrifying scene aboard a \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  flight. We`ll show you what caused the engine to burst into flames moments before takeoff.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Tom Brady plays against the patriots for the first time. We`ll show you how he did and why he`s not the only GOAT around. Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  says a bird strike caused this engine fire on an airport runway yesterday. That`s an Airbus A320 with 109 people aboard. It was taking off from Atlantic City, New Jersey, headed for Ft. Lauderdale, when the pilot aborted the takeoff and brought the plane to a stop.\par \par \uc2 Video shows passengers and sliding off the plane as crews put the flames out. Everyone was evacuated safely, and officials say only two people had minor injuries. All the passengers got a refund and a voucher to pay for a future flight on \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean, I guess --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds funny to hear they got a refund and voucher. You see the people going, "Fire, fire," and looking out the window.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But they got a voucher, though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say it`s better to have the bird strike before the takeoff than after takeoff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kudos to the crew.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m stressed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up -- very stressed. I need an adult diaper.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, rock star Dave Grohl and Foo fighters.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWSCAST AND WEATHER FORECAST)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower plans to testify to Congress this week that she believes the company lies about how it handles hate and misinformation. Former Facebook employee Frances Haugen revealed her identity last night on "60 Minutes." Her lawyers gave the Securities and Exchange Commission thousands of pages of Facebook`s internal research. And she says it proves the company knew its algorithms raised the profile of hate, divisive political speech, and misinformation.\par \par \uc2 Haugen also told Scott Pelley that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  chooses policies that emphasize profit over public safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, FORMER \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2  EMPLOYEE: So, you know, you have your phone. You may see 100 pieces of content if you scroll on for five minutes. But \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has thousands of options it could show.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: The algorithm picks from those options based on the kind of content you`ve engaged with the most in the past.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: And one of the consequences of how \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is picking that content today is it is optimizing for content that gets engagement, a reaction. But its own research is showing that content that is hateful, that is divisive, that is polarizing, it`s easier to inspire people to anger than it is to other emotions.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Misinformation, angry content is enticing to people and --\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: Very enticing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: -- and keeps them on the platform.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: Yes. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has realized that if they change the algorithm to be safer, people will spend less time on the site, they`ll click on less ads, they`ll make less money.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Haugen says \uc2 Facebook\uc2  understood the danger to the 2020 election. So it turned on safety systems to reduce misinformation. But many of those changes, she says, were temporary.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: And as soon as the election was over, they turned them back off or they changed the settings back to where they were before to prioritize growth over safety. And that really feels like a betrayal of democracy to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  told CBS News that, quote: The growth of people or advertisers using \uc2 Facebook\uc2  means nothing if our services aren`t being used in ways that bring people closer together. That`s why we are investing so much in security that it impacts our bottom line, including the 40,000 people working on safety and security at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and our investment of $13 billion since 2016.\par \par \uc2 The "60 Minutes" report also focused on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  which is owned by \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Haugen says company research shows the app can actually damage the mental and physical well-being of teens, in particular teen girls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: One of the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  internal studies that you found talks about how \uc2 Instagram\uc2  harms teenage girls.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: One study says 13.5 percent of teen girls say \uc2 Instagram\uc2  makes thoughts of suicide worse. Seventeen percent of teen girls say \uc2 Instagram\uc2  makes eating disorders worse.\par \par \uc2 HAUGEN: And what`s super tragic is Facebook`s own research says as these young women begin to consume this eating disorder content, they get more and more depressed, and it actually makes them use the app more. And so they end up in this feedback cycle where they hate their bodies more and more. Facebook`s own research says it is not just that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is dangerous for teenagers, that it harms teenagers, it is that it is distinctly worse than other forms of social media.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can read Facebook`s entire response to "60 Minutes" on our website, CBSNews.com. It`s a powerful report.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , as part of its response to "60 Minutes", says it actually welcomes regulation from Congress. I think there`s reason to take them at face value on that because they`re a race for attention with a lot of competitors. If there are rules for everybody to follow, they`ll follow them, too. But in the meantime, they`re being criticized and asked essentially to make less money, and any company that has investors is not going to voluntarily do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seems counterintuitive when you hear what she was saying, that they would welcome regulation. How -- what was she like -- what was her position on \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, this is interesting. She says Mark -- she`s empathetic toward Mark. She doesn`t think Mark was trying to create a product that hurts America or any other society. But he`s running a business, and decisions have been made that have led to bad outcomes.\par \par \uc2 So I think everybody agrees regulation needs to come, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  does, Congress does, she does. The question is, what should that regulation be?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, Zuckerberg, Mark and the company, seem to be trying to tap it down, but it doesn`t seem to be enough based on what she`s saying. Or --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t -- and by their own research, I mean, like one of their reports said that they estimate they get 3 percent to 5 percent of hate speech off the platform. They also said they get 6/10 of 1 percent of violence and incitement off the platform.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Negativity, hate speech, misinformation, it all creates conversation. Conversation creates engagement, engagement creates revenue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It dials down some revenue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well put. Well put.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, some things are bigger than revenue.\par \par \uc2 An active volcano off the coast of North Africa is getting more dangerous this morning. Reports from La Palma in Spain`s Canary Islands say one side of the volcano has just collapsed. Spain`s prime minister has pledged nearly $240 million in aid, insists that the island is still safe for tourists.\par \par \uc2 Dan Johnson from our partners at the \uc2 BBC\uc2  is on La Palma right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAN JOHNSON: Incredibly, this eruption is now into a third week, and it keeps getting stronger. The volcano is producing even more lava with even more force. That`s why all that lava, all that ash is pouring up into the sky higher and higher, and that means more of a risk to a bigger area.\par \par \uc2 The potential for more people to be evacuated on top of the 6,000 or so who have been out of their homes for a fortnight now.\par \par \uc2 I was talking to the director of the volcano institute who says he expects this eruption to continue for at least another ten days, potentially another two months. Even then when the volcanic eruption stops, when the lava stops flowing, that`s not the end of the story. He said it could take years to recover from this because there are vast lava trails right across the landscape here.\par \par \uc2 There are people still living here right in the shadow of the volcano. Some of them saying they`ve had enough now after a fortnight of that thing thundering, rumbling right through their lives day and night. Some have had enough. They can`t get sleep. They want to leave. Some are making the decision to get to safer places.\par \par \uc2 Then I`ve spoken to people who live with that volcano effectively in their back yard and who say, oh, as long as the authorities will let me, I`m going to stay, I`m going to see it out. The unanswered question still is when that eruption will actually cease, when the lava will stop flowing, and when the destruction will finally stop.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, this is Dan Johnson, \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , La Palma.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, shocking new examples of the way rich people hide their money. We`ll look at the Pandora Papers that identify hundreds of prominent tax shelter clients.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder -- you can always catch this morning`s news by subscribing to our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNING`s on-the-go podcast. Hear the top stories in less than 20 minutes. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A major investigation reveals how hundreds of powerful people including current and former world leaders hide gigantic sums of money to avoid paying taxes.\par \par \uc2 The Pandora Papers report led by multiple news organizations and the \uc2 international consortium of investigative journalists\uc2  identifies more than 330 politician and billionaires using offshore tax havens to shield money and assets, assets like yachts, artwork, also shell companies that buy and sell real estate.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  says that these kinds of tax schemes keep up to $600 billion a year in revenue from governments, that`s money that should be easing the burden taxes on the general public.\par \par \uc2 Now, historically, countries like the Bahamas and the British Virgin Islands have been used for these purposes, but that`s changing. Now places like South Dakota and Nevada are helping the rich shield their money by passing lenient laws and promising those people secrecy.\par \par \uc2 The report says that assets in South Dakota-based trusts have quadrupled in the past decade to $360 billion. Now this is just the beginning of coverage on this. More revelations are expected later this week.\par \par \uc2 And, guys, one of the big questions coming out of it already is, how come there are not more American billionaires and multibillionaires on the list? One distressing theory is, well, those guys already pay so little in U.S. taxes under U.S. Law, they don`t need to ship it offshore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, this is indeed opening up Pandora`s box. The question is, are they bending the rules or breaking them?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the companies that do this for high net worth individuals in statements to journalist are saying things like, look, we follow all applicable laws.\par \par \uc2 And that`s likely true. It`s not that this is illegal from a corporate side of things, but it might be unsavory. And I think it`s fair to say, it is unsavory for the general public.\par \par \uc2 If