: So cool. Your mom is a teacher. Dave Grohl`s mom is a teacher. What would they do -- if we had video like that back in the day, I would have gotten away with so much --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Dave Grohl, we`ve got an actual rock star in our Progressive green room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Real rock star.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: His name is Dave Grohl. He`ll be talking about his new memoir first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWSCAST AND WEATHER FORECAST)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ************* (08:00am ET)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. I`m Gayle King. Coming up this hour, Dave Grohl. He`s got stories. He`s here for his first TV interview about his new memoir.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil, we`re talking Hispanic Heritage Month with Rita Moreno, her fearless take on facing Hollywood racism.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Big Brother has its first black winner, Xavier Prather. He`ll join us and tell us exactly how he did it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear he had a strategy. But first, here`s today`s eye opener at 8. A catastrophe is unfolding off of Southern California. More than 100,000 gallons of oil have leaked into the ocean. Crews are racing to stop the spill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is one of the most important stretches of coastline along Orange County and possibly all of Southern California.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Biden is hitting the road to promote his ambitious agenda. But Democrats are still fighting over how to get it done.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Normally here in Washington work is slowed down because of fights between Democrats and Republicans over big legislation. Right now. It`s because of fighting amongst Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is getting closer to a big decision on whether the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine should be authorized for kids under 12.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, how soon can we see the first shots in the arms for younger kids?\par \par \uc2 DAVID AGUS: Well, the answer is not soon enough. 28 million kids, 9 percent of the U.S. population in that group. You have to move.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court is back in session today on the docket, cases involving gun rights, religion and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  win the right to face the \uc2 Red Sox\uc2  for the wild card, it all came down to this.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it when big men act like little kids and they are so happy. Dave Grohl, you know that feeling? Look, who we just found hanging in the greenroom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at him, Dave Grohl.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL, MUSICIAN: Nothing better to do, come and hang out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you had something better to do. But we were better than that. Thank you, Dave. Dave is here for his first TV interview. Why? Because he`s revealing a new memoir. It`s called The Storyteller, Tales of Life and Music. It`s a collection of moments from childhood to fatherhood, what a life this guy has had. It covers life on the road and playing alongside rock and roll`s greatest musicians like \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2 . Grohl is a 16 time Grammy Award winner. He`s a member of the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 He was a drummer for the band, Nirvana. We know that. And he`s lead singer of our executive producer`s favorite rock band, that`s new in the copy. But Dave, that is not why you are here because Shauna Thomas loves you. You`re here because you are Dave Grohl. And this is a fantastic book. So, welcome, welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is the thing. I was reading the book and I`m thinking if it wasn`t for COVID A, we wouldn`t have a book. And two, if it wasn`t for Sandy, who broke your heart when you were in high school, you might not have been a famous musician. True?\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Well--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Give the backstory.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: OK, so I started playing music when I was like 10 years old. And start playing guitar. I learned from Beatles records and Beatles song books and playing along to the albums and stuff. And when I was in seventh grade, first day of school, I see this girl, Sandy, she`s so beautiful. And it was like - it was the first time my heart had done that. I need to make her mine. But I was such a skinny, nerdy little dude that - I was not George Clooney. And so, I asked her to go steady with me and we like, walk through the halls and hold hands.\par \par \uc2 After about a week, she`s like, I`m new here and I don`t want to tied down, and my heart for the first time really just kind of broke. And that night, I had this dream that I was in a band and I was in an arena and I was doing a guitar solo and the audience is like, wow, and I looked down and I saw her in the front row and she was crying. This was my dream.\par \par \uc2 And it was that moment I woke up and I thought OK, I think maybe this is how it works like now that I can channel my heartbreak into my instrument. And like I was inspired to write to like to get rid of the pain of losing Sandy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I think it`s good that worked out because you also talk about you were not a good student. You include your report card here that says Dave G rushes to - David G rushes to work inaccurate, messy, does not turn in much of the required work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s all right.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: I mean--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On the flip side, though.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And your point is. But you knew at a very early age that music, you said you see life in music, you see moments in music, and this book is certainly an example of that.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Well, my mother was a public school teacher for 35 years and she was really supportive of me as a musician. I think she understood that this was my passion. This is the one thing I was putting all my energy into. Even as my grades were slipping. She tried to do - she did her best to try to keep me like in the books and keep me doing with it. But there was nothing that was going to get in between me and my instrument. And she forever--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She got that--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Facilitated--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dad not so much.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Well, my dad was a conservative Republican speech writer and journalist. And he was also a classically trained musician. So, he was a flutist. So, I think we had two different ideas of what music was and how to do it. But I was born with his DNA. And so, I`ve got - so he`s in here. And so, we kind of went like this for a while, until later on our life, we both became like great friends and had a great appreciation for each other.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, by the time you`re 18, you`re out of the house, you`re on the road with Scream, you were two high school buddies, and you`re out on the West Coast, and you get an invitation to try out with this band Nirvana, which had already had an album out, they were a big deal, at least among musicians. But going to the tryout means leaving your two best buddies who you went on this adventure with, that`s a really hard choice for people.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You call your mother. What advice did she give--?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, mom comes--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: I mean, she is a constant in this book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This changed your life, this decision.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: It did. I didn`t know what to do, because I was at a crossroads. And it`s those times in life where those decisions are scary, because you have to live with the consequences of that decision. But what - when I asked my mother, I said, what should I do? She said, sometimes in life, you have to do what`s best for yourself. And I think she had done that before in her life. Although, she was the most like altruistic, most giving generous person I`d ever met. She was telling me that sometimes you have to do what`s best for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: And if that was, if that was good enough for her, then--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was very good advice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to go back to your younger days, because you said you went to your first punk rock show when you were young. And here`s the quote I love. I`d been baptized by spit, sweat, and broken glass. What was that moment like?\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Well, so when I was a kid, like in my bedroom, I had these posters on my walls. I had like Led Zeppelin posters, and I had kiss posters. And it was all like, lasers, dragons and smoke machines. So that`s what I thought rock and roll was, but I`d never been to a concert. So, I went to this punk rock show when I was 13 years old in Chicago. And I walked in and it was just a small room with maybe a couple lights and there was a band they were called Naked Raygun with a couple of amps on stage. And they countered into the song was like one, two, and the place just exploded.\par \par \uc2 And it was so raw, and it was so real that I thought, this is rock and roll. And they only know four chords. I only know four chords, so I could do this too. And that was the first day of the rest of my life, I was like- -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were the ultimate dad girl because you have a girl dad because you`ve got three daughters.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here they are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s Harper, there`s Violet, Ophelia, who goes by the name Fifi. I think this is so cute. One of the most touching stories is you and Violet, because Violet is playing at her school. You all decide to do Blackbird and you said you were more nervous doing that with her than anything would surprise me because you are Dave Grohl. Why--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Well, here`s the thing. I mean, over the years, you hit these milestones where it`s like, Ok, now you`re playing arenas. Now you`re playing stadium. Now you`re on Saturday Night Live, these big, big moments. Academy Awards, huge moments.\par \par \uc2 But when your daughter wants to get on stage for the first time and sing a song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: And you`re doing Blackbird by The Beatles. It`s like that was the most nervous I`ve ever been in my entire life, because I was there to back her up in front of her teachers and her friends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And everybody`s watching.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Oh, my God. And then, so we do that at the talent show, then I get a call to do it on the Academy Awards, and I was nervous about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Violet says, you have to do it. I love how you wrote about Kurt Cobain. Was that hard for you? It must have been hard for you.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Absolutely. That was the last chapter I wrote. I had waited for a year to write that because I was afraid to write it. And yes, I mean over the years, I`ve lost more than a few people that I`ve loved and been closed to. It`s hard to put that into words rather than just kind of write about what happened, it`s like I wrote about the emotional process of dealing with loss and mourning and how it`s a lifetime of feeling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the connection that you`ve made. But you tell a great story. I know we`re going, John, but you tell a great story where you said you were trying to decide what to do after his death. You go to Ireland, you`re in a remote area. A guy is hitchhiking guys. And he`s wearing a--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: He`s wearing a Kurt Cobain t-shirt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kurt Cobain t-shirt. You have a lot of stories--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: And I looked at him--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of stories--\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: You know what, I`ve got to get home and start over--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The book--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The book - yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a storyteller. Something tells me there`s going to be a part two, just throwing it out there.\par \par \uc2 DAVE GROHL: Every day is a story to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you will, Dave Grohl. The Storyteller, Tales of Life and Music comes out tomorrow. Wherever you like to buy your books. Right now, it is what time - Brian, 8:10. Time to - I could look at my watch. 8:10 time to check--\par \par \uc2 MIRI MARSHALL: Notice some clouds out there as we start the week along with the chance for showers and a few storms by the afternoon. Temperatures 76 to 81 degrees. Now after about 4 o`clock or so, you notice some scattered showers and storms breaking out across the area that last us through the night.\par \par \uc2 And for the next several days, we`ll keep a rain chance in the forecast. Upper 70s on Tuesday, lower to mid-70s for the rest of the week with those rain chances sticking around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we`re sharing more of our exclusive conversation with R. Kelly survivor Azriel Clary. When we first spoke with Azriel back in 2019, she was just 21 years old, and she had lived with R. Kelly for almost five years. Later on, she told us she realized her relationship was abusive, very abusive. She testified against R. Kelly during his federal racketeering and sex trafficking trial right here in New York. He was found guilty on all charges and now could face life in prison.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News special, we`re calling it Love Doesn`t Hurt. That`s a line that Azriel told us. She`s the R. Kelly survivor. And she speaks, she has more of our - we have more of our recent conversation with her, it is out today on our \uc2 Viacom\uc2  streaming platform Paramount Plus. Here`s Clary talking about finally leaving R. Kelly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AZRIEL CLARY, R. KELLY SURVIVOR: If you are leaving something traumatizing more than you want to breathe, you need support. You need foundation that feels loved and that that loves you. And so, I`m very happy that I had that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you think anybody could have said or done to help you leave the situation sooner?\par \par \uc2 AZRIEL CLARY: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 AZRIEL CLARY: Nothing. I feel like that is something that I would have had to had woken up from myself, something that I would have had to realize on myself. And a lot of people don`t realize with victims, the more you try to help them, the more it upsets them sometimes, because this is all they know. This is the only person that they have been comfortable around. So, the more you try to separate that, the more they only run to what they know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: R. Kelly`s high profile trial brought new attention to domestic violence in this country. On average, nearly one in three women experienced domestic violence. That`s a very high number. And that`s according to the National Domestic Violence Hotline. Thema Bryant is a clinical psychologist. She`s also the Director of the Culture and Trauma Research Laboratory at Pepperdine University.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you Ms. Bryant.\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT, CLINICAL PSYCHOLOGIST: Good morning. Thank you so much--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad--\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: For having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad you`re here. I was on a plane this weekend. And three different people stopped me and said, you know she could have left if she wanted to. He wasn`t physically holding her. She was there for her career. She enjoyed being with him. And it`s so hard to try to explain to people who haven`t been in the situation. What do you say to - many people feel this way?\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: And first, let me say this is why it`s so important, you`re doing this segment. Because of the victim blaming that is so pervasive in society. People say, why didn`t you say anything? And then when people speak up, they find reasons about why you did it wrong, and why you should have done it differently. So, there are many reasons that make it hard to leave. And one of the languages sometimes that we use is, why didn`t you escape.\par \par \uc2 If you ask - if you used the word escape, instead of just leave, you realize how big you`re asking someone to push back on someone who has been really controlling their mind. So, one of the big factors is fear. And we know that you increase the risk for death, when you try to leave that abuser are more likely to try to actually take your life or increase the violence when you are trying to escape and there is a greater chance of homicide.\par \par \uc2 Not only is there the fear of death, but the fear of, can I make it on my own, that this person has undermined your self-esteem and so you really feel uncertain and many people have turned away from you over the years, people tried to help you. They gave up on you. So then, it`s I`m going to leave this person and go where.\par \par \uc2 Another big factor that makes it hard to escape is love. And what is the message we always tell people is nobody`s perfect, relationships aren`t easy. You have to stick to it. These days, people walk away too soon. So, there is this double message that if you love someone, you`re going to be ride or die, you`re going to stay with them no matter what, you`re going to help them through it. And the love actually also keeps hope alive, right? Because with the cycle of violence, usually people are not terrible every day all day.\par \par \uc2 And so, each time someone has that honeymoon phase where they are being kinder, you believe they`re changing, things are going to be better this time. And then I just say there`s also the cultural and religious pressures. A lot of people will say, if they are married, I made a covenant with God, right? And I promise--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Death do us apart.\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Yes, for better or for worse. So culturally, religiously, we have to start sending a different message that fear is not love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I want to talk about the signs of domestic abuse because when people are hopeful or they`re in love, they may not realize that what they`re experiencing is not normal and not OK, because in the context of their experience, it feels like it is--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she said, it was normalized too, to your point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, what is the line where it`s not just relationships are hard, but it`s abuse.\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Yes. So, we want to pay attention to physical violence, sexual violence and psychological violence. Usually, the physical and sexual people will notice, but I`ll say even the physical at first people can minimize like they`ll say, it wasn`t a punch, it was a push or it was a grab or they restrained me, it was open hands--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or I shouldn`t have said it--\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Right--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or I shouldn`t have said that--\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Yes. So, the blaming, because sometimes even the abuser will say, I wish you had made me do that. Right? I didn`t want to do that. So, any form of physical violence is abuse, sexual violence, and we don`t talk about this within relationships. Actually, within this nation, initially, sexual assault within marriage was not a crime. So, we had to really make the case that you still have ownership of your body, because people say well, you`re with them so then you agree to it.\par \par \uc2 But the big piece is also the psychological. It is the verbal, it`s the manipulation motion--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Emotional--\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Yes. And it undermines your self-worth and your self-value. So then, you have the public on top of that saying you`re stupid. you`re out for money and so it can really crush you. And--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sorry, we`re going to have to leave it there. You gave us some--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Bryant, this is very clarifying--\par \par \uc2 THEMA BRYANT: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can watch the CBS News special Love Doesn`t Hurt. R. Kelly survivor speaks, it`s tonight on Paramount Plus.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings". I like those words. I need someone to love me. Everybody does. It`s time to bring in some of the stories that we call - were in pole position, Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Alright. So when I was a kid, loved baseball loved playing baseball and the play by play announcers were kind of the voice of my dreams because when you`re playing in the front yard, with your buddy down the street and you hit a homerun, you hear that professional play by play in your head.\par \par \uc2 That all came back to me on Saturday when I heard an epic play by play call by Seattle Mariners announcer Dave Samson. Before we play it I just got to set it up by saying the Mariners have not made the playoffs in 20 years they needed to win this game in