e and don`t have to worry about it falling out or so. I can play with it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is that is that in --?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it`s a pocket inside of his jersey that they sew in there. So he gets to place that inside his jersey and play with his dad. That`s a team doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Remember it was somebody on the team, one of the trainers who was out there at 1:45 in the morning, it found the necklace and the first place. I love this story. I love this story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Awesome stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My talk on the table it`s about a photo that I saw on the news. I`m so craving these kinds of stories. It`s a hero with a very big heart. He`s in Billings, Montana. His name is remembering the same he`s a firefighter Ryan Benton. We love you, Ryan Benton.\par \par \uc2 Reading a book to a little girl was just involved in a car crash. So here`s Benton sitting on the curb with a girl using a flashlight to see the book. A colleague said he just took it upon himself to make this little girl feel safe after this accident.\par \par \uc2 This happened last week as firefighters cleared the car. Someone posted the photo on the Billings firefighters \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page and the caption says this. It`s the little things in that the truth. Benton`s wife, her name is Kay. She posted a comment saying makes me so proud when I get to see pictures like this.\par \par \uc2 According to fellow firefighters, they say Benton is a really humble guy. Listen to this. He didn`t want to talk to the media about that because he says he was just doing his job. So I`m thinking if you`re in Billings, Montana, you know Ryan Benton he deserves a special mommy hug. I was so touched that he would just take the time to do it.\par \par \uc2 He`s not doing it for a photo op he didn`t know someone who was even was - someone was even taking a picture of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For all the firefighters around the country if you can go drop off some food and say hi. Yes, come away honk the horn because they are true heroes. That`s absolutely yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is character right? Character is what you do when you think no one`s looking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what Mr. Benton was doing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Perfectly said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Alright, I had a conversation with Hollywood Legend Rita Moreno on a subject she knows very well. Surveys show one quarter of U.S. movie goers are Hispanic or Latino but actors from those groups have played only about 2 percent of leading roles in the past 13 years that`s according to a recent study.\par \par \uc2 Moreno who is an Oscar Emmy Grammy and Tony winner spoke to us as part of our coverage of Hispanic Heritage Month. And she told Lilia Luciano, what she faced as an actress and talked about the way forward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Nearly 60 years after becoming the first Latina to win an Oscar.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rita Marino --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Rita Moreno is still fighting to have her voice heard in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO, PUERTO RICAN ACTRESS: Little Puerto Rican girl who nobody thought much of at MGM with a contract.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): In her documentary, she looks back at her legendary career but also at the toll of being repeatedly typecast.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: I already speak English. It took a name the universal ethnic accent because I never knew because nobody cared what to do with these ethnic parts.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): We caught up with the icon at the University of California Berkeley Botanical Garden.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: I was doing American Indian girls.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How do you know my name?\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: With this kind of slightly Puerto Rican accent because nobody knows. Like nobody noticed.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Rita Moreno`s Oscar winning performance wasn`t enough to catapult her career. So she turned to a top female talent agent for help.\par \par \uc2 MORENO: I said, well, I have a dilemma. I can`t seem to get any work other than some offers of gang movies and doing people with accents, yet again, stared at dark makeup stereotypes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): The agent wouldn`t take her as a client.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: As she was very pleased, very direct. And he said, you don`t have what it takes to be a star and I only handle stars. I don`t know how to burst into tears then. But I guess I had at that point in my life, just heard so many terrible things about me and to me that I just hung on.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Moreno didn`t make another movie for the next seven years before returning to the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: You said the kids were going to have a different mother. Who is she?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): In film, Broadway and television. She also played over the top mother Lydia in the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reboot of Norman Lear`s comedy "One day at a time". The reimagined version centered around a Cuban American family inspired by Producer and Co-Showrunner Gloria Calderon Kellett.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA CALDERON KELLETT, PRODUCER, ONE DAY AT A TIME: I come from a line of beautiful hardworking people who came here and lived the American dream. I want to see them celebrated.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Calderon Kellett arrived in Hollywood around 20 years ago, but she quickly found herself only landing auditions for this same negative stereotypes and Moreno turned down decades earlier.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (on camera): Is that why you decided to write the stories?\par \par \uc2 GLORIA CALDERON KELLETT: That is exactly why. I mean you can how many times can you go in and be like Julie put the gun down. And I just tried to get my hands on everything I could consume that would teach me how to do this job and found my way through it so that I could be holding the pen.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Most recently Calderon Kellett wrote and created with love a new series out on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in December.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Welcome to the Diaz house.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice over): Where Latinos take the lead from the writer`s room to the cast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The de Atlantic`s New Year`s Eve very --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (on camera): What does Hollywood need to do to enable and empower people like you to tell more in different stories?\par \par \uc2 GLORIA CALDERON KELLETT: Well, we need to hire us. First of all right? We really need to be in actual positions of power. I think we need more executives. If you want to make stuff about us we were delighted, but we`re here to make sure that you`re doing it in a responsible way.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO (voice over): For Moreno, the journey has come full circle. This December, she`ll appear in Steven Spielberg`s remake of West Side Story as both an actress and an executive producer.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: It was done with love and profound respect for the Hispanic community.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (on camera): They`re certainly more Latino representation.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: Damn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think there`s a big effort. And I don`t want to completely dismiss the steps that Hollywood has made. But you know what? It has a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wear your nationality like a flag, be proud of that.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA CALDERON KELLETT: I mean she really fought the fight. She really walked so we could run. She did.\par \par \uc2 RITA MORENO: That means everything to me, everything to me. I want always to be someone that they can respect and look up to. Who tried? I fought the good fight that`s for damn sure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow! She`s still fighting. I was in Los Angeles Thursday night for the Variety Power Women Award and Rita Marino got one of the awards. Yep. And during her speech, she said, continue to keep fires burning for those who aspire be vigilant.\par \par \uc2 There I was presenting an award to Katy Perry, who also got one but Rita Moreno who`s 89 all of them got a standing ovation. And she just looks so good. She`s still so full of light and still has so much more to do she says.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, brown matters --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: --like Hollywood should be a direct reflection of society. And oftentimes it`s not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and it`s in their interest to be a direct reflection. They`re going to make more money that way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, that American Masters documentary Rita Moreno, just a girl who decided to go for premieres tomorrow night on \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now coming up, Xavier Prather tells us how he became "Big Brother`s" first black winner and its 21 year history. But first, it`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And - vote goes to congratulations it is Xavier. You are the winner of Big Brother.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is the moment that Xavier Prather made reality TV history as the first black winner of Big Brother here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . He wants $750,000 when he ended the show last week with a big win.\par \par \uc2 Prather and five other black house guests formed a group called "The Cookout Alliance", they worked together to see a black contest to win for the first time in the show`s 21 year history. Let`s take a look at how that alliance was created in secret.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The game brought us here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Absolutely. It also changed the culture. Think about the girl which is the whole \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  like it`s important for us to be here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And stick together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do we agree that this is going to be called "The Cookout"?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s what the culture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You got to do it for the culture. Everybody wants to be invited to "The Cookout".\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER, FIRST BIG BROTHER WINNER: I wasn`t invited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So true.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it is Xavier is joining us right now. Good morning. First off, congratulations!\par \par \uc2 PRATHER: Thank you and good morning and thank you guys for having me. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve had a few days to kind of process this has it set in yet that you are $750,000 richer?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Still not quite yet. Still haven`t seen it yet. But it`s starting to set in a little bit more the more you know press. I do the more interviews I do, the more I`m starting to set in what we accomplish.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So "the we" in that sentence, what was the significance of "The Cookout Alliance" and how did you block out the other contestants?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: The significance of "The Cookout Alliance" is that six individuals who had the same goal and vision in mind to help Big Brother crowned its first black winner came together, put aside any differences that they had to achieve the same goal and throughout the season, that`s all we did.\par \par \uc2 You know, we were strategizing we were scheming against the other houseguests. Not necessarily because they weren`t black but simply because that`s what you`re doing Big Brother, you form an alliance and then your goal is to get anybody who`s outside of that alliance out of the house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and you want to be the last man standing.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this is the thing you`re an attorney by trade.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you didn`t want people to know - what did you tell them that you did?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: I told him that I was a model and a bar attender.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Model and bar attender.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: The things that I actually do the bartending I do pretty infrequently, but the modeling I do part time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why wouldn`t you want people to know you`re an attorney?\par \par \uc2 PRATHER: Just to do what I could to minimize my threat level. I felt like if they knew I was an attorney g XAVIER oing into the game, I probably would have been one of the first ones out. So I felt like by keeping that under wraps, he gave me the best chance to do it for what I came to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What does your success and also being an attorney mean for representation of a black man in reality TV?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: I think it`s everything. The goal for this season was representation to show little black boys and little black girls like hey, to be successful in society you don`t have to, you know, be very good, a very, very successful athlete or like some outstanding entertainer you can be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or ratchet --\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: --exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t have to beat each other up. Yes.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: You could to be an upstanding citizen, while also making sure you`re staying true to your blackness and I think that`s something that was important for American society to see not just in myself, but the five other members of "The Cookout" as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But as you know, I love the - I love "The Cookout Alliance" because you`re right Nate everybody wants to go to "The Cookout". But there`s of course always chatter online as there always is the haters are coming for you saying but isn`t this reverse racism. What is your response to that?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: My response to that would be this wasn`t reverse racism. This was simply black unity. This is five - six black people coming together with a goal in mind. We weren`t targeting people necessarily outside of our race in the context of the game we came together and formed an alliance.\par \par \uc2 And in this game your goal is to get out people outside of that alliance and it was purely that. We weren`t trying to establish any type of black superiority that`s not what we were doing. We were simply playing the game and that`s it we did pretty well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`d say you engage in strategic that paid off. I was surprised to hear there never been a black winner in 21 seasons.\par \par \uc2 P XAVIER RATHER: The closest well and to clarify on the Celebrity Big Brother edition - Braxton was the first black winner and then on Big Brother Canada - Carter Newman was a black winner as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m talking about this game.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: This game yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t get --\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: On season three Daniel Ray has made it to the final two chairs and that`s the furthest a black person is going on the show. So to have six by people make it to the final six was incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was it like playing the game being isolated for how many days?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: 85 days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 85 days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow! You just got out of that.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Yes, so you know, life`s a little different.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ll be acclimating.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Catching up on a few things --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ll be friends with it - because you didn`t know any of these guys before.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: No, not at all. But yes, absolutely I think what we were able to accomplish we`re all going to be bonded for life. And yes, those are my brothers and my sisters no matter what happens in life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So now there`s a "Cookout" keep going?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Absolutely OK go sticking together. It seems like all y`all want an invite.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I do want you -- and guess who is paying? You are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Alright Nate, you paid it. Thank you very much and congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We hope you get that money soon. Pay this man.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER PRATHER: Direct deposit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On today`s "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings Podcast" we`ll talk with the Executive Producer and Head Writer of Impeachment American Crime Story. Her name is Sarah Burgess. She tells us why they decided to revisit the Monica Lewinsky Bill Clinton Affair 25 years later in the latest series of that FX series. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can watch the "CBS News Special" it`s called "Love Doesn`t Hurt" R. Kelly survivors speaks. Later today on the \uc2 ViacomCBS\uc2  streaming platform Paramount plus we call it that because that`s something that she said to us during the interview that love doesn`t hurt.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so much more than her talking about R. Kelly it`s really more about how she became involved. What was her parents` reaction? What about the other young woman we interviewed? That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow right here on "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings".\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eha40003y\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Begins A New Term\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Jan Crawford, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court begins a new term today. Court will be held in person for the first time since March of last year. The justices have a very controversial dock in front of them, including cases that challenge a longstanding abortion ruling. Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with a look at what`s ahead. Jan, good morning to you. First Monday in October. That`s what it means, court`s in session. Hello to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (CBS News Chief Legal Correspondent): Yeah. You know, there`s kind of-- there`s kind of like this first day of school vibe up here today, Gayle. And let me tell you, a lot has happened since the court last heard arguments in person. We`ve got a new justice, Amy Coney Barrett, and that`s really helped shift the court to the right. They are taking up a host of contentious social issues--they`ve got gun rights, religious rights, and the most divisive of all, abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): This is what democracy looks like.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Over the weekend, protesters left no doubt what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): My choice. My choice.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The most significant challenge to Roe v. Wade in a generation, a case asking the justices to uphold Mississippi`s ban on abortions after fifteen weeks.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER: This is the only clinic in-- in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Shannon Brewer is director of the clinic in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER: I`ve been here through a lot of different laws that we`ve fought, but this one-- this is the one that I can honestly say worries me more than any of them.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The justices will hear arguments in the case in December. With Justice Amy Coney Barrett replacing liberal icon \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , abortion opponents hope this court could finally overturn Roe, the 1973 case that said abortion was a constitutional right. Some states across the country already are preparing for the ruling, with mostly conservative states ready to outlaw or greatly restrict abortion if the court sides with Mississippi while some liberal ones are passing laws to guarantee that in their states abortion still would be available. The case comes with the court already in the political spotlight. Polls show public opinion of the court has declined. And there are other contentious cases that surely will divide the justices. In November, the court takes up a major gun rights case challenging a New York law that limits who can carry a concealed weapon for self-defense. It also will decide a religious rights case, whether a tuition assistance program in Maine that bans state money from going to religious schools amounts to discrimination.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now this week, the court is going to be one justice short, on the bench anyway. Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  tested positive last week for the coronavirus so he`s going to be working from home today, participating remotely. And then, you know, of course the building, of course not taking any chances. The building is still closed to the public. Most members of the media won`t be able to attend, but you can listen to these arguments on a live stream through the court`s website. Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are approaching a pivotal moment in the COVID fight. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory panel meets later this month to decide if the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine should get emergency approval for children aged five to eleven. This decision could affect more than twenty-eight million American kids. CBS News medical contributor Doctor David Agus, he joins us right now in studio. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS (CBS News Medical Contributor/CEO, Ellison Institute for Transformative Medicine of USC): It`s great to be here. I love the new studio.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. We do, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: It`s so nice to see you all in person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, it`s good to have you. So, how soon can we see the first shots in the arms for younger kids?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Oh, the answer is not soon enough. And so later this month, they are going to meet, five through eleven, I`ve seen the data. It is safe, and they work. So really the hope is, you know, towards Halloween or shortly thereafter we can start vaccinating kids. So by Thanksgiving, they may have received two dose and we can have a safe Thanksgiving. We want to push as quickly as possible. Twenty-eight million kids, nine percent of the U.S. population in that group. We have to move.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, you mentioned a safe Thanksgiving which speaks to the potential impact of vaccinating kids. Other than Thanksgiving, big picture, what impact could this have?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Well, we`re now getting mandates, right? And we`re seeing they are working. You know, when companies say you have to have a vaccine to come to work, we`re seeing the numbers go up pretty dramatically within companies, within schools. State of California said you need a vaccine to go to school. So, we will get out of this mess and get over Delta when we can increase the vaccination rate. And we are getting there. We add in to them these twenty-five million kids. We`re going to be much, much closer. So I feel good about, you know, February, March, April as we get to there, if we can really come through on these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we talk about boosters? I`m getting my booster today at high noon. I can`t wait. I can`t wait to go and get the shot. But--\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: I never thought I would see the day--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen-- me, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: --when Gayle King says--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is news.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: --I can`t wait until I can get the shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: This is--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you know I`m terrified of needles.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: You`re blowing my mind here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m terrified of needles. But this-- but it`s still only for certain groups of people who can get it. Do you think everybody should be getting the booster now, and what`s the downside to getting the booster?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Well, that`s the key thing is that, you know, the RNA vaccines you lose six percent immunity per month. With J&J, the-- the-- the immunity was not as high when they were given the RNA vaccine. So I do think forty and older we should be boosterizing, if there`s such a word, everybody. We`re going to see \uc2 FDA\uc2  meetings over the next ten days on the Moderna booster and the J&J booster. So, a lot more people will become eligible. But these criteria if you`re this age in these conditions, I think we have to make it simpler.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mentioned safe Thanksgiving. As we approach Thanksgiving, Christmas, even New Year`s, should we be hesitant about large gatherings?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Yes, we should be hesitant about large gatherings. As long as everyone is vaccinated--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hm.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: --and, you know, people of high risk can be tested--testing is still a big part of this--we can gather at families for Thanksgiving and Christmas. But you have to do it right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But people feel I`ve got the double shot and it`s still not working. What do you say to people that think I don`t want to get another-- more medicines, I don`t--\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --I don`t want anymore?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: You don`t have to. I mean, it is working. It is blocking most hospitalizations--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But--\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: --and deaths from COVID-19. But it`s not blocking all breakthroughs. You lose about six percent of immunity per month. So, a booster will give you protection against most breakthroughs. And it`s your option, but I do think if you`re higher risk, if you`re over sixty, you definitely want to be protected. It`s just not worth that risk. You want to step up and get that. Remember, it`s a new virus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. That`s what--\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: So, this is Delta virus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: So with the old virus, we probably wouldn`t have needed these boosters. But clearly, a new virus came and changed the game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. And you lose six percent a month, you said?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Six percent a month on a good day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. All right. Thank you, David Agus. Did I mention I`m getting mine at high noon today? I-- I really--\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Did I mention I`m proud of you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I-- I cannot wait. And I`m terrified of needles.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eha400041\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Begins Consequential New Term\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the Supreme Court began its new term. One that could be the most consequential in a generation with abortion rights topping the agenda. The justices met in person for the first time since the beginning of the pandemic. CBS` Jan Crawford is at the court for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (CBS News Chief Legal Correspondent): As the Supreme Court returned to the bench for its new session.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2  (recording): And the October 2021 term is now convened.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd protesting)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Protesters left no doubt what`s at stake. The most significant challenge to Roe v. Wade in a generation, a case asking the justices to uphold Mississippi`s ban on abortions after fifteen weeks. Shannon Brewer is director of the only abortion clinic in the state in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 If the court says, we`re overturning Roe v. Wade, it`s up to the states to decide what to do.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREWER: They would definitely ban abortion in Mississippi immediately. Yes, and several other states, too.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With Justice Amy Coney Barrett replacing liberal icon \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , abortion opponents hope that`s exactly what happens. Mostly conservative states are ready to outlaw or greatly restrict abortion if the court sides with Mississippi, while some liberal states have passed laws to guarantee that in their states abortions still would be available. The case to be argued in December comes with polls showing public opinion of the court has declined. And there are other contentious cases that surely will divide the justices, including gun rights and religious rights.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, the justices are all fully vaccinated. But Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  has some underlying health conditions. And so she wore a mask today on the bench. And Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  tested positive for COVID last week. He`s asymptomatic, but he participated remotely. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s going to be a busy year. Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211005eha4000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Gather In DC For 2021 Women`s March\b0\par\par\uc2 Jamie Yuccas, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Thousands of protesters marched at rallies across the country today, decrying Texas` tough new restrictions on most abortions. The biggest march was in Washington, DC, where demonstrators headed straight to the steps of the Supreme Court as justices gets set to hear a pivotal case on abortion rights. CBS` Nikole Killion was among the marchers today. Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (CBS News Congressional Correspondent): The Supreme Court will start a new term next week and will take up a case later this year that could test the bounds of Roe versus Wade, something demonstrators here want to defend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Crowd protesting)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Thousands descended on the nation`s capital for a rally on abortion justice. In the crowd, nine-year-old Alexis Brennan (ph).\par \par \uc2 Why? Why did you want to come?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS BRENNAN: Because I feel it`s important that women have rights.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd protesting)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: At the Supreme Court, riot police stood between protesters for and against abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL EICHHORN (Pro-Abortion Advocate): The restrictions that are being forced on women and are being discussed right now are unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 ANNA LULIS (Anti-Abortion Advocate): If we`re really marching for women`s rights, we shouldn`t exclude the preborn from that.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The face-off followed a day of demonstrations--\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): Abortion, any time, any reason.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: --that began near the White House.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Abortion is healthcare.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Nancy Traver (ph) says she had one in her twenties and supports a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 But how hard of a decision was that for you?\par \par \uc2 NANCY TRAVER: I never considered raising a child myself because I just couldn`t.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Around six hundred events were held around the country, from DC to Texas, to protest that state`s new heartbeat law, which bans most abortions at approximately six weeks, and to take a stand ahead of a critical case before the high court this fall, involving a similar ban in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (Michigan attorney General): We`re actually reaching a time and place where this is going to become a reality that abortion will be illegal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The court will hear that Mississippi case in December, and it will be watched very closely by both sides, given the implications it could have on Roe versus Wade. Jamie.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020211003eha20002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas Abortion Law Pushes Women To Clinics Elsewhere\b0\par\par\uc2 Jamie Yuccas, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: This week, the governor of Texas signed a bill putting more restrictions on abortion. The first legal actions also took place. A San Antonio doctor who admits he violated the law was sued twice. CBS` Janet Shamlian has more on the law`s impact on women.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT: Now, we`re about to make it law.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (CBS News Correspondent): The nation`s most restrictive abortion law--\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): Bans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --is forcing women to flee Texas--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Are you currently pregnant?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --in search of clinics like this one in Colorado that can barely meet demand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I am booked for the rest of this week.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: CBS News had rare access to the facility in Denver, where nearly half the patients are Texans.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN: It`s a six-week surgical with PO sedation.\par \par \uc2 When is the last time that you`ve seen an influx like this in this clinic to this degree?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN: We haven`t. We haven`t seen numbers like this ever.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Doctor Rebecca Cohen is the medical director.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN: An abortion can be painful, people can hurt. But this is different. We are seeing patients who are traumatized when they arrive.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Women like this thirty-four-year-old mother of three, who asked us to conceal her identity. She learned she was pregnant one day before the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 When you realized you could not get the procedure in Texas, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I cried. I cried all the way from when they told me through the counselor session. I was honestly-- I didn`t know what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Texas law bans almost ninety percent of abortions beginning at six weeks, before many women know they`re pregnant. Women have to cross state lines to get one--and they are. Colorado has no state mandated waiting period for the procedure. The woman we spoke with made the sixteen-hundred-mile round trip in one day.\par \par \uc2 So you just finished the procedure here--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --but you`re not finished.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: So after a procedure, you`re supposed to take it easy. I`m not going to get that option, because I`m going to have to walk through the airport and everything else to get back home.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She says she has an irregular heartbeat, which could make the pregnancy risky.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: How far along are you?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This Shreveport, Louisiana clinic is just twenty miles from the Texas border. Even with a twenty-four-hour state mandated waiting period, appointments are hard to come by.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Hope Medical.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Kathleen Pittman runs the Hope clinic.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN PITTMAN: They`re literally begging to get in and be seen as quickly as possible. Women should not be going through this. I mean, it`s unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Recently, there was up to a five-week wait to get an appointment at this clinic. Previously, women could get in just a couple of days. And while other Southern states are look to follow Texas`s lead on abortion, the greatest challenge to Roe v Wade comes December 1st, when the Supreme Court hears a Mississippi case.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, Shreveport.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Thank you, Janet.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020210926eh9p000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 U.S. Doubling Purchase Of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  Vaccine; Relentless Diplomacy: Pres. Biden Attempts To Reassure Allies In UN Speech; House Passes Bill To Avert Shutdown; Margaret Brennan On Pres. Biden`s Foreign Policy Message; Haitians Migrants Processed: AP: Biden Administration Allowing Some Asylum Claims To Proceed; \uc2 FDA\uc2  Decision On Boosters Likely Today; Kentucky`s COVID Crisis; Gabby Petito`s Body Identified; Lava Forces Thousands To Evacuate; TX Abortion Law Impact; Special Police Officer Shot & Killed In SE DC\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Manuel Bojorquez,  Margaret Brennan, Chester Lampkin, Jericka Duncan, Weijia Jiang, Nancy  Chen, Vladimir Duthiers, Tony Perkins, Dan Johnson \par \uc2 13,885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, \uc2 CBS\uc2  HOST: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, \uc2 CBS\uc2  HOST: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON, \uc2 CBS\uc2  HOST: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 KING: We are here ready to go to today`s Eye Opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Ending this pandemic, addressing the climate crisis, managing the shifts in global power dynamic.\par \par \uc2 KING: President Biden makes big promises to our allies but faces a new challenge in Congress.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For the first time President Biden is responding to the chaos along the Texas border.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will get it under control.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Authorities have confirmed human remains found in northern Wyoming are those of Gabby Petito.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Cause of death is homicide.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Some parents want to ignore warnings and vaccinate their younger kids now. Dr. John weighs in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you been tempted to ask your pediatrician? Come on. She`s 11 and a half.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have been absolutely tempted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Evacuations continued after a volcano eruption and earthquake hit the Canary Islands of Spain.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A magnitude 5.8 earthquake caused damage in Melbourne, Australia, there were no reports of serious injury.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: MMA fighter Conor McGregor, throwing out the first pitch.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And Drew Barrymore is here to share exciting news about season two of her show.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: To our very first New York studio audience. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN, HOST, THE LATE SHOW WITH JAMES CORDEN: The United States space force officially revealed the prototype for its dress uniform. Here is here, servicemembers will wear the uniform for formal business, ceremonial events, and from the look of it to prepare your tableside Caesar. No word yet on when they`ll be getting sued by Battlestar Galactica.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This morning`s Eye Opener is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: Oh, the new uniforms look snappy.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: They`re snappy, there`s something inherently funny about the space force and the uniforms don`t help. But it`s an important mission.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: I like the new uniform.\par \par \uc2 KING: I do too. I do too. We welcome you to \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings. We`re going to begin at a very critical moment for President Biden today in his ambitious agenda. Overnight, the House passed a bill to prevent a government shutdown for now, but it received no Republican support at all. The bill now goes to the Senate where it could fail. This comes after President Biden`s speech at the \uc2 UN\uc2  seeking to reassure our allies that the U.S. is committed to global leadership on the pandemic and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House with some breaking news for us this morning. Weijia, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG, CBS NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning to you. And good morning to everybody. Well today President Biden plans to announce that the U.S. will purchase more than 1 billion doses of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine to share with lower income countries. That is more than double the original purchase plan as he supports the goal to get 70 percent of the world vaccinated within a year.\par \par \uc2 It`s also part of President Biden`s promise to lead global progress as a trusted partner. But in recent weeks, some U.S. allies have questioned American credibility, forcing the president to defend some pieces of his foreign policy, all while his domestic policy goals are in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: During his first address to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  on Tuesday, President Biden called for closer international cooperation to tackle everything from the COVID-19 pandemic to human rights abuses.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let`s make our better future now.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: But ties with some U.S. allies are strained, in part because of how the president withdrew U.S. forces from Afghanistan. He framed the end of the war as a chance to reset relationships.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As we close this period of relentless war. We`re opening a new era of relentless diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And from foreign policy to the domestic agenda, Mr. Biden tied the climate change crisis to his $3.5 trillion infrastructure bill, but that legislation and a smaller bipartisan measure aimed at repairing roads and bridges is in jeopardy because of rifts in the President`s own party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: More than half our members will not move the bipartisan bill without the reconciliation bill being passed.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Republicans say the democrats` proposal is one reason why they won`t support raising the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): The reckless, taxing and spending spree that Washington Democrats are assembling behind closed doors would put more debt, more borrowing and more inflation on the shoulders of American families.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Overnight, the House passed a measure to raise the debt ceiling and avoid a government shutdown at the end of the month, extending funding through December 3. But it will be much tougher to clear this Senate.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): A government shutdown would be a very bad thing for our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: If the U.S. defaults on its debt, economists warn that would have a devastating impact on jobs and the stock market. Later today, President Biden plans to host Democratic leaders and house lawmakers here to talk about that and to round up more support for his infrastructure agenda. Tony.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Weijia, thank you very much and of course, the world is watching. So let`s bring in Chief Foreign Affairs Correspondent and Face the Nation Moderator Margaret Brennan. She joins us now. Margaret, good morning. The President seem to spend a portion of his speech yesterday arguing to Americans that there is value in the U.S. engaging more with the world. So let`s get into the details a little bit. What stood out to you about the President`s message on foreign policy?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN, CBS NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning to you, Tony. That phrase relentless diplomacy stood out because the administration isn`t practicing relentless diplomacy at the moment. This is very aspirational, \uc2 UN\uc2  addresses often are, but they seem to gloss over some of the complexities that the Biden administration is facing.\par \par \uc2 Their diplomatic overtures to broker transition of power in Afghanistan, for example, fell apart, and allies said they weren`t even fully consulted, the diplomacy with Iran over its nuclear program is stalling. And these attempts to broker a climate deal with China have largely failed. That`s not my analysis. That is what the Secretary General of the United Nations said on Sunday. So this has been a really tough start for the diplomacy here.\par \par \uc2 And the President made clear he has a very strong aversion to military force, and wants to try to avoid conflict with China. So the diplomacy may be more focused there.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes, a lot of world leaders have criticized the American pullout from Afghanistan, at least the chaos that remained. You spoke recently to President Erdogan of Turkey, who is facing an Afghan refugee crisis at his borders. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: This was an exclusive interview and air Dhawan came with a message that he is very frustrated to inherit the consequences of U.S. action abroad, including the chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan. His country has the most refugees in the world. And now they have a wave of Afghans headed towards their border. I asked him, what should be done.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: You said last month Turkey has no duty responsibility or obligation to be a refugee warehouse. Whose obligation is it? Is it America`s obligation to take in these refugees?\par \par \uc2 RECEP ERDOGAN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF TURKEY (through translator): Right now, the United States have failed to meet its obligations. We have more than 300,000 Afghan refugees, and we will no longer be able to afford to welcome any more Afghan refugees in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Does the U.S. need to do more?\par \par \uc2 ERDOGAN: Of course. The United States should do a lot and should invest a lot because the United States has been there for the last 20 years. But why? Why? First, these questions should be answered by the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Should the U.S. do more? And Margaret and he hedged a little bit in his answer. He didn`t - didn`t come up with a clear yes. What did you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, it - further on in the interview, he did say you know more needs to be done here. And - and really, he`s looking for some support from the rest of the international community. But he`s building a wall literally to try to keep Afghan refugees out at the moment. But the pressure here is back on the administration, which has up the number of refugees, it will accept to this goal of 125,000. But there is frustration from those in the neighborhood that they have been left to pick up the pieces and they would like something more from the United States. I suspect that means more financial aid and more support when it comes to dealing with the fallout from the withdrawal in Afghanistan. So this is something we`re going to watch.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is also one of the few countries that is being turned to by the United States to be a lifeline to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and perhaps help keep an idea of how to stabilize the country through them.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes, indeed. Margaret, thank you very much. And this Sunday on Face the Nation, you`ll be able to see more of Margaret`s interview with Turkey`s President Erdogan as they discuss human rights and America`s role on the world stage during the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: We have an update on the fate of 1000s of migrants camped out on the border of Del Rio, Texas. The Associated Press reports many people from the mostly Haitian group are now being allowed to remain in the U.S. while they seek asylum. That`s after a number of recent deportation flights back to Haiti. Manuel Bojorquez is in Del Rio. Manuel, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ, CBS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. That AP report claims that many have been released with notices to appear at an immigration office within 60 days. And in fact, we`ve seen for ourselves at one location here in Del Rio where dozens were allowed to enter as we await word from Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 This will likely only add to criticism of the Biden administration`s response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: President Biden says his administration is handling the influx of mostly Haitian migrants at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The Biden ministration is a manmade disaster of inhumanity of epic proportions.\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: Republican Texas Governor Greg Abbott visited the camp Tuesday, expressing doubts that all the migrants would be processed soon.\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: The only thing they`ve shown is an incapability of dealing with this crisis candidly in a way where they pretend it doesn`t even exist. And we`re here to tell you, it exists.\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: Border Patrol agents that were seen on horseback grabbing and lashing out at migrants are now on administrative duties pending a homeland security investigation. The administration has sent additional Border Patrol agents to the area as more than 8000 migrants remain in the camp.\par \par \uc2 With other sections of the river now off limits this shallower stretch of the Rio Grande has become a lifeline. Many come over here to the Mexican side to buy food to bring back to their families at the bridge camp. Gregorio Gianpierre arrived with his wife and child, he crossed into Mexico looking for food for them.\par \par \uc2 You heard about the deportation?\par \par \uc2 GREGORIO GIANPIERRE, HAITIAN MIGRANT: FOREIGN LANGUAGE.\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: And what do you think of this? Why do that?\par \par \uc2 GIANPIERRE: FOREIGN LANGUAGE.\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: Do you have anything in Haiti?\par \par \uc2 GIANPIERRE: No, no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: And as we visited the Mexican side, we noticed that authorities there are also cracking down and limiting the migrants movements, leaving many of them feeling stuck, and of course, fearing deportation, Nate.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Now we normally don`t see Haitian migrants in large numbers at the southern border. Why is this happening now?\par \par \uc2 BOJORQUEZ: Well, many of them fled the island nation after that devastating earthquake in 2010 and obtained jobs in South America including in the run up and during the Olympics in Brazil, but largely due to the pandemic, those jobs have dried up and rumors spread that this was the best place to try to enter the U.S.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Manuel Bojorquez in Del Rio, Texas. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KING: We expect new guidance today on COVID booster shots and who should get them. CBS News confirms the \uc2 FDA\uc2  intends to announce a plan for third shot of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. The committee recommended last week that patients 65 and over and those at high risk should get those booster shots.\par \par \uc2 COVID-19 patients are still crowding the ICUs all around the country. Nancy Chen is at one hospital in Kentucky that is stretched to the absolute limit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. BRAD BAKER, ST. CLAIRE REGIONAL MEDICAL CENTER: This is not a political party or religion in here. This isn`t - This isn`t about constitutional freedoms. This virus is an evil, evil thing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN, CBS CORRESPONDENT: For Dr. Brad Baker, the fight against Coronavirus is personal. He was born right here at St. Clair Regional Medical Center and came back for his residency. Right now, the virus is ravaging his home.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: You know we`ve been shouting from the rooftops for a long time now, how bad this is and what this can do. And to watch it despite your warnings and your - your pleadings to just come through and take life like it does that that`s hard.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: St. Clair is the largest rural hospital in northeast Kentucky. And it`s the only Hospital in a county of roughly 24,000 people. Baker says he has opened beds, but not enough nurses to staff them.\pa