ied by former presidents of the \uc2 California Medical Association\uc2 , which lobbies lawmakers from its headquarters here in Sacramento, on behalf of nearly 50,000 doctors in the state.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever run into an opponent as fierce as the \uc2 California Medical Association\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JERRY HILL, FORMER MAYOR OF SAN MATEO: No, never did.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: Few know more about the \uc2 California Medical Association\uc2  or the CMA and its lobbying power than Jerry Hill.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Their ability to bring money to the table, to bring support. All factors into their ability to control the medical board and control legislation going forward.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: The former state senator chaired the committee that oversees the board and spent years of his political career trying to make the medical board more accountable.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Today, I introduced the Patient Right To Know Act of 2018.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: The CMA in your view, is it a powerful organization.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Probably one of the most powerful special interests that we have here. They are masters at getting what they want from any legislation.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: We analyzed the California Medical Association`s political contributions, and found that since 2015, they`ve spent nearly $15 million on lobbying and in donations to state candidates. And during Governor Newsom`s recall election, CMA gave $750,000 to the California Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 The access that kind of money secures was evident when his photos surfaced during the pandemic of Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  mask less at a Napa restaurant, where you can find $50,000 bottles of wine on the menu, seated with the governor, the California Medical Association`s chief lobbyist, as well as its CEO, Governor Newsom has made five appointments to the medical board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: Governor Newsom`s office declined to comment. The medical board 