par \uc2 And I think, why not discuss it?\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: I hear you.\par \par \uc2 BARRYMORE: I did like that story.\par \par \uc2 KING: I like -- I wrote it down.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I think I`d be a bigger believer in second chances and a slow evaluation. People come out and -- and their dimensions show. Anyone can -- when you meet somebody, you don`t meet them. You meet their representative, their best face, their best foot forward. The real person comes out later.\par \par \uc2 KING: I don`t know. I think when you meet me you`re kind of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: -- I don`t have a slow burn.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: And the same with you, Drew Barrymore. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BARRYMORE: I think you might be hardballing options out of our lives because we do assess very quickly and I think you and I know what we`re looking for and even more importantly in relationships, just like in design, know what you don`t want.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 BARRYMORE: And that is the crucial element.\par \par \uc2 KING: Drew understands.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Drew Barrymore.\par \par \uc2 BARRYMORE: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 KING: So glad to see you. We are cheering you on and Nate is going to be on the show --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BARRYMORE: It`s going to be so much fun. I`m back with you.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, thank you so much for allowing me the privilege of filling in for you while you took care of your beautiful baby.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: The pleasure was mine.\par \par \uc2 KING: OK, They`re saying we got to go.\par \par \uc2 Drew, you know how time cues go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LAMPKIN, AMS METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. I`m Chester Lampkin. Here`s your Wednesday planner. We have a yellow weather alert today and one tomorrow. It is the first day of autumn. We have temperatures holding the 70s. There are scattered showers and thunderstorms throughout the day, with the best chances for downpour coming this afternoon. More storms tonight and more tomorrow. Especially in the morning.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Ahead, CBS News gets a rare and revealing look inside the FBI`s archives. We`ll show you evidence from some of the most notorious crimes in history. You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, we`re getting rare access to a treasure trove of evidence stored at the FBI`s archive inside the bureau`s headquarters in Washington.\par \par \uc2 From the Unabomber`s reconstructed cabin to the boat where the Boston marathon bomber was caught, the archive contains artifacts from some of the country`s most notorious crimes and only a handful of people have had access to this room. However, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  allowed our Jeff Pegues and our CBS News cameras to go inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Many of the artifacts in this secured room have never been on display for the public to see. This is evidence, some of it from recent \uc2 FBI\uc2  cases, some of it from the `90s, `80s, even the 1920s and `30s.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FOX, HISTORIAN, OFFICE OF PUBLIC AFFAIRS, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: Let`s put the gloves on.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): Fragile to the touch and treasure for FBI historian John Fox.\par \par \uc2 FOX: These are the parts of our past that tell our story in a very different way.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): The FBI`s story began in 1908 with the establishment of the Bureau of Investigation. The mission and make-up of the organization evolved and grew over the next two decades.\par \par \uc2 By the 1920s and `30s, the Great Depression and Prohibition spawned violent bank robbers and killers known as gangsters.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: These gangsters were like celebrities.\par \par \uc2 FOX: They were almost celebrities, yes.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): But J. Edgar Hoover, the FBI`s founding and longest serving director, used his crackdown on gangsters to make the \uc2 FBI\uc2  a household name.\par \par \uc2 FOX: Hoover thought of them as public enemies, that people who needed to be, you know, dealt with.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): Outlaws like John Dillinger and Baby Face Nelson were hunted down and killed in shootouts with law enforcement. And later, immortalized by their death masks.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: At that time, law enforcement would put the remains of some of these gangsters on display for everybody to see?\par \par \uc2 FOX: There was almost a public production. When Dillinger was shot outside of the Biograph theater, people were dipping handkerchiefs in blood for a memento.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: What do you think when you look around this room?\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): \uc2 Paul Abbate\uc2  is the FBI`s deputy director.\par \par \uc2 PAUL ABBATE\uc2 , DEPUTY DIRECTOR, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: I think it`s the history of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And I think about the mission we do, the work, keep people safe, protect the country. And it`s a reminder that we`ve -- constantly have to stay focused on that.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): The chronicles of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is also a record of the shifting threats to the country through the years. And in the last 40 years, a new threat emerged, home-grown terrorism.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We had some kind of an explosion downtown.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): In 1995 the \uc2 FBI\uc2  launched one of its most significant investigations to date in the aftermath of the Oklahoma City bombing.\par \par \uc2 This is a replica of the rental truck used to house and transport the explosives, along with a model of the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building.\par \par \uc2 FOX: It shows the cutout of what parts of the building were actually damaged.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): All evidence used to convict mastermind Timothy McVeigh, who was executed by lethal injection in June 2001.\par \par \uc2 And the Unabomber`s cabin has been reconstructed here inside the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It`s where Ted Kaczynski made bombs that killed three people and injured nearly 2 dozen others.\par \par \uc2 The manhunt for the 2013 Boston marathon bombers ended here in this bullet- riddled boat, where Dzhokhar Tsarnaev was found hiding.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: How does this inform what federal agents are doing today?\par \par \uc2 ABBATE: It`s the lessons learned from the past. That includes failures, mistakes.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): And loss.\par \par \uc2 JOANNE HATTON, LENNY`S WIFE: He was my soulmate.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): Joanne Hatton`s husband, Lenny, was an FBI agent in New York.\par \par \uc2 HATTON: He was everything I could have ever asked in a husband.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): On 9/11, he raced to the World Trade Center to help.\par \par \uc2 LENNY HATTON, FBI AGENT (from captions): This is Lenny. Just tell everybody I`m OK, all right?\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): Lenny Hatton died that day in the line of duty and his passport is on display in the archive.\par \par \uc2 FOX: The interesting thing about the passport is that the expiration date is actually --\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: September 11th.\par \par \uc2 FOX: 2001.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FOX: This was given to us by his wife to help us to tell the story of Agent Hatton and his career and sacrifices in fulfilling the bureau`s missions.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES: It`s near and dear to your heart.\par \par \uc2 FOX: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): Lenny Hatton`s story and many others, stored in this secure \uc2 FBI\uc2  archive in the Hoover Building, remind new and veteran agents of the lives lost and also the sacrifices made in pursuit of the values on the bureau`s seal.\par \par \uc2 ABBATE: Walking through here every day, I think about the victims and ensuring that we prevent those types of harm from ever happening again.\par \par \uc2 PEGUES (voice-over): For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Jeff Pegues in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 KING: Jeff Pegues, well done, Jeff Pegues. I love anything that you can see behind the scenes. But the passport, guys, with the expiration date, Lenny`s passport --\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Very spooky --\par \par \uc2 KING: September 11th, 2001.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: You realize how many of these stories help shape our collective thought on crime and punishment. We`re familiar with them because we`ve seen them so many times over and over again. Just to walk through those halls had to be eerie.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I like that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  cares enough to have an official historian. I hope he gets to flash the badge, kick down doors and confiscate things for history.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: The badge up --\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KING: I like that they showed what worked and didn`t work. Very important.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Yes. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 KING: Coming up, I have the tease -- thank you, Megan.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: Who`s talking next?\par \par \uc2 Oh, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, some parents are trying to get around the COVID vaccination age requirements for their children. Dr. Jon LaPook will explain what those dangers could be.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I love this song. Coming up, Ben Tracy travels to the Rocky Mountains to show us some awe inspiring landscape. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 ANNIE YU, WUSA9 NEWS ANCHOR: Good Wednesday morning. I`m Annie Yu. Police in Alexandria, Virginia, are investigating a shooting involving a child. We`re told it happened yesterday in a McDonalds parking lot on King Street.\par \par \uc2 They say they got a call about kids fighting. When officers showed up, they found a child was shot. Fortunately, the child is expected to recover. We are still looking to learn more from police about what led to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Over to you, Chester.\par \par \uc2 LAMPKIN: There is a yellow weather alert today. We`ve had some showers this morning. The bulk of the heavy action comes in tomorrow. These downpours will come this afternoon with temperatures upper 70s, as we squeeze in some more drive time. The first day of fall will be a damp one, 79 today and 72 tomorrow. Very fall-like with 70s all weekend.\par \par \uc2 MARC CLARKE, WUSA9 NEWS ANCHOR: Coming up on "GREAT DAY WASHINGTON," we will be talking to BD Wong. He is starring in the hit \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2  series called "Awkwafina Is Nora from Queens."\par \par \uc2 We will also be talking to Brandi and Cody Rhodes of the All Elite Wrestling League about the new show detailing their family life behind the scenes. See you at 9:00.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS. Thank you for waking up with us. Come on in. It is time to bring the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Come on in.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Start us off.\par \par \uc2 What do you have?\par \par \uc2 KING: We`ve been waiting for you.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a very creative campaign to promote COVID vaccines.\par \par \uc2 The ad agency -- remember this ad agency, they deserve a huge shoutout -- Boone Oakley, based in North Carolina, they recently hired a truck with an eye-catching ad that says, "Don`t get vaccinated. Wilmore Funeral Home" and a web address.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KING: The thinking is if you don`t get vaccinated, it`s good for the funeral business, which is true. So this truck drove around Bank of America Stadium in Charlotte during Sunday`s \uc2 Carolina Panthers\uc2  game.\par \par \uc2 People who went to the website found this -- "Wilmore Funeral Home, get vaccinated now. If not, see you soon."\par \par \uc2 The funeral home is not real, which -- at first I thought it was real. It`s not real. I think this is even better. When people clicked on the site, it brought them to the StarMed, a health care provider in the area.\par \par \uc2 So I really want to give a shoutout to you, David Oakley. I don`t know you but you`re president of Boone Oakley, behind the campaign.\par \par \uc2 I love the creativity. I think it`s very clever. He says that he wanted to use their resources for the common good. I love this so much because I feel so strongly about people getting vaccinated. I have some people still in my family -- I`ll use the pronoun their -- I`m very upset with them.\par \par \uc2 But I saw a doctor on the news the other day and he said, if you`re unvaccinated, it`s not a matter of if you`re going to get it, it`s when. You have two options. You end up in the hospital or you end up in the obituary pages. So when I see a clever campaign like this, I think it`s worth repeating and letting people know about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. Absolutely. It`s a roll of the dice.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: I can imagine the next campaign a picture of the Grim Reaper saying, "I`ll see you in a little bit."\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I hope it works. There`s an old saying about marketing -- I know I`m wasting half my marketing dollars but I don`t know what half. Some business humor for you. Business humor top of the morning.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Well, done, Tony.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: OK.\par \par \uc2 KING: It will resonate with somebody.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: OK.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table," sticking with the business theme, is about the surprising connections between the global supply chain. So try to follow this here.\par \par \uc2 There was an increase in the gas prices in the U.K., which led some fertilizer companies to shut down some plants because CO2 is a byproduct of fertilizer. It means there`s no CO2 in many parts of the U.K. now.\par \par \uc2 And they`re facing shortages of other products.\par \par \uc2 Like what, you say?\par \par \uc2 How about beer products, carbonated beverages like soda, certain fruits and vegetables, which are stored with CO2 and grown with CO2?\par \par \uc2 Even meat products could face shortages in the weeks and months ahead because of a lack of CO2. Apparently you used it to stun the animals before they slaughter them. Ick.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, I thought it was interesting that like a little bit of a gas price hike leads to this, leads to this, leads to, wait, there`s no beer at my pub?\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: The ripple effect. I know people are thinking beer, soda and meat. That`s my diet right there.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: That`s my Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: My "Talk of the Table" is a big-money announcement from \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 . Amazon`s founder is pledging $1 billion to support conservation efforts. He said his trip to space in July inspired him to act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFF BEZOS\uc2 , FOUNDER, BLUE ORIGIN: I`d heard that seeing the Earth from space changes one`s point of view of the world. But I was not prepared for just how much that would be true. Looking back at Earth from up there, the atmosphere seems thin and the world finite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: "The world finite." The money will come from Bezos` Earth Fund, created to fight climate change. The billion-dollar grant will focus on the Congo Basin, the tropical Andes, the tropical Pacific Ocean. The goal is to protect 30 percent of the Earth`s land and sea by 2030 to prevent mass extinction.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Mass extinction. When you put it like that --\par \par \uc2 KING: I like it --\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Mass extinctions. It becomes real.\par \par \uc2 KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: That`s the definition of putting your money where your mouth is.\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 . Well done.\par \par \uc2 Switching gears now, in this era of climate change, the great mountains and forests of the American West are now conjuring images of huge wildfires and crippling droughts. We`ve seen that.\par \par \uc2 But that should never overshadow the sublime beauty and grandeur that has lured people since well before the founding of our country.\par \par \uc2 CBS senior environmental correspondent, that`s Ben Tracy, went to the Rocky Mountains of Colorado to contemplate what`s at stake as our planet warms and what we can still save.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY, CBS SENIOR ENVIRONMENTAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): There is a reason the Rocky Mountains are so often described as majestic. Their jagged peaks forming cathedrals in the sky, deferring only to the sun and clouds above.\par \par \uc2 John Muir, the father of our national parks system, once said, "Going to the mountains is like going home."\par \par \uc2 TRACY: Of course this was home to the native people. And they`re the ones that gave these mountains their name. From across the prairie, they said these look like distinctive rocks.\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice-over): The Rocky Mountains stretch for more than 3,000 miles through six states, its spine so tough it divides a continent, sending rivers flowing to oceans in two directions.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice-over): These mountains have inspired melodies and metaphors burned into the imagination.\par \par \uc2 But now the fire is real. Unprecedented wildfires fueled by climate change are ravaging the Rockies. The scientists say the forest here are burning more than at any point in the past 2,000 years.\par \par \uc2 Three of the largest recorded wildfires in Colorado`s history all took place in 2020, charring hundreds of thousands of acres.\par \par \uc2 TRACY: The summers here are now hotter than they used to be and the winter has less snow. It`s making everything here a whole lot drier. And that`s making the fires more destructive.\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice-over): In a new study scientists say, if the fires here continue at this pace, every forest in this region could be burned by the end of this century.\par \par \uc2 Of course the Rockies were formed by adversity, raised by the shifting Earth and carved by glaciers. They have stood for tens of millions of years.\par \par \uc2 TRACY: John Muir liked to think of the mountains as home, which is why he also said, "The mountains are calling and I must go."\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice-over): If he were alive today, he might be surprised by what he finds when he gets here -- for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Ben Tracy, in the Rocky Mountains of Colorado.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, Ben Tracy.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to do more stories like that, just to keep reminding people what`s at stake and how important this is.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes. That nature`s out there. Needs us.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: I`m a Seattle guy. I consider myself a city slicker. But I went to Nevada/Reno and being in some remote areas of Nevada, I realized how much of this Earth and nature that we need to protect. I didn`t really get that message until I was 19-20 years old. So I`m glad that we`re doing stories like this because we need to hear this at a younger age.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: For a long time people thought, we`re just little people, we can`t hurt the big planet. Turns out we can.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: We`re consuming it at a rapid rate.\par \par \uc2 KING: I`m thinking being a city slicker is a good thing. Look at you.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: City slicker.\par \par \uc2 KING: The city slicker.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: He`s a city slicker. All right.\par \par \uc2 Some parents think they`re getting slick, too. They`re vaccinating their kids who are under 12. This is despite an \uc2 FDA\uc2  warning not to do it. There is not an approved course of action. Dr. Jon LaPook is here with why parents should wait, hold up and also when we can expect a green light from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LAMPKIN, AMS METEOROLOGIST: A yellow weather alert today. Temperatures will be held down because of clouds and downpours but it is still muggy on the final day of summer and first day of autumn. Autumn begins this afternoon at 3:21.\par \par \uc2 A high of 79 with 70s across the DMV. Showers and thunderstorms off and on throughout the day. There will be more prominent and numerous ones in the afternoon. More rain in the night and afternoon tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: The recent increase in COVID cases in children is leading some parents to bypass vaccine requirements so kids younger than 12 can get the shot. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  recently warned parents not to do this.\par \par \uc2 But the agency may be getting closer to authorizing the shot for younger kids. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  announced this week that its COVID vaccine is safe and highly effective in children ages 5 to 11 years old. CBS News chief medical correspondent Dr. Jon LaPook spoke with one parent who does not want to wait.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LISA SCHAPIRO, MOTHER OF COVID-19 PATIENT: It was probably the scariest thing as a parent I`ve had to deal with.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK, CBS NEWS CHIEF MEDICAL CORRESPONDENT: Lisa Schapiro said she felt helpless, watching her daughter suffer from COVID.\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: Is it going to turn on a dime and is, you know, shortness of breath, walking up the stairs going to turn into serious shortness of breath, where she can`t catch her breath?\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK (voice-over): While both girls are OK now, Schapiro knew she did not want them to go through it again. She got her 14-year-old daughter, Ann Payton (ph), vaccinated this summer but her younger daughter, Alexandra (ph), is only 11.\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: We`re very itching to get it. We`re impatiently waiting.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Have you been tempted to ask your pediatrician, come on, she`s 11.5, that`s almost 12?\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: I have been absolutely tempted.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT KRUGMAN, VICE CHAIR, PEDIATRICS, THE HERMAN & WALTER SAMUELSON CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL AT SINAI HOSPITAL OF BALTIMORE: I know of some families who have very large 11-year olds, who have sort of snuck into some of the mass vaccine sites and things like that.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK (voice-over): Dr. Scott Krugman is vice chair of pediatrics at The Herman & Walter Samuelson Children`s Hospital at Sinai Hospital of Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: When a parent asks you to vaccinate their kid who`s under 12, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 KRUGMAN: It`s important to wait for the test to be done, the studies to be done and the Advisory Council on the Immunization Practices and the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  to say now`s the time. And we`re hoping now`s the time is coming sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK (voice-over): Parents are also hoping authorization comes soon, especially since \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  said its vaccine is safe and effective in young children.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: What do you think about the news coming out of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  about their vaccine for 5- to 11-year olds?\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: I`m thrilled. I`m excited. I`ve been waiting for it and hoping for it. Having the vaccine is everything. It will take so much worry away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook is joining us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Now should the authorization process make parents a little bit more optimistic?\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: I think it should. You know, Nate, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  can`t win, right?\par \par \uc2 Some people say they`re moving too quickly, others saying too slowly. Five days ago, there was an advisory meeting of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to consider whether the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  booster should be given to people 16 and over.\par \par \uc2 You remember the president got a little bit ahead of this and had raised expectations that, on the 20th of September, that people 16 and above would be eligible.\par \par \uc2 KING: So when it happened, people were frustrated --\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Right. But he did say it had to be CDC and \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved. Sure enough, I sat through eight hours of them discussing, really smart people, and they said the data isn`t there yet. We`re going to say it`s OK for a different group but we`ll change our recommendation as more investigation - - because I think people should find that reassuring that there are checks and balances.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: What are the potential dangers -- what`s the big deal basically, if you give a kid, 11-year old, say, an adult dose of the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Well, we`re always taught that kids are not just little grownups. So there are changes in hormones; kids are going through puberty and they`re very careful about the kind of doses they give.\par \par \uc2 You give too much, you could get too much of an inflammatory response, pain at the site, fever, headaches, joint pains, even some rare inflammatory reactions.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and talked about the dose. Remember, 30 micrograms of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  dose vaccines given in adults. They tried the 10, 20 and 30; they found, you know what, 10, the lower dose, gave just a really good response. So you always in medicine want to give the lowest amount of a medicine that still gets the response.\par \par \uc2 KING: You said you mentioned that you talked to \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  yesterday because now they`re saying it`s OK for kids 5 to 11.\par \par \uc2 How soon can we get it then?\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Well, it`s another example of the regulatory process that should reassure people. So even though \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  just came out this week and said it`s safe and effective, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says, fine, we want to see the data and we think they`re going to get that -- \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  tells me they`ll get that to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  by the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 And then they`re going to not believe what \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  said. They`re going to go through the records and do their own statistics. It may take another month. So I`m hearing maybe around Halloween.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Does the spike in COVID cases in children dispel the narrative that kids -- they can`t get it?\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: Absolutely, kids can get it. About one in four of the new cases are in kids. You know, we see 28 million kids in this new age group, 5 to 11. So there`s a real opportunity to vaccinate this group. We`re seeing a rise in hospitalization; we`ve done a lot of stories about --\par \par \uc2 KING: But for so long we were told you don`t have to worry about kids.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And parents still lean on that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: I think that`s a false narrative. The general idea is that the kids are -- tend to get less severe illness but they can still get really sick and die. Remember now, as more and more grownups get vaccinated, it`s going to tend to gravitate to lower groups that are not vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 KING: What`s your advice on how you can keep your children safe until they get the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 LAPOOK: All right. So it`s multiple layers of protection. So everybody`s got to get vaccinated who can possibly get vaccinated. Thinking about a school, right. Everybody should be wearing a mask who can wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 Testing, not enough testing`s being done. \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2  , the head of the CDC, was talking with me a couple of weeks ago and said, you know, there are billions of dollars that have been allocated for testing for schools. But the schools aren`t asking for it.\par \par \uc2 Finally and super important is ventilation and filtration. We`ve been talking about this, trying to hammer this -- the aerosol scientists are jumping up and down, saying it`s really important. It travels through the air.\par \par \uc2 Think about cigarette smoke. It`s not just six feet or three feet. If you were smoking a cigarette and we`re indoors and it`s not well ventilated, am I going to smell it?\par \par \uc2 Then potentially you can do that. So MERV 13 filters, HEPA filters, open the doors, crack the windows. But I think parents out there should talk to their school boards and the local authorities and say, what are you doing?\par \par \uc2 What`s the plan for ventilation and filtration?\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: If the school isn`t testing, parents you can test yourself, as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Jon, we appreciate you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 KING: Artist Emily Zugay is going viral for those TikTok videos she does, showing redesigned logos of famous companies. She`s doing a bad job on purpose. We like how she`s thinking and getting a lot of laughs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMILY ZUGAY, ARTIST: The first logo I chose to work on was \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . I don`t like anything about it. I redesigned it with more of a -- a happier feel. There`s not even any words indicating which brand this is. You just have to guess. This is my redesign of Apple`s logo.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KING: It started as a joke but now some of the companies are actually using her designs on their TikTok profiles. For instance, the NFL`s -- you`ll like this, Nate -- \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2  took it a step further.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZUGAY: I don`t really understand your logo. It needed to be simplified quite a bit. So I just cleaned it up. And this will look a lot nicer jerseys, helmets, merchandise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my goodness. The Detroit Lines? It`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 This is what not to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: Detroit Lines, OK.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I love a little word play. Fantastic. I love that.\par \par \uc2 KING: We answer asked her to redesign our logo and she calls it her best work so far. I can`t wait to see this. Let`s see it. \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: It`s nice and simple.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, Emily. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ALLISON SEYMOUR, WUSA NEWS ANCHOR: Good Wednesday morning. I`m Allison Seymour.\par \par \uc2 Parents are asking questions about why they were not told sooner about a serious incident involving a teacher in Virginia. Twenty-three-year-old Brendan Henry is a music teacher at an elementary school. He was charged with assault.\par \par \uc2 Sheriffs` deputies say that he used a belt to restrain a 7-year-old girl after she disrupted the class. The student was not physically hurt but parents say that the school did not inform them in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We found out six days later. I don`t like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEYMOUR: The parent at a school there.\par \par \uc2 That teacher was escorted off campus and placed on administrative leave.\par \par \uc2 Just a reminder, "OPEN MIC WITH REESE WATERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 LAMPKIN: Today is the first day of fall. We are going to end up in the 70s today which is a little more fall like for this time of year. Temperatures are in the 70s but we are dodging rain with yellow weather alerts.\par \par \uc2 Showers and thunderstorms are in the afternoon. Cooler tomorrow at 72. More downpours for your Thursday. It will clear the way by Friday.\par \par \uc2 MARC CLARKE, WUSA9 NEWS ANCHOR: Coming up on "GREAT DAY WASHINGTON," we will be talking to BD Wong. He is starring in the hit \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2  series called "Awkwafina Is Nora from Queens."\par \par \uc2 We will also be talking to Brandi and Cody Rhodes of the All Elite Wrestling League about the new show detailing their family life behind the scenes. See you at 9:00.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eh9m0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Woman Who Says She Was Forced To Travel More Than A Thousand Miles For Abortion Care After Texas Passed The Most Restrictive Abortion Law In This Country\b0\par\par\uc2 GAYLE KING, JANET SHAMLIAN, NATE BURLESON \par \uc2 2,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are getting a much closer look this morning at one of the major ripple effects of the new anti-abortion laws in Texas. To obtain an abortion, listen to this, patients are crossing state lines and traveling greater distances for care if-- if they have the resources, including one woman that Janet Shamlian spoke with in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Okay. Are you currently pregnant?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (CBS News Correspondent): This is a rare look inside a clinic performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I am booked for the rest of this week.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Comprehensive Women`s Health has been inundated with calls from women who live in Texas but want to come here, even though it`s located hundreds of miles from Texas in Denver, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Why are they coming to Colorado?\par \par \uc2 Doctor Rebecca Cohen is the clinic director.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN (Comprehensive Women`s Health Center): Many of the clinics in surrounding states face restrictions on the number of appointments that someone needs, on waiting periods, on other barriers to care.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The appointments are back to back all day.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Ten o`clock, KM is a six-week surgical with PO sedation. No records. Ten thirty, JT.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Since the Texas ban, as many as forty percent of patients here are from the Lone Star State, including a woman who traveled more than sixteen hundred miles roundtrip for an abortion. We`ve concealed her identity.\par \par \uc2 When you realized you could not get the procedure in Texas--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --what did you think?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I cried. I cried all the way from when they told me, through the counselor session, I was-- honestly, I didn`t know what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The thirty-four-year-old mother of three says she used money saved for a family vacation for the airfare and other expenses. She wasn`t paid for the day she took off work.\par \par \uc2 So, you just finished the procedure here--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --but you`re not finished.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: So, after a procedure you`re supposed to take it easy. I`m not going to get that option because I`m going to have to walk through the airport and everything else to get back home.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She tells me she was past six weeks when her pregnancy was confirmed in Texas, making her ineligible for the procedure there. Colorado clinics have no waiting period. Why many women are traveling here, like, the mom we talked to. She couldn`t be away from her children overnight.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Okay. How old is she?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And it`s not just Colorado seeing an increase. Even with a twenty-four-hour waiting period, this Shreveport, Louisiana, clinic is booked at least three weeks out. They used to be able to see patients within a few days.\par \par \uc2 So what are they asking? What are they telling you?\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN (Director at Hope Medical Group for Women): They are literally begging to get in and be seen as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For women who can`t afford out-of-state travel, options are limited.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: So I was lucky enough that I had already had money put back, but there are women out there who don`t. And I`m not sure exactly what they are going to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: We watched as the phones rang nonstop here in Shreveport. Staffers telling me they`ve always had some patients from Texas because they are close to the state line. The difference now, the distances that women are traveling. Some women coming here are doing so from McAllen, Texas. Now that is close to the Mexico border, a nine-hour drive. Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yeah, regardless of where you may stand on this issue, voices like this, they give a face to what`s going on. Thank you so much, Janet.\par \par \uc2 Now up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s seven forty-four, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad Duthiers never rocks the cash bar, he pays at the cash bar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was like, this is like a piano version of "Rock the Casbah." Piano version of the cash--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know it`s Casbah, by the way, don`t tell me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, you`re going to get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like cash bar. That`s what I prefer as the lyric.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very, very cool.\par \par \uc2 Here are the stories we think you`ll be talking about today. We`re honoring the life of Willie Garson. He was best known for playing Stanford Blatch on Sex and the City, the witty and fashionable best friend of Carrie Bradshaw. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Sex and the City/HBO)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was the quintessential New York friend. He played that role in the franchise`s two movies also. He was featured in other TV shows like White Collar and Hawaii Five-0 reboot on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Garson had recently been filming \uc2 HBO\uc2  Max`s upcoming revival series, and just like that. And our own Jim Axelrod is a childhood friend of Garson who says he died yesterday of cancer. Garson was just fifty-seven. Tributes have been pouring in on social media. Sex and the City co-star Cynthia Nixon said, "He was endlessly funny and a source of light and friendship and show business lore." I was talking to Jim backstage. Jim really wants people to know that he was that character.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was everybody`s best friend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was a mensch, yeah, he said. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was a mensch, and he was also a dad. He worked on the board of an organization called You Gotta Believe which connects young people with families. He adopted his son Nathan back in 2010.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jim says he was all those things and more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jim said he saw him two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: (INDISTINCT).\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The outpouring of people who really, really admired and loved him. It`s really tremendous. Jim said he would have really gotten a kick out of this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of his son, Nathan, he had a post which was really heartfelt. It went on to say, "I will always love you, but I think it`s time for you to go an adventure of your own." And which means he lived for other people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He really did, he really did. We`re going to miss him. I loved that character, Stanford.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I did, too. I did, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was just a quintessential New Yorker. Loved it.\par \par \uc2 All right. "Ready or not, here I come."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Ready or not, here I come."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fugees are back, you all.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from "Ready or Not"/SME)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it slowly, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, this is-- so, I`m going to do it slow, nice and slow. Lauryn Hill, Wyclef Jean and Pras Michel--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --will hit the stage together for the first live show in fifteen years. Right, Nate? I feel you, brother.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I feel you. This is the hip-hop group`s first global tour in more than two decades celebrating the--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --twenty-fifth anniversary of the hit album The Score. Fugees are playing small popup shows. They are playing one in New York City. Location, TBD.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to say, where? Today?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Find out, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it today?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come through, Gayle. It`s today. I don`t know-- no one knows where it is. Tickets go on sale for the tour on Friday. They are going to be playing in small venues across the country and also in Nigeria and Ghana which is cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait for this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lauryn Hill is one of my favorite artists.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me too, dude. I`m-- I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you remember The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill? Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, it`s one of the greatest albums ever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ever, ever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the greatest of all time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Fugees is the name, refugees, this is what we`re talking about today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, spot on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Spot on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s great, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let us know when you get that location.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I`m looking to you guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are excited.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You guys are the stars--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Drew Barrymore is in our Progressive Green Room. Hey, Drew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe she knows.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Maybe she knows.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She will tell--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Drew, you know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --what`s in the new season of her talk show and how she`s bringing back a favorite character.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ready or not?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That`s right. I am.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (8:00 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS on this hump day. I am Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, we`re talking to Drew Barrymore about the surprises in store for season two of her show. Can`t wait to hear what she`s planning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, a rare look at \uc2 FBI\uc2  artifacts and evidence from some of the nation`s most notorious crimes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We take you to the Rocky Mountains to see how climate change is affecting some of America`s most beautiful scenery.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another kind of Rocky Mountain high.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s Eye Opener @ 8.\par \par \uc2 President Biden promises American leadership at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . in the fight against COVID and climate change.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: We are fixing our eyes on devoting our resources to the challenges that hold the keys to our collective future.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The AP reports some Haitian migrants at a camp in Texas will be allowed to stay in America while they seek asylum even as the Biden administration is also stepping up deportation flights.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In fact, we`ve seen for ourselves at one location here in Del Rio where dozens were allowed to enter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hospitals overwhelmed with COVID patients are being stretched to the limit. A doctor in Kentucky tells us it`s happening because of misinformation.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRAD BAKER: And to watch it despite your warnings and your-- your pleadings to just come through and take life like it does, that-- that`s hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An earthquake rocks southeastern Australia damaging buildings in the city of Melbourne. So far, we`re hearing no one was seriously hurt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: An MMA fighter Conor McGregor is making headlines again, this time for a terrible no-good first pitch at Wrigley Field.\par \par \uc2 CONOR MCGREGOR: Well, the venom is there. The power is there. I don`t think there was much power difference between my-- mine and them out there. Just the accuracy is a little off.\par \par \uc2 ELISE MENAKER: Just a little off.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025eh9m0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Texas Abortion Law Pushes Women To Clinics Outside Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 924 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Today, the governor of Texas signed a bill putting more restrictions on abortion and it comes as the state`s abortion law faces its first test. A Texas doc-- doctor who violated the law has now been sued twice. CBS` Janet Shamlian goes in depth tonight on the impact of that law on women in the state.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GREG ABBOTT: Now, we`re about to make it law.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (CBS News Correspondent): The nation`s most restrictive abortion law--\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): Bans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --is forcing women to flee Texas--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Are you currently pregnant?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --in search of clinics like this one in Colorado that can barely meet demand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I am booked for the rest of this week.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: CBS News had rare access to the facility in Denver where nearly half the patients are Texans.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s a six-week surgical with PO sedation.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: When is the last time that you`ve seen an influx like this in this clinic to this degree?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN: We haven`t. We haven`t seen numbers like this ever.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Doctor Rebecca Cohen is the medical director.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN: An abortion can be painful. People can hurt, but this is different. We are seeing patients who are traumatized when they arrive.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Women like this thirty-four-year-old mother of three who asked us to conceal her identity. She learned she was pregnant one day before the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 When you realized you could not get the procedure in Texas--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --what did you think?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: I cried. I cried all the way from when they told me, through the counselor session, I was-- honestly, I didn`t know what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Texas law bans almost ninety percent of abortions beginning at six weeks before many women know they are pregnant. Women have to cross state lines to get one and they are. Colorado has no state- mandated waiting period for the procedure. The woman we spoke with made the sixteen-hundred-mile roundtrip in one day.\par \par \uc2 So, you just finished the procedure here--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: --but you`re not finished.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: So after a procedure, you`re supposed to take it easy. I`m not going to get that option because I`m going to have to walk through the airport and everything else to get back home.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: She says she has an irregular heartbeat which could make the pregnancy risky.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: How far along are you?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Shreveport, Louisiana, clinic is just twenty miles from the Texas border. Even with a twenty-four-hour state-mandated waiting period, appointments are hard to come by.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: Hope Medical.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Kathaleen Pittman runs the Hope Clinic.\par \par \uc2 KATHALEEN PITTMAN: They are literally begging to get in and be seen as quickly as possible. Women should not be going through this. I mean, it`s unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: There`s been up to a five-week wait for an appointment at this Louisiana clinic previously. In the past, women could get in, in just a couple of days. And while other southern states are looking to follow Texas` lead on abortion, the greatest challenge to Roe v. Wade comes December 1st when the Supreme Court hears the Mississippi case. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is still much more news ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS, including new video from the volcano in Spain as lava consumes homes and fills swimming pools.\par \par \uc2 The Space Force shows off its new uniforms designed with women in mind.\par \par \uc2 And we remember Sarah Dash from the legendary R&B group LaBelle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, that volcano on the Spanish island of La Palma is sending a river of lava up to twenty-feet high toward more homes along the coast, destroying just about everything in its path including this swimming pool. Six thousand people have been evacuated. More than a hundred and sixty homes were destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Look at this, today the U.S. Space Force unveiled its new dress uniform. It was designed with women`s figures in mind. The uniform includes a navy coat with six buttons symbolizing the sixth branch of the military and gray pants.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tributes are pouring in tonight for R&B singer Sarah Dash, best known for this tremendous hit.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from "Lady Marmalade"; 1974, Epic Records)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Dash recorded "Lady Marmalade" with the all-female group LaBelle, co-founded with Patti LaBelle, who is remembering Dash as an awesomely talented, beautiful, and loving soul. Sarah Dash was seventy-six years old.\par \par \uc2 Who doesn`t love that song?\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, dozens of bouncing babies after a CEO`s surprising act of generosity.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210922eh9l000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Ban Lawsuit; Afghanistan Flights Resume; Remembering 9/11: \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  Reflects On Terror Attacks, 20 Years Later; Documenting 9/11: CBS News Cameraman & Audio Technician Reflect On Terror Attack; Kacey Musgraves` Next Chapter; Jeff Daniels On New Series, American Rust & Return To Broadway; Metro Red Line Closures Begin On Saturday; Metro May Take Control Of Silver Line In Nov.\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Miri Marshall, Lee  Cowan, Annie Yu \par \uc2 6,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this hour, we`re talking to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . What she sees when she looks at the world today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS CO-ANCHOR: I am Tony Dokoupil. Jeff Daniels is here. He`s back on Broadway and back on TV with a brand-new show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON, CBS NEWS CO-ANCHOR: I am Nate Burleson. Star-Crossed is the name of Kacey Musgraves new album. She tells girl how she was inspired by Romeo and Juliet.\par \par \uc2 KING: And you can really hear it her music. But first, here`s today`s eye opener at eight. President Biden announces sweeping new vaccine requirements and he`s got a sharp message for unvaccinated Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES OF AMERICA PRESIDENT: We have been patient, but our patience is wearing thin, and the refusal has caused all of us.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: The Justice Department is suing Texas over its new abortion law. They say it violates Roe v. Wade and the constitution.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , UNITED STATES OF AMERICA ATTORNEY GENERAL: The Act is clearly unconstitutional under long standing Supreme Court precedent.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , it`s 200 people leave Afghanistan, including Americans. Commercial flights have resumed at Kabul airport.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Taliban\uc2  members who are now guarding this border crossing.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: There are hundreds of people who are trying to get out, some in need of urgent medical attention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. We are just about one day, actually, believe it or not, 20 years since 9/11. A moment that changed this country in a very big way.\par \par \uc2 KING: We`re still feeling it.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: We are still feeling it. We`re going to talk about those changes in this hour. Starting with Secretary of State - former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . The background, of course, 3,000 Americans died on 9/11, more than that, in fact.\par \par \uc2 And on that day, day after, Clinton visited Ground Zero in Manhattan. It was a year after she had just been sworn in as New York`s junior senator, that was just eight months earlier. And on 9/11, she said that she hoped that catastrophe would unite the entire civilized world. Former Secretary of State Clinton joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Madam Secretary, good morning to you. I think your hope for a united world came true, if only briefly, and I want to get to where we are today, all these years later. But let`s stick with the memories for a moment. You were a new senator in Washington, D.C. on 9/11. When you think back to that day, what stands out to you?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER NEW YORK SENATOR: Oh, what stands out, is the overwhelming sense of devastation and tragedy. I was on my way to the Senate for a hearing about improving education. Actually, Laura Bush was scheduled to testify when the first plane hit, and after making sure that my entire staff was safe, and that they could relocate out of the capital area, so that we could begin working.\par \par \uc2 I was on the phone endlessly talking with elected officials in the state, obviously, people in Washington. And looking at the television, like every American was glued to the scenes of devastation. But I have to tell you that, when I went the next day with my colleague, Senator Schumer, we were the only plane in the sky other than fighter jets that were keeping cover over New York and other parts of our country.\par \par \uc2 We landed at LaGuardia. We took a helicopter, and we circled over Ground Zero. And I cannot imagine anything that looked more like the gates of hell. I thought I`d be prepared because I`d seen it on TV, but the TV screen contained it, and circling over it was something that I think about and we`ll never forget.\par \par \uc2 But then quickly, we had to support our first responders, other emergency workers who were running toward danger, not away from it. We had to support the families whose loved ones were literally lost in a second. We had so much work to do. And in a way, it kept me going in the face of such overwhelming tragedy.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we were concerned that there might be more attacks elsewhere in our country. And really, it`s the people who I met that I worked with over those years and still today, I was interviewing some for my podcast that reminded me of the heroism and determination and tenacity. And as you said, the overwhelming sense at that time was of unity. And it really was an incredible moment of the country and the world coming together.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes. Madam Secretary, I don`t think unity would be a word that people use to describe America today or the world today, those attacks changed us in more ways than we can enumerate. If you had to pick one or two, what are the biggest changes to our country, to the world because of what happened on 9/11?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: I think that we have to keep in mind that we did have the entity then it means it`s possible, and I would hate to give up on it, but clearly, we were changed. Once you`ve been attacked, as we were, we made some smart moves, some not so smart moves to try to protect ourselves and our friends and allies and people across the globe.\par \par \uc2 We also tried, I think, to show our spirit of resolution and resilience, and as a senator from New York working to rebuild Lower Manhattan. I saw that every single day, how people pick themselves up, how people in the midst of incredible grief and loss, figured out how they could keep going.\par \par \uc2 So, I keep almost this duality in mind, the tragedy, the devastation, the attacks, what we had to do in the immediate aftermath, some of the decisions that were made, but also the way that it lifted us up. And I know, we`re in a really divided country right now, I understand that completely. But boy, do I wish that we could be reminded of the courage and the resilience and the tenacity and the unity that we all experienced 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 KING: You know, Madam Secretary, we have a new home here on Times Square, and is thrilled as I am to be here. I admit, I`m a little nervous. Sometimes I`m looking around more than I normally do. And I think a lot of Americans are concerned a