g argument that Atticus Finch delivered right. And you at the end of last third of the closing argument. I turned to the audience. And I treat them like the jury. And you have 1400 of the most sophisticated theater goers, predominantly white sitting in the Shubert Theater not moving. Not I mean, they`re pinback.\par \par \uc2 KING: It`s so quiet.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Actors dream of roles like that, of moments like that. And it happens eight times a week on that show.\par \par \uc2 KING: I can`t wait. Listen, when you say talk about actor`s dream of certain roles. I`m curious about American Rust, because I read that you read this book 12 years ago and were you holding on to it saying one day I`m going to do this.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I`d read it I met Philip Meyer, he did a book reading here in New York City and I went down to see this new author, debut novel great book, read, bought the book, read the book. Yes, it`s really great. I`m not in a position to get anything made. I`m trying to keep the career goal.\par \par \uc2 But then that thing that happens that people say what do you know when you`ve made it? I knew I`d made it when my agent Eddie called and said yes, we could do this or we could do that. But what do you want to do? American Rust, the book I have and that was, I don`t know a couple years ago, two or three years ago.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Once you had that power of all the things you could do Why that? Why American Rust?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I hadn`t played him before character.\par \par \uc2 KING: Is that what you mean? You`d hadn`t played a police officer? Is that what you mean?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes that. I hadn`t played. So that was you know, that was on the list. But I knew Del Harris. I grew up in Michigan. I worked at my dad`s Lumber Company, I drove truck. I did all that, I delivered, I did all that stuff. I`m around those guys. I was that small town kid that had the acting thing not worked out. That`s where I`d be.\par \par \uc2 I`d be back at that lumber company with my brother and running the family Lumber Company in a smart - with that. I am that guy. So when I saw the Del Harris in American Rust, I`m going - I want to go in there. And Philip Meyer, what he did and what Dan Futterman did with the series just you go deeper and deeper and, and I`m doing things in this show that I`ve never done in any --\par \par \uc2 KING: like what Jeff?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Well, there`s the opioid deal, I`m on the VA cocktail, which is Benzedrine, Klonopin, and Zoloft, I think and I`m trying to detox myself off it and so there`s - there`s that and when it doesn`t go well, I mean, there`s the detox. I - there`s a crime that happens and I get a little too involved in it.\par \par \uc2 KING: Looks like you might have crossed a line or two.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes. And then later on in the series, I get to do some things that I have never done on screen.\par \par \uc2 KING: OK, that`s a good tease.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I know it`s a tease but I can`t say.\par \par \uc2 KING: No, no that - that`s good. We like a tease. When you think about Dumb and Dumber if you`re sitting at home and it comes on, do you stay and watch it? What do you think about it? How does that sit?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I`ll stick around a little bit.\par \par \uc2 KING: You do?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yep, still funny.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: It is very, very funny.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: We didn`t know at the time.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: My kids yell at me when I make them watch classic movies, which for them is movies from the 90s. They love Dumb and Dumber.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Thank you. It`s just I remember doing the toy scene. And I`m going, I should - Jim Carrey`s sitting next to me. I`m going - he goes, What`s man? What`s wrong, man? I`m going to the beginning of my career and he says it`s going to be great. Jim taught me to be fearless.\par \par \uc2 KING: Let`s just say --\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: A great gift.\par \par \uc2 KING: Jeff Daniels, your talent is very wide and vast.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: One of the best in business.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KING: And go Lions.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, Jeff. American Rust premieres this Sunday on Showtime which by the way is a division of \uc2 Viacom\uc2 , \uc2 CBS\uc2  and To Kill a Mockingbird. This is big, reopens on Broadway October 5. Jeff will be there. Very busy. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KING: This week we`ve been paying tribute to the 1000s of lives lost on September 11, which was 20 years ago tomorrow. \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday Mornings Lee Cowan shares his essay about that day and remembers those who did not come home and the loved ones that they left behind.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN, CBS CORRESPONDENT: Color. It was just one of the casualties of that bright Tuesday morning. Gray became the shade of everything, like fresh fallen snow minuses, tranquil purity. Journalists, poets, historians, musicians, they`ve all tried to describe it but words often fail in the face of pictures.\par \par \uc2 We remember the images of the lucky who got out. The honors given to those who didn`t and the words that made us feel just a little less vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FMR PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: The people who knocked these buildings down will hear all of us soon.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: In the 20 years since that awful day, we`ve seen births and deaths, marriages, divorces, even wars come and go. The number of souls who remember 9/11 is growing smaller. Like any great calamity time, unwittingly throws dirt on the past. And there will come a time when unearthing it will take some effort.\par \par \uc2 But behind every Memorial, and the crevices of every carved stone, are the ghosts of everything that changed that day, for generations to come. What is constant in the history of loss is the reverence for sacrifice, something that isn`t always voluntary. For those who survived, sacrifice is forced. And it is endearment, the descendants of those who went to work one day and never came home to the sons and daughters of those who rushed to the scene and never came home. The husbands and the wives of those who were deployed, and never came home, it`s for them, that we pause these 20 years on just as much as we remember those who were actually lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: It is so important - Lee Cowan, that was beautifully, beautifully done. Thank you so much. I think it`s so important that we do stories this way because there`s still there`s a generation growing up now that has no idea what it is. You pointed out Tony, earlier that it`s even not taught in schools.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes, there are only 14 States that require instruction. Others may do it, freelance it, but it`s not required. And so there is a duty upon all of us not only to remember, but to talk about it with that growing number of Americans who were too young to remember or when I`ve been born when it happened.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it. I`ve been working on a poem. And here`s just a small piece from it. There was a moment of atrophy, which united us nationally, then naturally through strategy, we rose from this catastrophe. The brilliance and resilience is a reflection of you. What we feel through this ordeal is the injection of truth. There is pain and --\par \par \uc2 KING: You wrote that, you`ve been writing poetry for a long time.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: I wrote a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 KING: You wrote, yes --\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Because of the pieces that we run here about 911. But there`s pain there. But there are also, there`s heroes.\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And resilience.\par \par \uc2 KING: We will never forget. Tomorrow, we hope you`ll take some time to remember, however you choose to do so that`ll do it for us. We`ll see you Monday on \uc2 CBS\uc2  morning.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANNIE YU, HOST, WUSA-TV: Good Friday morning. I`m Annie Yu. Two stations on the Red line are closing for repair starting tomorrow. That`s Shady Grove and Rockville. They won`t reopen until December 4. But during the shutdown, you can use free shuttle buses instead of the trains. We have all those details at WUSA9.com\par \par \uc2 And an update on the Silver Line Extension in Virginia. Transit officials say Metro could take control of the project as early as November. That could pave the way for a possible opening of the new line during the first quarter of next year. The 11 mile extension of track would link the Reston station to Dulles Airport and Ashburn. The project has been plagued by issues that have delayed its opening by nearly two years now. Mary over to you.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Well, it doesn`t get much better than this going into the weekend with sunshine and temperatures in the 70s, we`ll top out at 79. Look for temperatures around 77 degrees by five o`clock this evening. The sunshine continues tomorrow, 82 degrees on Saturday, heating up a bit to 90 by Sunday. By Monday we`re up to 91, rain chances are back in the forecast toward the middle and the end of the week with temperatures in the 80s. Let`s send it over now to Great Day Washington.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good Friday morning everybody. Coming up next here on Great Day Washington, Texas barbecue is in Riverdale Park. We`re highlighting 250 barbecue where you could feast on some authentic handcrafted wood smoked deliciousness. And we`ll take a look at a local nonprofit that is in the running for $50,000 from Chick-Fil-a, see what The Arc PG is doing and how you can help them win. See you at nine.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210910eh9a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Sweeping New Vaccine Mandates; Melissa Mills Remembers Her Mother Woman At Center Of Roe V. Wade; \uc2 NBA\uc2  Season Kicks Off; Closures For 9/11 Memorial 5K In Arlington; Melissa Mills Remembers Her Mother Woman At Center Of Roe V. Wade; \uc2 NBA\uc2  Season Kicks Off; Closures For 9/11 Memorial 5K In Arlington\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Miri  Marshall, Tony Perkins, Jan Crawford, Catherine Herridge, Michelle Logan,  Nancy Cordes, Omar Villafranca, Jan Crawford, Charlie D`Agata, Holly  Williams \par \uc2 6,981 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CBS NEWS HOST: Welcome to "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings" I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS HOST: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON, CBS NEWS HOST: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 KING: He is indeed. Let`s go to today`s eye opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have the tools to combat COVID-19 kind of, distinct minority of Americans are keeping us from turning the corner.\par \par \uc2 KING: President Biden talks tough on the COVID crisis and unveiled sweeping new vaccine requirements. This is not about freedom from personal choice. It`s about protecting yourself and those around you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Justice Department is suing the State of Texas over a law that restricts access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Afghanistan had the first international commercial flight takeoff from Kabul airport since the American withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And we remember 9/11 through the lens of our \uc2 CBS\uc2  crew who was there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The smoke was coming towards me, three zero turn and drop the camera run away as you`re there to document what happens.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Pentagon tower of light lit up a skylight over the flight 93 Memorial in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 NFL\uc2  football is finally back, in the final second`s radium the Bucks when it`s 31-29.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Plus, Gayle sits down with Kacey Musgraves. She`s got a brand new album, and it`s out today.\par \par \uc2 KACEY MUSGRAVES, AMERICAN SINGER-SONGWRITER: This record for me was about looking at trying to transform my trauma, my pain into something beautiful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Reaches for that one focus and file. If you put away your fanny pack, you don`t think it`s in style anymore. We`re telling you right now. It`s wrong. It`ll catch baseballs for you to get so a granola bar in there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And when things big enough he probably didn`t pay for parking. Just fold up the Buick and stick it in that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 KING: This morning`s eye opener is presented by progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: Nice catcher, we welcome you to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. But before we start with the news, let`s take a look at the 9/11 Memorial as we get ready to remember the 20th anniversary. It`s all something we remember very well unfortunately was a terrible, terrible day and we all remember where we were. We`ll be talking about it a lot today.\par \par \uc2 Still gets me. It still gets me anytime I have to write the date down anytime something comes up about it. We will still 20 years later. It`s still very, very painful. We`re going to begin with this so President Biden`s tough tome on the COVID crisis he has had enough and a sharply worded speech yesterday Mr. Biden says vaccine or regular testing`s will now be required for any company with more than 100 employees.\par \par \uc2 The President spoke directly to the 80 million Americans who are still unvaccinated saying his patience is wearing very thin. The deaths are rising sharply in this country from the coronavirus. More than 10,000 Americans have died in the last seven days alone. Nearly all the victims are unvaccinated people. Nancy Cordes is at the White House with the details of the President`s plan. Nancy, good morning to you, boy the president pulled no punches yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Right. And big changes are in store Gayle. Good morning, this new order will force millions of federal workers and contractors to get vaccinated with no option for regular testing instead. Also today, fines will be doubled for anyone who is traveling by air if they don`t wear their masks. But the real centerpiece here is this sweeping new rule for businesses that employ about a third of the entire U.S. workforce.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Even patient - put our patience is wearing thin and the refusal has cost all of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORDES: With millions of Americans still unvaccinated President Biden is taking his toughest stance yet. Now every company with more than 100 employees will have to require workers to either get vaccinated or get tested weekly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Some of the biggest companies are already requiring this \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , Tyson`s Food and even Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORDES: Businesses that don`t comply could face a $14,000 fine per violation. The same goes for any medical facility that gets reimbursed by Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you`re seeking care at a health facility. You should be able to know that the people treating you are vaccinated, simple.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORDES: Since this summer when the President declared independence from the virus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No longer controls our lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORDES: Cases have skyrocketed from about 12,000 per day to more than 100,000. But some unions are already balking at the new mandates. One saying the government should trust its employees to make their own medical decision under consultation with their doctor. Some Republican governors and lawmakers called the President`s move unconstitutional and the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  is already vowing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Los Angeles County the nation`s second largest school district, voted Thursday to mandate vaccines for students aged 12 and up. Biden had his harshest words yet Thursday for Republican governors in States like Florida and Texas, who have tried to block masks mandates in schools.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: These governors won`t help speak to pandemic. I`ll use my powers president to get them out of the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORDES: The President also announced that the federal government is going to be spending billions of dollars to make rapid tests more readily available and cheaper. And he`ll be talking about safety in the classroom once again today when he visits a local school here in Washington D.C. with the Secretary of Education and the First Lady, Nate.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: As the cases rise, so should our awareness on what is going on with COVID. Thank you, Nancy, so much. The Department of Justice is suing the State of Texas over its new abortion law. About two dozen clinics have already stopped abortion services. Omar Villafranca has been following this for us, Omar. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA, CBS NEWS, CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, the DOJ suit puts constitutionality at the center of the argument now the law which is also known as SB-8 bans abortion after around six weeks, which is before many women even know they are pregnant and it makes no exception for victims of rape, sexual abuse or incest. It also puts enforcement in the hands of citizens by allowing anyone to sue people who help women get an abortion, like a doctor or clinical staff or even drivers.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  argues in the suit that SB-8 infringes on constitutional rights and long standing court precedent. Now, the Supreme Court recently declined to block the law. But that really didn`t address its constitutionality, which is what a federal judge will decide in this suit. Texas Governor Greg Abbott did release a statement he said "he is confident that the courts will uphold and protect that right to life", Tony.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: You know, I think while we`ve had a high stakes legal conversation, Omar, thank you very much. Let`s go straight to CBS News, legal analyst and legal correspondent Jan Crawford, who is at the Supreme Court for us, Jan, good morning to you. What are the chances that the justices have another say on this law?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD, CBS NEWS, CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, I mean, Tony, I think it`s a question of when and not if they have to take another crack at this. And some of the justices actually acknowledged that and when they took a pass the first time saying, you know, someone has to actually Sue to try to enforce the law before they can take a crack at it. But before it gets to that there is another major abortion case the justices are going to take up that will affect the Texas law and all these other state laws from Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi officials are asking the court to overturn Roe v. Wade to allow their state`s ban on abortions after 15 weeks. The justices are going to hear arguments in that case later this fall, a decision sometimes next year. The future of Roe v. Wade is in the balance, Tony.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And that`s saying a lot Jan, thank you very much. And Jan will also be sharing a remarkable interview later this hour. It is the first TV interview with the oldest daughter of Jane Roe who is at the center of the Roe v. Wade case.\par \par \uc2 KING: And Afghanistan cobbles main airport is open to commercial flights once again, dozens of people including at least 21 U.S. citizens and green card holders left on this flight yesterday. But there are new signs of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  clamping down. Charlie D`Agata is just over the border in Pakistan. Our captures - our cameras rather captured all members of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  patrolling the streets right next to people who are desperate to leave, Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA, CBS NEWS, SENIOR FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, we`re at the Torkham crossing with Pakistan on that side. And that is Afghanistan on that side. We got \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flags flying high. \uc2 Taliban\uc2  members who are now guarding this border crossing, there are hundreds of people who are trying to get out some in need of urgent medical attention. And they`re being stopped not just by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , but by Pakistan who don`t want to encourage a huge influx of refugees until there`s a coherent international plan in place. Here`s just an example of some of the brutality that they`re leaving behind.\par \par \uc2 A couple of journalists said they were beaten badly by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  after covering a women`s protest. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have now cracked down on those protests, banning them until further notice. Now as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  continues to tighten its grip through intimidation, we can expect more people to try to flood to the borders, even though they`re being discouraged, any way they can to get away from \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule, Nate.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Charlie D`Agata in Pakistan. Thank you. Tomorrow is the 20th anniversary of 9/11. The Biden Administration is reviewing Saudi - Saudi Arabia`s connection to the attacks 15 of the 19 hijackers were Saudi citizens. Holly Williams got a rare access to a prison in Saudi Arabia, where inmates were convicted of terrorist activity are held, Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS, CBS NEWS, FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. The Saudi Government has consistently denied any involvement in the 9/11 attack. But inside a Saudi prison we heard from inmates convicted on terrorism charges, who said their upbringing in an ultra conservative Islamic Kingdom contributed to their radicalization. Saudi officials say they`re now fixing the problem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This is one of the rooms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, this is--\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We were given a guided tour of Al-Ha`ir Prison by one of its inmates. Yaser Esam Hamdi was born a U.S. citizen to Saudi parents in Baton Rouge, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YASER ESAM HAMDI, FORMER AMERICAN CITIZEN: Remember that going to Disneyland in Florida? I think it was very nice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: His family moved to Saudi Arabia when he was five he told us and just before the 9/11 attacks, he went to train with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in Afghanistan, where he was captured by U.S. forces.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAMDI: I am a young man, a teenager growing with these people. I started to have some extremist practice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Hamdi renounced his U.S. citizenship and returned to Saudi Arabia a free man, but was later arrested by Saudi authorities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAMDI: I didn`t want to speak about it that lot, yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAMDI: Yes. But talking and stuff like that. And because of that, I came back to prison.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Saudi Arabia says it`s recently revolutionize this prison and effort to reform inmates with music, sports and even a company run by the prisoners themselves, which manufactures perfume very nice and furniture. The prisoners in charge wore business suits during our visit. We were filmed by inmates and accompanied at all times by prison officials. Critics that the Saudi Government says political prisoners have also been held here. Saudi officials deny allegations of torture. Several of the inmates described growing up in a closed off Islamic country exposed to fundamentalism and the glorification of violence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Did you ever hear any extremism at a mosque? Did you hear? You know, extremist views at school?\par \par \uc2 HAMDI: Yes, of course, of course.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Now, Saudi Arabia says it`s promoting so called moderate Islam, extremist preaching has been banned. And school textbooks that once promoted violence against non Muslims have been rewritten. Yaser Esam Hamdi told us he`ll be released in a year and dreams of somehow reclaiming his U.S. citizenship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAMDI: I was an extremist, extremist once, but now I`m not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Saudi Arabia is one praise for its efforts to fight terrorism and modernize that continues to be criticized over its human rights record. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 KING: Holly Williams reporting from Saudi Arabia. Thank you. In the months leading up to 9/11 the CIA sounded the alarm that something was coming. A new CBS News documentary that airs tonight takes us inside the CIA`s hunt for Al Qaeda and its then leader \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 . This is a preview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MORELL\uc2 , \uc2 CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY\uc2 : From April to mid July of 2001. There was a significant increase in intelligence reporting about Al Qaeda planning attacks, catastrophic attack, multiple simultaneous attacks, history changing attacks.\par \par \uc2 CINDY STORER, FORMER SENIOR ANALYST: We`ve seen chatter before other operations. But they started using language that we had never heard before. They were calling it Olympic sized, Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There were real plots be manifesting. The world felt like it was on the edge of a rupture.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s early this Tuesday morning the 11th of September 2001, from the heart of New York City.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First job of any government is to protect the country and protect its people.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD CLARKE, FORMER NATIONAL COUNTERTERRORISM COORDINATOR: If you don`t get credit for what you stopped, if you don`t stop them all. And we were unable to stop the big one.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE TENET, DIRECTOR CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY: For us, there was a personal dimension. 3000 Americans, the city I grew up in and been blown up. There was an emotional piece of this because we had woken up every day of our lives trying to stop what just happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: It`s still so emotional. We`re joined now by Former Acting and Deputy CIA Director \uc2 Michael Morell\uc2 , whom you saw in that clip 20 years ago, he served as the CIA`s Presidential Daily Briefer and was with President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  on 9/11. He is now CBS News Senior National Security Contributor and joins us at the table. Mike, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MORELL: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KING: You aren`t forgotten that you were with the President that day. And you were there at that school because we didn`t even know what we were looking at. But here`s the question. You`re the perfect person to be here today. Because your knowledge is so vast, the question is, do you - do you think we`re safer today than we were back then?\par \par \uc2 MORELL: We are absolutely safer today than we were back then Gayle, for two reasons. One is immediately after 9/11, we increased our defenses. So it is much more difficult for terrorists to get into the United States, it`s much more difficult for them to move money, is much more difficult for anybody to hijack an aircraft.\par \par \uc2 The second thing we did is we took the fight to them overseas. So wherever we could find them, whether they were in Afghanistan, whether they were in Pakistan, whether they were in Yemen, we took the fight to them and degraded them so that they didn`t have the capability anymore to attack us. So, and we`ve done that consistently since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 KING: But now the Taliban`s in controlling Afghanistan. That`s why I asked the question, do you think we`re safer? Because for a lot of people, it seems like we`re right back where we started, right. Do you think that it`s possible that Al Qaeda could reemerge?\par \par \uc2 MORELL: Yes. And whether that happens depends entirely on us. So I told President Bush on 9/11, that I was highly confident that what just happened was the result of Bin Laden and Al Qaeda. I`m here to tell you that today that I am highly confident that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  will allow Al Qaeda to have safe haven in Afghanistan, and Al Qaeda will want to reconstitute. So the question is, will we allow that?\par \par \uc2 Will we have the intelligence capabilities to watch them, to see what they`re doing? Look at their plans and intentions, look at their capabilities? And then will we have the military capability to go in there and degrade them, if we need to? And will we have the will to do that? If we do that, they won`t be a threat? If we don`t they could be a threat within 12 months?\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Do you consider Al Qaeda and the situation in Afghanistan to be the single biggest threat facing America today?\par \par \uc2 MORELL: So not right now? Right, but I worry about \uc2 Al-Shabaab\uc2  in Somalia. I worry about ISIS in North Africa. I worry about domestic terrorism here. But if we don`t do what we need to do in Afghanistan, it will become the single biggest threat again.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: What will be the legacy of our war in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 MORELL: So I think about it as what`s the legacy of the entire war on terror, right. And I think there`s two pieces to it. One is we have protected the homeland since 9/11. There hasn`t been another 9/11. We all - we all worked very hard to make sure that that didn`t happen. That`s fantastic, that success, right? We won that war.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, I think we`ve weakened America a little bit because for those 20 years that we were fighting a counterterrorism war and a counterinsurgency war, the Russians and the Chinese were catching up to us conventionally. And some of the things we did post 9/11 the war in Iraq, for example, caused political division here at home, right, that we`re still paying for.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 KING: All right, \uc2 Michael Morell\uc2 , you have the last word on that. Thank you very much for joining us today. Race against time to CIA and 9/11 a production of our very own See It Now Studios, airs tonight at eight seven central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . And it`s available tomorrow on the Viacom CBS streaming service Paramount plus, right now it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 MIRI MARSHALL, CBS NEWS WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Seven days and sunshine to kick off the weekend, temperatures up to 76 by 3:00 o`clock this afternoon. We`ll continue to see the sun tomorrow with highs around 82 on Saturday and heating up to around 90 by Sunday with plenty of sunshine. On the seven day forecast as we see rain chances returning by the middle and the end of the week by Tuesday highs in the upper 80s, 86 Wednesday with an isolated shower and more showers and storms Thursday with highs in the 80s.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TONY PERKINS, WUSA9 CORRESPONDENT: Good Friday morning. I`m Tony Perkins. Today family and friends will honor the life of PJ Evans. The eight year old boy was shot and killed last month in Prince George`s County, his killer still out there. Funeral services will be held today at city of praise family ministries that`s in Landover. The church is also planning to live stream the service. PJ had a passion for football and recently he met chase young from the Washington football team.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Having PJ was crazy. And to be honest, I felt I got to violence and things like that. You know, obviously, we`re human and that`s going to continue but I`ll just say lead a woman, can children.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: While young did not know PJ that well. He said that growing up in the same community made him feel a connection to PJ, youngest been outspoken about gun violence in the county and hope something can be done to stop it and soon, Miri.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: But we do have temperatures that are in the 60s to start our day and then sunshine by the afternoon high temperatures in the Upper 70s with breezy winds. Occasionally those winds are going to gusts up to about 20 miles per hour, 76 degrees by 3:00 o`clock this afternoon with plenty of sunshine over the weekend, we`re up to 82 degrees on Saturday and Sunday things against the heat up for us with highs right around 90 by Monday we`re up to 91 back to the upper 80s Tuesday with a chance for few showers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CBS NEWS HOST: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Morning. The fight over Texas new abortion law is just the latest in the series of battles around Roe v. Wade. For decades, after the Supreme Court upheld the right to an abortion, the efforts to challenge this controversial decision while it`s been constant. The woman at the center of the case was known first as Jane Roe, but her real name was Norma McCorvey. She was a mother of two when she filed the case and a mother of three, by the time it was decided.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford spoke with McCorvey`s oldest daughter, her name is Melissa Mills. They spoke at her home in Texas. This is a candid conversation about a very complicated history. And this is her first TV interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MILLS, DAUGHTER OF JANE ROE: Not everybody`s meant to be a mother, man. I didn`t expect that of her. She was the mother she could be.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As a young girl, Melissa Mills didn`t know her mother, Norma McCorvey was known to everyone else as Jane Roe. A complicated at times dysfunctional woman. Melissa was raised by her maternal grandmother.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe your mother?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: She was really funny. She was the life of the party. I didn`t think of her as a mother figure. I thought of her more like a sister, because that`s how our relationship was.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: Melissa was almost a teenager when she realized her mother was Jane Roe of Roe versus Wade. That`s when she also learned she had two younger half-sisters, Jennifer Ferguson and Shelley Thornton. Shelley was the child known as Baby Roe. Journalist Joshua Prager spoke to the three women in his upcoming book, The Family Roe, An American Story, portion shared with the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 I think most people have no idea that there is a Baby Roe.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: We were all given up for adoption, but my grandparents adopted me. And the other two were given up at birth. She couldn`t take care of herself, much less anybody else.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: The sisters met back in 2013.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: We only had like 24 hours together. It was very short and we got as much in as we could.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: According to Prager`s book, Thornton refused to meet McCorvey, the woman who told the world she didn`t want her.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Shelley never met her. And I think it was just too much. And I understand that when somebody I mean, she wanted to abort her.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: And she told Shelley that she expected her to thank her for not doing so.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Mom had a sixth sense of humor. I mean, she was - her humor was pretty harsh. And she was mad at Shelley, because Shelley wouldn`t do a DNA test. And when Shelley wouldn`t, it`s kind of--\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: Kind of made her mad.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Made her mad.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: As the only daughter who knew McCorvey growing up, Mills vowed, she would be different. You wanted stability, and civility and family?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Family, yes.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: How are you able to do that?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Because when you want something you do it.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: What are you most proud of?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: My kids.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: And the way you`ve been able to be the kind of mother that you didn`t have.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: McCorvey felt she never fit in, used by both sides. At some point, she crossed over and started speaking on the other side.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: Against abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Yes, she felt guilty. And people made her feel bad for the part and the role that she played with the Roe versus Wade case and all the babies that were aborted through the years and people would call her a killer. They called her Satan. They called her all kinds of terrible things. It was cruel.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: McCorvey spent the last year of her life in Texas, near Melissa and Melissa`s two daughters. She died in 2017, leaving behind a legacy that remains as divisive as ever.\par \par \uc2 We all like feel protective of our moms and their memories.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: I felt protected.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: It seems like you - I think that`s human nature, right? Like you, even if your mom has flaws, that`s your mom. What was that like for you?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: It was hard. I didn`t want anybody to hurt her. And I felt, even though she was strong, she was fragile.\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: She was strong but fragile. And you could see that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS HOST: Jan Crawford reporting there. It`s a powerful piece, powerful interview. The Family Roe is the name for it. How fitting in a way that the complicated relationship we all have to abortion as a country is pertain within Jane Roe herself, who has been on both sides of the issue.\par \par \uc2 KING: I mean, it just goes to show you there`s family drama, it seems in all things. I think most people don`t even realize it. Baby Roe was actually born. she filed the case and then but it took so long the baby came. So, it became a moot point. I think it`s interesting that Shelley, we learned her name is Shelley didn`t want to meet her mother.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON, CBS NEWS HOST: And when you think about what she been through--\par \par \uc2 KING: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: And then saying, no, when you want something, you just do it. I know she grew up conflicted, given everything that happened to her as a child, but now she`s like, you know what, I want to be a great mother to my daughter.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes, there`s an endless legacy. And that`s one of the better parts of this legacy.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 KING: We`re going to be talking to the author of that book, by the way, on Monday, who actually sat down with all three of the daughters, including Shelley. I think he`ll have a lot to say.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I think so.\par \par \uc2 KING: It`s a very big book. A lot to say--\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Reporter and a great writer and this is a different kind of book for him. So, I`m looking forward to it. And Jan, thank you again. Great interview. Ahead, we`ll introduce you to a woman who is still fighting for justice after her fiancAc, a New York City firefighter was killed on September 11th. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: 9/11 was the worst ever terrorist attack on U.S. soil. Five prisoners in Guantanamo Bay are accused of helping killed thousands of Americans. 20 years after the attack, they`re still waiting to go on trial. Catherine Herridge spoke with a woman whose fiancAc was killed in 9/11, Melissa Long has visited Guantanamo to push for trial. She calls it a case of justice delayed; justice denied.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He was a firefighter. We just had a very different connection than anybody I`ve ever been with before. And I truly loved him.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Melissa Long is describing her fiancAc Brian Sweeney, who gave an interview just before 9/11 about landing his dream job with one of the most storied fire houses in New York City. Rescue 1.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN SWEENEY, RESCUE 1: Everyone gives us a lot of respect because they always see us running into the burning building because everyone`s leaving.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: I see your face completely light up when you talk about Brian.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He makes me happy. I would never people like would you have rather never met him? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: On 9/11 Brian got the call to the World Trade Center.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I personally had a sense like that first night that he was gone. I just felt this emptiness in my heart.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Two weeks later, the Red Cross took Melissa to the still smoldering Ground Zero.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They show you the pile and you could smell it. I think it was their way of telling us that he was gone.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: It took 76 days for rescue teams to find Brian Sweeney. They identified him by his tattoos. In total, 343 New York City firefighters were killed on 9/11.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I stood there and I said, God didn`t do this, people did.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Melissa saw justice for Brian at the military courts in Guantanamo Bay, Cuba.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not about people who are saying they`re innocent.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: The five 9/11 suspects, including the self-described architect of the attacks, Khalid Sheikh Mohammed are stuck in legal limbo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I really do believe that they should have their day in court and I want my day in court and I want to hear the evidence. Defense Attorney Edward MacMahon Jr. represented 9/11 Al Qaeda suspect Walid bin Attash, who is accused of working directly with Al Qaeda leader \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: What explains a delay of two decades.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD MACMAHON JR. DEFENSE ATTORNEY: The military courts are unable to handle these trials in any efficient manner whatsoever. They`ve never had a complex death penalty case with multiple defendants.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: In 2009. President Obama`s effort to bring the defendants to trial in New York City failed. Yet from the very beginning, defense attorneys say there has been a government secret so closely held that it`s paralyzed the court.\par \par \uc2 MACMAHON JR.: I don`t think the CIA would care if this trial never went forward.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Before the 9/11 defendants got to Guantanamo, the Bush administration held them in CIA secret prisons, where they were interrogated using extreme tactics that critics called torture. To this day, the program remains highly classified.\par \par \uc2 Did you see evidence that showed your client at the time had been tortured in CIA custody?\par \par \uc2 MACMAHON JR.: I can`t answer that question.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Is it a classification issue?\par \par \uc2 MACMAHON JR.: I`m trying to figure out how to say this. I don`t get arrested. But I don`t believe the agency itself has ever conceded that it occurred. And certainly, have never conceded that they tortured anybody.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Who has the power to say enough is enough to end this.\par \par \uc2 MACMAHON JR.: The President of the United States. My prediction is that the 9/11 defendants will die of natural causes in Gitmo before there`s a trial.\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: Have the families been let down?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I mean, for sure. Nobody cares about the trials. There`s no political will, I guess you`d say for that to happen. And that`s a shame.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERRIDGE: The spokesman for the military trials tells CBS News, the cases are complex. And they are committed to a fair and just outcome, but offered no timeline. The Biden administration wants to close the Guantanamo detention camps. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 KING: Catherine, thank you. She raised some interesting questions there. 20 years does seem a very long time.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: You know it`s a good interview when this person you`re talking to you says, let me see how I can say this without getting rest.\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURLESON: Right. And when you don`t have answers, you constantly reminded of the pain.\par 