r first Grand Slam title beating 18 year old Martina Hingis.\par \par \uc2 KING: This is really shaping up. I think I`m going to go tomorrow. Listen, I was disappointed. Serena wasn`t coming, then Naomi got knocked out and I`m still going to go because I like the game. This just got very exciting to me. I`m calling for Leylah.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: So unexpected.\par \par \uc2 KING: She`s from Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: So, Raducanu was born to Romanian father and a Chinese mother and Fernandez daughter a Filipino Canadians and an Ecuadorian father. All right, are we good?\par \par \uc2 (Crosstalk)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: I looked at the camera, no time left. Well, maybe - mom running with her son as he saw.\par \par \uc2 KING: I love that video.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: I know. That`s very, very cute. We got 10 seconds though. All right. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Ahead, former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  will join us. We`ll be right back. Thank you for watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TONY PERKINS, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good Friday morning. I`m Tony Perkins. There are several 9/11 remembrance events planned for this weekend in the DMV. Fairfax County is hosting an event at the Bailey`s Crossroads volunteer fire department where many first responders deployed from to assist at the Pentagon on that day. That is set for 2 o`clock tomorrow. The Arlington Police Fire Sheriff and ECC 9/11 Memorial Race is also set for tomorrow afternoon. That goes into the evening. Keep in mind, there will be a number of road closures around the Pentagon for that event.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to Michelle for more on that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LOGAN, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Tony, those road closures are at 3 tomorrow afternoon. So, here are some of those closures to watch for from about 3:30 to 8:30, Washington Boulevard Westbound will be blocked between Memorial Circle and I-395. Also, Columbia Pike between South Joyce Street and Pentagon South parking. Then from approximately 5:30 to 8:30, Route 110 South will close from I-66 to Richmond highway plus South Joyce Street will be blocked between Army Navy Drive and Columbia Pike.\par \par \uc2 Everything should reopen at 8:30 tomorrow night. To see a full list of these closures, you can head over to our website WUSA9.com. Right now, let`s go to Miri for a check of the forecast.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: And it looks like the weather will be cooperating today and for all of the memorial services tomorrow. So, we will see temperatures today right there in the 70s with sunshine 76 by 3 o`clock in the afternoon. Over the weekend, highs up to 82 on Saturday, it`s mostly sunny. And Sunday, things get a little bit warmer. We`re up to 90 degrees but with plenty of sunshine, still up to 91 degrees by Monday, Tuesday back to the upper 80s and isolated showers possible there between Wednesday and Thursday, you`re going to notice those rain check is going up just a bit, 86 degrees on Wednesday, but isolated showers and 83 on Thursday with showers and storms and the forecast.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  Mornings. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210910eh9a0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fighter Pilots Recall Mission To Take Down Flight 93 On 9/11\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 988 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And as we approach the twentieth anniversary of the deadliest terror attack on U.S. soil, we wanted to share a story with you that few people have ever heard, about two fighter pilots who took off on 9/11 to protect the nation`s Capitol knowing it might be their last mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL MARC SASSEVILLE: It was a normal Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Lieutenant General Marc Sasseville and Heather Penney flew F-16s for the Air National Guard. On September 11, 2001, they watched two planes hit the World Trade Center.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER PENNEY: We knew immediately, as soon as we saw the images, that we needed to protect and defend.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Protect and defend the nation`s Capitol as commercial airliners had become weapons of war.\par \par \uc2 What did you understand about the rules of engagement, Heather?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER PENNEY: We understood what the threat was. We were looking for a rogue airliner flying low that was not communicating with air traffic control.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The pilots had no time to spare, the country was under attack. They knew of at least one plane still in the sky flying low. It was United Flight 93.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (recording; September 11, 2001): Mayday.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (recording; September 11, 2001): You got United 93?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: They didn`t even have time to load missiles.\par \par \uc2 So you two were flying F-16s that weren`t armed with missiles. So how were you going to take down Flight 93?\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL MARC SASSEVILLE: We were going to have to hit the airplane and-- and disable it somehow.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A Kamikaze mission.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER PENNEY: Our only choice was going to be to ram the airliner. Sir, I remember you would take the cockpit to aim at the terrorists, and I would take the tail.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That`s not something you survive.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER PENNEY: No. As the military, we don`t send our service members on suicide missions. But it was clear what needed to be done that morning.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It was not an order through the chain of command, it was your call to ram the plane.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL MARC SASSEVILLE: We didn`t have any other choice. And we weren`t going to be caught on the ground, watching America get hit again.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What they didn`t know was that the passengers and crew of Flight 93 fought back and drove the plane into a field in Shanksville.\par \par \uc2 Twenty years later, how often do you think about that day, 9/11?\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL MARC SASSEVILLE: I think about it every day, those on Flight 93 that paid the ultimate price, those are the real heroes.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER PENNEY: Sass and I owe our lives to them. That`s also why, when I think of 9/11, instead of being overcome by the trauma and the horror and the tragedy, I`m actually overcome by hope. That the best of who we are was demonstrated on that day. So, in some ways, living my life as normally as possible is the biggest way that we can say that the terrorists did not win.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s such an incredible story, isn`t it? I mean, I just think about the courage of so many heroes that day.\par \par \uc2 And this programming note, I`ll be anchoring from New York City for \uc2 CBS\uc2  News` live coverage marking the twentieth anniversary of the 9/11 attacks. Our special report will begin at eight-thirty Eastern Time on Saturday morning. Hope you`ll join us.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS, Senator Amy Klobuchar`s secret battle with breast cancer, and her message about getting checkups during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And quick reaction from the Air Force. What it`s doing as a result of our investigation into domestic violence in the military.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Democratic senator and former presidential candidate Amy Klobuchar made a surprising revelation today about her secret battle with breast cancer. Klobuchar says in February, a mammogram detected calcifications. A biopsy found stage 1A breast cancer, which was removed through surgery and radiation. Klobuchar tells our CBS Minneapolis station, WCCO, she, like so many women, put off screenings during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR: Thousands of women have undetected breast cancer right now and one in three Americans are not moving forward with just routine exams and physicals during the pandemic. And so doctors are seeing people that are so much sicker than they should be.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Klobuchar tells us she got a clean bill of health last month from her doctors. And a reminder, get your preventative screenings done.\par \par \uc2 And we have this update to our investigation into domestic violence in the armed forces. Our reporting found that the military is failing spouses, partners and service members who report abuse. Well, Air Force Secretary Frank Kendall says he`s extremely troubled by our findings, and as a result he has directed the Air Force inspector general to conduct a comprehensive review of those cases. He added, "There is absolutely no place for sexual assault, sexual harassment, or domestic violence in the Department of the Air Force." Our investigation having impact.\par \par \uc2 Up next, she lost her father on 9/11. How she channeled her grief into helping others.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210910eh99000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Justice Department Sues Texas Over Six-Week Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, a major legal showdown over abortion is looming. The \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  is suing Texas over its strict new abortion law. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says the Texas law is in open defiance of the Constitution. Here`s CBS` Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (CBS News Correspondent): It`s the United States versus Texas. The country`s top law enforcement officer filed a civil suit looking to stop the Lone Star State`s new abortion law, also known as SB8, that went into effect September 1st.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (United States Attorney General): The act is clearly unconstitutional under longstanding Supreme Court precedent.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The Texas law bans abortion once embryonic cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks, before many women know they are pregnant. The law makes no exception for victims of rape, sexual abuse or incest.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (R-Texas): Texas will work tirelessly to make sure that we eliminate all rapists from the streets of Texas.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The Texas law also allows any private citizen to sue someone who helps a woman get an abortion, which includes doctors, clinic staff or even someone who drives a woman to a clinic. Those who win a lawsuit can be awarded at least ten thousand dollars.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 : The statute deputizes all private citizens without any showing of personal connection or injury to serve as bounty hunters.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (May): Now, we`re about to make it law.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: SB8 was a legislative priority for Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott who celebrated when he signed the bill into law. Today, he said, "We are confident that the courts will uphold and protect that right to life." Democratic State Representative Senfronia Thompson applauds the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 SENFRONIA THOMPSON (Texas State Representatives): Thank God for the guts that the Biden administration has in making this challenge because every time you turn around, women rights are always being chipped away.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Planned Parenthood applauded the DOJ`s lawsuit. And in a statement to \uc2 CBS\uc2 , they told us Texans deserve the freedom and power to control their own bodies, adding, they hope this will restore access to health care. No word on when a court will rule on this case. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210910eh990008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Former U.S. Deputy Director of National Intelligence Sue Gordon; Interview With Jefferson Parish, Louisiana, President Cynthia Lee-Sheng; Interview With National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases Director \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 ; Interview With Gov. Phil Murphy (D- NJ); Interview With \uc2 Rep. Veronica Escobar\uc2  (D-TX)\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessi Mitchell, Weijia Jiang \par \uc2 7,838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG, HOST: I`m Weijia Jiang in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this Labor Day weekend on FACE THE NATION, Americans may be more than willing to turn the page on what`s been a sobering summer.\par \par \uc2 Back-to-school time brings new concerns about COVID, and the questions are more urgent and pointed. When will children be eligible for vaccines? When will boosters be available? And when will the pandemic ever end? We will check in with \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in the aftermath of Ida, will her legacy be a wakeup call on both climate change and the need for boosting America`s infrastructure?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need to be prepared for the next hurricane, and superstorms are going to come, and they`re going to come more frequently and more ferociously.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: We will talk with the leaders in the two states hit the hardest, New Jersey Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  and Cynthia Lee-Sheng.\par \par \uc2 Plus: The Texas anti-abortion law is now the strictest in the country. After the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to block the controversial new law, will other states enact similar ones?\par \par \uc2 We will hear from Texas Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  about the fight to preserve Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Finally: President Biden struggles to recover politically from the tumultuous withdrawal from Afghanistan. Are we prepared for the consequences?\par \par \uc2 We will talk with the former Deputy Director of National Intelligence Sue Gordon and our own David Martin on what we have learned about the war in the 20 years since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 It is all just ahead on FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 On this traditional last weekend of summer, there is a lot of news to get to this morning, developments on Afghanistan, abortion rights, and the extreme weather that`s wreaking havoc across the country. We will get to all of that very soon.\par \par \uc2 But We begin this morning with America`s COVID crisis and President Biden`s chief medical adviser, \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Fauci. It`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: I want to start with boosters, because we are just over two weeks away from September 20. That is the date that the administration had planned to start administering vaccine boosters for adults.\par \par \uc2 Is that still the plan?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: In some respects, it is.\par \par \uc2 We were hoping that we would get the -- both the candidates, both products, Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , rolled out by the week of the 20th. It is conceivable that we will only have one of them out, but the other will likely follow soon thereafter.\par \par \uc2 And the reason for that is that we -- as we`ve said right from the very beginning, we`re not going to do anything unless it gets the appropriate \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulatory approval, and then the recommendation from the \uc2 Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Looks like \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  has their data in, likely would meet the deadline. We hope that Moderna would also be able to do it, so we could do it simultaneously. But, if not, we`ll do it sequentially. So the bottom line is, very likely, at least part of the plan will be implemented, but, ultimately, the entire plan will be.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: So, I know that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the CDC have -- have said that there`s insufficient data, as you just mentioned, about the Moderna booster.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you can tell us about what data is still missing that you still need and how long it`s going to take to collect that information?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, the data are in two elements. One is safety, in other words, to get enough people that you`ve followed significantly along enough to say that it`s safe.\par \par \uc2 We feel almost certainly that it is, but you want to make sure -- when you`re dealing with allowing the American public to receive an intervention, you want to make sure you are absolutely certain. The other is the immunogenicity or efficacy.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIO GAP) you would predict would be protective. The companies getting their data together will submit it or even has submitted some of it, if not all of it, to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . The \uc2 FDA\uc2  will examine it and then make a determination whether, from a regulatory standpoint, it`s OK to go ahead.\par \par \uc2 So, it looks good. I mean, I think it`s going to be at the most a couple- of-week to few-week delay, if any.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And if the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is available on the 20th, has the eligibility changed for people who can start getting it?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, no, actually, the eligibility, as we`ve mentioned, is a regulatory decision and a recommendation from the advisory committee, so nothing has really changed with (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, if I had the Moderna vaccine, and I`m hearing that \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is going to be available come September 20, is it OK for me to mix and match? And what about for people who got the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: We are doing -- no, that`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 We are doing studies right now, which are just what you said. They are mix- and-match studies. Namely, we`re lining up \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  against \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Pfizer for Moderna, and vice versa. Hopefully, within a reasonable period of time, measured in a couple of weeks, we will have that data.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, we are suggesting -- and, hopefully, it will work out that way -- that, if you got \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , you will then boost with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . If you get Moderna, you`ll be boosting with Moderna.\par \par \uc2 But we are doing the studies to determine if we can do just that, switch one with the other.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Got it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I want to move on to kids who have COVID, because so many are returning to the classroom after this Labor Day weekend. And I know the CDC just released two reports this week, one that showed hospitalization rates in the U.S. for children and teenagers increased by nearly five times from the end of June to mid-August.\par \par \uc2 Can you help us understand that spike?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, it`s pretty easy to understand, because we`re dealing with the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 The Delta variant, as opposed to the Alpha variant, is much, much more transmissible. It has an efficiency of transmitting from person to person much, much more readily than previous variants. And so many more people, including children, are getting infected.\par \par \uc2 And that`s something that is not so (AUDIO GAP) variant that is so easily transmissible. You`ll get more children infected. In fact, when they get infected, just on a pure basis of the relative number of people that will actually get into the hospital, you`re going to wind up seeing more children in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And what can you tell us about the severity of those cases, in light of the Delta variant?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, we`re looking at that very carefully.\par \par \uc2 There is some indication in adults that the Delta variant might be more severe. But all the data that we are collecting right now does not give us any definitive information that the Delta variant is more severe in children.\par \par \uc2 We know, certainly, more children are getting infected, and, therefore, more are getting hospitalized. But we don`t have definitive enough data to say that, in fact, on a child-by-child basis, that it`s any more severe.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And I know that Delta remains very dominant.\par \par \uc2 But you said this week that you`re also keeping a close eye on the Mu variant. And the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has listed it as a variant of interest. What does a close eye mean?\par \par \uc2 What are you looking for?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, you`re looking to see if it becomes more dominant, namely, if the relative proportion of isolates in a given place, including in this country, becomes more.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we`re not seeing that. The Delta variant is over 99 percent dominant. So, when we say we`re keeping an eye on the Mu variant, we want to make sure it doesn`t become more dominant. We actually don`t know what the consequences would be.\par \par \uc2 The concern is that it has a few, a constellation of mutations that would indicate that it might evade the protection from certain antibodies. That`s what we mean when we say we`re keeping an eye on it. But, right now, it is not an immediate threat, even though we take all of these variants very seriously.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And is there any data available to gauge how effective the vaccine might be against Mu?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, no, I don`t think there`s any indication right now because we don`t have enough data.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the level of antibodies that our vaccines induce, particularly following the boost, I mean, we have data now that, when you give a third boost to either the Moderna or to \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , you (AUDIO GAP) that it`s very effective against any variant that we`ve tested.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the good news about our vaccines. If you get the level of antibody high enough, which boosters actually do, then you can feel pretty confident that you`re going to be protected against virtually any variant.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: OK, Dr. Fauci, thank you so much for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 And we`re sorry about some of those technical glitches you saw, but we heard you loud and clear. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: No problem. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: We turn now to the devastation left in the wake of Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 Ida`s path measured more than 1,000 miles from where it made landfall in Louisiana through the Northeast, bringing torrential rain and flooding and spawning tornadoes in at least six states.\par \par \uc2 At least 65 people are dead. In New Jersey alone, 27 people were killed, largely due to flooding.\par \par \uc2 The state`s governor, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , joins us now from Middletown.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Governor. Thank you for making time for us.\par \par \uc2 GOV. PHIL MURPHY (D-NJ): Good morning. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: I want to get the latest on the recovery from Hurricane Ida, but I do want to start with a quick question on COVID, since children are returning back to school in your state this week.\par \par \uc2 The "Star-Ledger" editorial board, the largest newspaper in your state, published an op-ed criticizing your decision not to require school staff to show proof of vaccination or submit to any testing until October 18.\par \par \uc2 Given how transmissible the Delta variant is, why not use every weapon you have at your disposal now?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I would just respond to say that we are, period.\par \par \uc2 Everyone in our school buildings will be masked, students, educators, staff, no exceptions. Educators, as we sit here today, are already at a very high level of vaccination. We think that time frame is a realistic one, and they will be required to have a vaccination and, if not, be subject to multiple tests per week.\par \par \uc2 We think that package of steps is what we need to have a safe environment, because we desperately need to get our kids back in person back into school, and we are confident we can achieve that.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: OK, thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of families are not only thinking about sending kids back to school, but right now how they`re going to get their kids and families back in their homes. President Biden is heading to New Jersey this Tuesday to look at all of the damage.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to ask him for any additional resources or assistance that you already know you need?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, the president and his team have been outstanding.\par \par \uc2 We have already asked for assistance. We`ve gotten the first step. And we will continue to ask for more because we need it. This was a historic storm, deadly, tragically, the loss of 27 lives, and still at least four persons missing, significant destruction of both homes, small businesses, roadways, in some cases, by the way, schools.\par \par \uc2 Folks -- first responders were extraordinarily heroic. Folks up and down the state were extraordinary. But there is a significant loss associated with this storm. We`ll do all that we can in the state, but we need the federal government in a big way. I`m confident they will be there for us. And I`m looking forward to having the president with us on Tuesday and seeing it up close with our own eyes.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: You said after this storm that New Jersey needs to update its playbook for storm responses -- quote -- "As it relates to our infrastructure, our resiliency, our whole mind-set, the playbook that we use, we have got to leap forward and get out ahead of this."\par \par \uc2 What was it about this storm that led you to that conclusion?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, I mean, this is something that we have been spending all of our hours thinking about, and we will be doing that, I suspect, for many years, if not decades to come.\par \par \uc2 We screamed loud and clear tornado warnings, flood warnings, flash flood warnings. We begged people to get off the road. And, still, you`ve got 27 losses of life and enormous destruction. We had rain in many communities in two or three hours that were equivalent to what they normally get in a month or two.\par \par \uc2 And this, sadly, we think, is part of the -- part of what we`re going to be facing, more frequency and more intensity. So, I mean that in terms of the playbook in every respect. We need a much more resilient infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 That infrastructure bill that`s being debated in Congress would have a huge positive impact on states like New Jersey. We`re the most densely populated state in America, a location second to none. But we`ve got infrastructure that was built for a different reality.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s the big piece of this. And we want to make sure that folks, when they hear these warnings, bless their souls, that they take them as seriously as we need them. And, God willing, we will be able to sharpen that as well going forward.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Mr. Governor, that reconciliation bill that you mentioned, it is going to have a tough road ahead to passage.\par \par \uc2 If it fails, what can you do, as a governor, to recreate that playbook?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think the failure would be historic.\par \par \uc2 I think it would be, sadly, an example where America did not meet the moment. The alarms have been going off for decades, if not longer. And the intensity and the loss of life and destruction that we`ve seen just this week is a stark reminder.\par \par \uc2 So, if it does not happen, with the heaviest of hearts, we will do everything we can inside of our state. But this is one example where the resources and support of the federal government is -- is vital. There`s no other -- there`s no other player that could fill that role.\par \par \uc2 As I say, the president and FEMA have been extraordinary in the -- in the reaction to the storm, but, as a proactive matter, we need Congress to step up right now.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Governor Murphy of New Jersey, thank you so much. And best of luck to you as you try to get your state through this.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: FACE THE NATION will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: We turn now to the situation in Louisiana following Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 CBS News reporter Jessi Mitchell has been there since the storm made landfall last Sunday.\par \par \uc2 JESSI MITCHELL, CBS NEWS REPORTER: Weijia, the Louisiana heat and humidity has become unbearable for the hundreds of thousands here still without power.\par \par \uc2 Officials said yesterday full restoration might not happen until the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voice-over): A week after Ida slammed the Gulf Coast, much of New Orleans is still in the dark and drying out, after deadly flooding in high winds.\par \par \uc2 Barrier island Grand Isle is uninhabitable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hopefully, we can start, get this cleaned. We`re going to take it one day at a time.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Supplies aren`t the only thing for residents to worry about. There`s more bad weather on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOHN BEL EDWARDS\uc2  (D-LA): Even if it visits our area as a tropical storm, we`re in no condition to receive that much rainfall.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And tragedy continues. Louisiana has ordered the closure of seven nursing homes following the discovery some of its residents relocated to a makeshift shelter at a warehouse. At least six have died.\par \par \uc2 State health officials said conditions in the warehouse were unhealthy and unsafe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To think that they would just put these people in a warehouse and leave them like that, I mean, why? Like, they`re not even human.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The owner of those nursing homes, Bob Dean Jr., spoke with local reporters last week.\par \par \uc2 BOB DEAN JR., NURSING HOME OWNER: We only had five deaths within the six days. Normally, with 850 people, you will have a couple a day. So, we did really good on taking care of people.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Louisiana`s governor said the state is going through a tough time. Ida`s death toll stands at 12. In the same period, 350 have died of COVID.\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: We had a winter storm, a flood, and all of this during the course of a pandemic. And, quite frankly, it`s more than a lot of people can bear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: At last count, some 2, 400 people remained in Red Cross shelters across the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 For some, it could be months before they return home -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Jessi Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go now to the president of Jefferson Parish in the greater New Orleans area, Cynthia Lee-Sheng.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Cynthia.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA LEE-SHENG, PRESIDENT OF JEFFERSON PARISH, LOUISIANA: Good morning. Thank you so much for having me on the show.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 You said in a recent briefing that the dangers after the storm are just as serious as during the storm. What are the most imminent dangers you are worried about right now? And how are you coping with them?\par \par \uc2 LEE-SHENG: Well, I mean, that`s definitely the case.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the storms that we`ve had in Louisiana in the past year, I think we`ve lost more lives post-storm. In Jefferson Parish, we only lost one life during the storm, unfortunately. I happen to know the person very, very tragic in Lafitte, where the -- where the waters rose very high.\par \par \uc2 But then, a couple of days later, I feel like, two days later, we lost three people to carbon monoxide poisoning. We lost another resident to a nursing home, with an issue with that, with the people who evacuated. And then we also had -- which is not directly storm-related, but we had a fatal shooting at a gas station because we`re living in a community that has just completely broken down.\par \par \uc2 And people are stressed. And it`s just a very fractured community right now. So, that`s absolutely the case. We`ve lost more people post-storm than we did during the storm.\par \par \uc2 And we`re, day by day, trying to put our community back together. You know, the fuel situation just hit us as a double whammy, in the fact that eight refineries got hit. So, the fuel at the source of it got hit. If this storm would have been anywhere else, they -- and the roads were passable, we would have been shipping our gas to them.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that our eight refineries got hit right where the storm hit land was just a double whammy for us in terms of trying to rebuild and trying to make sure our first responders have fuel, our critical needs have fuel. So, that was very unfortunate for us with this storm.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Well, I know the Biden administration has tapped into its emergency fuel reserve to try to help you guys out, but it sounds like that`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 Did you ask President Biden for more when you met with him on Friday? And what else did you ask from him?\par \par \uc2 LEE-SHENG: Yes. Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 We appreciated the visit, and I`m so glad that he got here to see the destruction firsthand. But fuel was absolutely the essential need. We talked about the assistance for our medical -- medical special needs people, because, as the days go on and people were staying at home, it was getting harder and harder to kind of take care of these people.\par \par \uc2 So, that was great news yesterday. I was at the New Orleans Convention Center meeting DMAT teams from Rhode Island and New Hampshire and law enforcement officers from California. It was such a bright way to start my day yesterday, to know that my -- the most vulnerable citizens we have in Jefferson Parish, we will be able to send.\par \par \uc2 They are -- if they can`t handle a bus ride outside to a mega-shelter in Bastrop, Louisiana, but they`re not critical enough to be in a hospital, we have major resources right here locally that we can send our vulnerable people. And that was, like I said, a great way for me to start the day yesterday to see all this, additional resources.\par \par \uc2 And then I told my team, when it`s just the days after the storm, it`s so difficult, because the problems are there. We don`t have the resources. We`re trying to do search-and-rescue, save lives. But, every day, additional resources come in from outside, and we feel better about things.\par \par \uc2 And that`s been really a relief, just getting the reinforcements in from outside.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And I know that, despite all of that help right now, you`re still asking some people who evacuated not to come back just yet.\par \par \uc2 Is that right?\par \par \uc2 LEE-SHENG: Yes, because we still have the fuel issue.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was very grateful that the school systems are not starting this week. My teams -- and this is my departments -- we have to get the water pressure up. We`re still under a boil water advisory. Our sewer system is largely dependent on the -- it is dependent on electricity.\par \par \uc2 Thank goodness all our hospitals are -- have electrical power now, our emergency operations center. Our core of communication for first response actually lost generator power. So, the electrical company got us powered up.\par \par \uc2 So, the critical infrastructure needs need to get powered up. And then \uc2 Entergy\uc2  is working on getting the neighborhoods back up slowly. But if you don`t have fuel, you don`t have electricity, water pressure is low, the sewer system is fragile, having the masses come back, it`s a community not ready for it.\par \par \uc2 But I think, as electricity comes on, every day, I think this gas situation hopefully will get better, we will bring people back. We -- all my job is, is to tell people this is the situation, this is the real-life situation. It`s up to them to make the personal decisions as to whether they want to return if they think life`s too difficult, financially, if they can stay out longer.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s an issue with the hotel bills. So, we`re not -- we`re just trying to get people accurate information for them to make their own decisions. But I really feel good about it. Every day, we`re going to get better and better on all of our systems across the board.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And, Cynthia, we are thinking of you as you do that.\par \par \uc2 And I`m so sorry about your personal loss. Thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 LEE-SHENG: Thank you. Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: If you`re not able to watch the full FACE THE NATION, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: We will be right back with a lot more FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. I`m Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 Texas now has the strictest anti-abortion law in the country. It bans the procedure after the sixth week of pregnancy and provides a $10,000 incentive to the public to police abortions. Last week, in a 5-4 decision, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to block that law from taking effect and now lawmakers in at least six states are considering enacting similar laws.\par \par \uc2 Texas Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  condemned the Supreme Court`s decision. She joins us now from El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR\uc2  (D-TX): Good morning, Weijia. And it`s a pleasure to be with you.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: It`s great to have you.\par \par \uc2 You called the new law one of the most draconian state laws to date. Since it took effect, I wonder, have you heard from constituents and health care providers about any immediate impact?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: I have, Weijia. People are shocked, horrified, outraged. We knew it was coming down the pipe because it was debated in our state legislature, but actually having it become law on September 1st has been pretty horrifying because of two really awful consequences. The first is the fact that neighbors and friends and family members are being incentivized to be bounty hunters. It`s incredible that we live in a state that is willing to incentivize that.\par \par \uc2 But there are also other real life consequences that are happening right now. Deadly consequences. There are folks who want to believe that you can eliminate abortion, but what -- what this law and other laws like it will do is simply make it deadlier, more dangerous. Women are going to take their health into their own hands. It will impact young women, poor women and women of color. And I am really afraid of the lives that will be lost as a result.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: And to help handle those immediate consequences and what you`re talking about, women seeking care, is there anything you`re asking from the Biden administration that they can provide? Whether that`s transportation to another state or funds for a trip like that, what are you asking from the president?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: Well, I`m grateful that the president wants to fight for women`s health care and wants to protect women from a state, and frankly a party, that is not pro-life, but is, instead, pro-birth, willing to put women`s lives on the line. So the Biden administration is looking at a whole of government approach, asking its agencies to take action. So I`m grateful for that.\par \par \uc2 But Congress needs to act as well. We are going to bring to the floor the Women`s Health Protection Act, which will put into law the protections that women need and require, that we`ve had for decades under Roe v. Wade. But, in the meantime, Weijia, I am afraid of what will be happening to women who are desperate right now, especially women who`ve suffered traumatic experiences, such as incest or sexual assault, whose -- who are trapped in the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said she would bring a vote on that act, which you are a co-sponsor of. But this week you also tweeted, expand the court, abolish the filibuster. Do you think that is the only way the bill stands a chance of passing?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: I am really afraid that because of the Senate`s desire to hang on to a relic of the past, a Jim Crow relic, the filibuster, that while the House will move expeditiously to protect women from states like Texas, and from a Supreme Court that is an activist court, my fear is, is that some members of the Senate will use the filibuster as an excuse to not take action. And in the face of inaction, we will see more deaths in Texas. Texas is now a very dangerous place for women and children. We are going to see more states basically import this law and do everything possible to create the most hostile conditions for women in our country.\par \par \uc2 And I do believe -- I am a supporter of eliminating the filibuster so that the American people will be able to see progress in government. And I believe the filibuster, which voters never voted for, which is not in the Constitution, and which is a relic of the past, it needs to be eliminated.\par \par \uc2 But I do think we also need to expand the court. We have now seen SCOTUS basically engage in late-night decisions through their shadow dockets. We`ve seen them become an activist court. We need to restore integrity to the court.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Earlier you did mention the way that this law is written and the fact that it outsources enforcement to private citizens. Are you worried that because of the way it`s structured, that`s going to make it really hard to challenge in court?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: It really will. And -- and the consequences -- I don`t think that folks really understand the legal consequences involved because this -- it`s so new to the American public. But basically here`s what can happen.\par \par \uc2 If, for example, I had a young daughter who was the victim of a sexual assault and I was taking my young daughter to a clinic, her assault -- the person who perpetrated the assault could take me to court, incentivized by that $10,000, and let`s say that -- that my young daughter was not pregnant, and there was no abortion involved, but I still had to defend myself in court, I can`t even recover legal fees. There are layer upon layer upon layer of injustices written into this law, intended to really send the most severe, chilling effect on women and women`s productive rights in the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: But, Congresswoman, you represent a state where 41 percent of people oppose abortion rights. Some of them not only approve this law, but they have spent years fighting for it and now they are celebrating.\par \par \uc2 How do you find common ground with those constituents on such a clearly emotional issue?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: And it really is a tough issue to discuss because each side is equally as dug in. And it is very hard to find common ground.\par \par \uc2 But what I have long talked about is if we want to lower the number of abortions taking place in our country, we have to do more for young women and men in terms of sexual -- sex education, in terms of access to contraceptives, in terms of legal protections for women. Instead, what we are seeing is an elimination of those things. And so it should be no surprise that more young women end up pregnant. But then we have a government that wants to limit their options once they are.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very backward way of addressing what is a concern. But that`s why I say there is no law that you can create that will eliminate abortion. All that`s happening is you`re eliminating safe, legal abortion, causing women significant harm.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Well, we are going to have to leave it there, Congresswoman, but thank you so much for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: Thank you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: With American troops now out of Afghanistan, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is working on securing its leadership to govern the country.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  News` senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata has the details from Doha, Qatar.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA, CBS NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, government officials here in Doha say they`ve been working very closely with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in Kabul to try to get that airport up and running again. It could mean a resumption of humanitarian flights for Americans and Afghan civilians holding special visas who are still trying to get out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 D`AGATA (voice over): In Kabul, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  broke up a demonstration by dozens of women demanding rights under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule. Eyewitnesses say shots were fired into the air. Images showed one woman bleeding from her forehead and suggests the latest in a series of protest after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  said women would not hold any cabinet positions in the new government. They have yet to announce that new government, likely to be led, anyway, by Taliban co- founder Mullah Abdul Ghani Baradar. The delay is apparently down to different \uc2 Taliban\uc2  factions jockeying for power under this new leadership.\par \par \uc2 A different battle is underway at the Tangier Valley, where the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have surrounded the last region not to have fallen to the militants. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  say they`re making progress in that valley, which is just north of Kabul. Those resisting inside say they continue to hold on.\par \par \uc2 In the capital, they`re kind of getting used to a new life under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Kids in soccer jerseys praying next to heavily armed \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters during Friday prayers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is literally white-washing the capital, repainting the blast walls of Kabul, as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  put it, getting it clean and ready for the new government.\par \par \uc2 And some surreal scenes few would have predicted under the extremist regime, a full-house turnout to a cricket match over the weekend. \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flags flying right beside Afghan flags.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 D`AGATA: U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is en route to Doha, partly in thanks to the government for helping to evacuate tens of thousands of Afghans and others over the past couple of weeks. As we understand it, there are no intentions to meet with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  delegation here in Doha.\par \par \uc2 Weijia.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here with us now is Sue Gordon, former principle deputy director of national intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Sue. It`s so great to see you.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN GORDON, FORMER DEPUTY DIRECTOR OF NAITONAL INTELLIGENCE: Good morning. Great to see you as well.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: As we heard from Charlie, there are still Americans and Afghans allies who are trying to get out. And one of the challenges is the ongoing threat from ISIS-K. The Biden administration has repeatedly said that their ability to carry out an attack like the one we saw at the airport is very different from their capability of striking the homeland.\par \par \uc2 Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: It is. I think one of the things to understand is the 20 years of our counterterrorism efforts, including that time in Afghanistan, has seriously degraded the ability of extremist forces to rally, gather their resources, and then to operate at distance. That does not mean that they can`t create havoc within Afghanistan, disrupt the formation of any new kind of government, and over time reconstitute themselves in a way that could be problematic for us.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: So 20 years later, can you answer if we are safer or not than we were on 9/11?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Oh, I think from a counterterrorism perspective, we are decidedly safer. We have collectively, with the help of allies and partners, waged great effort to disrupt the networks, to stake away the geography, and to make -- improve our efforts to detect things going on.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t mean that holds forever if you don`t continue to invest, and now, in the partnerships that are going to do the work in regions, where we decided to step away.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Well, the president`s critics have said that this withdrawal has set the stage for another 9/11 attack. Do you share any concerns that terrorists will use Afghanistan once again as a hub to breed more capacity?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Yes, I think you have to be concerned about that. Almost no matter what government the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is able to put into effect, they aren`t a government in the way we think about it, in terms of absolute control. Remember, they`re the extremists that we went in originally to fight. So it is a fraught area where you have tribal and extremist influences. So it`s worrisome.\par \par \uc2 I think the Biden administration now, when presence isn`t the advantage we have, when intelligence isn`t going to be as good because you don`t have that presence, really going to have to double down on the regional partnerships. And you`re going to have to rely on non-traditional partners, like Russia and China and Pakistan, to really do that work in the region, to try and influence the stability that is necessary to keep that threat from reemerging.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: You just said the intelligence not being as good. President Biden has insisted that the over the horizon capabilities are enough to monitor these threats. But I wonder if you think that`s true, and what we miss out on by not being on the ground.\par \par \uc2 GORDON: So truth in advertising, I have, over the course of my career, seen this nation develop amazing capability to discover information that we needed to have that we typically didn`t have access to. But especially in remote regions, where the data you want is not held in neat serving rooms, and physical presence allows you to the humans that have the information, you get a considerable amount from the interaction with those humans, the information they have, and the intention that comes with human intelligence. So I`m not going to say that we can`t develop over the horizon capabilities that are expanding on what we have today, but I will say that intelligence with presence is really important as well.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: I want to turn to the intelligence that we may have had before the chaotic withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Reuters published a report that includes excerpts of a phone call between President Biden and the former president of Afghanistan, \uc2 Ashraf Ghani\uc2 , dated on July 23rd. So three weeks before the takeover.\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: The president reportedly said, as you know, and I may not tell you the perception around the world and in parts of Afghanistan, I believe, is that things aren`t going well in terms of the fight against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And there`s a need, whether it`s true or not, there is a need to project a different picture.\par \par \uc2 The president is reportedly characterizing this threat as a perception, when clearly it was also the reality on the ground. Was that a failure in intelligence?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: I think any time lives are lost, and if you look at our withdrawal, there is a collective responsibility between the policy operational intelligence communities for that outcome. I would not characterize this as an intelligence failure for a couple of reasons.\par \par \uc2 Number one is, I think of our 20-year history involvement, the intelligence community has been pretty clear-eyed in terms of what the on the ground situation was and what the likelihood of persistence of the government if the U.S. withdrew.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think you have to acknowledge they apparently didn`t have precision in their estimate on the speed of the loss.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Well, I want to -- I want to -- I`m glad you mentioned that because later on in that same conversation, President Ghani said, Mr. President, we are facing a full-scale invasion, composed of \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , full Pakistani planning and logistical support. That does not match with what he has publicly said, which is, we had no idea the collapse was going to happen so fast.\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Is this another example of the president`s claims changing the perception from the reality?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Yes, I think a couple of things are clear to me with the caveat that I don`t know what other conversations were had and I wasn`t in the room when decisions were made. It seems clear from that conversation that they understood that the situation was fraught, that as least we could have foreseen the outcome, if not the speed.\par \par \uc2 I stop short of having a problem with him telling Ghani that he needed to project strength, right? Regardless of what the situation was, I is probably a reasonable comment that he needed to project strength. But it seems clear from that conversation that he knew the situation was fraught. And so I think his challenge is going to be how he speaks to the American people as more and more of these facts become evident.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Well, Sue, I could talk to you for another hour, but we are out of time. So, thank you so much for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: Saturday marks the 20th anniversary of 9/11. The attack that started the war in Afghanistan. And for the first time since then, there will be no U.S. troops in the country.\par \par \uc2 Senior national security correspondent David Martin was at the Pentagon on the day of the attack. He and his long-time producer, Mary Walsh, have brought \uc2 CBS\uc2  News` viewers every development in the war on terror since that devastating day. They`ve covered the war through four administrations and eight defense secretaries, and, most poignantly, they`ve brought us the stories of American forces fighting that war in person.\par \par \uc2 Here`s David and Mary`s look back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN, CBS SENIOR NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: With the Pentagon still burning and bodies trapped in the rubble, it was a small victory near the end of the day on 9/11 for Defense Secretary \uc2 Donald Rumsfeld\uc2  simply to hold a press conference.\par \par \uc2 DONALD RUMSFELD\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: The briefing here is taking place in the Pentagon. The Pentagon`s functioning. It will be in business tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Behind him, in a show of undivided political support, stood the heads of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 . Three days later, Congress passed a resolution authorizing the use of force. The U.S. unleashed its fire power, and by December, Rumsfeld was taking his first trip to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 RUMSFELD: They`re captured a number of al Qaeda. They`ve killed a number of al Qaeda. They`ve wounded a number of al Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: The U.S. military looked invincible, until March of 2002, when seven Americans were killed in the Battle of Robert`s Ridge. One of them, \uc2 Sgt. John Chapman\uc2 , can be seen on drone video charging across a snow field directly at an al Qaeda machine gun position.\par \par \uc2 American troops were tough and they were brave, but so was the enemy, fighting under the constant threat of being annihilated by airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 As in all wars, it came down to fighting as much for each other as for any cause.\par \par \uc2 Army Capital Will Swenson (ph) put his wounded sergeant, Kenneth Westbrook (ph) on a medevac and gave him a kiss before returning to battle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, we had a moment, we looked at each other. He - - he almost had a smile on his face.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): Did you ever see him again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I never saw him again. One month later he passed away at Walter Reed.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  was killed in 2011, but the war ground on.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, we visited the wounded at Walter Reed, where amputations, many of them multiple, were running at more than 20 a month.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): What was the damage?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Both legs amputated.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voice over): Staff Sergeant Eric Myers (ph) was on his third tour in Afghanistan when he stepped on a booby-trap.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): You know, most of the people back here think the war is winding down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Over there on the ground, it`s not winding down.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s still just as intense as it was.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voice over): The hard truth at Walter Reed was that Afghanistan changed them more than they changed Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Now the war has come full circle. Major General Chris Donahue (ph), the last man out of Afghanistan, was a junior officer serving in the Pentagon on 9/11.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: America`s longest war has come to a close.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Except now, no politicians were standing behind Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin and Joint Chiefs Chairman Mark Milley when they appeared in the Pentagon briefing room.\par \par \uc2 MARK MILLEY, JOINT CHIEFS CHAIRMAN: This is tough stuff. War is hard. It`s vicious. It`s brutal. It`s unforgiving. And, yes, we all have pain and anger.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I need something, please. It hurts.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: War is the dying cries of Specialist Chazray Clark (ph), bleeding to death waiting for a medevac to come to him, and the heartbreak of his young widow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My husband laid there wondering why nobody is coming to get him. For how long? Like 40 minutes? An hour?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: A 20-year war is one in which some of the last to die, those Marines killed by a suicide bomber at Kabul airport, were infants on 9/11, with no idea what was happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JIANG: David and Mary, plus all the \uc2 CBS\uc2  personnel who have been to Afghanistan and Iraq over the years, and those who have covered it here in Washington and New York, we thank you for your work.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 JIANG: That`s it for us today. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Margaret is winding up her last weekend off for maternity leave, and we are so excited for her return next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020210905eh9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Declines To Block Texas Abortion Law\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ben Tracy \par \uc2 529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, President Biden is blasting the Supreme Court decision allowing Texas` new abortion law to take effect. It is the strictest law in the nation and leaves women seeking abortions with few options. CBS` Ben Tracy is at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (CBS News Senior National Correspondent): The blockbuster decision from a sharply divided Supreme Court effectively allows almost all abortions to be banned in the state of Texas, affecting millions of women. Five of the conservative justices allowed the law to stand despite acknowledging there are serious questions regarding its constitutionality. Antiabortion advocates declared victory.\par \par \uc2 MARY CASTLE (Texas Values Policy Adviser): With this bill, we do hope to see an end to abortion.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  sided with the three liberal justices. In a blistering dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called her colleagues` decision ".stunning." and the Texas law "flagrantly unconstitutional.." This clinic in Fort Worth is nearly empty today, forced to turn away patients or send them across state lines.\par \par \uc2 MARVA SADLER (Whole Woman`s Health Clinical Services Senior Director): Most of them this morning told us it`s not about the finances of getting to another state. It`s the days off of work. It`s finding someone to take care of their children and their families, and it`s impossible.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The Texas law bans abortion after about six weeks of pregnancy, when a so-called "fetal heartbeat" is detected. A controversial term because of how undeveloped the embryo is at that stage. The law makes no exception for rape or incest. Texas Right to Life is now encouraging people to provide tips about anyone trying to break the new law. And clinics in Oklahoma and Kansas say they are now seeing patients arrive from Texas. President Biden slammed the high court`s decision not to intervene, calling it an ".unprecedented assault on a woman`s constitutional rights under Roe v. Wade." and directed federal agencies including the Justice Department to try to ensure women in Texas have access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Ben Tracy joins us now. So, Ben, this Texas law used a novel legal approach by deputizing citizens to enforce it. The justices noted that, right?\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That`s right, Norah. This Texas law actually encourages private citizens to sue anybody who provides or helps a woman get one of these now- banned abortions. And if they win one of those lawsuits, they`re entitled to at least ten thousand dollars. In his dissent, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said it`s not only unusual, it`s unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ben Tracy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210903eh920005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New York, New Jersey Declare States of Emergency After IDA`s Heavy Rain, Tornadoes; Deadly, Historic Flooding Hits Northeast; Stranded by IDA; Supreme Court Vote on Texas Abortion Law; Ahead: Busted for Fake Vaccine Card\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Vladimir Duthiers, Tony Dokoupil, Chester Lampkin,  Mola Lenghi, Jeff Berardelli, Omar Villafranca, Carter Evans, Ben Tracy,  Annie Yu \par \uc2 7,282 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, \uc2 CBS\uc2  HOST, \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING: Good morning to you. It`s Thursday, September 2nd 2021. I`m Gayle King. That`s Vlad Duthiers and that`s Tony Dokoupil. Welcome to "\uc2 CBS\uc2  This Morning". Let`s go straight to today`s "Eye Opener" it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s like a war zone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are enduring an historic weather event.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is what climate change looks like that.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice over): IDA slams in northeast with historic rain flash flooding and tornadoes. Why these massive storms could be much more common?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The rainfall rates have been off the charts.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice over): There are some dramatic scenes tonight - all that rain left commuters trapped.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This has been a tremendously devastating storm but catastrophic damage.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice over): The situation in Louisiana remains dire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No power, no gas, no food for hundreds of thousands of people. President Biden set to visit on Friday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Supreme Court has refused to block Texas`s six week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are no exceptions under this new law for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDNETIFIED FEMALE: Firefighters saw progress against the Caldor fire weather forecast say gusty winds could become a problem all that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We visit a so called gaming detox facility in China as the government cracks down on screen time.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice over): All that matters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Plus the economic fallout of working from home we talk to people who rely on others to go into the office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is pre COVID business booming, big cart?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMAE: Why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now something is empty.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice over): On "\uc2 CBS\uc2  This Morning".\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pew Poll found that American parents are divided on school mask mandates. 48 percent of parents want all students to be mastered school while 41 percent say that they oppose a mandate, but 100 percent of parents agree that they can`t take another single day of zoom school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by "Progressive" making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: That doesn`t need to be a universal feeling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING CO-HOST: --confirmed.\par \par \uc2 KING: But we`re going to begin with some really rough news. We had a really tough night here on the East Coast and we`re going to begin with the extreme weather now being slammed by what`s left of Hurricane IDA. This system is currently over New England after cutting a destructive path through multiple states affecting at least 57 million people.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York the entire subway system was shut down due to the flooding. Take a look at these pictures. And many roads were inundated trapping drivers in their cars. At least nine people were killed across the area, New York and New Jersey declared states of emergency.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS, \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING CO-HOST: The remnants of IDA also spun off multiple tornadoes including this one in Bristol, Pennsylvania. Mola Lenghi is in Middlesex, New Jersey Mola what do you see this morning? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning, Vlad. Yes, Middlesex, New Jersey, we`re nearby Brown Brook flooded overnight creating this mess that you see here behind me. As you can see the roads impassable authorities have shut down.\par \par \uc2 Nearby homes had to be evacuated this morning and overnight, but really no matter where you were in the northeast, in the last 24 hours or so you likely got clobbered by some sort of severe weather between the flooding and the tornado damage. Statistically, in the New York City area this type of rain happens only once every 200 years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LENGHI (voice over): The tornado, a tornado tore through Southern New Jersey Wednesday possibly the strongest to ever hit the state and rip the sides off some homes and turned others into a pile of rubble as the remnants of Hurricane IDA spun off other possible twisters in Pennsylvania the storm set water pouring into New York City subways, forcing all trains to stop.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 LENGHI (voice over): Rain flooded the streets reaching some car windows --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENITIFIED MALE: The cars are floating --\par \par \uc2 LENGHI (voice over): And lifting others right off the road. The National Weather Service issued a flash flood emergency for the first time ever in New York City as strong winds blew rain sideways into the covered U.S. open Stadium in Queens during a match forcing play to be suspended.\par \par \uc2 In Brooklyn rescue crews worked to save drivers trapped in the flood. And this biker found himself in waist deep water apparently trying to deliver someone`s dinner. Meanwhile, some buildings in Manhattan were no match for the flood. Water rushing into these apartment parts of the region got three inches of rain in just an hour believed to be a record in the area.\par \par \uc2 The destruction in the northeast came after Ida wrecked-havoc in parts of Virginia and Maryland burying cars in mud. In Indianapolis area a tornado tore down power lines leaving this tangled mess of trees and wires.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LENGHI: --nearby Raritan River is expected to get 14 feet above flood stage by tomorrow but as far as the storm the remnants of IDA Tony that appears to be behind us.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Well, that is good news Mola. Thank you very much. For more on the storm`s path we are joined now by CBS News Meteorologist and Climate Specialist Jeff Berardelli. Jeff, good morning to you what are you looking at?\par \par \uc2 JEFF BERARDELLI, CBS NEWS METEOROLOGIST AND CLIMATE SPECIALIST: Good morning, Tony. Good morning, everybody. If you`re watching yesterday, you heard me say that this was a once in a century event?\par \par \uc2 Well, it turns out by some measurements, it`s a once in a 500 year type event, meaning that we should only experiences once since our country`s founding, that`s how epic this was. Now, the system made its way across New Jersey, Pennsylvania, New York City, Connecticut, producing rain rates up to four inches an hour now the heavy rains about to exit Southeast New England.\par \par \uc2 Well, what`s amazing about this system is it produced all this heavy rainfall across the Gulf Coast, then a bit of a break, and then a tremendous amount of heavy rain also in the northeast. So what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 Storm made landfall, 17 inches of rain in places along the Gulf Coast. And then it was supercharged again, once it got to the New York City here is that now in getting 13 to 14 inches of rain. Here`s one of the reasons tropical connection of moisture, you can see this tale of moisture extending into the Gulf into the Atlantic and all of it dumping right on top of the New York City and Pennsylvania area.\par \par \uc2 And then number two, IDA merged with a jet stream and a cold front. So it`s super charged IDA. And it wrung out all the moisture, all that tropical moisture connection, wrung it out right on top of New York City on top of New Jersey on top of the Hudson Valley, Connecticut. And now that system is moving out to the east.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line here is that a supercharged storm does obviously a lot more damage. Remember, it didn`t do much along the way. And then all of a sudden, it just kind of came back to life.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: So Jeff, we talked about these storms as once in a century events or once in the history of the country events, and yet they`re happening more frequently, it sure seems like we have to update our numbers.\par \par \uc2 BERARDELLI: For sure so you know, what I like to say is climate change makes the impossible not only possible, but it makes it probable. The bottom line is a warmer atmosphere holds more moisture; it`s like a bigger sponge, that sponge can hold more moisture than it can wring out even heavier rainfall.\par \par \uc2 So in the northeast, you can see the clear trend on this graphic. Over the last 60 years we`ve seen a 50 percent increase in heaviest rain events in the New York City area and all across New England. And you can see that clear trend. When the air is two degrees warmer, which it is right now it holds 8 percent more moisture.\par \par \uc2 But storms do a really good job pulling all that extra moisture and kind of congregating and kind of bolstering that even more. In Harvey we saw 20 to 40 percent more rain than we would have otherwise seen without climate change and infrastructure and asphalt in the New York City - makes a big difference.\par \par \uc2 KING: I did go to New Jersey last night for a funeral. It was very, very, very scary on the roads last night and this is just a - you call it a remnant. This is what a remnant looks like.\par \par \uc2 BERARDELLI: This is going to happen a lot more it`s going to get a lot worse in the future as we keep warming the planet.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: And as you say Gayle a remnant and we`re going to go to Louisiana now where they got the brunt of it.\par \par \uc2 KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Jeff as always my friend thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BERARDELLI: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: All right. President Biden is also expected to visit Louisiana tomorrow to survey IDA`s catastrophic impact there. So we`ve been telling you hundreds of thousands of people are still without power and many cannot get food, water or fuel. Omar Villafranca is in Kenner, Louisiana, Omar. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Vlad. More generators are being brought in. So we`re starting to see some lights pop up across the city. That`s going to be really helpful when you`re trying to clean up damage like this at this warehouse.\par \par \uc2 The Governor says there are more line workers in the state than at any other point in history. But for the hundreds of thousands of people who are still without power things can`t move fast enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (voice over): Four days without power and New Orleans is starting to boil.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s hot and tempers are fraying.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (voice over): Long lines for military rations Ice and fresh water. Ms. Emily Joel Simmons (ph) says she walks 10 miles just to fill her cart with supplies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I woke up this morning. I got me a pillow and a blanket. And I say I will go to sleep.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (voice over): There has been some progress. Power was restored for more than 11,000 people in East New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There was a breath you know.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (voice over): But many are still in the dark.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And there is not by morning I`m leaving.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (on camera): Without water and power the frustration is growing. But here on Grand Isle, the residents who live here haven`t even had a chance to see what`s left of their island.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (voice over): And for most of those 1400 residents, there`s not much to come back to. Homes were blown apart by IDA`s ferocious winds. Power line snapped and twisted. Seven miles of barrier islands` stripped of life sustaining infrastructure. We surveyed the destruction with Mayor David Camardelle.\par \par \uc2 DAVID CAMARDELLE, NEW ORLEANS MAYOR: 100 percent of the - the homes and businesses major damage. 85 percent is total.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (on camera): 85 percent?\par \par \uc2 CAMARDELLE: 85 to 95 percent is total. We have no food. We have no water. We shut the gas off and we have no electricity. I will never talk I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (on camera): Well, you got you got time here you got memories here.\par \par \uc2 CAMARDELLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA (on camera): Those who stayed behind have started to rebuild and supplies are starting to arrive. But while the road to recovery will long Camardelle is determined to bring Grand Isle back better than ever.\par \par \uc2 CAMARDELLE: We`re born and raised here. This is paradise. You know - is one grain of sand of Grand Isle I`m going to a plant American flag. I can promise you today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VILLAFRANCA: At last check nearly 3000 people were staying in about 30 shelters across the state and in the age of COVID. That is a concern, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 KING: Oh, I feel for that man. Thank you very much Omar. Firefighters in California braved severe weather conditions overnight to protect the resort town of South Lake Tahoe from the massive Caldor fire. Now the wind has died down just a bit but the fire has now expanded to more than 320 square miles.\par \par \uc2 Officials say it is expected to grow much larger before they can get it fully under control. Carter Evans is in Myers with more on the story Carter, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning. The good news now those high winds have finally calmed down but the fire is still burning them. We`re less than 50 yards away from homes that are heavily guarded by exhausted firefighters who`ve been battling this place for weeks with no clear end in sight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVANS (voice over): The Caldor fires pushing east toward the Nevada state line over to rain so dry and dangerous officials say they may be forced to let it burn for miles.\par \par \uc2 BILL MONDAY, CALL FIRE: This is some steep rugged and nasty country and it`s just not safe in a lot of it to put our firefighters up there in that stuff.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (voice over): Many of the more than 4200 firefighters attacking this place have now been working nonstop for 19 straight days using everything from helicopter water drops to bulldozers in-hand tools. So far they`ve been able to keep the flames away from South Lake Tahoe partly by lighting backfires and clearing brush to slow the fires advance.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (on camera): The wind is picking up right now but this fire is actually backing down the hill and that`s what firefighters want to see a big concern though are dead trees like this one. If this were to catch fire, it could fall across the fire break - built endangering homes on the other side.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (voice over): As flames now approach the Nevada State line the threatened heavenly ski resort is being transformed into a base camp for firefighters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s a lot of work to be done down here. A lot of threat still down here with these small spot fires that are still burning down here.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (voice over): For Battalion Chief Dave Lochner (ph) the battle is personal. He`s in danger of losing his parents cabin.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (on camera): Fatigue has to come into play. You`ve been on this fire since the beginning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, there are days where we`re exhausted but that are the job. We`re ready to go and we push through it. We`re here till the end.\par \par \uc2 EVANS (on camera): One setback for firefighters this weekend entire strike team of 16 people was sidelined because of a COVID outbreak. In California now Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is asking President Biden for an emergency declaration so they can get federal assistance fighting this fire, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Carter Evans for us, thank you. We have breaking news overnight from the Supreme Court in a 5-4 vote the court refused to block a new Texas law that is the most restrictive abortion law in the country. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  sided with the three liberals.\par \par \uc2 The court`s decision does not prevent it from considering this case in the future. Abortion rights supporters are protesting law calling it unconstitutional. Ben Tracy is outside the Supreme Court Ben, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY, CBS NEWS, SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Vlad, good morning. In her dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called the court`s decisions stunning. She says this Texas law flouts nearly 50 years of federal precedent. But for now, this Texas law which bans abortion after just six weeks of pregnancy, which is before most women even know they`re pregnant, will remain in place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice over): Abortion rights protesters at the Texas State Capitol Wednesday vowed to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SARAH ECKHARDT (D-TX): That it is our right our individual right to choose whether and when we`re going to have children.\par \par \uc2 TRACY (voice over): Across Texas abortion providers warn they may have to close their doors and that the new law will only lead to illegal and dangerous abortions.\par \par \uc2 KATHY KLEINFIELD, HOUSTON WOMEN`S REPRODUCTIVE SERVICES ADMINIS