es are too complicated and too hard to fix, had put the National Owners Association, which is a group of franchisees said this, we are tired of being the butt of late night jokes. So, are our customers and crews.\par \par \uc2 My first thought was over to you, Colbert and Corden.\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Over to you, Colbert.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Two words, Dairy Queen.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Blizzard.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Blizzard.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: OK, Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: That`s the original.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: All right, there was little love at the U.S. Open last night over a disruptive bathroom break. Stefanos Tsitsipas of Greece, the number three seed left the court for nearly eight minutes. Eight minutes in the middle of his match against Adrian Mannarino of France. You could see Mannarino trying to stay warmed up while waiting. Tsitsipas defended himself to reporters after he won the match. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEFANOS TSITSIPAS, TENNIS PLAYER: I haven`t done anything wrong. So, I don`t understand. Sometimes we need a short break to do what we have to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: The official Grand Slam rule says the player leave to the court for a reasonable amount of time for a toilet break. The rule doesn`t say how long is too long. I thought of Tony Dokoupil because of your bathroom break.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: All right, hold on. I go for a little tinkle break. I don`t know what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: They`re never eight minutes long though.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: That`s a very serious conversation about a bathroom break. Vlad, thank you very much for that embarrassing commentary. Ahead, an update on the extreme weather in the Northeast. And the latest on the historic flooding in New York and New Jersey. You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  This Morning. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANNIE YU, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good Thursday morning. I`m Annie Yu. The cleanup continues this morning after severe weather hit our area. Some of the worst of it is in Edgewater and Anna Rondo County and a lot of the significant damage is at South River High School, specifically the football field there.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said thankfully the school itself didn`t suffer much damage and no one was injured. This is all when a tornado came through the area. A school board meeting was also going on at the time, so staff were inside the building, as were some students helping out with orientation.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with brothers, a freshman and senior who said they were outside when they heard the principal say that they had to come in and take cover.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was a little bit all inspiring, but also terrifying knowing the fact that if we were outside and we didn`t hear the principal come on and say, come inside that we could have been caught in the crosshairs of that damage, just playing spike ball. And the fact that you know, it could have wiped us out too, because it was really right there. It was certainly scary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YU: School officials say they are still addressing if there`s any real structural damage to the school, but they`re hoping all will be well enough to start school on Wednesday, September 8th, as scheduled. Chester?\par \par \uc2 LAMPKIN: All right, good luck to all the cleanup crews out there. And here, locally, temperatures not too bad. We`ll be in the 70s around 80 degrees including our friends out into Annapolis as they do the cleanup work out there. A lot of sunshine, dry skies. We need that, a lot of folks did get some flooding yesterday and last night. That should help us mitigate those issues next couple of days, looks really nice. Near 80 on Saturday, 82 Sunday with a couple showers, won`t see any severe weather this weekend. And back to the mid-80s on Monday on Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KING: It`s Thursday, September 2nd, 2021. We welcome you back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  This Morning.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210902eh9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas` Near-Total Abortion Ban Takes Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ben Tracy \par \uc2 529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, it is nearly impossible to get an abortion in Texas. The most restrictive abortion law in the country went into effect after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  declined to act on a request to block it. CBS` Ben Tracy on what makes this Texas law different from others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): Bans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (CBS News Senior National Correspondent): Abortion rights advocates protested outside the Texas state Capitol today as clinics began turning away patients. Abortion providers say Texas has essentially outlawed eighty-five percent of abortion procedures which will force many clinics to close. Amy Hagstrom Miller runs four clinics providing abortion services, and is one of the plaintiffs asking the Supreme Court to intervene.\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER (Whole Woman`s Health): It`s heartbreaking to deny them the care that our staff are fully trained and-- and ready to provide. So, really, most people are going to be forced to carry a pregnancy against their will.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The law, signed by Republican Governor Greg Abbott, bans abortion once a so-called fetal heartbeat is detected, usually around six weeks. That`s before many women even realize they are pregnant. The law appears to contradict the Supreme Court decision in Roe v. Wade, which guarantees the right to abortion until fetal viability, about twenty-two weeks. It is also highly unusual because it bars state officials from enforcing the law, but instead, allows private citizens to sue doctors, clinic staff, and those helping women get abortions. Anyone who wins a lawsuit is entitled to ten thousand dollars. Antiabortion advocates say even a person driving someone to a clinic should be held responsible.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER GARDNER (Central Texas Coalition for Life): If he knows and he`s taking her there fully aware, you know, that this is illegal to do, then, yeah, I think he should be held accountable. This is very much like an accomplice in-- in other types of crimes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Now, this fall, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will review Mississippi`s ban on abortion after fifteen weeks of pregnancy. Conservatives now have a six-three majority on the court, and, Norah, that case could overturn Roe v. Wade, which established that right to abortion back in 1973.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The legal battle heating up. Ben Tracy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the desperate search continues for five crew members of a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  helicopter that crashed into the Pacific Tuesday afternoon, sixty miles off San Diego. One crew member was rescued. The chopper had been aboard the USS Abraham Lincoln. There is no word on the cause.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210902eh910008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Are Celebrating The Withdrawal Of American Troops And Proudly Showing Off The Equipment The U.S. Military Left Behind\b0\par\par\uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS, CHARLIE D`AGATA, TONY DOKOUPIL \par \uc2 1,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are celebrating the withdrawal of American troops and proudly showing off the equipment the U.S. military left behind. The victors still face what could be an even bigger challenge than the war. Charlie D`Agata reports on what the future holds for Afghanistan under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule. He is in Doha, Qatar. Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (CBS News Senior Foreign Correspondent): Good morning. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  says they are now close to announcing a new government, just one or two days away now. But for a country on the verge of collapse, it can`t come quickly enough.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Taking over the country was the easy part. Now comes the job of running it, like the airport where \uc2 Taliban\uc2  members holding American weapons badmouth outgoing U.S. forces.\par \par \uc2 (Man #1 speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "As you can see, these infidels have destroyed the entire airport," he says. "We had a team ready to fix this mess. Now the Americans have gone, we are ready to clean it up." It`s now a graveyard of U.S. military hardware and bereft of the technical know-how to even get a reliable air traffic control system in place, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are now turning to Qatar and Turkey to get it back up and running. Even with flights stopped, some Afghans tried to get in. \uc2 Taliban\uc2  guards turned them away. Then there`s the financial system. There`s been a run on the banks with panicked people trying to withdraw cash in the chaos. Some have waited in line for days.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I should build a future. I should study. So definitely if the situations are like that, you should stay for one week just to take ten thousand Afghani-- one hundred dollar from a bank. So, it`s not possible to live here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: With many employees now too terrified to return to work, even basic necessities like electricity and water are under threat. The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . has already warned of an absolute catastrophe of economic ruin and even famine. Yesterday, crowds attended a mock funeral held by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , coffins draped in flags of America and coalition allies. But as celebrations subside, the country may soon be facing more funerals of its own.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In fact, between the conflict and a widespread drought, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . now warns the country is marching towards starvation and some regions could run out of food within a month. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie D`Agata for us in Doha. Again, Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A search and rescue operation is underway for five missing people after a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  helicopter crashed in the Pacific Ocean. Navy officials say the chopper went down more than sixty miles off the coast of San Diego yesterday. The aircraft and crew from the USS Abraham Lincoln were conducting a routine operation at the time. At least one crew member has been res-- rescued, and the Coast Guard and Navy are taking part in the search.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of the strictest abortion laws enacted in this country since Roe V. Wade takes effect in Texas today. Now, it bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. The law`s opponents say that`s before many women even know that they are pregnant. It comes amid an uproar over another Republican-backed bill that Democrats say will limit people`s ability to vote. Nikole Killion is following both of the measures. Nikole, intense feelings on both sides about these. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (CBS News Congressional Correspondent): Yeah. Absolutely, Gayle. And the Supreme Court did not act overnight on an emergency request to stop the new Texas abortion law from going into effect. Opponents warn it would essentially overturn Roe versus Wade by outlawing nearly all abortions in the state. The law is known as SB8 and went into effect at midnight. Not only does it ban abortions as early as six weeks into pregnancy, it also allows individuals to sue anyone who helps a woman get an abortion. And those who are successful in their lawsuit could be awarded at least ten thousand dollars. This is happening in a much larger context. State legislatures set the record this year for the most abortion restrictions signed in one year since Roe versus Wade in 1973. Meanwhile, the Texas state legislature approved a Republican-backed voting restrictions bill Tuesday. The measure would ban drive-thru voting, set new limits on early voting, and provide protections for partisan poll watchers. Republicans say it`s to strengthen election security. Democrats argue it would disproportionately impact voters of color and those with disabilities. They even came here to Washington for more than a month to try to stop the measure. So now, as the bill heads to Governor Abbott`s desk, voting rights groups are threatening legal challenges. Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Nikole Killion, we`ll see what happens ahead. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a crackdown on kids and video games. The new rules in China that spell out how long kids can play online.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is seven seventeen, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Doctor David Agus shares a new idea to keep international travel open as we fight this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a look at a 48 HOURS special in the murder case against Chad Daybell and his second wife Lori Vallow Daybell.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: I am Jonathan Vigliotti, 48 HOURS. He`s charged with the murders of Tylee and JJ. Chad Daybell`s children speak out together for the first time.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MURRAY: He was framed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: That`s coming up on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Britney Spears is making an explosive new allegation in the fight to have her father step down as conservator of her estate. Her lawyers are accusing him of extortion, according to newly filed court documents. Britney`s attorney alleges Jamie Spears is trying to get two million dollars in payments before he steps down as conservator. Her attorney said, quote, "Britney Spears will not be extorted." Jamie Spears has not responded to requests for comments. He has controlled much of his daughter`s life since 2008 when the conservatorship began. He said last month that he had planned to step down, but did not give a timetable. Next court hearing is September 29th.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all-- we always hear that there are two sides to every story. But the side we`re hearing is not a good look for dad, as you said--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --as the kids of today said--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --optic`s not good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Strategic decision from dad not to speak up, but I would love to hear from him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looking forward to that comment, Mister Spears.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. The multi-platinum selling pop band OneRepublic is out with new music.\par \par \uc2 (OneRepublic performing; Interscope Records)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s "Someday" from their new album. Coming up, OneRepublic`s frontman, Grammy winner Ryan Tedder talks about his career after taking time off to focus on his mental health. Plus, find out why he`s interested in collecting much more than just musical hits.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (7:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210902eh910005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CDC Urges COVID Vaccines During Pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tonight, the CDC urges pregnant women to get vaccinated as Delta variant infections continue to spread. The new safety data and concern tonight that more than seventy-five percent of mothers-to-be are currently unvaccinated. We`ll have the details.\par \par \uc2 School mask anger.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): No more masks. No more masks.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: California orders all teachers to be vaccinated or tested regularly.\par \par \uc2 Making history. New York`s first female governor-in-waiting wastes no time distancing herself from \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 : I think it`s very clear that the governor and I have not been close.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Summer danger. Nearly two hundred million Americans under heat alerts, triple digit temperatures across the country. Plus, tracking Tropical Storm Fred. Will Florida get a direct hit?\par \par \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takes more territory, Afghan soldiers surrendering. What the U.S. is saying about Afghanistan`s future.\par \par \uc2 Unprecedented murder case. Two coaches are charged in a teenage athlete`s death after she collapsed on a hot summer day. Our interview tonight with her father.\par \par \uc2 Prices on the rise. From groceries to hotels, why seemingly everything costs more. When could prices come down?\par \par \uc2 Out of focus. How the pandemic is impacting children`s eyesight and what parents can do about it.\par \par \uc2 And the purr-fect match. Senior citizens, meet your new senior companion.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS EVENING NEWS with Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us. Norah is off. I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 We begin with scenes and statistics we thought were well behind us. Hospital admissions in Texas are higher than they`ve been since February. Ventilators are being sent in bulk to Florida. And a field hospital is going up in Mississippi. All because of COVID`s summer surge fueled by the Delta variant that is tearing through every part of our country. Today, the CDC urged all pregnant women to get vaccinated. California became the first state to order its public school teachers and staff to do the same or submit to weekly testing. And there`s other breaking news on the vaccine front. Sources tell CBS News that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  plans to allow people with certain underlying health conditions to get a booster dose of either the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Moderna shot. CBS` Janet Shamlian leads us off in Houston. Janet, please tell us, how critical is this moment in Texas?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (CBS News Correspondent): Major, good evening. Take a look. We are inside of a tent. It`s equipped with supplies and hospital beds, just like an emergency room, even though there is a hospital emergency room just outside of this tent. But tonight, it is so full, they needed a backup plan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Tonight, the Delta variant is devastating the South. They`ve put up an overflow tent outside Houston`s LBJ Hospital. Inside, a dozen emergency room patients are waiting for beds in the ICU.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DARNAUER (Administrator, LBJ Hospital): We are challenged. We are stressed in our hospital capacity.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: More than twenty thousand new cases reported across Texas on Tuesday, the highest in six months, and spiking just as kids are going back to school. The San Antonio and Austin districts defying the governor`s no-mask mandate, telling teachers, students, and staff masks will be required. And two Texas judges ruling local officials can require masks.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): No more masks. No more masks.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Anger spilling over in Franklin, Tennessee, Tuesday outside a school board meeting, parents yelling at medical professionals, who advocated for masks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We know who you are.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Florida, the number of new cases is now averaging more than twenty thousand a day for the first time since the pandemic started. Two hundred ventilators and other supplies were sent to the state from the national stockpile, though on Tuesday, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said he was unaware of the shipment.\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS\uc2  (Tuesday): We have-- I mean, I-- I would honestly doubt that that`s true, but I`ll look.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And tonight, there`s new guidance, the strongest yet, from the CDC urging women who are pregnant or plan to become pregnant to get the vaccine, saying there is no increased risk of miscarriage. It`s guidance Jamal Chubb wishes he and wife, Cierra, had sooner. Cierra gave birth to a healthy baby boy two weeks ago while COVID positive. Her condition deteriorated and she`s now on a ventilator in the ICU. The South Carolina mom of three had not been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 JAMAL CHUBB: For us, it was because she didn`t know enough, and she didn`t feel confident when it came to getting the vaccine that it wouldn`t hurt our child.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Chubb told me doctors are not optimistic about his wife`s condition. He is.\par \par \uc2 JAMAL CHUBB: I believe in miracles, and I believe that when I look into my oldest daughter`s eyes, she reminds me so much of her mother that I want to be able to tell her that I did everything I could to get her mom back to her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The administrator here told me that prior to COVID, the last time she had to get a mobile unit up and running like this was when she was commander of a combat support unit ten years ago in Afghanistan. Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Janet Shamlian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210812eh8b000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A New Restrictive Abortion Law In Texas And The High School Valedictorian Who Went Off Script To Denounce It\b0\par\par\uc2 ANTHONY MASON, KATE SMITH, GAYLE KING \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Governor Greg Abbott signed the legislation last month. It bans abortions after cardiac activity is detected as early as six weeks into pregnancy. The legislation has key differences compared to similar laws now being challenged in the courts. CBS News reporter Kate Smith is here to explain. Kate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH (CBS News Reporter): Good morning. Under this law, private citizens will be able to sue doctors that perform abortions in violation of the ban. They`ll also be able to sue anyone who, quote, "aids and abets those patients," including nurses, family members, rape counselors, and clinic volunteers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT (Volunteer): Hello.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hi.\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: I`m an escort with the clinic. Is this your first or second appointment?\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: Poppy Northcutt has greeted women in the crowded parking lot of this Houston, Texas, clinic for nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: You`re good to go then.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: What`s the need for escorts at a clinic?\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: Because we usually have protesters here.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: On the day we visited, nearly ninety percent of the patients seeking abortion would have been turned away under the new law, clinic administrators told us.\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: They call it a heartbeat bill. It`s basically a bill that`s going to just cut off access to legalized abortion.\par \par \uc2 Hi, I`m a volunteer with the clinic.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: And anyone, including Poppy, who helps a woman after cardiac activity is detected at around six weeks--\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: --could be sued and fined a minimum of ten thousand dollars for every patient.\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: We would have a lot of closures of clinics. But that`s the purpose of it. That`s the real purpose is harass them out of business.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: Will you continue your work?\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: Are you worried about the lawsuits?\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: At twenty-six, Northcutt made headlines as NASA`s first female engineer in a mission control.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Two, one, zero. And liftoff.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: That experience fueled a decades-long legal career fighting for women`s equality.\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: It`s personal to me because I`ve seen what happens when abortion is not legal. I had a good friend from college who nearly died from an illegal abortion. I really feel like it`s a fundamental right of women to be able to control their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 SHELBY SLAWSON (R-Texas State Representative): Once a baby`s heartbeat is detected, that little baby`s life is protected.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: For Texas State Representative Shelby Slawson, it`s also personal. She says her mom gave birth to her despite a doctor`s recommendation to abort. Slawson co-wrote the bill with state Senator Bryan Hughes.\par \par \uc2 This bill doesn`t offer any exceptions for victims of rape or incest. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HUGHES (R-Texas State Senator): We do make sure that if the mother`s life is in danger, serious issues to her health, that`s taken care of. As far as rape, let`s punish the rapist. Let`s don`t punish the little unborn baby.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: So-called heartbeat bans passed in fourteen other states have been blocked by federal courts. But in Texas, private citizens will be able to sue anyone they believe violates the law, meaning abortion rights groups will need a new legal strategy to fight this ban.\par \par \uc2 Do you think this law will go into effect?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HUGHES: I do. I believe it will.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: Are you surprised that we`re having this conversation today about whether or not Roe v. Wade survives?\par \par \uc2 POPPY NORTHCUTT: I`m angry about it. I`m angry that we`re still fighting the same battle.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: The law goes into effect September 1st. The Supreme Court recently announced it would consider a Mississippi law banning most abortions after fifteen weeks. Now, Gayle, that will give the newly conservative bench the very first time to look at Roe v. Wade since it was decided fifty years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m in awe of Poppy Northcutt, though.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honestly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. What a-- what a career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. Incredible law. I`m a little uncomfortable with empowering private citizens--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --and rewarding them for policing their neighbors and friends and turning them in and getting ten grand for it.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: Now this is a law unlike we`ve ever seen in the abortion space but it`s also a very-- excuse me, it`s just a unique law in general. And when I was speaking to people, they were saying that not only does this open up the, you know, the floodgates for, again, abortion restrictions, but you could see this being used for other things like, you know, gun control or other--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very much so.\par \par \uc2 KATE SMITH: --you know, contentious issues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it was interesting to see Paxton Smith talking at her graduation and then cut to Poppy Northcutt--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --still having this conversation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you, Kate Smith, at the table. Always nice.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, country music icon and Kennedy Center honoree, that would be Garth Brooks, looks back at how he`s handled his fame over his decades-long career.\par \par \uc2 How do you reconcile that, you`re speaking your truth and people get upset with you for speaking your truth.\par \par \uc2 GARTH BROOKS: Oh, that`s all right. They`ve given me what we have. It`s fair if they take it away, right? I`m the hardest set of person you`ve ever met. But if you think for one moment I`m going to change the way I feel so you can love me, sorry, man, just ain`t going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Garth Brooks talks about his break from music and life with his wife and fellow country singer, Trisha Yearwood, who he calls Ms. Yearwood. I like that.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s eight-oh-seven, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, meet the ninety-one-year-old lox slicer who`s returned to the counter after a pandemic furlough. See what it shows about New York City`s spirit.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210608eh64000jm\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Texas Has Just Adopted One Of The Most Restrictive Anti-Abortion Laws In The Nation, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Mason, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 1,890 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Texas has just adopted one of the most restrictive anti- abortion laws in the nation. Republican Governor Greg Abbott signed the bill yesterday. It bans the procedure as early as six weeks into a pregnancy, before many women even know they`re pregnant. It also allows private citizens to sue medical professionals who offer abortions after the deadline or anyone helping a woman get an abortion at that point. Planned Parenthood CEO says her group plans to, quote, "fight back like hell" to overturn the law in the courts. The law is scheduled to take effect in September.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Newly-released bodycam video of an arrest in 2019 seems to show Louisiana State Troopers tasing, punching and dragging a Black man who later died in their custody. Forty-nine-year-old Ronald Greene had led the troopers on a high-speed chase. On the video, obtained by the Associated Press, you can hear Greene repeatedly apologizing after crashing. Police originally told Greene`s family a false story--that he died on impact after crashing into a tree. Mola Lenghi has the video. We must warn you again, it is disturbing to watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RONALD GREENE (bodycam video): I`m scared.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (bodycam video): Get out of the car. Stand up. Get out of the car.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI (CBS News Correspondent): Ronald Greene can be heard apologizing and pleading with troopers once the high-speed chase came to an end. They tased him before he could get out of his car. Officers then wrestled him to the ground and tased him several more times.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (bodycam video): Put your hands behind your back (EXPLETIVE DELETED). Give me your (EXPLETIVE DELETED) hand.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3 (bodycam video): Put your hands behind--\par \par \uc2 MAN #4 (bodycam video): Taser. Taser. Taser.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5 (bodycam video): You`re about to get it again if you don`t--\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: One of at least six officers can be seen dragging Greene several feet after his legs had been shackled and his hands cuffed behind him. The AP says the troopers, who were White, left Greene unattended, facedown and moaning for more than nine minutes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6 (bodycam video): Stupid mother (EXPLETIVE DELETED).\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (bodycam video): You alright?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3 (bodycam video): Got blood all over. I hope this guy ain`t got (EXPLETIVE DELETED) AIDS.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Experts say officers nationwide are often trained to turn people handcuffed on their side so they can breathe easily. Troopers didn`t do that to Greene. Instead, pushing him facedown as Greene tried to turn. Police initially told Greene`s family he died after crashing into a tree during the chase. But later, State Police said Greene struggled with troopers and died on his way to the hospital. A federal civil rights investigation is underway. In a statement to CBS News, Louisiana State Police would not comment on the content of the video. It said the release undermines the investigative process and compromises the fair and impartial outcome for the Greene family, LSP employees, and the community. Greene`s family has filed a federal wrongful death lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, I`m Mola Lenghi.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, they can`t comment on that video because it`s indefensible. I`m so tired of being tired about these stories. I mean, you hear them calling him a stupid MF--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would say he wasn`t stupid, sir. He was scared. He was scared. And-- and then when you look at the initial police report--died crashing into a tree, died on the way to the hospital--that`s why the videotape is so important. And what you`re going to see in this case is sometimes the body-- bodycams were turned off--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --the audio was turned off. And even, Anthony, when-- Jesus, even when take-- taken to the hospital, the doctor on the report said the police account does not add up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right. The-- we should note that the trooper who tased Greene and later--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --admitted on audio to beating him was-- was told he would be fired for his role in this case. He later died in a single-vehicle highway crash that happened right after he was told that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why would you--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sorry that he died because I`d like hear to his account.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. And another officer was arrested in connection with another excessive force case. One of those officers was-- was arrested in another case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thing that always gets me, Vlad, is the lack of humanity that is shown people of color in encounters with the police. We`ve seen it time and time and time again.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mister Greene was saying repeatedly to those troopers, I`m you brother.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m your brother.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m-- I`m your brother. I`m sorry, and I`m scared.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m scared. That`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Moving on.\par \par \uc2 Deadly spring storms continue to drench parts of the Deep South this morning. Millions are under flash flood watches, and at least four people have been killed after more than three days of severe weather in Louisiana, Texas, Oklahoma and Arkansas. Janet Shamlian is watching the rain that`s coming down in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Across the South, businesses lost, homes destroyed, cars wrecked and abandoned, and lives lost.\par \par \uc2 JANA POOL (Homeowner): I can`t believe it. And sometimes you ask, like, I`m like, why us, why God, us?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This family north of Dallas, gutting their three-year-old home after it was hit by lightning. Adding to the misery, the house is now soaked from water damage.\par \par \uc2 JANA POOL: It can all be rebuilt, but the lives could never come back if something were to happen to my husband or my children.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Houston, the flooding overwhelming this driver stuck inside his car. Roads and highways washed away leaving cars stranded, forcing some to use whatever they could to get around. In Central Arkansas, at least fifteen people were rescued from the rising waters. The heavy rain overwhelming this lake near Tulsa. The dam holding back all that water partially breached.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE THOMAS (Baton Rouge, Louisiana Resident): I was running around the house trying to think of what was most important.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Louisiana, Danielle Thomas` friends carried out furniture and personal belongings destroyed by flooding as she worked to salvage what`s priceless.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE THOMAS: Yeah. I spent a lot of time yesterday crying about this particular-- just this stuff. Most of it is replaceable. Right now, I`m working with essentially just kind of family heirloom-type stuff trying to salvage these letters.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Janet Shamlian, Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, two former police officers are charged for the violent arrest of a seventy-three-year-old woman with dementia. We hear from her daughter only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is seven sixteen, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, Ford`s big bet that Americans are ready to plug in their pickups. Errol Barnett is tracking the effort to electrify an American icon.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: You`re about to see something you`ve never seen before. I`m Errol Barnett at the Michigan Proving Ground for \uc2 Ford\uc2 , where test vehicles like this one get their final adjustments. Coming up, we`ll take you inside the company`s first all-electric F-150.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The oldest living survivor of the Tulsa race massacre gave harrowing testimony on Capitol Hill yesterday. She is one hundred and seven years old. This is Viola Fletcher, describing living through the deadly violence that killed hundreds of Black people and destroyed the all-Black community of Greenwood one hundred years ago this month. She was just seven at the time.\par \par \uc2 VIOLA FLETCHER: The night of the massacre, I was awakened by my family. My parents and five siblings were there. I was told we had to leave. And that was it. I will never forget the violence the White mob when we left our home. I still see Black men seen being shot, Black bodies lying in the street. I have lived through the massacre every day. Our country may forget this history, but I cannot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy. Fletcher, her younger brother, and a third survivor testified before a U.S. House subcommittee that is discussing compensation for survivors and their descendants. Survivors have filed a reparations lawsuit that claims that the city of Tulsa is responsible for what happened during the massacre, which was not discussed in public for generations. You know, I didn`t even know this story for many, many, many years. I think it`s important that this story is told.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I lived in Tulsa for a couple of years. And-- and I heard about it while I was there for the first time. But there`s-- there was-- there was not a lot of information about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. Yeah. I interviewed her, they called her Mother Fletcher. That`s why if you have older people in your family, it`s so important to sit down and have a conversation with them.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because this is living history.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It`s living history.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is living history and people forget that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s her life experiences. We could all learn a lot from her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah. I think people tend to forget they think its ancient history, but it`s not. There are people who have lived through this--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that we`re talking about--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --and they`re still around. We should talk to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s important to talk to them.\par \par \uc2 Memorial Day weekend, by the way, marks one hundred years since the Tulsa massacre, so CBS News will host a primetime special. We`re calling it Tulsa 1921: An American Tragedy to commemorate the anniversary--the special which includes stories from survivors and their descendants. It airs on Monday, May 31st, at ten/nine Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, a bombshell interview with Princess Diana more than two decades ago back in the spotlight. How an investigation into that famous TV moment could impact royal history.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210521eh5k0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 A Very Significant Abortion Case Is Headed To The Supreme Court, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 1,268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A very significant abortion case is headed to the Supreme Court. The justices said yesterday they will decide if a new law in Mississippi is constitutional. It would impose the strictest state limits on abortion since the Roe v. Wade decision forty-eight years ago. Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court. So, Jan, when will the justices decide?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (CBS News Chief Legal Correspondent): Well, the court won`t hear arguments in this case until next term which starts in October, so we probably won`t get a decision until next summer. But, Vlad, I mean, already, this case is striking fear among abortion advocates and raising hope among abortion opponents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: At issue, 2018 Mississippi law that would ban almost all abortions after fifteen weeks of pregnancy. Its similar bans recently passed in other conservative-leaning states all with an eye on getting a newly conservative Supreme Court to reconsider Roe.\par \par \uc2 PHIL BRYANT (March 19, 2018): We`ll probably be sued here in about half an hour. That`ll-- that`ll be fine with me.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Lower courts have struck down the bans as unconstitutional under existing Supreme Court decisions which prohibit most restrictions until after the second trimester, about six months, when a fetus can live outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And I will be appointing pro-life judges.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Conservatives have what could be the most solid majority in modern history. The three justices appointed by President Trump, two of them Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett replaced justices who had refused to overturn Roe. During the confirmation hearings, both said they would follow the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT (October 13, 2020): I do see as distinct my personal moral religious views and my task of applying the law as a judge.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But according to the National Constitution Center`s \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen\uc2 , even a decision to review the Mississippi case indicates this new court could see the issue differently.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN\uc2  (\uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  CEO): It takes at least four justices to agree to hear a case. And this decision suggests that because the composition of the court has changed, its view towards Roe may have changed, as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now last June, the Supreme Court struck down a Louisiana law that could have left the state with only one abortion clinic. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  who is a conservative joined the court`s liberals, but this is a different case and a much more conservative court. Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rain is pounding much of Louisiana causing significant flooding. First responders in Baton Rouge rushed to this apartment complex to ferry families to safety. More than a foot of rain around Lake Charles left roads and some businesses underwater. In West Texas, a tornado sighting in Sterling City is linked to damage at a nearby power plant. The possibility of more heavy rain, tornadoes and hail is in the forecast for the region throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t believe the video of those people sitting in a restaurant with the floodwaters acting like this is normal.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know, calm in the midst of a storm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That very definition.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In Los Angeles, a suspected arsonist is under arrest accused of setting a large brushfire near the Pacific Ocean. Officials say the flames are reaching hillside areas in Pacific Palisades that have not burned in more than fifty years. Hundreds of people who were ordered to evacuate in the area were allowed to return home last night.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, why COVID vaccines may not help some of the people who need them most. Doctor Jon LaPook shows how the medicine is protecting patients who are immunocompromised could stop the vaccines from working.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s seven sixteen, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, Republican leaders in Phoenix rebel over an election audit. Why they believe the recount conducted by supporters of former President Trump is giving their state a black eye.\par \par \uc2 Plus, have you heard the buzz? Buzz, buzz.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Trillions of cicadas.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the sound of cicadas, Anthony. They are starting to emerge in the eastern United States. Why this week`s warm weather could bring them out even faster.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And some critters.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Two people in California are lucky to be alive after surviving a plane crash near Lake Arrowhead. A home security camera captured the moment the plane went down Saturday afternoon. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says the single-engine Cessna slammed into the side of a mountain crashing into a heavily wooded area. That made it difficult for crews to reach the scene. One of the first rescuers to arrive, Marcus Golding, says he was amazed by what he discovered.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS GOLDING (San Bernardino County Fire Department): Pulling a live victim out of a plane crash, it was a first for me. I`ve been on few other plane crashes. But typically, you know, the-- the victims don`t survive.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This time they were lucky. The fire department says they were alert and talking when they found.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s one of my biggest nightmares--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good news.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --right there. It`s the--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --it`s the thing about-- listen, I`m not afraid to die, I don`t want to die. But I don`t want to die terrified.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the thing about going down in a plane--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Plane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --you know that that`s happening--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --it`s very, very frightening. So--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They would have to check the shorts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They washed away-- that`s-- that`s good physically. Yeah, they have to check the shorts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They would have-- that`s the first thing you got to check, the shorts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, they had-- I`m not aware yet, I`m not there yet, but they have these things called the pants.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Every time I get on a plane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I hear a very effective--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You got to wear them. You got to wear them every time you get on a plane.\par \par \uc2 All right. Ahead in our Spring into Summer series, Meg Oliver shows us what you can expect on your next trip to an amusement park or a safari adventure.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: This morning, your family can experience extreme wildlife and extreme thrills. We`ll show you the step they are taking to get you back into amusement parks. That`s coming up on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. Trunk up. Trunk up. Almost. Almost. Here you go. Oh, oh, oh. Hang on. Oh, my gosh. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210519eh5i0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court To Take Up Major Abortion Rights Case\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 466 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, the debate over abortion rights is back in the national spotlight. The Supreme Court says it will take up the most direct challenge to Roe v. Wade in a generation this fall. CBS` Jan Crawford reports on what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (CBS News Chief Legal Correspondent): On one of the most contentious issues of our time all sides agree, the case could pave the way for the strictest restrictions on abortion since the court ruled in 1973 that women had a constitutional right to the procedure. At issue, a Mississippi law that would ban almost all abortions after fifteen weeks of pregnancy. It`s similar to bans recently passed in other conservative- leaning states, all designed to get the newly conservative Supreme Court to confront Roe v. Wade head on. Lower courts have struck down the ban as unconstitutional under existing Supreme Court decisions, which generally prohibit restrictions until after the second trimester, about six months when the fetus can live outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (May 11, 2016): I will appoint judges that will be pro-life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But with three justices appointed by President Trump, conservatives have what could be the most solid majority in modern history. Two of them, Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett replaced justices who would refuse to overturn or greatly restrict Roe. As a standard, neither gave any signs during their confirmation hearings of how they would rule on abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (Supreme Court; September 5, 2018): My position as a judge is that there`s forty-five years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Barrett, the most openly antiabortion rights of the justices, said she would set aside her personal views and follow the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT (Supreme Court; October 13, 2020): But I do see as distinct my personal moral religious views and my task of applying the law as a judge.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Most conservatives don`t believe that the Constitution contains a right to an abortion. The question is whether Roe and the other decisions are so settled that the court will refuse to rethink all that to allow the sweeping restrictions. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A lot of people watching that. Jan Crawford, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210518eh5h0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 A New Report In The Medical Journal, The Lancet, Says There Is A Serious Lack Of Gender Specific Research Like Heart Disease, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King \par \uc2 4,369 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A new report in the medical journal, The Lancet, says there is a serious lack of gender-specific research and data when it comes to heart disease in women. Heart disease, as you may know, is the leading cause of death for women globally. The report recommends more education on early detection for health care providers and the patients, recruiting more women for cardiac studies and prioritizing research on heart disease in women. CBS News senior medical correspondent, that`s Doctor Tara Narula, met with a woman whose cardiac scare during pregnancy caught her by surprise.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 YULIA NURIKYAN (Heart Complication Survivor): Ooh.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Just weeks before her baby was due, Yulia Nurikyan knew something wasn`t right.\par \par \uc2 YULIA NURIKYAN: I felt nauseous, I felt dizzy. And I felt like I was about to faint.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Concerned for the baby, Yulia rushed to a local hospital in Queens, New York, where her daughter Olivia was delivered via emergency C-section. Test showed Yulia had suffered an aortic dissection, a potentially deadly tear in the lining of the aorta that affects blood flow to the body and can potentially rupture the entire aorta.\par \par \uc2 DR. ISMAIL EL-HAMAMSY (Mount Sinai Hospital Cardiovascular Surgeon): Obviously, if this happens, then the patient literally dies instantly.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Doctor Ismail El-Hamamsy is an aortic surgeon at Mount Sinai Hospital in Manhattan, where Yulia was taken for life-saving surgery, leaving her newborn baby behind.\par \par \uc2 YULIA NURIKYAN: Bye, baby girl.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: After six days of only seeing Olivia on FaceTime calls, Yulia finally got to hold her.\par \par \uc2 What did it feel like to hold her in your arms?\par \par \uc2 YULIA NURIKYAN: It felt like she belonged there.\par \par \uc2 Say, hi.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Had she not been pregnant, Yulia says she likely would have ignored her symptoms, and heart disease symptom in women can differ from those in men. Rather than chest pain, women sometimes report indigestion, back pain, or shortness of breath. That`s one reason the authors of a new report in The Lancet say it is critical to have more women-specific studies.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROXANA MEHRAN (\uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  Cardiologist): Women are not small men. We need to think about women as their own individual biology, and-- and come up with the answers for them.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Explain to us how the current system is failing us when it comes to research and women.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROXANA MEHRAN: We keep saying that we`re under-studying, under- recognizing, under-diagnosing these women. And we need to make all kinds of efforts to make sure that our clinical trials have good representation of women. So when we have new devices, new drugs, the application is not just for men.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Doctor Tara Narula, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, coming up, we have an exclusive clip from my conversation with Dylan Farrow. She explains how an HBO documentary affected her relationship with her mother, Mia Farrow.\par \par \uc2 But right now, your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, she was in studio.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we call Talk of the Table because this is the table and we`re talking. And we`re going to let our guest go first because Anthony and I are very good hosts.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We`re very polite.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we`re very polite.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That is so incredibly special.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, you`re going first.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Thank you, guys. All right. For my Talk of the Table, I`m sharing an exclusive clip of my interview with Dylan Farrow. We talked about everything, including her experience watching Allen v. Farrow. The documentary series is about the history of her family. And she says that it led to some complicated conversations with her mom, Mia Farrow.\par \par \uc2 (The Drew Barrymore Show): How did your mom feel watching the documentary?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN FARROW (The Drew Barrymore Show): I mean, it was tough because I would have loved to have been able to watch the series with her, and at the time that just wasn`t possible. We were-- we were in separate quarantine pods unfortunately. So, I really sort of heard everything from her after the fact. You know, it was just a very strange pathway that we navigated separately and together.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE (The Drew Barrymore Show): Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN FARROW (The Drew Barrymore Show): But I think it-- it definitely changed a lot about how we-- how me and my mom relate to each other and interact with each other. And I think there`s a very renewed level of just respect-- just woman to woman.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We should say that CBS News reached out to someone who represents Woody Allen, and he`s not commenting. But you can see the whole thing later today on The Drew Barrymore Show. And what`s extraordinary is there is confirmation from Dylan that her family had nothing to do with the production, producing--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --or any aspect of the documentary. And I think--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Which a lot of people had thought.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I think they went into it thinking that there had been some bias or one-sidedness.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN FARROW: And that is not accurate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I mean, and Drew, her story has-- not Drew, Dylan, you`re Drew. Dylan`s story has been consistent from the very beginning. She was six. She was a little girl at the time, seven years old. Her story`s been consistent. Woody Allen supporters would say his story has been consistent, as well. Was there anything that surprised you when you were talking to her? Number one, it was great to see her in the studio.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because she doesn`t-- she doesn`t do interviews very often. So, it was great to see her.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: She had claimed that she felt disarmed by me because I`m someone that she has grown up with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And I had worked with her-- I had worked with Woody back in the day and I established an interview--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, did you? Uh-Huh.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --by saying that I wish I had had children and been a woman of the world. But when you`re young, it is career suicide not to do a Woody Allen movie.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And that it is wonderful that we are here in this moment very much due a lot to her brother, Ronan Farrow--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --who has single-handedly picked off bad guys left, right and center. So, their family has evolved. But what`s extraordinary is there are several new revelations that come out in the show. She speaks very candidly about when she sees them out in public, how it feels to see--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --Woody and Soon-Yi.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And what her relationship is like with her family now that they are truly communicating about it. This was not something-- one of the hardest questions I felt I had to ask which-- what was it like watching the video that your mother took of you as a little girl?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And what is both of your reactions to that video now? It`s an incredible conversation. She`s full of strength. You know, she`s a mom--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s a good way to say it. Yeah, she`s a mom of a little girl.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, she is. She is full of strength.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I asked, how did she decide to trust in order to marry and then--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --proceed to have children. So, we really cover it all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. The documentary is very well done. Did you know her? No?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: But I feel like I know her now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see your show today.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And I know you do, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I do. I-- I do feel that. I`m glad she is okay. I`m glad she is okay.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yeah. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My story is about the worst colors to wear for a date night. My favorite color is yellow. What`s your favorite, Drew Barrymore?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Gayle, you know my favorite color is yellow. And I text you every time I see you wearing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You-- you should see-- you should see Drew said it`s all yellow. You, me and Andy Cohen, both love yellow. And yellow, by the way, is the worst color to wear on a date night. That`s the worst. I actually thought it would be the best, but they said it`s the worst.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why is it the worst?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They said yellow is the worst because it`s associated with-- Big Bird is one thing.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: By the way, I went to Sesame Street and I got a feather out of--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big Bird.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --Big Bird.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, that`s--\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And I have it on my wall.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Those are real-- those are real feathers.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big Bird--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Those are not fake feathers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. That was great. I was there, too, recently. Big bird and taxis. Yellow is the color of that catches the eye quickly but not good. Gray is also not good because they think its drap and sad.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The ugliest color, though, they say of olive brown, don`t wear that. But you`re very much in trend Drew Barrymore because brown is the new black of 2021. All different shades of brown work for most people.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Well, you and I both love yellow and we`re both single.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: So, I suppose that says a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, what does that say?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You know what? I think the color we should start trending with now, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What now? What?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Red.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Red. That`s a passion color.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m game.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I am, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m game.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Uh-Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthony, what you got?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: A conspiracy was born.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Here`s my story, a-- a store in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, called Shawnniecakes Specialty Treats saw a huge spike in the busy-- in business over the weekend. And the owner, LeShawn Smith, couldn`t figure out why. And then one of her customers told her that this had happened. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT (The Late Show; \uc2 CBS\uc2 ): We heard from great places to eat like Shawnniecakes in Cedar Rapids, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s right. She had been given a shout-out by Stephen Colbert on his show and all of a sudden business at Shawnniecakes exploded. They sell toffee butter cookies, lemon pound cake, chocolate cakes known as Jordies. They sold out almost two days` worth of baked goods just in the next morning. Things had exploded. Very exciting. It turns out Stephen Colbert on his show is promoting small businesses, and sometimes just giving them a shout-out. And the reason the owner was surprised that she had not submitted the name of her business and ended up coming--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even better.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --it had come from her customers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even better.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But here is her reaction.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even better.\par \par \uc2 LESHAWN SMITH: There are times that I`ll be over doing something and you just hear this like little grin-- I mean, this little laugh. And I`m grinning because I`m just thinking about, oh, my God, we were on national TV. And my husband`s like, it was international. I was like, yay.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. That`s LeShawn Smith enjoying the sudden surge in business. At Shawnniecakes, if you`re--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I just say that Shawnniecakes looked good. I was looking on the screen.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Looks real good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not often I want to lick my TV screen, but that was good.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wonder if she does mail order, too.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Maybe Goldbelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It`s the best delivery service.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gooldbelly would do that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Incredible. You can get anything--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good idea.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --from the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can get anything from anywhere.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Anything from anywhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 Go, Shawnniecakes. And thank you, Stephen Colbert, for that shout-out. That worked.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`ll talk to powerhouse \uc2 Netflix\uc2  executive Bozoma Saint John. Her advice for companies seeking greater diversity and tips to help us all thrive. Bozoma`s got lots of ideas. She always does.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s eight thirty-seven, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN (Netflix Chief Marketing Officer; July 2017): I want things to be great for people of color and for women.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING (July 2017): Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN (July 2017): For us to be able show up at work, do our best work, and be appreciated for that work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING (July 2017): Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN (July 2017): So if I can contribute to that, and it`s not going to be easy, you know, there`s going to be lots of challenges, you know. I`m sure there will be frustrating days. I`m sure there will be times I cry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING (July 2017): Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN (July 2017): I don`t mind crying, by the way. But I`m hopeful, you know. And I think that`s really what`s driving me is that I`m hopeful. I can see the larger picture.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That was marketing executive Bozoma Saint John in 2017. And she says, quote, "There is nothing more badass than being yourself." Bozoma is now the chief marketing officer at Netflix and she oversees more than two billion dollars to promote shows and films in more than thirty countries. She previously worked as an executive at companies you might have heard of--Uber, Apple and Pepsi, to name a few. Bozoma Saint John joins us now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Good morning, Drew. How are you?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I have to tell you, I have been wanting to speak with you. You are one of the first people I talked about actually when we launched our show, The Drew Barrymore Show. I did a little feature on you. I`ve been a total fan of yours. And, you know, you`ve been given this two-billion- dollar budget, but I was studying and saw that you worked at Spike DDB, which is a nod to Doyle Dane Bernbach, the famous advertising agency. And that you guys talked about the importance of marketing and messaging. Why is that so important?\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Well, messaging is all about storytelling. You know, I learned that lesson very early on. I was probably twelve when my family moved from Ghana where we`re originally from, to Colorado Springs, Colorado. And really the only thing that connected me to my classmates-- because so many things separated us, you know, race, culture, language, food, so many things. The only thing that really connected us was pop culture.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: You know, the storytelling, the connections of music and fashion and sports--\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --you know, the things that were happening daily and constantly. So for me, that`s-- that`s really where it all started. And-- and that`s why I love marketing because it`s all about storytelling and making the connections between people who may not otherwise see their connections.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: What I love about that is it parlays one of the reasons I`ve been such a fan of yours. It`s because I, too, love pop culture, it absolutely connects us. And when you go in to all of these incredible companies that have such power, so much money behind them, so much ability to get these storytelling out in commercials and ad buys, like, I want to know as the queen of cool, like where do you get your news? What sites do you think we should all be watching? Where should we be turning to be on top of and ahead of the curve on pop culture?\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Well, the first trick, Drew, is to be endlessly curious. You know, just to be curious about people, about their stories, about where they are from, what their backgrounds are. And when you`re curious about people, you`ll be-- you`ll be surprised by how much you`ll learn just in the simple questions. I can pick up things from anywhere. You know, from the grocery store to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to ear hustling in the airport-- well, back when we could all travel--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --to just about any-- any place. I mean, it`s-- it`s really just about being curious about where-- what people are and what they are doing and why they are doing things. You know, I`m-- I`m probably one of those people that if you ever meet at a-- at a cocktail party or-- or someplace where we`ve never met, I`m probably going to be asking you a lot of questions about why it is that you are the way that you are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, Boz, when you walk in a room, people want to know, who is she, and let me go over and talk to her. But I-- I-- I know that you are a big champion of women. I know that you are big champion of people of color.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But yet there is still so much work to be done in all areas, in-- in all of those areas and certainly in the companies. What would you like to see done in your company in particular, an advice for others?\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Oh, Gayle, well, this is-- this is the question that I-- I love most, right, about how we can continue to further not just diversity and inclusion, but what role we all play in it. You know, sometimes I think we-- we let the responsibility go to someone else. You know, it`s the government or it`s the CEO or-- or somebody else who can make a policy--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --to make us change the way that we behave. And that is what I want us all to realize that actually the power is within us. You know, and that`s not just some trite statement. That-- it`s true. It`s really true. It`s liked, how do you behave? Who are your friends? Who do you get your information from? Who are you connected to? You know, and then if we understand who we are and how we behave and the information that we`re getting and the way that we treat other people, then we show up in our full selves in every situation. So even for me, it`s like regardless of the company that I work for or the environments I`m in, I`m coming in as my full self, you know, fully informed with opinions of other people and how they are interacting in the world. And hopefully, it`s the way that I`m able then to also put those opinions into my work. And it`s the way that I hope my colleagues are also behaving and, therefore, creating work that serves our world.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bozoma, it`s Anthony. You`ve created a-- a series of Badass Workshops--I love-- I love this name--to help people find their greatest self. What-- what`s your-- to you, what does being a badass mean?\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Oh, being a badass means that you are completely in full control of who you are, flaws and all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: You know, it doesn`t mean perfection.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Flaws and all. I like that.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: It doesn`t mean that you are bigger or better than anybody else. It`s just that you fully value who you are and what you contribute. And for me, that`s-- that`s what`s true. You know, I-- I walk around the planet knowing that-- like the lesson in biology. You know, it`s like if-- if one molecule enters a matter, the whole matter changes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: And I`m that molecule that changes matter.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I have a question about award shows. Within the pandemic, I think we had a real lift of the veil of award shows. They are not as fancy, they are more at home.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We`ve had the Oscars--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --you know, so white. The-- you know, HFPA, you know, \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  came out and said we want to stop working with you guys until you guys change. You are a fixer. You come in and you literally fix it. Is there a way to come in and fix award shows? Because when I watched the NFL Draft, there`s just such excitement. And--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The energy is great.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: --how can we get behind award shows again? Because I think they are off track right now.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Yeah. Yes. Well-- well, I agree with that. I mean, I was-- I was really so proud of-- of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , obviously, and Ted with the letter to the HFPA--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --because for us it`s really about making sure that we`re holding ourselves accountable and everyone we work with accountable. You know, this-- the change doesn`t happen just because we say we want it to change. We have to hold everyone accountable.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: The entire industry, and then further than that, all of corporations. You know, so I-- I do believe that there`s a-- there`s a possibility for us to continue to see the change that we want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: But again, I-- I won`t leave it to policymakers. You know, it`s all of us. It`s the folks who watch the shows. It is the folks who go to movies. It is the people who support. We`ve got to say--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the point you`re making, Boz--\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --we`ve got to say something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the point you`re making when you say--\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --we all play a part. But this stuck out to me about you, you said that you have reclaimed--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --the, quote, "angry black woman." What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Well, there`s quite a stereotype. It`s in there--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re talking to a black woman right now. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Okay. Exactly, exactly. Well, the stereotype exists that if you`re a black woman or-- let`s just call a woman of color--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --you know, who expresses any kind of passion, excitement, sometimes aggressiveness, you know--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --in your opinion, that you are hysterical and then the worst name--angry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: And sometimes the B-word. You know, and-- and for me, especially in light of last summer and with the murder of George Floyd, and we saw all of the racial reckoning that was happening across America, and really across the world--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: --I just wanted to reclaim it. I was like, hell, yeah, I`m angry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: I`m mad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: And you should be, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I-- I hear you.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: And so if you`re not, then you got to fix it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear you Bozoma Saint John. Congratulations on your new role. We`ll talk to you later on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 BOZOMA SAINT JOHN: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. Good news, Drew is coming back tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yay.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 cMMXXI CBS Broadcasting Inc. All Rights Reserved.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210518eh5h000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 A Year Into The Pandemic, Medical Experts Are Worried About The Potential Long Term Health Effects Of Missed Cancer Screenings., \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King \par \uc2 3,917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A year into the pandemic, medical experts are worried about the potential long-term health effects of missed cancer screenings. Hundreds of thousands of cancer screenings and tests were delayed, with facilities closed amid concerns about the coronavirus. And now the experts fear this could delay critical diagnoses. Mireya Villarreal introduces us to two women who experienced just that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Let`s be gentle, okay?\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Shaving her head was cathartic for Rhonda Johnstone of Roswell, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA JOHNSTONE (Cancer Patient): It was actually really special.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Johnstone is fifty-two and battling cancer. She has a mass of tumors in her right breast.\par \par \uc2 When you got the news, what was the first thing that ran through your mind?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA JOHNSTONE: Fear. I`ve got five little grandkids, and I want to be here to watch them grow up.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: But after she discovered the first lump, six long months passed before she was finally diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 What has been the hardest part for you?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA JOHNSTONE: I`ve really had to fight. I`ve had to fight for testing. I`ve had to fight to be seen. I`ve just had to fight for answers. And I don`t think that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Johnstone is just one of hundreds of thousands whose cancer screenings were deferred when the pandemic shut down clinics and labs.\par \par \uc2 DR. LINDA ANN SMITH (Breast Surgeon): I`m seeing people with advanced cancers.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Doctor Linda Ann Smith is a breast surgeon in Albuquerque. She`s now treating Johnstone, half a year later.\par \par \uc2 DR. LINDA ANN SMITH: There were no words for how striking that picture was, how big the tumor was. It was everywhere. It was all four quadrants. It was underneath the nipple.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Johnstone is getting chemotherapy to shrink the tumors so Smith can operate.\par \par \uc2 DR. LINDA ANN SMITH: Yeah, mammogram saved your life.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Surgery has become increasingly common during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DR. LINDA ANN SMITH: We`re doing harder surgery, sicker patients, people who`ve had to go through extensive treatment before we can take them to surgery.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: For fifty-nine-year-old Yvette Lowery of Rock Hill, South Carolina, that meant chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE LOWERY (Cancer Patient): I have chemo, baby.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Her annual mammogram was canceled during the early outbreak of COVID, and the backlog of screenings made it hard to reschedule. In August, Lowery felt two lumps under her arm. After three months of delayed screenings and tests, she finally got her diagnosis-- stage three breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE LOWERY: It`s been a little emotional. But I just keep my faith and trust in God.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: If her cancer had been detected last May or June, doctors believe they may have been able to prevent the spread. Last week, Lowery had a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE LOWERY: They put our life on a standstill.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: What was the hardest part for you?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE LOWERY: The hardest part was telling my kids. I didn`t want them to worry. And I didn`t want to be crying.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Experts predict the number of people who will die from breast or colorectal cancer in the U.S. will increase by nearly ten thousand over the next decade because of COVID-19`s impact on cancer care.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN FINKELSTEIN (Oncologist): And you decide at some point.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Doctor Karen Finkelstein, who treats gynecologic cancers, has seen a thirty percent decline in cancer referrals.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN FINKELSTEIN: I`m being paralyzed, I`ve been frozen by COVID because it`s made-- it`s changed the landscape of-- of-- of medicine and patients.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Last year has taken a toll on her personally.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN FINKELSTEIN: You start to feel a sense of despair. The hurdles COVID has forced us to face this year, some of them have been insurmountable.\par \par \uc2 RHONDA JOHNSTONE: I love you.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: It`s that love from Johnstone`s family and for her family that gives her the strength to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t seem like you`re going to take this one sitting down, are you?\par \par \uc2 RHONDA JOHNSTONE: No, I`m not. I`m going to get it. I`m going to get it good.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Mireya Villarreal, Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we want her to get it good too.\par \par \uc2 Our Doctor David Agus, who specializes in treating patients with advanced cancers, joins us now. Good morning to you, David. This is such a great wake-up call to those of us who have put off screenings for whatever reason. So here`s a question, what screening should we absolutely not put off or delay?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS (CBS News Medical Contributor): You know, during COVID, there was almost a fifty percent drop in the diagnosis of breast cancer week by week. And so those breast cancers are still there. They`re going to be diagnosed. So you need to get your mammogram. You need to get a PSA test if you`re a man, a lung screening test if you`re a smoker, colonoscopy, all the standard screening tests. And you need to go to your doctor to get that standard blood test. Other things are found in the blood tests. Hospitals have remarkable programs now to prevent-- and most doctors are actually-- and-- and nurses are vaccinated, so it is very low risk to go get the screening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, and then some vaccinated people are reporting swollen lymph nodes underneath their armpits, which can sometimes be mistaken as a sign of breast cancer. What do you have to say about that?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Yeah, Gayle, I mean, you`re right, is that after the vaccination for six to eight weeks, lymph nodes get enlarged, and that`s part of your-- your immune response. And there are actually lymph nodes in the breast. So you can get false positives if you have a mammogram or other medical tests during that time period. So if you can, if they`re elective, if they`re part of your routine screening, delay it six to eight weeks after the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I talk about the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , the latest thing that we`re hearing there, that now they`re saying the efficacy is now seventy-six percent down from seventy-nine percent. It`s very slight, I get it, but should people be concerned? It seems every day there`s something new about \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 . Are you worried?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: Yeah, I`m worried about the communications group. I mean, this was a problem of omission. They put the-- the data they had in February, but February to March there were another forty-nine cases, and the data went from seventy-nine percent to seventy-six percent. But the data in the elderly went from eighty to eighty-five percent. So these are small changes. It is still an amazing vaccine, and we shouldn`t let this noise affect our acceptance of something that could save our lives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Doctor David Agus, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead and only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, the billionaire owner of the \uc2 Cleveland Cavaliers\uc2 , \uc2 Dan Gilbert\uc2 , talks about his journey of recovery nearly two years after suffering a major health challenge.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are Talk of the Table this morning. And Mister Mason is up first.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2 , one of the wealthiest charities in the world, is making a major announcement. It`s committing fifteen million dollars to help launch the Black Feminist Fund which supports organizations around the world whose work benefits Black women. The fund will be the first of its kind created and led by Black women for Black women. Its priorities include creating safe communities and supporting the leadership of young Black girls. The pandemic has made their efforts even more urgent. Last summer, I asked Ford Foundation President \uc2 Darren Walker\uc2  about the organization`s plan to borrow one billion dollars and give that money to those affected by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DARREN WALKER\uc2  (August 11, 2020): We are at a time when foundations have to do more.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (August 11, 2020): Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 DARREN WALKER\uc2  (August 11, 2020): The question in 2020 for wealthy, privileged people and institutions is not how much do I get back, it`s how much am I willing to give up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: As of last year, the \uc2 Ford\uc2 -- the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2  has a 13.7 billion dollar endowment. That was incredibly daring thing to do, borrow that billion dollars in the middle of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Because-- because as Darren said, people need the money now more than ever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. But it says created and led by Black women for Black women.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Exactly. Underlined.\par \par \uc2 (Cross-talking)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --for us, by us. I like it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got another great story of Black women doing well. Her name is actress Issa Rae. She has just inked a blockbuster deal. This is according to Variety. She signed a five-year deal with \uc2 WarnerMedia\uc2 . \uc2 WarnerMedia\uc2  valued at forty million dollars under the pack. You go, Issa. Issa`s production company could reportedly make TV shows or movies for \uc2 HBO\uc2  and Warner Bros. HBO, as you know, is already the home of Issa`s hit show, it`s called Insecure. I love the show. Rae told Variety they wived me up. When people believe in you and build with you, I tend to want to further that relationship. I like the way she thinks. I`m looking forward to seeing what this marriage could look like, she says. The chief content officer for HBO and HBO Max, that`s Casey Bloys, says it`s been very good to work with Issa personally but also as a company because she has seeded a lot of talent elsewhere. Back in 2016, we spoke with Issa about the impact of her show, Insecure, before its debut.\par \par \uc2 (October 5, 2016): I`ve heard from many young women, Black women in particular, that you are telling their story. Is that the best compliment you could hear?\par \par \uc2 ISSA RAE (October 5, 2016): A hundred percent. And I`m-- I`m just like, where were y`all when I needed friends in middle school, because we could have gone through this together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We love Issa Rae.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s filming her fifth and final season of Insecure. I`m going to miss the show. But I love the name of her production company, it`s Hoorae.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. That`s a great-- that`s a great name.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: H-O-O-R-A-E. I think that`s great.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And I love the-- I love the phrase she used, they wived me up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that. She`s got to come on the show soon. Get rid of these reportedly-- we want to hear from Issa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, we would like that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`d like to see her again. I bet it`s hard to get her now, Tony. It was hard back then, I bet it`s really hard now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, you`re Gayle King. You know people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I`m-- we will definitely ask, please, Issa, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My Talk of the Table is about a helicopter, sort of. Next month a small \uc2 NASA\uc2  helicopter called Ingenuity will attempt the very first powered, controlled flight on another planet. That planet being Mars. But here`s the cool thing--Ingenuity, well, that is cool, but even more cool, Ingenuity is carrying something cooler than the flight itself. It is a postage-sized stamped piece of museling (sic) from the wing of Flyer I. That is the Wright Brothers` plane that took its first historic flight in Kitty Hawk in 1903. It is wrapped around a cable. The reason I think this is so amazing--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --is 1903 is not that long ago.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We were earthbound as a people, then we took flight a hundred years ago, and now we`re going to fly again on another planet--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --in a hundred and twenty-five years. That is amazing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That is extraordinary when you think about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It really is. I`m fascinated by that helicopter, too--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --on Mars.\par \par \uc2 All right. Shifting gears, billionaire businessman \uc2 Dan Gilbert\uc2  is speaking only on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING about tackling some huge challenges. Gilbert, who owns the \uc2 Cleveland Cavaliers\uc2 , founded \uc2 Rocket Companies\uc2 , the parent of the mortgage giant \uc2 Quicken Loans\uc2 . In 2010, Gilbert moved his business headquarters from the Detroit suburbs to downtown. His companies have since acquired and developed more than one hundred properties to help transform the Motor City. Today, he`s announcing a five-hundred-million-dollar initiative to revitalize neighborhoods in his hometown. \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING: SATURDAY co-host Dana Jacobson sat down with Gilbert and his wife in their Detroit area home nearly two years after he suddenly became ill. Dana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON (\uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING: SATURDAY Co-Host): Good morning, Anthony. \uc2 Dan Gilbert\uc2  has always been a determined self-starter. As a kid, he bought candy wholesale and sold it to his friends for a profit. As a teenager, he made Chef Boyardee pizzas in his mom`s kitchen and started a delivery service until the health department shut him down. But when this hard- charging entrepreneur had a stroke nearly two years ago, everything changed in an instant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : I`m feeling good. I`m getting better. It`s like an inch a day, you know. It`s a marathon this thing coming back from it.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: \uc2 Dan Gilbert\uc2 --\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : Welcome to Detroit, everybody.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: --the charismatic billionaire who built a mortgage lending empire and owns the NBA`s \uc2 Cleveland Cavaliers\uc2  is still recovering from the stroke he suffered in May, 2019.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2  (WWJ-TV): It was long enough I`m ready to have a baby, it`s been nine months.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: A journey that`s taught him the importance of gratitude.\par \par \uc2 In that moment where you realized this has all changed, what is that like for someone like you?\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : It`s very scary for anybody. And you start to appreciate everything and everybody much more than you ever did.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Humbling?\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : Yeah, it`s definitely humbling.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You said that just everything is hard when you have a stroke.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : Right. That`s the problem with it. Half your body is-- is gone; in my case, the left half. Luckily, I`m a righty.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Gilbert remembers his near-death experience vividly.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : That night, there happened to be a light show on the river. All of a sudden, I started seeing double vision. I thought it was because of the lights. And luckily, one of our good friends who was there happened to be an ER doctor. I mean, how about that for luck, huh?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Incredible luck.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : But he made sure-- and my wife, as well, made sure we went to the hospital right away.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: His wife, Jennifer, has been by his side throughout his recovery.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : When you have a stroke, it`s like the whole family had the stroke.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It changes the whole dynamic.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GILBERT: It does. But I-- I will say that, you know, in a crisis, our family rallied.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Has the battle to come back been tougher mentally or physically for you?\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : I think it`s been tougher mentally. It plays with you because you always ask yourself every day, is this ever going to be over or not? And you hope it will be.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: But even in the midst of his challenges, Gilbert remains committed to his decade-long mission to help revitalize Detroit.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2  (\uc2 Rocket Companies\uc2 ): We need to gain momentum. We need to have a lot of companies that want to be there because it`s smart for them to be there.\par \par \uc2 I`d like to see the people of Detroit benefit. When you look at the energy level downtown right now, we`ve got to carry that through to the neighborhoods and have the whole city have that energy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So Gilbert and Rocket Companies` CEO \uc2 Jay Farner\uc2  are announcing a new initiative, to invest five hundred million dollars into Detroit`s neighborhoods over the next ten years using the first fifteen million to relieve property tax debt for homeowners.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : It was very clear to us that it was property taxes that caused the majority of the blight in Detroit. And the vast majority of citizens at one point were in tax foreclosure. With interest and penalties, that debt kept building. In some cases, more than they owed on the mortgage, and people would just walk from the houses because they just couldn`t afford it anymore.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That money will cover everyone in the city that`s delinquent?\par \par \uc2 JAY FARNER\uc2  (\uc2 Rocket Companies\uc2 /Quicken Loans CEO): It will cover about twenty thousand homes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JAY FARNER\uc2 : Probably forty thousand or more family members. The idea here was how do we really, A, stabilize the families in Detroit, and then, B, start creating wealth. And the first thing you need to do is make sure that people have a place to live.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: To make sure they understand what the community wants, Jennifer Gilbert participated in listening sessions in Detroit`s neighborhoods which are about eighty percent African-American.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GILBERT: Probably the worst thing we could have done is had everything figured out and written a check. So we did a lot of-- of listening.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: There are people that look at it sometimes that it`s-- here are these people from the suburbs, White people coming in to change a Black community or save the Black community, and we don`t need saving. Are you sensitive to that? How does that even make you feel when that is raised?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GILBERT: Well, the more you listen, the more you learn, and the more you realize you have to learn. As long as we continue with that philosophy and we stay close to it and we stay close to the people and the communities, we can`t help but have a positive impact. We`re empowering the people from within the communities to come up with the solutions.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Whether it`s rebuilding Detroit or recovering from a stroke, Gilbert says he`s learned a big lesson.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : America loves a comeback story, right?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You already knew that with the Cavs. Come on.\par \par \uc2 DAN GILBERT\uc2 : Yeah. There you go.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Has there been a lesson learned here that you would pass on to other people?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GILBERT: You know, I would say it`s just the old cliche of the only thing that matters is your health and your loved ones. You can`t control bad things from happening.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GILBERT: But you control how you respond to them. And so for us, it really is just being mindful of how can we make the best of this situation, and how can we find moments of joy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Gilbert says that the stroke also taught him that he doesn`t have to be the one to do everything, learning to rely on family at home and his team at work. At the time when we spoke a few weeks ago, he was going into the office twice a week, still using a wheelchair, and said he was doing physical therapy three to four hours almost every day to regain strength and movement. Tony, two things that need no rebuilding: his fighting spirit and sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. You can see that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. It definitely came across, Dana. That was a beautiful piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you can also tell how-- how hard a battle is for someone as much of a-- as a go-getter as he is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s a big wall to climb.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just think some cliches are cliches because they`re powerful and true. And the-- the importance of gratitude, how family takes center stage when your health falls away, all these things are just fundamentally right and true. We need reminding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But to go through all that, I have to confess, I didn`t know that it even happened to him. So it`s so good to see that he`s going to be okay. Seems to be okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But even in the middle of all this--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --that he`s still trying to figure out how he can help other people, that`s extraordinary. And Jennifer Gilbert, I thought what she said was so important, that she decided to listen.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because sometimes you think if you have a lot of money, I can show you what`s best for you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I can go and fix this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can fix it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But to listen to what the-- what the people want and how it benefits--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s always-- it`s critical--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So smart to do.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --something like. That it`s-- it`s great what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Says a lot about them as a couple.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Great what they`re doing for Detroit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tens of thousands of people could benefit.\par \par \uc2 All right. Ahead in our series, A More Perfect Union, how some high schoolers are working hard to close the education gap, helping students who`ve been out of the classroom for the past year.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210326eh3p0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Inside Look At The First COVID Vaccine Trial Involving Pregnant Women In The U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, NIKKI BATTISTE \par \uc2 1,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The first coronavirus vaccine trial involving pregnant women is underway in the U.S. and around the world. Pfizer`s study will include four thousand expecting mothers. It will assess vaccine safety and efficacy and look at whether the vaccine can transfer protective antibodies to newborns. The CDC says vaccines should be offered to pregnant women, classifying pregnancy as a risk factor for more severe COVID infections. More than fifty-one thousand pregnant women in the U.S. have already been vaccinated without waiting for those trial results. And I share a household with one of them. Nikki Battiste is following these trials. Nikki, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (CBS News Correspondent): Tony, good morning. Pregnant women have historically been excluded from initial vaccine trials due to concerns about risk to their unborn babies which results in a lack of safety data. But the COVID-19 pandemic made these trials a priority. The women in this \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  trial do not know whether they`re getting a vaccine or a placebo. We spoke with one expectant mother just after she received the first of her two doses.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Have you had any severe reactions to any vaccines or anything?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER (Vaccine Trial Participant): No. Never.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In her eighth month of pregnancy, Kelsey Carpenter rolled up her sleeve for a shot at protection.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All right. Just relax your arm.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Not just for her but more importantly, she says, for her unborn daughter.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried at all? Are you happy you`re doing this?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER: I`m happy I`m doing this. I would be totally off base if I said I wasn`t a little nervous.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The twenty-eight-year-old from Fort Worth, Texas, who is already mom to a toddler, says she and her husband discussed the vaccine extensively before signing up.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER: At the end of the day, it just seemed like the best decision for me and my family.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  trial will include four thousand healthy pregnant women from at least nine countries who are between twenty-four to thirty-four weeks of gestation, half will receive a placebo. Each woman is asked to keep a trial diary, recording any symptoms such as fever. Doctor Cynthia Robbins is Carpenter`s OB/GYN.\par \par \uc2 DR. CYNTHIA ROBBINS (Obstetrician-Gynecologist): Our hope is that by getting the vaccine, it transfers over the immunity through the placenta to the baby.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: That seems to me like that would be a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 DR. CYNTHIA ROBBINS: It is a big deal because their immune systems are so small and weak. So the only immunity they`re ever going to get when they`re born is from what mom passed to them.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Pregnant women are prioritized for vaccines in at least twenty-one states. But a recent poll in the U.S. found that more than half are reluctant to sign up, citing concerns over lack of data.\par \par \uc2 As an OB/GYN, were you nervous at all to administer vaccines to pregnant women?\par \par \uc2 DR. CYNTHIA ROBBINS: No, I wasn`t because I knew that the actual disease, the COVID-19 infection in pregnancy, was going to be worse than in the general population.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  recommends that COVID-19 vaccines note be withheld from pregnant and nursing women. The CDC says no reports of miscarriages, stillbirths, or preterm births have been linked with the vaccines in women who became pregnant while participating in the initial vaccine trials.\par \par \uc2 DR. CYNTHIA ROBBINS: If you`re not happy to be in a trial, don`t do it. I`m just really excited that pregnant women will have this chance to be immunized, for us to make history with some answers.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Carpenter`s daughter is helping pave the way for future generations, too. As part of the trial, newborns will be monitored for their first six months of life.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CARPENTER: That`s what sold me on it. Like, okay, this could help my daughter. This can keep me safe. It just seemed like a win-win.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: There`s at least one documented case in the U.S. of a baby born with COVID-19 antibodies after its mother was vaccinated when she was pregnant. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  told us the women in this trial who receive a placebo will get a real vaccine one month after birth. That`s important because it`s believed nursing mothers pass antibodies to their newborns through breast milk. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  expects the results from their trial in the summer of 2022. Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nikki, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This is such an important study. I mean, we`ve talked about this. And-- and you`ve experienced it personally.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know, many-- many expectant-- expectant mothers are very nervous about this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So this will give them hopefully some comfort.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Important study and a very personal decision.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you pointed out that-- what-- you guys decided to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As my wife, Katy--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As my wife, Katy, decided to do. She was more worried about getting COVID than she was about the side effects. That was her calculation. But everyone will come down differently.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said when you talk to all the doctors, all the doctors say go ahead and do it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. And the CDC points it out that, you know--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --pregnancy is considered a high-risk category for COVID.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Complications--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. If the immunity does transfer to the-- to the baby, though, that`s huge. It really is huge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s big, yeah. That`s really big.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Ahead, we look at the places suddenly left behind a year ago when people fled offices, classrooms, and other spaces to go on lockdown.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is eight-oh-eight, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Women have lost more than five million jobs during this pandemic. Ahead, we`ll follow one woman`s struggle to find work after she was laid off from the travel industry. People aren`t traveling these days. And find out how these massive job losses could permanently change the workforce if nothing is done. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210320eh3j0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Nearly All Abortions Could Be Banned In South Carolina Under A New Law To Be Signed By The State`s Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 ANTHONY MASON, CATHERINE HERRIDGE, GAYLE KING \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nearly all abortions could be banned in South Carolina under a new law to be signed by the state`s governor. The legislation was approved in the state House yesterday after the Senate passed it last month. According to a research group that supports reproductive rights, eleven states have passed a so-called Heartbeat Abortion Ban or something more restrictive, although all have been blocked by courts. Meanwhile, a handful of states have moved to protect or expand abortion rights. Senior investigative correspondent Catherine Herridge is following the story from Washington. Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (CBS News Senior Investigative Correspondent): Well, Anthony, good morning. Planned Parenthood says more than two hundred anti- abortion bills are pending at the state level and thirty states have at least one abortion ban under consideration. Yesterday in South Carolina, the state`s Democratic caucus walked out during debate of the bill which bans abortion if fetal cardiac activity is detected which happens at around six weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD RUTHERFORD (D-South Carolina State Representative): The Democratic caucus will exit the House floor and leave you all to the farce which is a debate about life.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In the end, it was passed by a vote of seventy-nine to thirty-five. There are exceptions for rape, incest, and if a mother`s life is in danger. If signed into law, an abortion provider who violates it could face prison time and a ten-thousand-dollar fine. Data shows a surge in state legislation restricting abortion after the Trump administration confirmed Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in 2018 and more recently Amy Coney Barrett in October. Later this week, the Supreme Court will consider whether to take a case challenging Roe v. Wade which legalized abortion in 1973. If it`s overturned, it would fall back to the states where these more restrictive laws could govern. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Catherine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, our candid-- our candid and emotional interview with singer- songwriter FKA Twigs. She discusses her abuse allegations against her former boyfriend, that`s actor Shia LaBeouf. This is her first TV interview since filing a lawsuit against him.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is eight-oh-four, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in our More Perfect-- we`re having a good time during at the commercial break. Ahead in our More Perfect Union series, Nikki Battiste meets a family that is turning flour into a force for good.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: We`re here in Teaneck, New Jersey, in the home bakery of the Challah Back Girls--four sisters who are fighting two pandemics at once, the coronavirus and racial inequity with their challah. So far, they have sent loaves to forty-two states and raised tens of thousands of dollars. Coming up on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, find out how these four women are uniting our country one challah at a time.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210219eh2i000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 National Wear Red Day To Raise Awareness Of Good Heart Health Among Women\b0\par\par\uc2 ANTHONY MASON, GAYLE KING, DR. TARA NARULA \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: As you may have noticed, today is National Wear Red Day. It`s a day when we`re all encouraged to wear red to help raise awareness for women`s heart health. Cardiovascular disease is the number one killer of women in the U.S. The disease affects about forty-four million. An alarming new survey by the \uc2 Cleveland Clinic\uc2  found coronavirus concerns are preventing some heart disease patients from going to the doctor. CBS News senior medical correspondent Doctor Tara Narula joins us now. She`s a cardiologist and spokesperson for the American Heart Association. Tara, good morning. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthony, she`s wearing red.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She-- is she wearing red?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, she`s wearing red.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tony`s wearing. We`re all wearing red.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA (CBS News Senior Medical Correspondent; Cardiologist; Lenox Hill Hospital-Northwell Health): I am.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tara, the numbers just we mentioned really are alarming. What-- what do women need to know about this is disease?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Well, Anthony, the famous Chinese military leader wrote in "The Art of War," "Know your enemy and know yourself." So one word "awareness." That is how we`re going to win this battle. Women need to understand this is their biggest health threat. So if every woman-- whoever`s listening this morning--put down your coffee, stop what you`re doing and remember these three things: Cardiovascular disease kills one woman every eighty seconds in this country. It kills more women than all forms of cancer combined. And it is the leading cause of maternal mortality. This is a disease that affects women of all ages, all backgrounds. So the same way that it`s ingrained in women to get her Pap smear or her mammogram, we need to start to have women understand that they need to know their cardiovascular risk and have a discussion with their doctor starting early.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What are the risk factors for women, Tara?\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Well, there`s certainly the traditional ones that you`ve heard about. So hypertension, high cholesterol, obesity, diabetes, smoking, alcohol, family history. But then there`s ones that many women don`t often think about. So early menopause, migraine, polycystic ovarian syndrome, lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, depression, anxiety, and finally a really important one, things that happen to a woman during pregnancy. So, gestational hypertension or diabetes, preterm delivery, pre-eclampsia--all of those things are going to raise her risk. So women should start being screened at age twenty. She should know her numbers. And by those we mean blood pressure, cholesterol, body mass index, and blood sugar, and she should know her family history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Tara, you sort of answered the question I was going to ask you, what age twenty seems so young. And does it matter if you have heart history, bad heart history in your family? I`m surprised about the twenty number.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That seems young.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Yeah. I think what`s so surprising to people and what we try to educate them about is that the seeds of cardiovascular disease are planted so early. And so much of it can be silent until the day that you have your heart attack or your stroke. But those-- that disease process is starting at a very early age.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Tara, why do you think so many women ig-- ignore symptoms when-- when they get them?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to know what are the symptoms.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Well, so I can answer both. Symptoms, obviously, most common is chest pain.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DR. TARA NARULA: Pressure, burning, squeezing. But certainly women can also have shortness of breath, decreased exercise tolerance, fatigue, nausea, lightheadedness, passing out, jaw pain, arm pain, back pain. All of those, if they`re new or they`re concerning, should signal a woman to speak up and talk to her doctor. And as far as why they ignore them, I think they may not recognize that this is-- could be signs or symptoms of cardiovascular disease. But also women tend to not prioritize their health, particularly their heart health. And they downplay things. They prioritize their children, their work, everything else. So we need to have women move themselves up to the top of the list. But one other important thing, Anthony and Gayle, is that as a medical community we also downplay. We know that if a woman arrives at the hospital, she`s going to wait longer than a man to be seen for chest pain. Less often she`s going to get aspirin or guideline directed medical therapy or diagnostic testing. This is a big problem. I have a lot of patients telling me my doctor told me I`m just anxious, I`m just stressed out, when in fact they have a real problem. So awareness needs to happen on both sides.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. And women need to keep speaking up and pay attention to your bodies. Thank you very much, Tara. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Every year, as the Super Bowl nears, we like to show how the teams` hometowns compare. Anthony is right when he said this is one of the most fun stories we do every year. And this year`s no different. So Jamie Yuccas got the call to go to Tampa. Michelle Miller, she headed to Kansas City. Ahead, how their friendly competition unfolded. I can`t wait to see this.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s eight-thirty-four, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210207eh250015q\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Lack of data prompts COVID vaccine concerns among pregnant women\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mireya Villarreal \par \uc2 503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, we`re starting a new series, Women & The Pandemic. Coronavirus has taken a devastating toll on the lives of women. More are leaving the workforce, and many are faced with difficult health decisions. CBS` Mireya Villarreal has the first in our series with one of your most-asked questions, should pregnant women get a COVID vaccine?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: After three months in a Wisconsin hospital battling COVID-19, Kelley (sic) Townsend reunited with her husband late last week and met her twelve-week-old daughter, Lucy, for the first time.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY TOWNSEND: I love you so much.\par \par \uc2 They kept me asleep for almost two months, so my body could start healing.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: You were near death.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY TOWNSEND: I was.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: At thirty-nine weeks, Lucy was delivered via C-section while her mom was in a medically induced coma.\par \par \uc2 Would you have taken the vaccine if it had been available to you?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY TOWNSEND: I`m all for vaccines. We just need to be careful and go off of what experts are telling us.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: But the guidance has been hard to interpret because pregnant women were excluded from the vaccine studies, so there`s no safety data available. The CDC, the WHO and the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  say pregnant women should discuss risk factors with their doctors and make a decision themselves. Still, some expectant mothers are on the fence.\par \par \uc2 CHARLENE MCCRANEY: It`s definitely hard to decipher what you should be doing.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Charlene McCraney is nineteen weeks along and decided not to get the shot.\par \par \uc2 CHARLENE MCCRANEY: I think the-- the main thing that comes up with moms, it`s long-term birth defects.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENNA HUGHES (\uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 ): Protecting the mom protects the child.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Doctor Brenna Hughes says the COVID vaccines do not contain live virus and are unlikely to cross the placenta.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRENNA HUGHES: The vaccine is not thought to be directly delivered to the fetus. It really is geared towards keeping the mom healthy.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Studies are now underway on how the vaccine could affect mothers and their babies.\par \par \uc2 What would you tell pregnant women about this virus and your experience?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY TOWNSEND: To be extremely cautious. It almost ended my life.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL: Mireya Villarreal, CBS News, Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we`re going to continue our series tomorrow, focusing on the economic toll.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210203eh210008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 A Michigan Couple Is Being Forced To Adopt Their Own Biological Child After They Were Born Through A Surrogate, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 3,580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a bizarre legal case, a Michigan couple is being forced to adopt their own biological children after they were born through a surrogate. Jordan and Tammy Myers` twins were born two weeks ago today, but two judges denied them the legal rights to the infants citing a decades-old anti-surrogacy law. Nikki Battiste has been looking at the very strange case. Nikki, good morning to you. This is just bizarre.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (CBS News Correspondent): it`s really bizarre. Tony, good morning. Tammy and Jordan Meyer were hoping for a sibling for their two- year-old daughter when Tammy was diagnosed with breast cancer several years ago. Her medical treatment left her unable to go through another pregnancy, so the couple found a gestational surrogate to carry their biological child. But now to get the legal rights to their two-week-old twins, they have to adopt them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TAMMY MYERS (Mother): Initially I, you know, I-- I thought, I`m too young and there`s no way it could be breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Nearly six years ago, Tammy Myers learned she was in a battle for her life.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY MYERS: My first two questions for her were, how long do I have? And my second question was, can I have more children? Can I still have another baby?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Now, Tammy and her husband Jordan are facing a new fight-- this time for the rights to their newborn twins. Tammy froze her eggs after her breast cancer diagnosis. She is doing better, and in 2019 she and her husband decided to grow their family. They turned to gestational surrogacy.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever think you would be in the situation you are having to adopt your own two children?\par \par \uc2 TAMMY MYERS: We really, truly didn`t believe that someone could-- could hear the history and not give us rights.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Tammy and Jordan found their surrogate, Lauren Vermilye, after posting on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Lauren, who is married with two children of her own, lived nearby and volunteered to carry the Myers` child for free.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN VERMILYE (Surrogate): I lost my dad to pancreatic cancer eleven years ago. And just, I know what cancer can take away from you. And just to be able to help bring that hope back to somebody just really, really appealed to me and my husband.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Soon after, two of the Myers` embryos were transferred to Lauren. They learned they were having twins. The process was going well until the couple tried to get legal parental rights.\par \par \uc2 You were denied the pre-birth rights, and then also, Jordan, you were denied.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN MYERS (Father): My rights as a father, as well, yeah.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Two judges in Kent County, Michigan dismissed the Myers` efforts to be named the legal parents. They cited Michigan Surrogate Parenting Act of 1988, which states surrogacy contracts are ".void and unenforceable." One judge wrote that there are ".genuine concerns." about the 1988 act, but those concerns are better left to the legislative and political arena.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN MYERS: I just sobbed. It`s all I could do. I don`t understand it. It doesn`t make any sense.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Eames and Ellison Myers were born January 11th, a few weeks premature. Their birth certificate names the surrogate, Lauren, and her husband as the parents.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN VERMILYE: Michigan law states that I am a married woman. And so, of course, my husband would be the father of my children, which they are not my children either.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Melissa Neckers is the Myers` attorney. She said since 2005, there have been at least seventy-two cases in Michigan where judges have granted parents in similar situations pre-birth legal rights to their biological children.\par \par \uc2 Did they understand the risk that they faced based not only on Michigan`s law, but also that they had a stranger as a gestational carrier?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA NECKERS (Family Attorney): They knew that there was risk. But they really believed that no judge would actually hear their story, which had so many additional layers of heartache and trauma, that a judge wouldn`t just want to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Only two other states have similar strict surrogacy laws, Nebraska and Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 BARBRA HOMIER: Michigan would be the riskiest state to ever try and do a surrogacy contract.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Michigan family law attorney Barbra Homier said the Myers may have been able to avoid this if they went to another state.\par \par \uc2 BARBRA HOMIER: It`s also very expensive to go out of state, hire lawyers and find surrogates and do everything else, right, to-- to go out of state.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Do you think the law in Michigan needs to be updated?\par \par \uc2 BARBRA HOMIER: Yeah, I do. Some say legalize gay marriage, too. This is a much bigger issue. There are a lot of couples who want to have kids and they need to use surrogates to do that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: To adopt their children, the Myers will now have to go through months of home inspections, paperwork, and even an \uc2 FBI\uc2  background check. They are speaking out to help others.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN MYERS: Just want the law to be updated.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY MYERS: Our hope is that we will be the last family in Michigan in this situation where we are being denied our own biological children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The Myers currently cannot get the twins on their health insurance. The babies are still in the newborn ICU, but they are doing well and will hopefully go home to their parents and big sister Corryn soon. We reached out to a clerk for both judges, but we have not heard back. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s very strange. One thing after another. We`ll see if the political arena picks it up. Nikki, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, some of the more creative mega vaccination sites all around the world.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, supermodel Maye Musk made history at sixty-nine years old when she became the face of CoverGirl. You go, Mrs. Musk. She`ll talk about her long career and raising three very successful children, including somebody named Elon, we know him.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eight thirty, Talk of the Table time. Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. Anthony Mason, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We all-- we all know that music venues have had a really tough time--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --getting through COVID. Well, that includes New York City`s legendary Birdland Jazz Club which is struggling to stay open. Some big names, though, appeared last night for a virtual concert to help raise money. Singer Norm Lewis performed. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Norm Lewis singing; Save Birdland)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The Broadway star, film star, Norm, we love him. Other performers joined the cause, including Leslie Odom Junior, Jeff Daniels, \uc2 Wynton Marsalis\uc2 . They also-- we also heard from former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , Elvis Costello and Sting. Birdland opened back in 1949. It closed in `65 but reopened in 1986 and has been open ever since. Some of the legends who played there, Thelonious Monk, Miles Davis, Billie Holiday, and online fundraiser has raised more than three hundred and eighty-two thousand dollars to save Birdland. That`s the good news. It`s really an extraordinary room. We used it when I interviewed Iggy Pop last year for the show. Iggy really wanted to be there because it`s got such an incredible vibe there. That`s the room there where we were at.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, the (INDISTINCT).\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. And so I-- you know, it`s-- it`s so many clubs are going through there right now. It`s great to see the people turn up. Sting said, you know, jazz music is one of the things that made America great. You can`t lose Birdland. And-- and I know a lot of New Yorkers feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we know how great Leslie Odom sings because he was here the other day. But it was great to show Norm Lewis.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love his voice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He sang for-- he sang for our show out at Lincoln Center.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We`re doing his story on that, so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that. I love his voice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really nice guy.\par \par \uc2 Someone became an instant billionaire over the weekend. Was it you? Do you live in Michigan? Do you live in Novi, Michigan? There was one grand prize winner in the Mega Millions lottery jackpot worth more than one billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The ticket close to one-billion-dollar. The ticket was sold at \uc2 Kroger\uc2  supermarket in Novi, Michigan, near Detroit. So, the one-time cash payment would be eight hundred and seventy-eight million dollars if you took it in thirty-year payments or if you take it in one lump sum then you get five hundred--where is that figure--five hundred and fifty-seven million dollars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Five hundred and a whole lot of--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Five hundred and fifty-seven million after taxes. So, here`s a question on the table--would you go with the thirty-year option? Because then you get more.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This is a problem I`d like to have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then you get more or you go with the lump sum. I-- I would go with the lump sum.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would take the lump sum.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`d take my money now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know if I`m going to be here-- I`m not going to be here in thirty years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I would-- I would hire a very good investment manager and I would put that money to work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the thing in Michigan, you have to come forward and identify yourself. But you have a year to do so. So you got time to change your numbers, move, get a new lawyer, get a new finance person.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`d change my name, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. Really. Can you wear a costume? You have to show your face and name, really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have to give your identity. Yep.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have a year to do it, Tony. So, if you needed plastic surgery in the meantime, you could do it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So, you don`t have to tell us, and you can think about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think you get the plastic surgery after maybe. Come out as yourself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then the name change.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. I got some canine news because this morning, the first dogs are waking up inside the White House for the first time. Champ and Major are their names. They are German shepherds. They arrived yesterday. And the first lady`s office tells us that Champ is, quote, "enjoying his new dog bed by the fireplace." Major, meanwhile, there`s Major, loves running around on the South Lawn. Very sweet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No word on who picks up the South Lawn after he runs around out there. But that`s another story. Maybe someone could do these chores.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-Huh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s the first shelter dog to move into the White House. And some history about dogs in the White House. The White House still was not finished. It was still under construction when the first dogs came in in 1800. Another fun fact, President Trump was the first President in more than a hundred years not to have a dog in the White House.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Said he likes dogs. Said he likes dogs, but didn`t have time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There is staff at the White House to take care of some of those--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s what I was thinking about with the South Lawn problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING Joe Biden and Jill Biden are not-- they are not on pooty duty. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are not-- they are not picking up those presents?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`re sure about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go, Chopper.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go, Chopper.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is very--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the dogs are good looking, the Biden dogs.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. Very handsome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good-looking dogs. I like that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Great to see dogs back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love to see that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Maye Musk is a supermodel, registered dietician, nutritionist and public speaker. She`s also the mom of three successful children, including Elon Musk. You know the name, billionaire founder of \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and co-founder of Tesla. Her modeling career spans nearly six decades. Maye Musk first appeared in catalogs as a teenager. She then landed magazine covers and walked in designer runway shows at age sixty- nine. She made history when she became the oldest spokes model in CoverGirl history. Her memoir, "A Woman Makes a Plan: Advice for a Lifetime of Adventure, Beauty, and Success," is out now in paperback. And Maye Musk joins us. Good morning. Welcome. Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK (Author, "A Woman Makes a Plan"/Supermodel): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I was struck by--\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Lovely to be with you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s great to be with you. Your family has a history of adventure. Your father loved adventure, but he also had a motto which was: "live dangerously carefully." Can you explain?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: I know. Yes. Well, we were the only family to go across the Kalahari Desert for three weeks seeing no one except lots of animals, and with three-week supply of water, gas, and food. And I think that is quite dangerous. We did come across some graves of people who had died in the desert. And I think-- and he was always very careful and planned ahead. And so did my mom. And that was his motto.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you also-- you also caution in the book that-- that you shouldn`t plan too far ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, the thing is I always plan, and it never comes right. And so then you make another plan. And you always have to have more plans. So when I say a woman makes a plan, you can plan your life, and if it`s not going the way you want it to do-- to go, then you need to change your plan. And you need to be in a good situation, and I hope people will learn from my mistakes so that they get out of a bad situation quicker than I did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. You got to be nimble. You got to be ready for what life throws at you. You-- you also say that in writing the book-- you-- and looking back at-- at your life and your children growing up that you realized all of your children, essential by the age of twelve, had kind of locked into where they were going in life. Which made me curious, what-- what was Elon-- a young Elon Musk at twelve already doing that suggested what he might do in the rest of his life?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, at twelve he actually made a computer program and a game. And I showed it to the university students and engineering students and they said, wow, he knows all the shortcuts. So, I said to him, well, you should submit this to a magazine. And he did, and then he got five hundred grant, which is like five hundred dollars. And I don`t think they knew he was twelve. So that was a good start. And then Kimbal was cooking for us already because he didn`t like my food. I`m not a great cook. And so that`s why he has restaurants. And then he also does vegetable gardens in underserved schools because he feels children should always have vegetables and fruit. And then, of course, Tosca was in the movies. He just loved every movie, remembered every one. And she wanted to get into the movie business, which I discouraged a lot, of course. Who wants to be in the movie business? And now she has her own platform, Passionflix. So, they are all doing the work that they love doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hey, Maye Musk, listen, I`m not a good cook either. My children will have no fond memories of the anything I ever made. And your kids seem to have turned out okay. So when did you know that Elon was a genius child? I mean, as he sits here today as an adult, you know, all of your children are well accomplished. But the name we know most is Elon Musk. When did you know that he was going do great things?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, at three I knew he was a genius. But you still don`t know if he`s going to do great things. Because many geniuses just end up in a basement being a genius but not-- not applying it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s true. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And right. So I was so excited when he started, too, because it just made life easier with door-to-door directions, and the newspapers could have a link that took you to a restaurant. I mean, I know that`s common now, but that was highly unusual. And people didn`t believe that that`s possible. And so that`s why I invested in that at the very beginning, although I didn`t have much money which we managed to survive. And then, of course, then he thought the banking system needed some help. So then, he did \uc2 PayPal\uc2 . And then after that, he said, well, should I do space--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: --research or solar energy or electric cars? And I said, just choose one. And, of course, he didn`t listen to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was-- he was always thinking-- he was always thinking outside the box. Do you have a Tesla, Mrs. Musk? Do you have a Tesla?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Always. Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. I bet you got.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: He gives me the best. He gives me--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I bet you got the nice one. But the other thing that`s so impressive about you, you are a model, you`re seventy-two. And you-- you-- your hash tag on social media is # #ItsGreatToBe72. I think it such a great strong message and empowering message to send to women. Have you always embraced your age? What-- what is it about you that you embraced your age?\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: Well, my mother never had a problem with age. Nobody could tell as she was getting hold. She did retire at ninety-six. But then before that, she never spoke about wrinkles or getting old or anything. She was just always positive and always learning, educating herself. And, I guess, that`s a good example.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And then when I-- as I say, as I got into my sixties, people were talking about aging and being scared of aging. And I`m saying, why are you scared of aging? I mean, for example, when you turn fifty, then women are scared of losing their jobs. But then men become CEOs and presidents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: So, what`s that about?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: We need to change that around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK: And-- and if somebody`s making you feel bad about your age, just say goodbye. Goodbye. You don`t need that person in your life.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I like that philosophy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Maye musk, thank you so much for being with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, massive COVID-19 vaccine sites are popping up in unique places around the world, including an eight-hundred-year-old cathedral in England and an ice rink in Berlin. How countries are getting creative to vaccinate as many people as possible.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210126eh1p001xh\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\